msgid "The performance test is built for native, wasm (run by `moonrun`) and js (run by node), which is how the runner measures the floating side on those runtimes (`--target`). It is not built for wasm-gc: the module initialiser of the `moonbitlang/async` dependency (a cast between two struct types) is rejected by the runtime at validation there. The environment variables and the files are read the same way on the three targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/bench/external.md:73
msgid "Six plan tests run in normal test runs (`external input rows group by case`, `external binary and decimal_gda plans compile`, `external operand parsing is exact`, `external validation compares text: the sign of zero, the cohort and a wrong value fail`, `canonical binary text: the formats of MPFR and of to_hex agree on one value`, `canonical decimal text: one value, the cohort and the sign of zero are kept`). The performance test `external baseline floating` is marked `#skip(\"performance benchmark\")` and builds on native, wasm and js; the runner prints a `MAREMARK_JSONL=` line for every event, `EXTBENCH_CASE=kind/op/size round=R passed=P failed=F` for every case, and `EXTBENCH_FAILED=N` at the end."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/bench/interval.md:1
msgid "bench/interval API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/bench/interval.md:5
msgid "`bench/interval` holds the interval arithmetic benchmarks of `floating`. It is a test-only package: all content is in `*_test.mbt` files built on the [`bench`](../bench.md) toolkit, and it exports no MoonBit items. This page records the benchmark cases it defines; the [tutorial](../../tutorial/bench/interval.md) shows how to run them and the [design page](../../design/bench/interval.md) explains the workloads."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/bench/interval.md:36
msgid "Every specification has a plan test that runs in normal test runs and checks that it compiles (`interval benchmark plans compile`), and a performance test marked `#skip(\"performance benchmark\")` that only `tools/benchmark.py` runs. In the reported lines `DATASET` is the 0-based index into the datasets column. The performance tests stream every observation as a `MAREMARK_JSONL=` line and print the reduced results as `MAREMARK_HOTSPOT=interval/OP/DATASET core_pct=… full_pct=…`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1
msgid "binary API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:5
msgid "`binary` is the binary floating-point core of `floating`. A `Binary` represents a signed dyadic number $(-1)^s \\cdot c \\cdot 2^{e}$ with an arbitrary-precision coefficient and an attached working precision. It also has the IEEE 754 special values: signed zeros, infinities, and quiet or signaling NaNs with payloads. Every arithmetic operation rounds the exact result once. `BinaryContext` sets an operation's precision, exponent range, rounding direction and tininess rule. The `*_ctx` methods return the value with the IEEE status flags. The [tutorial](../tutorial/binary.md) shows common workflows; the [design page](../design/binary.md) derives the rounding, range and certification rules used here. The verified IEEE 754 scope is listed in [conformance](../conformance/binary.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:17
msgid "Throughout this page, $p$ is a precision in bits, $\\circ(x)$ is the rounding of the real number $x$ under the active context, and \"the flags\" are the five IEEE exception flags of a `BinaryFlags` value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:23
msgid "Add the package to your `moon.pkg`, together with the two packages whose types appear in its signatures:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:34
msgid "The examples call the package as `@binary.`. `@lf_arith` is `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`. Its `RoundingMode`, `ArithmeticContext` and `ArithmeticError` types appear in signatures; the interface file prints their package as `@arithmetic`. `@def` is `Luna-Flow/floating/def`, which provides `Sign`, `PartialOrder` and the `Floating` trait. No helper functions are shared between examples."
msgstr ""

