msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:51
msgid "A plain binary operation uses the larger precision of its two operands; `fma` uses the largest precision of its three operands. It uses the extendable precision format of IEEE 754-2019 3.7 specified by $p$ alone (see `BinaryContext`). Its exponent range is the implementation range below, so overflow produces infinity and underflow produces a signed zero or a tiny value only at about $2^{\\pm 2^{30}}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:58
msgid "Values and limits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:62
msgid "The largest and smallest exponent of the leading bit that a finite `Binary` may carry."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:70
msgid "The constants are $2^{30}-1 = 1073741823$ and $1 - (2^{30}-1) = -1073741822$. Thus $e_{\\min} = 1 - e_{\\max}$, as IEEE 754-2019 3.3 requires of every format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:74
msgid "A context given by its precision alone, and every plain operation, uses these constants for $e_{\\max}$ and $e_{\\min}$. A result whose leading bit lies above $e_{\\max}$ overflows. Below $e_{\\min}$, it is subnormal with quantum $2^{e_{\\min}-p+1}$. The context given by its precision alone has this same range, and every stored value is a member of that context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:82
msgid "The largest precision accepted by `BinaryContext` and the largest precision carried by `Binary`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:85
msgid "Every constructor that accepts a precision checks it against `1..=binary_precision_max`. A value-returning constructor aborts and reports the constructor, the range and the invalid value. A constructor that returns a `Result` (`from_string`, `from_hex`) or a `BinaryChecked` returns an `ArithmeticError` of kind `DomainError`. A precision is never replaced by another one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:96
msgid "It is $2^{28} = 268435456$ bits. Together with the exponent range it keeps every coefficient exponent inside the 32-bit `Int` range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:99
msgid "Exact coefficients"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:103
msgid "`BinCoeff` is a non-negative arbitrary-precision integer used as a `Binary` coefficient and as the bit pattern of an interchange encoding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:110
msgid "The inner type `@binary_kernel.Coeff` belongs to the internal package `internal/binary_kernel`; only code in `Luna-Flow/floating` can import that package. The inner type carries no compatibility promise. Outside the module, `BinCoeff` is abstract: a user cannot call a method on the kernel value, read it, compare it or build one. Neither its constructor nor its field is visible outside `binary`. Other packages in the library use the two functions to pass the kernel value to or from `BinCoeff`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:118
msgid "The coefficient is exact. On native, LLVM and Wasm targets, it uses an inline 64-bit or 128-bit word or an array of 64-bit limbs; on JavaScript, it uses a host `bigint`. Callers cannot observe the representation. The [design page](../design/binary.md#the-coefficient-kernel) describes the algorithm selection: schoolbook, Karatsuba, Toom-3, number-theoretic transform and staged division. `Debug` prints the integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:124
msgid "`BinCoeff::to_kernel`, `BinCoeff::from_kernel` (internal bridge)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:126
msgid "`to_kernel` and `from_kernel` are bridge functions between `BinCoeff` and the kernel type for use by packages in this module. Code outside `Luna-Flow/floating` cannot name the kernel type. The functions carry no compatibility promise because the type they exchange may change in any release."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:136
msgid "`to_kernel` and `from_kernel` carry `#internal`, which changes neither the generated interface nor the documentation tool; the heading and doc comments mark them as internal. Both are free functions. `BinCoeff` is a one-field tuple structure over the kernel type, so the bridge reads or wraps that field."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:143
msgid "Build a coefficient from a machine value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:153
msgid "Convert between a coefficient and digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:161
msgid "`parse` reads digits in base `radix`, which defaults to 10. Letters represent digits above 9, and one leading `+` is optional. It returns `Err` for an empty string, a `-` sign, a digit outside the radix, or a radix outside $[2, 36]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:165
msgid "`to_string` prints base 10. `to_radix_string` prints in any radix in $[2, 36]$, using lower-case letters and no prefix; it aborts for another radix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:170
msgid "Convert between a coefficient and big-endian bytes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:177
msgid "`to_bytes_be` uses the fewest bytes needed to represent the coefficient. `from_bytes_be` ignores leading zero bytes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:182
msgid "Return the value as a `UInt64`, or `None` if it needs more than 64 bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:190
msgid "Queries."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:197
msgid "`bit_length` returns $\\lfloor \\log_2 c \\rfloor + 1$ for $c > 0$, and $0$ for zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:202
msgid "Exact comparison."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:209
msgid "Compare two coefficients and test whether they are equal. `compare` returns $-1$, $0$ or $1$. For natural numbers, `equal` compares values, which is the same as comparing their representations. The operators `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `==` and `!=` use these methods; see [Trait implementations](#trait-implementations)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:214
msgid "`BinCoeff` also implements `Eq`, `Compare`, `Show` and `Debug`. Its promoted trait methods are listed under [Trait implementations](#trait-implementations). `BinCoeff` has no arithmetic, shift, bitwise or GCD operations: they are internal to the library (the package `internal/binary_kernel`). Arithmetic on values is done with `Binary` or, on a coefficient read with `coefficient()`, after converting it with `to_string` or `to_bytes_be`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:233
msgid "The value type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:237
msgid "`Binary` represents a finite dyadic number, a signed infinity or a NaN, and carries a working precision. Its representation, `@binary_kernel.BinRepr`, is internal, carries no compatibility promise and is read only inside the package. Like `BinCoeff`, the representation uses an internal kernel type. A value is one heap object."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:248
msgid "A finite `Binary` is $(-1)^s \\cdot c \\cdot 2^{e}$ with $s$ the sign bit, $c$ a `BinCoeff` and $e$ = `exponent2()`. Every value built through this API has a canonical representation: a nonzero $c$ is odd (factors of two are moved into $e$), a zero has $c = 0$ and $e = 0$ and keeps its sign, and $c$ has at most `precision()` bits. The precision is an attribute of the value: plain operations work at the larger precision of their operands, and contextual operations stamp the context precision on their result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:256
msgid "`==` agrees with `compare`: `a == b` exactly when `a.compare(b) == 0`. This comparison tests whether the values are the same number, regardless of precision: for example, `one(precision=53) == one(precision=24)` and `-0 == +0`. Every NaN equals every other NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:262
msgid "The `<`, `<=` and sorting order is a total preorder: every NaN compares equal to every other NaN and above every number. `Decimal` uses the same rule. It is not IEEE equality: use `compare_quiet_equal` (IEEE 754-2019 5.6.1, 5.11) when a NaN must be unordered. `Binary` has no `Hash`. Use `identical` to compare representations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:274
msgid "`identical` compares the representation: class, sign (including the sign of a zero), coefficient, exponent and precision. For a NaN it also compares the signaling bit and payload. It distinguishes `-0` from `+0`, precision 53 from precision 24 for the same value, and NaNs with different kinds or payloads."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:279
msgid "Use `identical` when a result is checked as a datum, such as when its zero sign or the precision stamped by a contextual operation matters. `identical` is what `==` was before 0.12. Use `==` when the number is what matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:285
msgid "Builds a finite number $(-1)^{\\text{negative}} \\cdot c \\cdot 2^{e}$ rounded to `precision` bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:292
msgid "The arguments are the coefficient, exponent $e$ and precision. If the coefficient has more significant bits than the precision, `make` rounds it with `mode`, which defaults to `RoundTiesToEven`. The result is in canonical form. A precision outside `1..=binary_precision_max` aborts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:297
msgid "If a value lies outside the implementation exponent range, `make` replaces it as an overflow or underflow would. Above the range, it returns $\\pm\\infty$ or, for a mode that rounds toward zero, the largest finite magnitude $(2^p - 1)\\,2^{e_{\\max} - p + 1}$. Below the smallest implementation subnormal $2^{e_{\\min} - p + 1}$, it returns $\\pm 0$ or, for a mode that rounds away from zero, that subnormal. With `RoundTiesToEven` and `RoundTiesToAway`, it returns the subnormal when the value exceeds half of it. `RoundTiesToAway` also returns the subnormal when the value equals half."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:306
msgid "`make` rounds precision and range together in `mode`. For a value between the smallest subnormal and $2^{e_{\\min}}$, it keeps only bits on the subnormal grid $2^{e_{\\min} - p + 1}$, with at least one bit. The stored value therefore has no bit below that grid. This matters only at magnitudes near $2^{-2^{30}}$. `apply_ctx` also reports the flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:314
msgid "Build a value from an integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:321
msgid "Both constructors default to precision 53. When the integer has more significant bits than the precision, the constructor rounds to nearest-even. `from_int(n)` is exact for every `Int` at the default precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:327
msgid "Decode a host binary64 or binary32 value exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:334
msgid "The default precisions are 53 and 24 bits, so these conversions are exact. `from_double` preserves signed zeros, infinities, the NaN sign, the quiet and signaling distinction, and the NaN payload (the fraction field without the quiet bit)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:339
msgid "`from_float` first converts the `Float` to a host `Double`, then decodes it. Numbers, zeros and infinities are unaffected. For a NaN, it keeps the low $p - 2$ bits of the binary64 payload after conversion (the binary32 payload times $2^{29}$). At the default precision of 24, the payload is 0 and a signaling NaN becomes quiet. On the native target, a signaling `Float` NaN arrives as a quiet NaN. Use `BinaryInterchange::from_hex` with `Binary32` to decode binary32 NaNs bit-exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:347
msgid "The value of `from_double(0.1)` is the value that the host already rounded, $3602879701896397 \\cdot 2^{-55}$, not one tenth. Use `from_string` to round a decimal literal directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:353
msgid "Build the constants $+0$, $-0$, $1$ and $\\pm\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:362
msgid "The default precision is 53 and must be in `1..=binary_precision_max` (see `binary_precision_max`). `inf(negative=true)` returns $-\\infty$; the default returns $+\\infty$, as `Decimal::inf` does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:368
msgid "Build NaNs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:376
msgid "`nan()` returns a positive quiet NaN with payload 0. A signaling NaN always has a nonzero payload; the default and a requested payload of 0 both become 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:379
msgid "A NaN of precision $p$ has $p - 2$ payload bits. IEEE 754-2019 6.2.1 gives the trailing significand field $p - 1$ bits, the first of which is the quiet bit; there are no payload bits when $p \\le 2$. `quiet_nan` and `signaling_nan` abort if the payload is too wide. `signaling_nan` also aborts when $p < 3$, because a signaling NaN needs a nonzero payload bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:385
msgid "Conversions never abort: a payload that the destination cannot hold keeps its low bits (IEEE 754-2019 6.2.3). A signaling NaN becomes quiet if its payload does not survive. This rule applies to `with_precision`, `from_double`, `from_float`, `from_hex` (an `snan` literal below precision 3 becomes a quiet NaN), every operation that returns a quiet NaN under a context (which cuts the payload to the context precision), and interchange encodings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:392
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:311
msgid "Observing a value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:396
msgid "Report the class and sign of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:404
msgid "`classify` returns `Finite`, `Infinity` or `NaN`; zero is `Finite`. `signum` returns `Zero` for every zero and NaN, and `Positive` or `Negative` otherwise. `is_sign_minus` returns the sign bit, which is set for $-0$, $-\\infty$ and negative NaNs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:409
msgid "The `Floating` trait supplies `@def.is_finite`, `@def.is_nan`, `@def.is_infinite` and `@def.is_zero` for `Binary`. The next section lists methods that return the same answers without the trait."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:415
msgid "Return the ten-valued class of IEEE 754-2019 5.7.2 and classify normal and subnormal values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:427
msgid "`class_of` returns a value of [`IeeeClass`](def.md#ieeeclass), as shown in the table."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:429
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1144
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1889
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1986
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:261
#: manual/api/def.md:46
#: manual/performance/binary.md:139
msgid "Input"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:429
#: manual/design/interval.md:702
#: manual/performance_audit.md:25
msgid "Class"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:431
msgid "signaling NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:432
#: manual/api/binary.md:2035
msgid "quiet NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:433
msgid "negative infinity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:434
msgid "negative normal value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:435
msgid "negative subnormal value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:436
msgid "negative zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:437
msgid "positive zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:438
msgid "positive subnormal value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:439
msgid "positive normal value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:440
msgid "positive infinity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:442
msgid "A nonzero finite number is normal when the exponent of its leading bit is at least the context's $e_{\\min}$; below that exponent, it is subnormal. `Binary` carries no format, so the context determines the class. Use `BinaryInterchangeFormat::context` for the context of a binary interchange format. A context given by its precision alone has the implementation limit `binary_value_e_min` as `e_min`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:449
msgid "`is_normal` and `is_subnormal` return true exactly for the normal and subnormal classes. `is_finite` is true for zeros, subnormals and normals; `is_infinite` is true for either infinity, and `is_nan` for either NaN. None of `class_of` and these predicates rounds or raises a flag. `classify` keeps its three-valued meaning."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:456
msgid "Special-value predicates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:466
msgid "`nan_payload` is zero for any value other than a NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:470
msgid "Return the stored representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:478
msgid "For a finite number, apply the sign to `coefficient()` $\\times 2^{\\texttt{exponent2()}}$. The coefficient and exponent returned for an infinity or NaN have no meaning."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:482
msgid "For a value with at most 128 coefficient bits, `coefficient()` builds a `BinCoeff` from the one or two machine words that store the coefficient, so the call allocates. A wider value returns its stored coefficient. A loop that reads coefficients from small values therefore allocates once per call."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:487
msgid "`Binary` has no public word reader. Use `BinCoeff::to_uint64` when the coefficient fits in 64 bits. The exponent of the leading bit, which is what most callers read the coefficient for, is `ilogb()`, which does not allocate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:493
msgid "Return the canonical representation of a finite number at its own precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:499
msgid "Public constructors already return canonical values, so `normalized` leaves them unchanged. It also returns infinities and NaNs unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:504
msgid "Rounds a value to a new working precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:510
msgid "A finite number with more significant bits than the new precision is rounded in the given direction, including ties to away, as specified by `BinaryRoundingMode`. A zero or infinity keeps its value and takes the new precision. A NaN keeps the low $p-2$ bits of its payload at precision $p$; a signaling NaN becomes quiet if its payload does not survive."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:516
msgid "`with_precision` reports no flags. Use `apply_ctx` when flags matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:520
msgid "Returns the unit in the last place of a finite number at its own precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:526
msgid ""
"For $x \\n"
"e 0$, let $t = \\lfloor \\log_2 |x| \\rfloor$ be the exponent of its leading bit. `ulp` returns $2^{t-p+1}$. For zero it returns $2^{1-p}$; for an infinity or NaN it returns a quiet NaN. The spacing ignores any context's exponent range, so it is not the subnormal spacing of an IEEE format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:554
msgid "Plain arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:558
msgid "The four operations and negation, also available as `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and unary `-`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:574
msgid "The result is the exact sum, difference, product or quotient rounded once to nearest-even at the larger operand precision, with the implementation exponent range. Special values follow IEEE 754: NaNs propagate (the first NaN operand, quieted), $\\infty - \\infty$, $0 \\cdot \\infty$, $0/0$ and $\\infty/\\infty$ are NaN, and a nonzero number divided by zero is a signed infinity. Flags are discarded; use the [contextual forms](#contextual-arithmetic) to observe them. `neg` flips the sign bit of every value, including zeros and NaNs, and never rounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:585
msgid "Clear or copy the sign bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:592
msgid "Both are quiet IEEE sign-bit operations: they never round, never raise a flag and keep NaN payloads. `x.copy_sign(y)` has the magnitude of `x` and the sign bit of `y`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:598
msgid "Correctly rounded square root."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:604
msgid "`x.sqrt()` rounds $\\sqrt{x}$ to nearest-even at the precision of `x`: it is `sqrt_ctx` under the working context and never fails. An invalid operation (IEEE 754-2019 7.2: a negative nonzero argument, $-\\infty$ included, or a signaling NaN) is a quiet NaN, and the flag is read through `sqrt_ctx(...).1.invalid_operation()`. $\\sqrt{-0} = -0$, and a quiet NaN of either sign gives a quiet NaN. For a square root at another precision or rounding direction use `sqrt_ctx`; its results under `RoundTowardNegative` and `RoundTowardPositive` enclose $\\sqrt{x}$ and coincide when the root is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:615
msgid "Raise a value to an integer power."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:621
msgid "The result is $x^n$ correctly rounded to nearest-even at the precision of `x`, computed as in `pown_ctx`, and never fails. A zero base with a negative exponent is an infinity (the sign of the base for an odd exponent; `pown_ctx(...).1.division_by_zero()` is the pole), and a signaling NaN base a quiet NaN for every exponent, zero included (IEEE 754-2019 §9.2.1 excludes it from $x^0 = 1$; `invalid_operation` through `pown_ctx`); $x^0 = 1$ for every other $x$, a quiet NaN included."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:631
msgid "Fused multiply-add: $x \\cdot y + z$ with a single rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:637
msgid "The precision is the largest of the three operand precisions. See [`fma_ctx`](#binaryfma_ctx) for the special cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:642
msgid "IEEE 754 remainder $x - n y$, where $n$ is the integer nearest $x/y$ with ties to even."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:649
msgid "The result is exact whenever it fits the larger operand precision, which is always the case for operands of that precision (the proof is on the [design page](../design/binary.md#ieee-remainder-is-exact)). See [`remainder_ctx`](#binaryremainder_ctx)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:668
msgid "Contexts, rounding and flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:672
msgid "The rounding-direction attribute of a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:685
msgid "These are the five IEEE 754-2019 rounding-direction attributes (clauses 4.3.1 and 4.3.2); `RoundTiesToAway` is not required of a binary format but is defined. There is no away-from-zero mode: `@lf_arith.RoundingMode::AwayFromZero` has no IEEE 754-2019 counterpart and is refused by `from_arithmetic` and `from_arithmetic_context`. Each mode is a monotone map $\\mathbb{R} \\to F \\cup \\{\\pm\\infty\\}$; the [design page](../design/binary.md#rounding-functions) defines them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:695
msgid "Convert to and from `@lf_arith.RoundingMode`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:702
msgid "`to_arithmetic(RoundTiesToAway)` is `None`, because `@lf_arith.RoundingMode` has no ties-to-away mode, and `from_arithmetic(AwayFromZero)` is `None`, because IEEE 754-2019 4.3 has no such attribute; the other modes map one to one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:708
msgid "When a nonzero result counts as tiny for the underflow flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:718
msgid "`BeforeRounding` calls a result tiny when the exact value has $|x| < 2^{e_{\\min}}$. `AfterRounding` calls it tiny when $x$ rounded to $p$ bits with an unbounded exponent range has magnitude below $2^{e_{\\min}}$ (IEEE 754-2019 clause 7.5). The default is `AfterRounding`. Underflow is signaled only for a tiny result that is also inexact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:726
msgid "The format and the attributes of one operation: the precision $p$, the exponent $e_{\\max}$ (with $e_{\\min} = 1 - e_{\\max}$, IEEE 754-2019 3.3), the rounding-direction attribute and the tininess rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:737
msgid "A context is an immutable value; there is no global or thread state. $e_{\\min}$ and $e_{\\max}$ are exponents of the leading bit, as in IEEE 754: a normal number satisfies $2^{e_{\\min}} \\le |x| < 2^{e_{\\max}+1}$, the largest finite value is $(2 - 2^{1-p})\\,2^{e_{\\max}}$ and the smallest positive subnormal is $2^{e_{\\min}-p+1}$. A context has no separate $e_{\\min}$: it is $1 - e_{\\max}$. Without an `e_max` the context is the extendable precision format of IEEE 754-2019 3.7 specified by $p$ alone, and $e_{\\max}$ is `binary_value_e_max`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:748
msgid "Build a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:755
msgid "Defaults: $p = 53$, `e_max = binary_value_e_max`, `RoundTiesToEven`, `AfterRounding`; `new()` is therefore the extendable format of 53 bits, and every operation of the package runs in it. `new` aborts when $p$ is outside $[1, 2^{28}]$ or $e_{\\max}$ is outside $[1, 2^{30}-1]$, naming the parameter; `try_new` returns a `domain_error` in those cases. $e_{\\max} \\ge 1$ is what makes $e_{\\min} = 1 - e_{\\max} \\le e_{\\max}$; IEEE 754-2019 3.7 NOTE 3 recommends $p \\ge 3$ and $e_{\\max} \\ge 2$, which are not enforced. There is no one-sided or asymmetric range: `unbounded(p)` and the `e_min` argument no longer exist."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:766
msgid "Contexts of the IEEE 754 interchange formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:775
msgid "Each is `BinaryInterchangeFormat::context` of the format:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:786
msgid "Converts an `@lf_arith.ArithmeticContext`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:792
msgid "Precision and rounding are copied; the `clamp` field has no binary meaning and is ignored; tininess is `AfterRounding`. A given `e_min` must equal $1 - e_{\\max}$, and `e_min` alone gives $e_{\\max} = 1 - e_{\\min}$. The result is an `Err` for `AwayFromZero`, an `e_min` that is not $1 - e_{\\max}$, and a precision or `e_max` outside the limits of `try_new`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:800
msgid "Read the fields of a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:810
msgid "`e_min` is always $1 - e_{\\max}$; both are plain integers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:814
msgid "Copy a context with one parameter changed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:823
msgid "`with_precision` and `with_e_max` abort outside the limits of `new`; there is no `try_with_*`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:828
msgid "Read and set the refinement limit of the certified elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:835
msgid "Every certified elementary function (`exp`, `ln`, the trigonometric, hyperbolic and inverse functions, `pow`, `rootn`, `hypot`, `atan2` and their `try_*_ctx` forms) evaluates an enclosure at a growing working precision until both ends round alike. The limit is the number of refinement steps: `Some(n)` allows `n` of them (a value below 1 counts as 1), the default is `Some(12)`, and `None` removes the count limit. When the limit is reached without a decision, `try_*_ctx` returns the `CertificationFailure` error with the refinement count, and the plain and `*_ctx` forms return a quiet NaN with `not_computed`. With `None` the loop still stops when the working precision passes a cap, the larger of 8,000,000 bits and 256 times the first working precision, at most 1,000,000,000 bits, and the trigonometric work limit of 8,000,000 bits still refuses an argument that would need more. The default changes no result. The field takes part in `==`: two contexts that differ only in the limit are different."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:869
msgid "The five IEEE 754 exception flags raised by one or more operations, and `not_computed`, which reports a result the library could not compute."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:877
msgid "Two flag sets are `==` when all six flags agree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:881
msgid "Build an empty flag set and read the individual flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:893
msgid "`new()` has every flag clear. There is no `contains`: that method exists only where a package has a signal enum, and the six accessors cover the six flags. A contextual operation returns only the flags it raised itself; nothing is sticky until you combine flags. The flags mean: `inexact`, the returned value differs from the exact result; `underflow`, the result is tiny and inexact; `overflow`, the rounded result exceeded the largest finite value (always together with `inexact`); `division_by_zero`, an exact infinite result from finite operands (such as $1/0$ or $\\log 0$); `invalid_operation`, no useful real result exists and a quiet NaN was returned, or a signaling NaN was an operand. The elementary functions also raise it when they return NaN for a domain case, in the `try_` forms as in the plain ones. `not_computed`, the operation could not be carried out within a limit of the implementation (a certification that did not conclude, a context beyond what the kernels support) and the value is a NaN; it is not a flag of IEEE 754-2019 and is distinct from `invalid_operation`. The non-`try` elementary functions raise it when the `try_` form returns an `Err` of the certification-failure kind, and the checked tiers turn it into that `Err`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:912
msgid "Returns the union of two flag sets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:918
msgid "`combine` is a bitwise OR: associative, commutative and idempotent with `new()` as identity, so the flags of a computation can be accumulated in any order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:922
#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:264
msgid "Contextual arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:924
msgid "Every method in this group returns `(value, flags)`, rounds the exact result once under the context, and applies the context exponent range: an overflow returns $\\pm\\infty$ or the largest finite magnitude depending on the rounding direction, and a tiny result is rounded on the subnormal grid with quantum $2^{e_{\\min}-p+1}$. The returned value carries the context precision. A NaN operand produces the first NaN operand quieted, with its sign and payload, and `invalid_operation` only when some operand was a signaling NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:934
msgid "Rounds a value into a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:940
msgid "This is the IEEE conversion of a wider value to a narrower format. Finite values are rounded with the full overflow, subnormal and tininess rules; infinities are kept; a signaling NaN is quieted with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:946
msgid "The four operations under a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:959
msgid "Invalid operations ($\\infty - \\infty$ with equal signs after `sub`'s negation, $0 \\cdot \\infty$, $0/0$, $\\infty/\\infty$) give a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation`; a nonzero finite number divided by zero gives a signed infinity with `division_by_zero`. An exact zero sum of operands with opposite signs is $+0$, except $-0$ under `RoundTowardNegative`; $(-0) + (-0) = -0$ (IEEE 754-2019 clause 6.3)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:968
msgid "Square root under a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:974
msgid "$\\sqrt{\\pm 0} = \\pm 0$, $\\sqrt{+\\infty} = +\\infty$, and a negative nonzero argument gives a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:979
msgid "Fused multiply-add $\\circ(x \\cdot y + z)$ under a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:985
msgid "The product is formed exactly and the sum is rounded once (IEEE 754-2019 clause 5.4.1). A NaN operand propagates the first NaN of `self`, multiplier, addend. `invalid_operation` is raised for a signaling NaN, for $\\infty \\cdot 0$ (also when the addend is a quiet NaN, as SoftFloat does), and for $\\pm\\infty \\cdot y + (\\mp\\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:993
msgid "IEEE 754 remainder under a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:999
msgid "The result is $r = x - n y$ with $n = \\operatorname{round\\_ties\\_even}(x/y)$, so $|r| \\le |y|/2$. A zero result has the sign of $x$. $x = \\pm\\infty$ or $y = 0$ gives a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation`; $y = \\pm\\infty$ or $x = 0$ returns $x$ rounded into the context. The exact $r$ is then rounded; for operands representable in the context this rounding is exact and no flag is raised. The quotient is never formed: the operands are reduced modulo $2y$, so huge exponent gaps cost $O(\\log)$ multiplications."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1009
msgid "Integer power $\\circ(x^n)$ under a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1015
msgid "This is IEEE 754 `pown`. $x^0 = 1$ without a flag for every $x$ except a signaling NaN (a quiet NaN included); a signaling NaN base gives a quiet NaN with its payload and `invalid_operation` for every exponent, zero included (§9.2.1, §7.2 a); $(\\pm 0)^{n<0} = \\pm\\infty$ (sign for odd $n$) with `division_by_zero`; $(\\pm\\infty)^n$ is an infinity or zero with the sign of an odd power. Small powers are computed exactly; otherwise a Ziv loop rounds an enclosure, with an exact fallback, so the result is always correctly rounded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1036
msgid "IEEE 754 operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1040
msgid "Round to an integral value in the context's rounding direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1047
msgid "These are IEEE `roundToIntegral` (in the context's direction) and `roundToIntegralExact`. Only the rounding direction of the context is used; the result keeps the operand's precision and the sign of a zero result. `to_integral_exact_ctx` raises `inexact` when the value changed; `to_integral_value_ctx` never raises it. Infinities are returned unchanged and a signaling NaN is quieted with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1056
msgid "Round to an integral value in a fixed direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1066
msgid "They are `roundToIntegralTowardNegative`, `…TowardPositive`, `…TowardZero`, `…TiesToAway` and `…TiesToEven`. `round` rounds halfway cases away from zero: `round(-2.5) = -3`, while `round_ties_to_even(-2.5) = -2`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1072
msgid "IEEE `convertToInteger` to a machine integer, rounding in the context's direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1082
msgid "The value is first rounded to an integer. If that integer fits the target type the result is `Some`; with `exact=true` (`convertToIntegerExact`) `inexact` is raised when rounding changed the value. A NaN, an infinity or an integer outside the target range gives `None` with `invalid_operation`, where a C implementation would return an unspecified sentinel. A negative value that rounds to zero converts to `0` for the unsigned targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1091
msgid "IEEE `nextUp` and `nextDown` in the context's format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1098
msgid "`next_up_ctx(x)` is the least value of the context's format (precision and exponent range, subnormals included) that is greater than $x$; `next_down_ctx` is $-\\texttt{next\\_up}(-x)$. The context's rounding direction is ignored. The operations are quiet: $\\operatorname{nextUp}(\\pm 0)$ is the smallest positive subnormal, $\\operatorname{nextUp}(-\\infty)$ is the most negative finite value, $\\operatorname{nextUp}(\\Omega) = +\\infty$ with no overflow flag, and only a signaling NaN raises `invalid_operation`. An operand with more bits than the context precision is accepted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1109
msgid "IEEE `scaleB` and `logB`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1117
msgid "`x.scaleb_ctx(n, ctx)` is $\\circ(x \\cdot 2^n)$ with the usual overflow and underflow handling; it is exact unless the operand has more bits than the context precision or the result leaves the normal range. The count `n` is a `Binary`, the kind `logb_ctx` returns, as in `Decimal::scaleb_ctx` and `decimal_gda`: it must be an integer value. A NaN count propagates (invalid for a signaling NaN); an infinite or non-integer count is a NaN with `invalid_operation`, for a receiver that is not a NaN (a NaN receiver propagates like a NaN count; an infinite receiver with a finite count is returned as it is); an integer count beyond the `Int32` range acts as the largest count of its sign, which overflows or underflows every finite non-zero value of any format. IEEE 754-2019 5.3.3 defines scaleB for integral counts only and leaves a non-integral second operand (an infinity is not integral) to the language: the NaN with `invalid_operation` is this library's choice, as in `decimal`. `x.logb_ctx(ctx)` is $\\lfloor \\log_2 |x| \\rfloor$ as an exact integral value (also for subnormal $x$): $\\operatorname{logB}(0) = -\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`, $\\operatorname{logB}(\\pm\\infty) = +\\infty$, and a NaN propagates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1133
msgid "`x.ilogb()` is the same exponent as an integer: the $e$ with $2^e \\le |x| < 2^{e+1}$, that is $\\texttt{exponent2()} + \\texttt{coefficient().bit\\_length()} - 1$, read from the value without building its coefficient. It is `logB` of IEEE 754-2019 5.3.3 with an integral `logBFormat`: the standard defines $\\operatorname{logB}(x)$ as the exponent of $x$ \"determined as though x were represented with infinite range and minimum exponent\", so a subnormal $x$ gives the exponent of its leading bit. For $\\operatorname{logB}(0)$, $\\operatorname{logB}(\\pm\\infty)$ and $\\operatorname{logB}(\\text{NaN})$ with an integer `logBFormat` the standard returns language-defined values outside $\\pm 2 (e_{\\max} + p - 1)$ and signals the invalid operation exception (5.3.3 and 7.2); `ilogb` renders those three cases as `None` and raises no flag. `logb_ctx` is the operation with a floating-point `logBFormat` ($-\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`, $+\\infty$, NaN)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1173
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1337
msgid "Comparison and ordering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1177
msgid "Numerical three-way comparison that is total on every value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1184
msgid "`compare` returns $-1$, $0$ or $1$ by numerical value, with $-0 = +0$ and precision ignored. NaN has no numerical order, so `compare` places every NaN equal to every other NaN and above every non-NaN value; it never aborts. The result is a total preorder, which is what `Compare` (and sorting) needs, but it is not the IEEE comparison: under it `nan > 1` holds. The operators `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and the promoted `op_*` methods use `compare`. For IEEE semantics use `compare_checked`, the quiet and signaling predicates below, or `total_order`. The [design page](../design/binary.md#ordering-nan-in-compare) explains the choice."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1196
msgid "Numerical comparison that rejects NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1203
msgid "Returns the same value as `compare` when neither operand is a NaN, and an `unordered_comparison` error otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1208
msgid "IEEE comparison as a four-valued relation with flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1215
msgid "The relation is `Less`, `Equal`, `Greater` or `Unordered` (some operand is a NaN), with $-0 = +0$. The quiet form raises `invalid_operation` only for a signaling NaN; the signaling form raises it for every unordered comparison (IEEE 754-2019 clause 5.11)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1222
msgid "The IEEE comparison predicates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1234
msgid "`compareQuietEqual`, `compareQuietLess`, `compareQuietLessEqual`, `compareQuietUnordered` and the signaling `Equal`, `Less`, `LessEqual`. Each is derived from `compare_quiet` or `compare_signaling` and returns its flags; every predicate except `compare_quiet_unordered` is false on an unordered pair."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1239
msgid "`Binary::compare_quiet_not_equal` and the other comparison predicates of Tables 5.1 and 5.2"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1241
msgid "The fifteen predicates of IEEE 754-2019 5.11 not listed above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1261
msgid "Clause 5.11 lists 22 predicates, 10 in Table 5.1 (with the common mathematical symbols) and 12 in Table 5.2; with the seven above every one of them has a function. Each is defined from `compare_quiet` or `compare_signaling` through the relations that make it true, which is how the tables define it, and returns the flags of that comparison: a quiet predicate raises `invalid_operation` for a signaling NaN only, a signaling predicate for every NaN operand. The doc comment of each names its row. Name spelling follows the seven above: `compare_`, `quiet` or `signaling`, then the predicate name of the table in snake case. The pairs the standard states as negations of one another (the two predicates of one line of a table) return opposite values and the same flags."
msgstr ""

