#: manual/api/binary.md:1300
msgid "The IEEE 754 `totalOrder` relation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1308
msgid "`total_order_compare` orders all values as"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1314
msgid "with NaNs of one sign and kind ordered by payload (reversed for the negative side). `x.total_order(y)` is `total_order_compare(x, y) <= 0` and `total_order_mag` compares absolute values. Values that are numerically equal but differ in precision compare equal. The operations are quiet."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1321
msgid "The smaller or larger of two values, ignoring a NaN operand."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1328
msgid "When exactly one operand is a NaN the other is returned (the IEEE 754-2008 `minNum`/`maxNum` convention for quiet NaNs); a signaling NaN is ignored in the same way, without quieting or a flag, and two NaNs give the argument. When the operands compare equal under `compare` (for example $-0$ and $+0$) the receiver is returned. No flags are produced."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1336
msgid "Restrict a value to `[min, max]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1343
msgid "A NaN receiver is returned unchanged. `clamp` aborts when a bound is a NaN or `min > max`; `clamp_checked` returns a `domain_error` in those cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1367
msgid "Text and hexadecimal conversion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1371
msgid "Prints the exact stored value as `coefficient p exponent`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1378
msgid "A finite nonzero value prints as `[-]<c>p<e>` with $c$ in decimal, meaning $c \\cdot 2^e$ (for example `3p-1` is $1.5$). Zeros print as `0` and `-0`, infinities as `inf` and `-inf`, and every NaN as `nan`. The form is exact and unambiguous but not a decimal rendering; use `to_shortest_string` for that."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1385
msgid "Parse a decimal literal with correct rounding (IEEE `convertFromDecimalCharacter`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1393
msgid "The accepted syntax is `[+-]digits[.digits][e[+-]digits]` (a leading or trailing point is allowed, the exponent marker is `e` or `E`), and the case-insensitive words `inf`, `infinity`, `nan`, `qnan` and `snan`, with surrounding whitespace ignored. Anything else is a `parse_error`. The exact decimal value $D \\cdot 10^k$ is rounded once under the context, for any number of digits and any exponent; overflow, underflow and inexact are reported as for arithmetic. `from_string(s, precision=p)` uses `BinaryContext::new(precision=p)` (default 53) and drops the flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1404
msgid "Formats a value with a fixed number of significant decimal digits (IEEE `convertToDecimalCharacter`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1411
msgid "The output is `[-]d.ddd…e±x` with exactly `digits` significant digits (at least 1), correctly rounded in the context's rounding direction; `inexact` is raised when nonzero digits were dropped. Only the rounding direction of the context is used. Zero prints as `0.00…e+0` with its sign, infinities as `inf`/`-inf`, NaNs as `nan`/`snan` with their sign. The text parses back with `from_string_ctx`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1420
msgid "Formats the shortest decimal that reads back as the same value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1427
msgid "`to_shortest_string_ctx(x, ctx)` returns the decimal with the fewest significant digits that `from_string_ctx(_, ctx)` maps back to $x$ under round-to-nearest-even (the context's own rounding direction is ignored). When two candidates of that length read back, the nearer one is chosen, then the one with an even last digit. The layout follows ECMAScript `Number#toString`: positional notation for decimal exponents in $[-6, 21)$, otherwise `d.ddde±x`; unlike ECMAScript, $-0$ prints as `-0`. For binary64 values in `BinaryContext::binary64()` the result equals the host `Double` formatter. `to_shortest_string()` uses `BinaryContext::new(precision=precision())`, so it is the shortest string that `from_string(text, precision=x.precision())` maps back to `x`. An operand that is not representable in the context is first rounded into it (`apply_ctx`, in the rounding direction of the context, with the overflow and underflow of the context), and the result is the shortest string of that rounded value: `inf` or `-inf` on overflow, `0` or `-0` when the value rounds to zero (the smallest subnormal for a direction that rounds away from zero), and the largest finite value on overflow for a direction that rounds toward zero. A value of the format prints as itself. The second component is the flags of that rounding (`inexact`, `overflow`, `underflow`); it is empty for a value of the format, a quiet NaN or an infinity; a signaling NaN is quieted with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1448
msgid "Read and write the exact value with a hexadecimal coefficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1456
msgid "The syntax is the IEEE 754-2019 §5.12.3 hexSequence: an optional sign, `0x` or `0X`, hexadecimal digits with at most one point (`1.8`, `.8` or `1.`), and a required `p` or `P` exponent with an optional sign and decimal digits. The value is the hexadecimal significand times $2^{\\text{exponent}}$, so `0x3p-1` and `0x1.8p0` are both $1.5$. `from_hex(s, p)` rounds once to precision `p` with nearest-even and reports no flags; a `p` outside `1..=binary_precision_max` is an `Err`. `from_hex_ctx(s, context)` is the §5.12.3 conversion: it rounds the exact value once under the rounding direction, precision, exponent range and tininess rule of `context` and returns the `inexact`, `underflow` and `overflow` flags, like `from_string_ctx`. An exponent field of any length is a valid text: it is read with saturation at $2^{60}$, as the decimal reader does, so an exponent outside the 32-bit range overflows or underflows there (the result of the context with its flags, in every rounding direction) instead of being a parse error, and a zero significand is the zero of the sign for any exponent. `from_hex`, which stores the exact value, reports an exponent outside the 32-bit range as a parse error (a zero significand is still a zero). Both accept `inf`, `infinity`, `nan` and `snan` in any case with an optional sign (§5.12.1); `snan` gives a signaling NaN. Surrounding whitespace is trimmed; any other text is a `parse_error`. `to_hex` prints the stored coefficient in lower case, `0x0p0` or `-0x0p0` for a zero, and `nan`/`inf` with a sign."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary.md:1498
