msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:190
msgid "These methods combine two wrappers with the `Binary` operator."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:198
msgid "If `self` is an error it is returned; otherwise if `other` is an error that error is returned; otherwise the result is `Ok(lhs op rhs)`. The `Binary` operators round to nearest-even at the larger of the two operand precisions and never fail: invalid cases such as $\\infty - \\infty$ produce a successful NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:205
msgid "`div` divides two wrappers and reports invalid divisions and division by zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:211
msgid "After the operand errors (left first), the result is `DivChecked::div_checked` of `Binary` under the larger precision of the operands: a `DomainError` for $0/0$, $\\infty/\\infty$ or a signaling NaN operand, a `DivisionByZero` error for a finite non-zero dividend over a zero divisor ($\\pm 0$), and the IEEE 754-2019 result otherwise: a quiet NaN for a quiet NaN operand (NaN$/0$ included), $\\pm\\infty$ for $\\infty/0$, and the rounded quotient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:220
msgid "These methods take the smaller or larger operand with `Binary::min_num` / `Binary::max_num`, which ignore a NaN operand in favour of the other one and return the receiver when the operands compare equal (so `min(-0, +0)` is $-0$ and `min(+0, -0)` is $+0$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:232
msgid "`clamp(min~, max~)` restricts a value to an interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:238
msgid "Errors are taken in the order `self`, `min`, `max`; then `Binary::clamp_checked` returns a `DomainError` when a bound is NaN or when `min > max`, and the clamped value otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:268
msgid "These methods apply the `Binary::*_ctx` operation under an explicit `BinaryContext` and keep only its value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:278
msgid "The result has the context's precision, rounding direction and exponent range, with IEEE overflow, underflow and subnormal handling. The flags of the step are mapped as in `div` and the elementary methods: invalid operation is a `DomainError`, divideByZero a `DivisionByZero` and `not_computed` a `CertificationFailure`; overflow, underflow and inexact are dropped. So `div_ctx` by a zero divisor is `DivisionByZero`, and $0/0$, $\\infty/\\infty$, $\\infty - \\infty$ and $0 \\cdot \\infty$ are `DomainError`, whereas `sub` and `mul` without a context give a successful NaN for $\\infty - \\infty$ and $0 \\cdot \\infty$ (only `div` reports $0/0$ and $\\infty/\\infty$ as errors without a context); a quiet NaN operand is still a successful NaN. The full table is in the [design page](../design/binary_checked.md). Use `Binary::div_ctx` and its siblings when the raw default result and the flags matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:306
msgid "Powers and roots"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:310
msgid "`sqrt` takes the square root at the operand's precision; `sqrt_ctx` under a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:318
msgid "`sqrt` is `Binary::sqrt_ctx` under `BinaryContext::new(precision=x.precision())`, with the flags mapped as for `div`: a `DomainError` for a negative non-zero argument, including $-\\infty$, and for a signaling NaN; $\\sqrt{-0} = -0$ and a quiet NaN of either sign gives a successful NaN. `sqrt_ctx` does the same under a context: a negative argument is a `DomainError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:326
msgid "These methods raise a value to an integer power."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:334
msgid "`pow_nat(n)` calls the Luna-Flow/arithmetic trait method `PowNatChecked::pow_nat_checked` with `ArithmeticContext::new(x.precision())`, which `binary` maps to the binary context of that precision (`BinaryContext::new(precision=p)`) with nearest-even rounding. `pown` returns a `DivisionByZero` error for a zero base with a negative exponent, a `DomainError` for a signaling NaN base, zero exponent included (as does `pow_nat`), and the rounded power otherwise, at the operand's precision. The `_ctx` forms map the flags the same way: a `DivisionByZero` error for a zero base with a negative exponent and a `DomainError` for a signaling NaN base."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:345
msgid "`rootn(n)` computes the real $n$-th root with `Binary::try_rootn_ctx`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:352
msgid "`rootn` uses `BinaryContext::new(precision=p)` at the operand's precision; `rootn_ctx` the given context. Both report a `DomainError` for degree $0$ (for every operand, a quiet NaN and $\\pm 0$ included, and never `DivisionByZero`: IEEE 754-2019 Table 9.1, \"n = 0: invalid operation\"), for an even root of a negative number ($-\\infty$ included) and for a signaling NaN, a `DivisionByZero` error for $\\operatorname{rootn}(\\pm 0, n)$ with $n < 0$ (IEEE 754-2019 §9.2.1), and can report a `CertificationFailure`. Odd roots of negative numbers are negative: `rootn(-8, 3)` is $-2$. The full table of special values is in [`Binary::rootn`](binary.md#binaryrootn-binaryrootn_ctx-binarytry_rootn_ctx)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:362
msgid "`pow(y)` computes $x^{y}$ for a binary exponent with `Binary::try_pow_ctx`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:369
msgid "`pow` uses `BinaryContext::new(precision=p)` at the larger operand precision. Errors of the operands come first (base, then exponent); the operation itself reports a `DomainError` for a negative finite base with a non-integer exponent (for example $(-2)^{0.5}$) and for a signaling NaN operand, a `DivisionByZero` error for a zero base with a finite negative exponent ($(\\pm 0)^{-1.5}$, IEEE 754-2019 §9.2.1), and can report a `CertificationFailure`. A base of $-0$, a negative base with an integral exponent of any size and an infinite exponent all give the IEEE 754 values listed in [`Binary::pow`](binary.md#binarypow-binarypow_ctx-binarytry_pow_ctx), for example $(-1)^{\\infty} = 1$ and $(-0)^{0.75} = +0$."
msgstr ""

