#: manual/api/binary_checked.md:496
msgid "Each unary elementary function `name` has two wrapper methods. `name()` is `bind` of `Binary::try_name_ctx` under `BinaryContext::new(precision=x.precision())`, that is nearest-even rounding to the operand's precision with the exponent range `binary_value_e_max` of the extendable format; `name_ctx(ctx)` uses the given context instead. A success is the correctly rounded value of `binary`. The flags of the step are mapped as for `div`: the invalid operation exception (a signaling NaN operand, or an argument outside the domain of IEEE 754-2019 Table 9.1) is a `DomainError`, divideByZero (a pole, §9.2) is a `DivisionByZero` error, and overflow, underflow and inexact are dropped. Besides the errors listed below, every function can report a `CertificationFailure` when `binary` cannot certify the rounding within its refinement budget (see [`binary` elementary functions](binary.md#elementary-functions)); the `CertificationFailureDetail` names the function. The messages of the other errors name it too: `name: invalid operation` and `name: division by zero`, so that in a chain of steps the message says which one failed. A quiet NaN operand gives a successful NaN."
