#: manual/api/decimal.md:1
msgid "decimal API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:5
msgid "The `Luna-Flow/floating/decimal` package in `floating` implements IEEE 754-2019 decimal floating-point arithmetic. A `Decimal` stores an arbitrary-precision value, its exponent $q$, the sign of zero and any NaN payload. For a finite value, its quantum is $10^q$, the unit in its last digit position (IEEE 754-2019 2.1). A `DecimalContext` is an IEEE 754-2019 format with a precision, an exponent range and one rounding-direction attribute. Each context operation returns the rounded result and the `DecimalFlags` it raised. The package also encodes and decodes the decimal32, decimal64 and decimal128 interchange formats in DPD and BID, and evaluates elementary functions with certified rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:15
msgid "Use the [decimal tutorial](../tutorial/decimal.md) for common tasks. The [decimal design](../design/decimal.md) explains the arithmetic model, encodings, rounding and certification; [decimal conformance](../conformance/decimal.md) records the finite evidence for the IEEE claim. The separate [`decimal_gda`](decimal_gda.md) package provides General Decimal Arithmetic status that accumulates across operations and trap handling. [`decimal_checked`](decimal_checked.md) accumulates flags across a pipeline of `Decimal` operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:26
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:34
#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:18
msgid "Add the package to your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:34
msgid "The examples refer to this package as `@decimal.`. Some also use `@def.` for `Luna-Flow/floating/def` (the shared `RoundingMode` and `Sign`), `@lf_arith.` for `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`, `@lf_alg.` for `Luna-Flow/luna-generic`, and `@bigint.` for `moonbitlang/core/bigint`; import any of these packages used by an example you copy. Most examples build values with the helper `d(s)`, defined as `@decimal.Decimal::from_string(s).unwrap()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:41
msgid "Notation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:43
msgid "For a finite value, $(-1)^s \\cdot c \\cdot 10^q$ denotes its value, where $s$ is its sign, $c$ its non-negative integer coefficient and $q$ its exponent. The quantum is $10^q$, the unit in the last digit position (IEEE 754-2019 2.1). The context precision is $p$; its exponent limits $e_{\\min}$ and $e_{\\max}$ bound the adjusted exponent $\\hat q = q + \\operatorname{digits}(c) - 1$. The smallest exponent of a subnormal is $E_{\\text{tiny}} = e_{\\min} - p + 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:51
msgid "Values and representation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:55
msgid "`Decimal` is an immutable decimal floating-point value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:64
msgid "A `Decimal` represents a finite value $(-1)^s c\\,10^q$ (including $\\pm 0$), $\\pm\\infty$, or a quiet or signaling NaN with a sign and a non-negative integer payload. Each value also carries the precision of the value: the precision of a format a context could have, in `1..=999999999` (`decimal_precision_max`). The plain operators and conversions without a context argument use it. The fields are private; use the observers below. Values with the same mathematical value but different exponents, such as `1.2` and `1.20`, are members of the same *cohort*. Numeric comparison treats them as equal. Their different exponents are observable through `quantum`, `same_quantum`, `total_order_compare`, formatting and interchange encoding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:75
msgid "The derived `Debug` implementation is promoted as `Decimal::to_repr`; see [Trait implementations](#trait-implementations)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:80
msgid "Return parts of the stored representation of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:88
msgid "`precision` returns the precision of the value. A constructor returning a `Decimal` aborts on a precision outside `1..=999999999` and names the function; an operation returning `Result` returns `Err`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:92
msgid "`coefficient` returns the non-negative coefficient $c$, the payload for a NaN, or `0` for an infinity. The sign is not part of the coefficient; read it with `is_sign_minus`. `exponent10` returns the stored exponent $q$, or `0` for a special value. IEEE 754-2019's `quantum` operation is `quantum_ctx` below; it is not an accessor."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:111
msgid "`signum` reports the sign classification; `is_sign_minus` reports the stored sign bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:119
msgid "`signum` returns `@def.Sign::Zero` for both zeros and every NaN, and `Negative` or `Positive` otherwise. `is_sign_minus` returns `true` for $-0$, $-\\infty$ and a negative NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:125
msgid "`classify` returns the coarse class of a value. `class_of` returns one of the ten classes defined by IEEE 754-2019 5.7.2, using a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:133
msgid "`classify` returns `Finite`, `Infinity` or `NaN`. `class_of` returns one of the ten [`IeeeClass`](def.md#ieeeclass) values. The context's $e_{\\min}$ determines whether a finite nonzero value is normal or subnormal. `IeeeClass` has no text form; use `match` on its value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:140
msgid "Test a value's class with these predicates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:155
msgid "`is_canonical` returns `true` for every value. A `Decimal` has no non-canonical form; non-canonical *encodings* belong to [`DecimalInterchange`](#decimalinterchange-decimalinterchangeformat-encoding-to_hex)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:159
msgid "Under a context, a finite nonzero value is normal when its adjusted exponent is at least $e_{\\min}$, and subnormal when its adjusted exponent is below $e_{\\min}$. Zeros, infinities and NaNs are neither."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:165
msgid "Read or replace a NaN's payload with these functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:174
msgid "`nan_payload` returns the payload for a NaN and `0` for any other value. `get_payload` implements getPayload of IEEE 754-2019 9.7: it returns the NaN payload, or `-1` for a non-NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:178
msgid "`set_payload` and `set_payload_signaling` implement setPayload and setPayloadSignaling of IEEE 754-2019 9.7. If the argument is a non-negative integer with at most `precision - 1` digits, the result is a quiet or signaling NaN, respectively, with that payload and the receiver's sign bit. This limit uses the receiver's `precision`, like the trailing significand of an interchange NaN. Otherwise the result is `+0` with exponent 0. The receiver need not be a NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:186
msgid "Construction and conversion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:190
msgid "These constants give the implementation limits on the exponents of stored finite values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:198
msgid "A finite `Decimal` can have an exponent no smaller than `decimal_value_e_tiny` ($-2\\,147\\,483\\,645$), and an adjusted exponent no larger than `decimal_value_e_max` ($2\\,147\\,483\\,646$). Its adjusted exponent is $q + \\operatorname{digits}(c) - 1$. A zero has one digit in this calculation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:203
msgid "These are the `e_max` and `etiny` of the private working context used by the plain operators. They are the largest and smallest values allowed by the 32-bit exponent field once `e_max`, `e_min` and `etiny = e_min - p + 1` must also be representable. This range lets `x + 0 == x`, `x - 0 == x` and `x * 1 == x` hold for every stored value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:209
msgid "Exact constructors (`make`, `try_make`, `from_string`) refuse values outside these implementation limits. Constructors from integers and floating-point inputs cannot produce such a value. Operations that round (the plain operators and `with_precision`) overflow to infinity or the largest finite value in the rounding direction, or underflow to the smallest exponent, as a context result does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:216
msgid "These are not context limits. Context limits are narrower, so a value outside every context range can still be stored and is rounded when a context operation reads it. The context limits are [`decimal_context_e_max_limit`](#decimal_context_e_max_limit-decimal_context_e_min_limit-decimal_precision_max) and its related parameters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:224
msgid "`make` builds a finite value from a signed integer coefficient and an exponent, rounding it to a precision. `try_make` returns the same result through `Result`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:233
msgid "`Decimal::make(c, q, p, mode~)` represents $c \\cdot 10^q$, with the sign taken from `c`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:236
msgid "Coefficient length"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:238
msgid "At most $p$ digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:238
msgid "Keeps exponent $q$ and is not reduced: `make(100, 0, 5)` is `100`; `make(0, 5, 5)` is `0E+5`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:239
msgid "More than $p$ digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:239
msgid "Rounds to exactly $p$ digits with `mode` (default `RoundTiesToEven`) and is not reduced: `make(1000000, 0, 3)` is `1.00E+6`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:241
msgid "No context exponent range applies. After rounding, the value must satisfy the [implementation limits on stored values](#decimal_value_e_max-decimal_value_e_tiny). `make` aborts with a message naming the exponent and both limits; `try_make` returns a domain error. Both also reject $p$ outside `1..=999999999`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:248
msgid "Construct zeros, one, infinities and NaNs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:260
msgid "The default precision is 34. `zero`, `negative_zero` and `one` use exponent `0`. `inf(negative=true)` returns $-\\infty$; `inf()` returns $+\\infty$. `nan` returns a positive quiet NaN with payload 0. `quiet_nan` and `signaling_nan` default to payload 0 and a positive sign."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:265
msgid "The payload is the trailing significand field (IEEE 754-2019 3.5.2). It must be a non-negative integer with at most `precision - 1` digits; otherwise the constructor aborts. Construction does not truncate a payload, while conversion to a narrower format keeps its low digits. The precision must be within `1..=999999999`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:273
msgid "Convert an integer to a `Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:280
msgid "Both implement convertFromInt (IEEE 754-2019 5.4.1) with preferred exponent 0 and default precision 34. An integer with at most `precision` digits keeps exponent 0: `from_int(1000)` returns `1000`, not the reduced `1E+3`. A longer integer is rounded half-even to exactly `precision` digits (IEEE 754-2019 5.2), so `from_int(99999, precision=3)` returns `1.00E+5`. Zero is $+0$ with exponent 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:289
msgid "Convert a binary floating-point value to a `Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:296
msgid "A finite `Double` is a dyadic rational $m \\cdot 2^{k}$ and therefore has a finite decimal expansion $m \\cdot 5^{-k} \\cdot 10^{k}$ for $k<0$. The conversion forms that exact expansion. If it fits in `precision` digits (default 34), the result uses the exponent closest to preferred exponent 0: `from_double(0.5)` returns `0.5`, and `from_double(100.0)` returns `100`. Otherwise, the result is rounded half-even to exactly `precision` digits, using the least possible exponent (IEEE 754-2019 5.2). For example, `from_double(0.1, precision=16)` returns `0.1000000000000000`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:305
msgid "Signed zeros and infinities are preserved. Every NaN becomes a quiet NaN with payload 0 and the input's sign. `from_float` first widens to `Double`; that conversion is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:311
msgid "Convert between `Decimal` and the binary [`Binary`](binary.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:319
msgid "`from_binary(x, precision~, mode~)` is exact whenever the decimal expansion of `x` fits in `precision` decimal digits, with the exponent closest to 0 as for `from_double`, and is otherwise rounded once to exactly `precision` digits in `mode` (default `RoundTiesToEven`, the rounding type of the radix of the result). The precision is required and counts digits; it was the number of bits of `x` by default, a count of the wrong kind."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:326
msgid "A binary zero keeps its sign, so $-0$ converts to $-0$ (IEEE 754-2019 5.4.2 and 6.3). A NaN becomes a quiet NaN with payload 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:329
msgid "`to_binary(precision~, mode~)` rounds the exact decimal value once to a `Binary` of `precision` bits with `mode`, one of the five rounding-direction attributes of IEEE 754-2019 4.3.1 (default `RoundTiesToEven`). The precision is required and counts bits, from 1 to `@binary.binary_precision_max`; outside that range the call aborts (it was the number of digits of the decimal by default). Most decimal fractions are not dyadic, so this conversion is usually inexact. Using `RoundTowardNegative` and `RoundTowardPositive` gives a binary enclosure of the decimal value. Zeros keep their sign; NaN payloads are not kept. The same conversions are available in `decimal_gda`, with `GdaRoundingMode` for a decimal result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:342
msgid "Convert a `Decimal` between decimal formats or between decimal and binary formats; each operation returns flags. They implement convertFormat, IEEE 754-2019 5.4.2."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:352
msgid "`convert_format(format)` converts to decimal32, decimal64 or decimal128. It rounds the value once to the format's precision and exponent range, to nearest even, and returns the flags from that rounding. The preferred exponent is the quantum of the source: for conversions between decimal formats, IEEE 754-2019 5.4.2 specifies $Q(\\mathrm{source})$. Conversion to a wider format is exact and keeps the cohort member. Conversion to a narrower format rounds and can overflow or underflow; a large exponent is folded down."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:360
msgid "`from_binary_ctx(x, ctx)` rounds the exact value of a binary float once under the decimal context (precision, exponent range, direction) with the flags of the rounding. For an exact conversion, the preferred exponent is 0, the exponent closest to 0 that holds the digits: `0.5` is `5E-1`, and `96` is `96`. An inexact conversion has `precision` digits because the preferred exponent is the least possible. A value beyond the exponent range, or far below it, overflows or underflows without its digits being formed. An in-range value is correctly rounded at any binary exponent, at a cost that does not grow with the exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:370
msgid "`to_binary_ctx(ctx)` rounds the exact decimal value once under a `BinaryContext`. The destination may be binary16 through binary128, or a format with any precision and range. It returns `BinaryFlags` for `inexact`, `overflow` and `underflow`. This is the conversion of `Binary::from_string_ctx`, correctly rounded for any exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:376
msgid "A zero keeps its sign in every rounding direction. A signaling NaN becomes a quiet NaN and raises the invalid operation exception. A NaN keeps its sign and its payload when the destination holds it, IEEE 754-2019 6.2.3: `p - 1` digits for decimal formats and `p - 2` bits for a binary format. Otherwise the conversion keeps the low digits or bits. `from_binary` and `to_binary` are unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:383
msgid "The `FromNat` and `FromInteger` implementations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:385
msgid "`Decimal` implements the `luna-generic` traits `FromNat` and `FromInteger`, the canonical maps from naturals and integers. To construct a `Decimal` directly, call `Decimal::from_bigint(n)`; it uses exponent 0, the cohort of the integer. The trait functions are the other entry points. There are no `Decimal::from_natural` or `Decimal::from_integer` methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:391
msgid "The trait functions return `from_bigint(n).normalized()` with precision 34, which removes trailing zeros. To convert another Luna-Flow integer type, first choose its representative with `Integral::normalize`, or use `lift_to`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:395
msgid "Parsing and formatting"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:399
msgid "`from_string` converts decimal text to a `Decimal` and keeps its exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:405
msgid "The accepted syntax is an optional sign, digits with an optional decimal point, an optional exponent `E` or `e` with optional sign, or one of `Inf`, `Infinity`, `NaN` and `sNaN` (any letter case) followed for NaNs by optional decimal payload digits. Conversion rounds once, half-even, to `precision` (default 34), preserving the preferred exponent as far as precision and the representation permit. For example, `\"1.2300\"` has coefficient 12300 and exponent $-4$; `\"1.0001\"` at precision 3 rounds to `1.00`; the exact `\"1.000\"` at precision 3 is also `1.00` because its four coefficient digits do not fit. `\"10001\"` at precision 3 rounds to `1.00E+4`. Invalid text returns `Err(parse_error)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:416
msgid "No context exponent range applies. The [implementation limits on a stored value's exponents](#decimal_value_e_max-decimal_value_e_tiny) set the minimum exponent to `decimal_value_e_tiny` ($-2\\,147\\,483\\,645$) and the maximum adjusted exponent to `decimal_value_e_max` ($2\\,147\\,483\\,646$). These limits are checked after rounding to `precision`, including a carry, and apply to zero as to any finite value. Text whose value is outside the limits is refused rather than replaced. `from_string` returns the domain error, which names the exponent and both limits: `\"1e3000000000\"`, `\"12e2147483647\"` and `\"0e-2147483646\"` are refused. At precision 3, `\"1249e-2147483646\"` becomes `1.25E-2147483643`; its exponent after rounding is inside the range. Read the text with [`from_string_ctx`](#decimalfrom_string_ctx) when you need a rounded value (infinity or zero) and the `overflow` and `underflow` flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:431
msgid "`from_string_ctx` converts text under a context and returns its conversion flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:437
msgid "`from_string_ctx` accepts the syntax of `from_string`. It rounds the value to the context precision and raises `inexact` when it discards non-zero digits. It checks the exponent range for overflow and underflow and folds down large exponents. A NaN payload longer than the format allows (precision $-1$ digits) is a syntax error. Invalid text returns a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:458
msgid "Format a value using the General Decimal Arithmetic `to-scientific-string` or `to-engineering-string` conversion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:466
msgid "Both format the value they receive. To format text after converting it under a context, first call `from_string_ctx`; this also returns the conversion flags. For example, `Decimal::from_string_ctx(\"1000000000\", ctx).0.to_sci_string()` formats the converted result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:471
#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:56
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:473
msgid "Scientific"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:473
msgid "Writes the coefficient with an exponent `E±n` when the exponent is positive or the adjusted exponent is below $-6$; otherwise writes plain digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:474
msgid "Engineering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:474
msgid "Uses an exponent that is a multiple of three."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:475
msgid "Special values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:475
msgid "Writes `Infinity`, `-Infinity`, `NaN` or `sNaN`, with a payload such as `NaN7`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:477
msgid "`Decimal::to_string` formats a value without a context using the same scientific rule. It is the `Show` implementation; see [`Show` and `Debug`](#show-and-debug-decimalto_string-output-to_repr)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:481
msgid "Contexts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:485
msgid "`DecimalContext` stores the arithmetic parameters used by context operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:494
msgid "An IEEE 754-2019 decimal format has precision $p$, maximum adjusted exponent $e_{\\max}$ and minimum adjusted exponent $e_{\\min}=1-e_{\\max}$ (3.3). A `DecimalContext` selects one such format and one rounding-direction attribute, `rounding`, whose value is a `DecimalRoundingMode`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:499
msgid "For a finite result with exponent $q$, the format requires $e_{\\min} \\le q + p - 1 \\le e_{\\max}$. Therefore $q \\le e_{\\max}-p+1$; larger exponents are folded down. IEEE 754-2019 7.5 requires decimal formats to detect tininess before rounding. The context has no option for these format rules. Each context operation receives its context as an argument and reads no ambient state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:508
msgid "These constants bound the context parameters accepted by `DecimalContext::try_new`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:517
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:128
msgid "Constant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:517
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:128
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:286
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:326
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1549
#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:42
msgid "Value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:519
msgid "Largest accepted `e_max`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:520
msgid "$1-e_{\\max}$, the smallest `e_min` of a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:521
msgid "Largest accepted precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:523
msgid "These are the decNumber `decContext.h` limits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:525
msgid "`try_new` and `new` check `precision` against `1..=decimal_precision_max` and `e_max` against `1..=decimal_context_e_max_limit`; the constants used for the checks are also the public limits. These values bound contexts only. The certified elementary functions have a lower implementation limit: $e_{\\max} \\le 999{,}999$ and $p \\le 999{,}999$. See [Elementary functions](#elementary-functions)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:534
msgid "Construct an IEEE 754-2019 decimal-format context from a precision and `e_max`. `new` aborts for invalid parameters; `try_new` returns a `Result`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:542
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:519
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1549
msgid "Parameter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:542
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:519
msgid "Default"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:544
msgid "coefficient digits $p$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:545
msgid "The maximum adjusted exponent $e_{\\max}$; $e_{\\min}=1-e_{\\max}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:546
msgid "The rounding-direction attribute (IEEE 754-2019 4.3)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:548
msgid "`precision` and `e_max` must each be in `1..=999,999,999`. IEEE 754-2019 3.7 requires a language that supports an extendable format specified by precision alone to define $e_{\\max}$ as at least $1000p$ when decimal precision is at least 51 digits. The library sets its default `e_max` to $\\max(999{,}999,1000p)$; 999,999 is the widest range in which every package operation runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:555
msgid "`new()` therefore creates format $(34,999{,}999)$, not decimal128; use `decimal128()` for that format. A precision above 999,999 has no default range because $1000p$ exceeds the limit; pass `e_max`. `try_new` returns `Err(domain_error)` naming the invalid field. `new` aborts with a message that names it, as `BinaryContext::new` does. Use `try_new` for parameters from outside the program."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:562
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 3.7 NOTE 3 recommends $e_{\\max}\\ge2$. The library accepts 1, the least value for which $e_{\\min}\\le e_{\\max}$ under 3.3."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:565
msgid "The context's `rounding` attribute applies to finite results, infinities and every other use of the context; `rounding()` reports exactly the rounding direction applied. The commercial GDA rounding algorithm `round-half-up` corresponds to `RoundTiesToAway`. GDA's `round-half-down`, `round-up` and `round-05up` are not attributes of an IEEE 754-2019 decimal format and exist only in [`decimal_gda`](decimal_gda.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:573
msgid "A context has no `clamp`, `extended` or `tininess` setting and no unlimited precision option. An IEEE format always has the exponent range associated with its quantum; subset arithmetic from the General Decimal Arithmetic appendix is not an IEEE format. To obtain exact results, choose a precision large enough for the digits. For example, a context with `digits(a) + digits(b)` digits and an `e_max` at least the exponent of the product makes `mul_ctx` exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:580
msgid "[!NOTE] Certified elementary functions accept contexts with $e_{\\max}\\le999{,}999$ and $p\\le999{,}999$. For $p\\le999$, `DecimalContext::new` defaults to the largest range inside that limit. A larger range, from explicit `e_max` or $p\\ge1000$, makes them return NaN with `not_computed`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:588
msgid "Construct the contexts for the IEEE 754-2019 interchange formats (IEEE 754-2019 3.6)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:602
msgid "All three use `RoundTiesToEven` by default (pass `rounding~` for another attribute)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:607
msgid "`from_arithmetic_context` converts the shared Luna-Flow context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:613
msgid "The conversion copies precision and rounding. It ignores `clamp`, which every IEEE format has and whose default in `arithmetic` is `false`. A supplied `e_max` is used. A supplied `e_min` must equal $1-e_{\\max}$; otherwise the conversion returns `Err(domain_error)`. If only `e_min` is supplied, `e_max = 1 - e_min`; if neither is supplied, the default range of `new` is used. The conversion also returns `Err(domain_error)` for `AwayFromZero`, which is not an attribute of IEEE 754-2019 4.3, or for precision and `e_max` values outside their accepted ranges. GDA 1.70 sets no fixed limits; these limits are adopted from decNumber `decContext.h`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:625
msgid "Read the precision, rounding direction and exponent limits of a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:634
msgid "The context's `e_min` is always $1-e_{\\max}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:638
msgid "Read or set the refinement limit for certified elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:645
msgid "The elementary functions evaluate an enclosure at increasing working precision until both ends round to the same result. The limit counts evaluations: `Some(n)` allows `n` evaluations (a value below 1 counts as 1), `Some(12)` is the default, and `None` removes this count limit. If the function does not decide a result within the limit, `try_*_ctx` returns the `CertificationFailure` error with the refinement count; the plain and `*_ctx` forms return NaN with `not_computed`. With `None`, evaluation stops when the working precision exceeds the larger of 8,000,000 bits and 256 times the initial working precision. The default changes no result. This field takes part in `==`, so contexts that differ only in this limit are unequal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:672
msgid "Read or set the precision limit of the elementary functions and the `pow` family."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:680
msgid "For an ordinary operand, `Some(n)` causes the `pow` family and elementary functions to return `not_computed` when the context precision exceeds `n` digits. The `try_` forms return `Err`. The default is `Some(999_999)` digits, and `None` removes this implementation limit. Above the default limit, the library does not bound running time or memory and makes no guarantee; this range is not covered by the conformance gates. Results returned by these operations still come from the same certified kernels and are correctly rounded. The measured times for `pow(2, 0.5)` are 0.6 s at 100,000 digits, 14.7 s at 1,000,000 digits, and more than 300 s at 10,000,000 digits (release build, loaded machine)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:690
msgid "When the certified kernel cannot represent the working precision (above 80,807,093 digits), functions that use it return the context-limit error, regardless of this limit. The square-root route of `pow` (exponent 0.5, `sqrt_ctx`) and the exact routes are not refused there. The cases listed in IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 return values regardless of the precision limit. This field participates in `==`; `with_precision`, `with_e_max` and `with_rounding` preserve it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:713
msgid "Derive a context by replacing its precision, exponent range or rounding direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:721
msgid "`with_precision` replaces the precision, and `with_e_max` replaces the exponent range; in the latter, `e_min` follows from `e_max`. Either method aborts when its argument is outside the limits accepted by `new`. `with_rounding` replaces the context's rounding direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:726
msgid "Rounding modes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:730
msgid "`DecimalRoundingMode` lists the five IEEE 754-2019 4.3 rounding-direction attributes for decimal formats, using the spellings of `BinaryRoundingMode`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:744
msgid "Let $x>0$ lie strictly between adjacent representable coefficients $c$ and $c+1$, measured in units in the last place. `RoundTowardZero` returns $c$. `RoundTowardPositive` and `RoundTowardNegative` round toward $+\\infty$ and $-\\infty$, respectively, so their results depend on the sign. The nearest rounding directions, `RoundTiesToEven` and `RoundTiesToAway`, return the nearer coefficient; at an exact tie they choose the even coefficient and the one farther from zero, respectively. The kernels also use three rounding algorithms that a context cannot select: `HalfDown`, `Up` and `ZeroFiveUp`. Division uses `ZeroFiveUp` for its double-rounding guard digit, and certified functions use directed bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:756
msgid "Map between `DecimalRoundingMode` and the shared `@arithmetic.RoundingMode`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:769
msgid "none (`from_arithmetic` returns `None`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:771
msgid "`to_arithmetic` returns `None` for `RoundTiesToAway`, which the shared enum does not contain. `DecimalContext::from_arithmetic_context` and the `_contextual` forms return `Err` for `AwayFromZero`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:775
msgid "Every context detects tininess before rounding (IEEE 754-2019 7.5). A non-zero exact result whose magnitude is below $10^{e_{\\min}}$ is tiny, regardless of the delivered value. An inexact tiny result raises `underflow`. Contexts have no option for the binary after-rounding rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:780
msgid "Status flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:784
msgid "`DecimalFlags` records the conditions raised by one operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:793
msgid "Flag"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:793
msgid "Set when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:795
msgid "the result differs from the exact result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:796
msgid "the result is tiny and inexact"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:797
msgid "the rounded result's adjusted exponent exceeds $e_{\\max}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:798
msgid "finite operands produce an exact infinite result ($x/0$, $\\ln 0$, $\\log_2 0$, $\\log_{10} 0$, $\\operatorname{logb} 0$, $\\operatorname{log1p}(-1)$, $\\operatorname{log2p1}(-1)$, $\\operatorname{log10p1}(-1)$, $\\operatorname{rSqrt}(\\pm 0)$, $\\operatorname{compound}(-1, n<0)$, $\\operatorname{powr}(\\pm 0, y<0)$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:799
msgid "the result is not usefully definable (signaling NaN, $\\infty-\\infty$, $0\\times\\infty$, $0/0$, bad quantize, text that is not a number)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:800
msgid "the library cannot compute a result within an implementation limit (a context beyond the elementary kernels, an operand beyond their range, an exact sum with an exponent gap beyond the work bound, or an undecided certification): the value is a NaN, `invalid_operation` is not raised, and the `try_` form returns `Err` of the certification-failure kind"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:802
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 7.2 to 7.6 defines the five IEEE flags; `not_computed` is an additional library flag. The General Decimal Arithmetic flags `rounded`, `subnormal`, `clamped`, `lost_digits`, `conversion_syntax`, `division_impossible`, `division_undefined` and `invalid_context` belong to `decimal_gda`, not `decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:807
msgid "The fields are private. Read each flag with a method of the same name, such as `flags.inexact()` or `flags.division_by_zero()`. Flags do not accumulate implicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:812
msgid "Create an empty flag set, combine two sets, or test whether a set contains an error condition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:820
msgid "`new` clears every flag. `combine` takes the field-wise OR; it is associative, commutative and idempotent, and `new()` is its identity. `has_error` tests"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:827
msgid "This is the set of conditions for which IEEE 754-2019 delivers no usable number: 7.2 defines invalid operation as having no usefully definable result. A failed `from_string_ctx` raises `invalid_operation`, so `has_error` returns true. `has_error` excludes `overflow`, `underflow` and `inexact`: those deliver a usable result (IEEE 754-2019 7.4 to 7.6 define it, as 7.3 does for division by zero). `division_by_zero` is in the set only so that the checked forms of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` can return `Err` for division by zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:850
msgid "Plain operations without a context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:852
msgid "The operators and functions in this group take no context and return no flags. Use them for exact work or generic code over the Luna-Flow algebra traits. Use [context arithmetic](#context-arithmetic) when rounding, the exponent range or flags matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:857
msgid "Implement the arithmetic operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and unary `-`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:867
msgid "Each operator runs the matching [context operation](#context-arithmetic) (`add_ctx`, `sub_ctx`, `mul_ctx` or `div_ctx`) in a private context and drops the flags. That context uses the larger precision of the operands, $\\max(p_a,p_b)$, round-half-even, the stored-value range (`decimal_value_e_max` = $2\\,147\\,483\\,646$, `decimal_value_e_tiny` = $-2\\,147\\,483\\,645$), and no fold-down. The result has precision $\\max(p_a,p_b)$. For checked division, call the trait method `DivChecked::div_checked(a, b, ctx)` (see [`parse_checked` and the checked traits](#decimalparse_checked-sqrt_checked-pow_nat_checked-pow_int_checked)); `Decimal::div_checked` is not an inherent method."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:878
msgid "An exact result uses the cohort member closest to the preferred exponent (IEEE 754-2019 5.4.1): $\\min(q_a,q_b)$ for `add` and `sub`, $q_a+q_b$ for `mul`, and $q_a-q_b$ for `div`. Thus `1.20 + 3.40` is `4.60`, `6.0 / 2` is `3.0`, `1.25 * 2.50` is `3.1250`, and `1.20 - 1.20` is `0.00`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:882
msgid "An inexact result has exactly $p$ digits at the least exponent, IEEE 754-2019 5.2, and is not reduced. At seven digits, `1 / 3` is `0.3333333`; a product of two five-digit values is rounded to five digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:885
msgid "For every value, `neg` flips the sign bit; this includes zeros and NaNs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:887
msgid "Special values follow the context operations:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:891
msgid "A NaN operand"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:891
msgid "A quiet NaN with the payload of the first NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:892
msgid "$\\infty-\\infty$, $0\\times\\infty$, $0/0$ or $\\infty/\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:892
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:215
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1589
#: manual/api/def.md:51
#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:551
#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:599
msgid "NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:893
msgid "Infinity with the sign of the exact quotient; the sign of zero counts, so `1 / -0` is $-\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:894
msgid "Zero product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:894
msgid "Sign is the exclusive or of the operand signs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:895
msgid "Finite value divided by infinity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:895
msgid "Signed zero at the smallest exponent of the private context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:896
msgid "Result above the private range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:896
msgid "Overflow result of that context, which is infinity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:897
msgid "Result below the private range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:897
msgid "Round half-even to the smallest exponent, possibly producing signed zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:899
msgid "For example, `1E+2147483646 * 1E+2147483646` is `inf`, and `123E-2147483640 * 1E-10` is `0E-2147483645`. For every finite value inside the private range, `x + 0 == x`, `x - 0 == x` and `x * 1 == x` hold at any exponent. The sum or product uses the cohort member closest to the preferred exponent that holds the value in $p$ digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:920
msgid "Change only the sign bit of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:928
msgid "Clear, copy or replace a sign bit without rounding or raising flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:930
msgid "They preserve the exponent, payload and NaN kind. `abs` clears the sign, `neg` flips it, `copy_sign` copies the second operand's sign bit, and `copy` returns the input value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:936
msgid "`normalized` changes the cohort member; `with_precision` sets the precision of the value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:944
msgid "`normalized` returns the reduced member of the cohort: it removes all trailing zeros and gives zero exponent 0. If the coefficient has more digits than the precision of the value, it first rounds half-even to that precision. It is the only operation in this package that strips trailing zeros; IEEE 754-2019 defines no such operation. To apply a context before reducing, call `x.apply_ctx(ctx).0.normalized()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:951
msgid "`with_precision(p, mode)` rounds a finite value to $p$ digits with `mode`, reduces it and sets its precision to $p$. It changes only the precision of a special value. Neither function raises flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:957
msgid "Compute the square root of the value under its own format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:963
msgid "`sqrt` runs `sqrt_ctx` in the plain operators' private context, with precision $p$. A negative non-zero operand returns NaN; IEEE 754-2019 7.2. The [`sqrt_checked` entry](#decimalparse_checked-sqrt_checked-pow_nat_checked-pow_int_checked) returns `Err` for this case. Both forms are correctly rounded half-even."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:968
msgid "Context arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:970
msgid "Each function in this group takes a `DecimalContext` and returns `(result, flags)`. It rounds the exact result once to the context precision in the context's rounding direction, then applies the exponent range. Overflow gives the result for that rounding direction; see [overflow](../design/decimal.md#overflow). A result whose exact magnitude is below $10^{e_{\\min}}$ is rounded directly to the subnormal grid $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$. A normal result is rounded only to $p$ digits even when its exact exponent is below $E_{\\text{tiny}}$. Large exponents are folded down to $e_{\\max}-p+1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:979
msgid "When the exact result fits, the operation uses its *preferred exponent*, so cohort information is preserved. NaN operands propagate as a quiet NaN with the sign and payload of the first NaN; the payload is cut to its low $p-1$ digits, as in `from_string_ctx` and the interchange encodings. A signaling NaN operand raises `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:985
msgid "Near the underflow threshold, the single rounding determines the result. In decimal32, the exact product `3.000001E-45 * 1.500001E-45` is $4.500004500001\\cdot 10^{-90}$. It is a normal number even though its exact exponent is below $E_{\\text{tiny}}=-101$, and rounds once to seven digits as `4.500005E-90`. The quotient `1 / 1.9999999999998E+101` is $5.0000000000005\\cdot 10^{-102}$, which is subnormal. Its guarded quotient rounds once to the grid $10^{-101}$, giving `1E-101` with `inexact` and `underflow`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:996
msgid "Add, subtract, multiply and divide return correctly rounded results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1005
msgid "The preferred exponents are $\\min(q_a,q_b)$ for addition and subtraction, $q_a+q_b$ for multiplication, and $q_a-q_b$ for division. An exact quotient uses the cohort member closest to its preferred exponent. An inexact quotient has $p$ digits, except a subnormal quotient, whose exponent is $E_{\\text{tiny}}$. A zero sum is $+0$ except under `Floor`, when it is $-0$ if either operand is negative, or when both operands are $-0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1012
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1114
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:479
