msgid "Operands"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1012
msgid "Result and flag"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1014
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1015
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1016
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1017
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1171
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1172
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1173
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1174
msgid "NaN with `invalid_operation`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1018
msgid "finite non-zero $x/0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1018
msgid "Signed infinity with `division_by_zero`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1019
msgid "finite$/\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1019
msgid "Signed zero with exponent $E_{\\text{tiny}}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1021
msgid "When an operand of `add_ctx` or `sub_ctx` lies below every rounding boundary of the sum, the operation does not align it digit by digit. It replaces that operand with a sticky unit of the same sign just below those boundaries, then rounds the sum once. See [addition with a far smaller addend](../design/decimal.md#addition-with-a-far-smaller-addend). At precision 7, `1598617.000000000001 - 0.000000000002` is `1598616` under `Down`, while `6.0000005E-73 + 1E-101` is `6.000001E-73` under `HalfEven`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1061
msgid "`fma_ctx` computes $x \\cdot y + z$ with one rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1067
msgid "The product and its sum with `z` are formed exactly; only the sum is rounded. For $0\\times\\infty+z$, the result is invalid when `z` is a number or infinity. When `z` is a quiet NaN, the result is that NaN without `invalid_operation` (IEEE 754-2019 7.2 leaves whether to signal invalid operation implementation-defined). The expression $\\infty \\cdot y + (-\\infty)$ with opposite signs is invalid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1076
msgid "`sqrt_ctx` returns the correctly rounded square root of its operand."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1082
msgid "The preferred exponent is $\\lfloor q/2 \\rfloor$. An exact root uses the cohort member closest to it; for example, `sqrt(0.0400)` is `0.20`. An inexact root has $p$ digits and raises `inexact`. The result preserves $\\sqrt{-0}=-0$ and $\\sqrt{+\\infty}=+\\infty$. A negative non-zero operand or $-\\infty$ gives NaN with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1090
msgid "Round a value to the context or return its absolute value rounded to the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1097
msgid "`apply_ctx` rounds a value to the precision and exponent range of the context. An exponent above $e_{\\max}-p+1$ is folded down. It quiets a NaN and keeps the sign of zero. `abs_ctx` returns $|x|$ rounded to the context; a signaling NaN raises `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1103
msgid "Compute the IEEE remainder of a value and a divisor."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1109
msgid "`remainder_ctx(x, y)` returns $x-y\\cdot n$, where $n$ is $x/y$ rounded to the nearest integer, with ties to even. IEEE 754-2019 5.3.1 calls this operation *remainder*; GDA calls it *remainder-near*, which `decimal_gda` keeps. The result is exact when it fits and has preferred exponent $\\min(q_x,q_y)$. A zero result has the sign of $x$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1116
msgid "$x$ and zero, including zero and zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1116
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1117
msgid "invalid operation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1117
msgid "infinity and $y$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1118
msgid "$x$ and infinity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1130
msgid "Quantum and exponent operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1134
msgid "Return the quantum of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1140
msgid "For a finite $x$, `quantum_ctx` returns $(0,q,1)$, or $10^q$, where $q$ is the exponent of $x$; this includes zeros. Thus `quantum_ctx(1.2300)` is `0.0001`. The exact result has preferred exponent $Q(x)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1144
msgid "Result and flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1146
msgid "Infinity, either sign"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1146
msgid "$+\\infty$, no exception"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1147
msgid "Quiet NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1147
msgid "propagated with its payload, no flag"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1148
msgid "Signaling NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1148
msgid "quiet NaN with its payload; raises `invalid_operation` (6.2)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1150
msgid "The stored exponent is `exponent10`. IEEE 754-2019 5.3.2 says *should* for `quantum` and *shall* for `quantize`. Only a signaling NaN can raise a flag, but the operation takes a context and returns flags, like `logb_ctx` and `next_up_ctx` (`doc/manual/conventions.md`); there is no plain `quantum`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1157
msgid "Round a value to the exponent of another value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1163
msgid "`x.quantize_ctx(y, ctx)` returns $x$ with exponent $q_y$, using the context's rounding direction when digits are dropped. It raises `inexact` if any dropped digit is nonzero. IEEE 754-2019 5.3.2 specifies `quantize` and its preferred exponent $Q(y)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1167
msgid "The result is NaN with `invalid_operation` in these cases:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1169
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:182
#: manual/api/interval.md:732
msgid "Condition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1171
msgid "The target exponent is outside $[E_{\\text{tiny}},e_{\\max}]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1172
msgid "The resulting coefficient needs more than $p$ digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1173
msgid "The adjusted exponent exceeds $e_{\\max}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1174
msgid "Exactly one operand is infinite"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1176
msgid "Two infinities give the infinity of $x$. The quantum does not silently change to another value of the cohort range: if the result cannot have exponent $q_y$, the operation fails. A target exponent in $(e_{\\max}-p+1,e_{\\max}]$ is accepted as the fold-down exception; the result then has exponent $e_{\\max}-p+1$, like every other result. For example, quantizing `1E+384` to `1E+384` in decimal64 gives `1.000000000000000E+384`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1199
msgid "Test whether two values have the same exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1205
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 5.7.3 defines `sameQuantum`: two finite values have the same quantum when their exponents are equal. Two infinities and two NaNs always have the same quantum; every other pair does not. The operation never raises flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1210
msgid "Round a value to an integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1217
msgid "A finite value with exponent $q \\ge 0$ is already integral and the functions return it unchanged, even when it has more than $p$ digits; the format's fold-down of its exponent still applies. For a value with $q < 0$, they quantize to exponent 0 using the context's rounding direction and an internal precision of $\\max(p, \\text{the digit count of the value})$. Thus the integral part is not rounded to $p$ digits: at precision 3, `12345.6` gives `12346`. `to_integral_exact_ctx` raises `inexact` when it discards non-zero digits; `to_integral_value_ctx` returns the same value with `inexact` cleared. Infinities are unchanged, and NaNs are quieted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1227
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 5.9 defines roundToIntegral for an operand in the context's format, which has at most $p$ digits; for such operands, these functions return the specified results. An operand with more than $p$ digits is not a value of the format, so 5.9 does not cover it and its result is implementation-defined. For such operands, this package leaves a longer integer unchanged and quantizes a fraction using $\\max(p, \\text{the digit count of the value})$ digits. This is the package's chosen GDA-style behavior: General Decimal Arithmetic rounds to an integral value at the precision of the operand."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1238
msgid "Convert a decimal to a fixed-width integer, rounding in the context's rounding direction as specified by IEEE 754-2019 5.4.1 and 5.8."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1248
msgid "These functions follow the interface of the `Binary` conversions with the same names. They round using the context's rounding direction: ties to even, toward zero, toward positive, toward negative or ties away, the five directions specified by IEEE 754-2019 5.8. A quiet or signaling NaN, an infinity, or a value outside the integer type's range produces `None` and raises `invalid_operation`. A negative value that rounds to zero produces `0`, also for an unsigned result; any other negative value is out of range for an unsigned type. With `exact=true` (`convertToIntegerExact`), a changed value raises `inexact`; without it, a valid conversion has no flags. Context precision and exponent range do not affect conversion: any integer that fits the target type converts exactly, regardless of its digit count. The conversion does not form $10^q$: `1E+999999` is out of range and `1E-999999` rounds to zero, or to one unit in a direction away from zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1264
msgid "Scale a value by a power of ten or return its adjusted exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1271
msgid "`x.scaleb_ctx(n, ctx)` adds $n$ to the exponent of $x$ and returns $x \\cdot 10^n$. IEEE 754-2019 5.3.3 defines scaleB for integral counts only; the language defines behavior for a non-integral second operand, including an infinity. This package also requires $n$ to be finite and integral, with exponent 0, and $|n| \\le 2(e_{\\max}+p)$; otherwise it returns NaN and raises `invalid_operation`. The count bound follows the GDA `scaleb` rule, while `Binary::scaleb_ctx` clamps a larger count."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1279
msgid "The scaled value is rounded once as a context result. A coefficient longer than $p$ digits is rounded to $p$ digits: scaling `12345678901234567890` by 0 in decimal64 gives `1.234567890123457E+19` with `inexact`. A subnormal result is rounded to $E_{\\text{tiny}}$. Overflow produces the result selected by the context's rounding direction and raises `overflow` and `inexact`; with decimal64 and `TowardZero`, scaling `9E+384` by 1 gives `9.999999999999999E+384`. Large exponents are folded down. A zero keeps its sign and its exponent is clamped to the range: scaling `0` by 700 in decimal64 gives `0E+369`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1304
msgid "`logb_ctx(x)` returns the adjusted exponent $\\lfloor\\log_{10}|x|\\rfloor$ as an integer `Decimal`. For $\\pm0$ it returns $-\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`, and for $\\pm\\infty$ it returns $+\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1308
msgid "Adjacent values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1312
msgid "Return the neighboring representable values in a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1319
msgid "`next_up_ctx` returns the least value in the context greater than `x`; `next_down_ctx` returns the greatest value less than `x`. The range includes subnormals down to $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$ and the largest finite value $(10^{p}-1)\\,10^{e_{\\max}-p+1}$. For example, `next_up_ctx(-∞)` returns the most negative finite value. Finite results raise no flags. IEEE 754-2019 5.3.1 defines nextUp and nextDown only: the next-toward operation of GDA 1.70 is `next_toward` of `decimal_gda`, and this package has no `next_toward_ctx`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1341
msgid "Compare two values numerically with `compare`. `Compare`, `==` and the comparison operators use this ordering."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1348
msgid "`compare` returns $-1$, 0 or 1 by numeric value. It treats $-0$ and $+0$ as equal and treats every member of a cohort as equal. NaN has no numeric order, so `Compare` stays a total preorder (sorting never aborts): every NaN compares equal to every other NaN and greater than every non-NaN. `equal` (`==`) follows the same rule, including `NaN == NaN` as `true`. This is *not* IEEE equality; use [`compare_checked`](#decimalcompare_checked), `compare_quiet` or the predicates; these leave NaN unordered. The operator methods are listed under [trait implementations](#eq-and-compare-decimalequal-not_equal-op_lt-op_le-op_gt-op_ge)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1372
msgid "Use `compare_checked` for IEEE numeric comparison that returns an error for NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1378
msgid "When neither operand is NaN, it returns `Ok(compare(x, y))`. Otherwise it returns `Err(unordered_comparison)`. It implements `CompareChecked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1383
msgid "Compare values using the four-valued IEEE 754-2019 5.11 relation and return the associated flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1391
msgid "The relation is `Less`, `Equal`, `Greater` or `Unordered` when an operand is NaN. Both forms treat $-0$ and $+0$ as equal and equate all members of a cohort. The quiet form raises `invalid_operation` only for a signaling NaN; the signaling form raises it for every unordered comparison. These functions match the corresponding `Binary` functions. The removed `compare_ctx` and `compare_signal_ctx` returned the GDA-style `Decimal` result $-1$, 0, 1 or NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1398
msgid "`Decimal::compare_quiet_equal` and the other comparison predicates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1400
msgid "The predicates of IEEE 754-2019 Tables 5.1 and 5.2 that `Binary` has."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1412
msgid "Each predicate applies its corresponding quiet or signaling comparison and returns its flags. On an unordered pair, only `compare_quiet_unordered` returns true; the other predicates return false."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1416
msgid "`Decimal::compare_quiet_not_equal` and the other comparison predicates of Tables 5.1 and 5.2"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1418
msgid "These fifteen predicates complete the comparison predicates of IEEE 754-2019 5.11."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1438
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 5.11 lists 22 comparison predicates: 10 in Table 5.1, including the predicates associated with the common mathematical symbols, and 12 in Table 5.2. Together with the seven predicates in the preceding entry, all 22 have a function here. Each function returns a Boolean and the flags raised by `compare_quiet` or `compare_signaling`; a quiet predicate raises `invalid_operation` only for a signaling NaN, while a signaling predicate raises it for every NaN operand. Each function's doc comment names its table row. Its name starts with `compare_`, followed by `quiet` or `signaling` and the table's predicate name in snake case. Predicates paired as negations in a table return opposite Boolean values and the same flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1477
msgid "`total_order` and `total_order_mag` implement the IEEE 754-2019 5.10 predicates `totalOrder` and `totalOrderMag`; `total_order_compare` gives the same order as a three-way comparison."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1487
msgid "`total_order(x, y)` is true when `x` is ordered at or before `y`. `total_order_compare(x, y)` returns `-1`, `0` or `1` for that order. It covers every representation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1496
msgid "For equal finite values, the exponent orders the representations. Positive values with the smaller exponent come first ($1.00 < 1.0$); for negative values, those with the larger exponent come first. NaNs with the same sign and kind are ordered by payload, in reverse order for negative NaNs. `total_order_compare` returns 0 only for identical representations. `total_order_mag(x, y)` applies `total_order` to the absolute values. These functions take no context, perform no rounding and raise no flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1506
msgid "Test whether two values are the same datum with `identical`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1512
msgid "`x.identical(y)` is true when the class, sign, signaling bit, coefficient (the payload for a NaN), exponent and precision label are all equal. Unlike `==`, it distinguishes values with equal numerical value but different cohort members (`1.20` and `1.2`). Unlike `total_order_compare`, it also compares the precision label. A zero and its negative are not identical, nor are NaNs with different payloads."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1531
msgid "Select values by numeric order without a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1540
msgid "`min_num` and `max_num` select the other operand when exactly one is a quiet NaN. They return a quiet NaN when both operands are NaNs or either operand is signaling, and return the receiver when the values tie."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1544
msgid "`clamp` returns `min` below the range, `max` above it, and the value, including a NaN, otherwise. It aborts when a bound is NaN or `min > max`. `clamp_checked` returns `Err(domain_error)` for those inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1550
msgid "Compute the IEEE 754-2019 9.6 minimum and maximum operations. The General Decimal Arithmetic `min`, `max`, `min-magnitude` and `max-magnitude` are in `decimal_gda`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1565
msgid "`minimum_ctx` and `maximum_ctx` return a quiet NaN when either operand is a NaN. The `_number_` variants return the number when exactly one operand is a NaN. All four raise `invalid_operation` when either operand is signaling."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1569
msgid "The magnitude variants compare $|x|$ and $|y|$. When the magnitudes are equal, they use the signed order. Ties are resolved by `total_order_compare`, so $-0 < +0$. The selected value is rounded to the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1575
msgid "Each elementary function has two forms. `f_ctx(x, ctx)` returns `(result, flags)`. `try_f_ctx(x, ctx)` returns the same pair in `Ok`, or returns `Err` with an `ArithmeticError` when it cannot certify the result. The error's `certification_failure_detail()` names the operation, target precision and exhausted refinement limit. On certification failure, `f_ctx` returns NaN with `not_computed`; `try_f_ctx` returns `Err` of the certification-failure kind."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1582
msgid "Transcendental results are **certified**. The implementation evaluates a guaranteed enclosure of $f(x)$ in [`interval`](interval.md) and accepts it only when both bounds round to the same `Decimal` with the same flags. The [design page](../design/decimal.md#certified-elementary-functions) describes the method. Since rounding is monotone, an accepted result is the rounding of $f(x)$ in every `DecimalRoundingMode`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1589
msgid "The implementation decides exact cases before the enclosure loop. For example, $\\log_{10} 1000 = 3$, $e^0 = 1$, $\\sin 0 = 0$, $\\operatorname{cospi}(1) = -1$, $\\operatorname{tanpi}(0.25) = 1$, $\\operatorname{acos}(1) = 0$, $x^{0.5}$, exact roots such as `rootn_ctx(0.008, 3)` $= 0.2$, and exact norms such as `hypot_ctx(0.3, 0.4)` $= 0.5$ return without `inexact`. An exact binary result such as `pow_ctx(4, 1.5)` $= 8$ is returned exactly by the enclosure itself. Integer powers are not certified. [`pow_ctx`](#decimalpow_ctx-pown_ctx-rootn_ctx-hypot_ctx-try_pow_ctx-try_pown_ctx-try_rootn_ctx-try_hypot_ctx) rounds them once from the exact power or from refined directed bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1598
msgid "[!NOTE] **Exact non-integral powers.** A power whose exact result is representable decimal but not a binary fraction is decided in decimal before the loop, so in decimal64 `pow_ctx(0.0016, 0.25)` is `0.2`, `pow_ctx(32, 0.2)` is `2` and `pow_ctx(0.04, 1.5)` is `0.008`, all without `inexact` and in every rounding direction. The exponent is reduced to $p/q$ in lowest terms — a decimal exponent is $c \\cdot 10^{-k}$, so $q$ keeps only the twos and fives the coefficient cannot cancel — and the power is exact exactly when $x$ itself is a perfect $q$-th power. The root is taken first and then raised to $p$, so the test never handles a number longer than the result. It finds every exact result of at most `precision + 1` digits, the only ones that sit on a rounding boundary; a longer exact result lies inside its rounding cell and the enclosure rounds it like any other."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1612
msgid "These operations return NaN with `not_computed`; the `try_` forms return `Err` of the certification-failure kind when $p > 999\\,999$, $e_{\\max} > 999\\,999$, $e_{\\min} < -999\\,999$, or a finite operand has more than 999,999 digits or an adjusted exponent beyond about $\\pm 10^6$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1617
msgid "In such a context, cases listed in IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 or Table 9.1 return values; other cases return `not_computed`. The listed cases are a NaN operand; an infinite or zero operand; the exact points (`ln_ctx(1)`, `log2_ctx(1)`, `log10_ctx(1)`, `acos_ctx(1)`, `acospi_ctx(1)`, `acosh_ctx(1)` are `0`, `atanpi_ctx(+inf)` is `0.5`, `sinpi_ctx(n)` for an integer `n`, `cospi_ctx(n + 0.5)`, `tanpi_ctx` at integers and half-integers); domain errors (`invalid_operation`) and poles (`division_by_zero`); `compound`, `rootn` and `rsqrt` for the operands the clause lists; and the constants of `atan2`, `atan2pi` and `atan(+-inf)`. The context limit is tested after these cases and before the first computation. Exact results of ordinary operands not listed by the clause still return `not_computed`: `hypot_ctx(3, 4)`, `rsqrt_ctx(4)`, `log10_ctx(1E+5)`, `atanpi_ctx(1)`, `acospi_ctx(0)`, `sinpi_ctx(0.5)`, `cospi_ctx(1)`, `exp2_ctx(3)`, `rootn_ctx(8, 3)`. Accepting every context for ordinary operands is the subject of [#478](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/478). `decimal_gda` keeps the other order, which the decTest corpus requires."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1634
msgid "The constants of `atan(+-inf)` and multiples of pi in `atan2` are computed at the context precision. At 1,000,000 digits that takes about 100 seconds each. From 80,807,094 digits, the result is `not_computed` at once because `decimal_certified_work_refusal` refuses a first working precision above `binary_precision_max`, 268,435,456 bits (`binary/context.mbt:43`); the first working precision is 3.321929 bits per digit plus 31 guard bits. The largest precision served is 80,807,093 digits. Integral powers in `decimal` support counts through 999,999,999 for positive exponents and -1,999,999,997 for negative exponents; these are adopted decNumber implementation limits, not limits imposed by GDA 1.70. Integer powers above those counts use bounded square-and-multiply when the exact exponent coefficient has at most 4,096 decimal digits. Larger coefficients are decided without expansion (see below). Thus `pow_ctx(2, 1E+9)` returns `inf` with `overflow` under `DecimalContext::new()`, and so does `pow_ctx(2, 1E+9999999)`, without expanding the exponent. The same large input to `decimal_gda` still follows the GDA adapter's decNumber count limit and returns `invalid_operation`; IEEE `decimal` and `decimal_gda` therefore intentionally differ on this input. Non-integral powers such as `pow_ctx(2, 1.5)` and `exp2_ctx(2)` remain subject to the elementary-function context limits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1656
msgid "Compute the General Decimal Arithmetic exponential and logarithms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1667
msgid "$e^x$ returns $1$ for $x=\\pm 0$, $+\\infty$ for $x=+\\infty$, and $+0$ for $x=-\\infty$. IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 requires $\\ln(\\pm 0)$ and $\\log_{10}(\\pm 0)$ to return $-\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`; `log2_ctx`, `log1p_ctx` and `logb_ctx` do the same. General Decimal Arithmetic, followed by [`decimal_gda`](decimal_gda.md), raises no condition for these inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1673
msgid "Both logarithms return $+\\infty$ for $+\\infty$. They return NaN with `invalid_operation` for a negative value or $-\\infty$. In addition, $\\ln 1=0$, and $\\log_{10}$ of a power of ten returns its exact integer exponent. The implementation decides arguments that certainly make $e^x$ overflow or underflow without evaluating the exponential."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1681
msgid "Compute powers, roots and the Euclidean norm."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1683
msgid "The `pow` family (`pow_ctx`, `pown_ctx`, `powr_ctx` and their `try_` forms) has an implementation limit on context precision. By default, the limit is 999,999 decimal digits, the precision component of `DecimalContext::elementary_precision_limit`. Setting it to `None` or a larger value removes this limit without a guarantee. Above the default limit, the functions return the context-limit error immediately (`not_computed` in the plain form) for an ordinary operand. They do not compute even the exponent `0.5`, which would otherwise be a square root at that precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1692
msgid "The cost is the reason for the limit: `pow(2, 0.5)` takes about 0.6 s at 100,000 digits, 14.7 s at 1,000,000 digits and more than 300 s at 10,000,000 digits. The special cases for `pow` listed in IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 still return values at every context precision. `pown_ctx` applies `pow_ctx` to an integral exponent and shares the same limit, including for an exact integral power. `rootn_ctx` and `compound_ctx` were already under it. `sqrt_ctx` has no such limit because it is a basic operation (IEEE 754-2019 5.4.1)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1711
msgid "`pow_ctx(x, y)` implements the power operation of IEEE 754-2019 9.2. A signaling NaN operand is quieted and raises `invalid_operation` before the identities below apply. Otherwise, $x^{\\pm 0}=1$, including when $x$ is zero, infinite or a quiet NaN. Also, $\\operatorname{pow}(+1,y)=1$ for every $y$, including a quiet NaN, and $\\operatorname{pow}(-1,\\pm\\infty)=1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1717
msgid "For any other base and an infinite exponent, the magnitude of the base determines the result: $|x|<1$ tends to zero for exponent $+\\infty$ and infinity for exponent $-\\infty$; $|x|>1$ gives the reverse. A negative base does not make these cases invalid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1722
msgid "For a zero base and negative integer exponent, the result is a signed infinity selected by the zero sign and exponent parity, with `division_by_zero`: $(-0)^{-1}=-\\infty$ and $(-0)^{-2}=+\\infty$. Positive integer powers preserve the zero sign according to parity: $(-0)^3=-0$ and $(-0)^2=+0$. Zero raised to a negative non-integer exponent returns positive infinity with `division_by_zero`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1729
msgid "For integer powers within the bounded count, the implementation uses binary square-and-multiply. It returns an exact power when it fits in $p$ digits and otherwise returns the correctly rounded result. It also supports integral exponents outside the bounded count by certifying the magnitude. For an odd power of a negative base, it swaps the directed rounding directions before restoring the sign."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1736
msgid "For large integer exponents, the implementation decides zero and infinity cases from the sign and parity without expanding the exponent. For example, `pown_ctx(-0, Int::max_value())` returns `-0`, `pown_ctx(-0, Int::min_value())` returns `+infinity` with `division_by_zero`, and `pown_ctx(-infinity, Int::min_value())` returns `+0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1742
msgid "For other integral exponents, the result is exact when the power fits in $p$ digits and correctly rounded otherwise. In decimal32, `pow_ctx(3.339434, 3)` returns `37.24077`; the exact cube is $37.240765000\\ldots$. The operation treats $x^{0.5}$ as `sqrt_ctx`. It certifies positive non-integer powers and treats a negative base with a finite non-integer exponent as invalid. These IEEE rules intentionally differ from [`decimal_gda`](decimal_gda.md), which follows General Decimal Arithmetic, including its invalid result for $0^0$ and the `powx2015` vector rounded to `1.00`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1752
msgid ""
"`pown_ctx(x, n)` converts the integer $n$ exactly, so $(-1)^{12345679}=-1$ at any precision. `rootn_ctx(x, n)` returns $x^{1/n}$ for integer $n\\n"
"e 0$. An even root of a negative value and $n=0$ are invalid. For $n<0$, `rootn_ctx` returns infinity for $\\pm 0$ with `division_by_zero`. It returns an exactly representable root exactly; for example, `rootn_ctx(0.008, 3)` returns `0.2` with no flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1759
msgid "`hypot_ctx(x, y)` returns $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. It returns $+\\infty$ if either operand is infinite, even when the other is a quiet NaN. An exactly representable norm is returned exactly; when $y$ is zero, it returns $|x|$. For example, `hypot_ctx(0.3, 0.4)` returns `0.5`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1764
msgid "The implementation never expands an integral exponent with more than 4,096 digits. For a base other than $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 0$ or $\\pm\\infty$, the magnitude $|y\\ln|x||$ is far beyond any context. The result is therefore the overflow or underflow value selected by the rounding direction; the base's relation to 1 and the exponent's sign determine which. The result sign follows parity. This case does not raise `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1771
msgid "A base with more than about 4,000 fractional digits still returns NaN with `invalid_operation` when it does not meet that bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1774
msgid "For an exact result, `pow_ctx` and `pown_ctx` select the cohort member whose exponent is closest to the preferred exponent in IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2. For `pow_ctx(x, y)`, the preferred exponent is $\\lfloor y\\,q_x\\rfloor$; for `pown_ctx(x, n)`, it is $\\lfloor n\\,q_x\\rfloor$. Here $q_x$ is the exponent stored in $x$, and the cohort member must fit in $p$ digits. In decimal64, `pown_ctx(1.00, 2)` returns `1.0000`, `pown_ctx(-1.0, 3)` returns `-1.000`, and `pown_ctx(-1E+5, 5)` returns `-1E+25` exactly with no flags. The results of `pow_ctx(1.00, 1.5)` and `pow_ctx(4.00, 1.5)` are `1.000` and `8.000`. A negative exponent cannot reach a preferred exponent above the value, so `pown_ctx(1.00, -2)` returns `1`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1785
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 defines no preferred exponent when $y$ is infinite or a NaN; `pow_ctx(1.00, Infinity)` therefore remains `1`. `decimal_gda` instead follows the exponent from repeated multiplication in General Decimal Arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1791
msgid "Evaluate the base-2 and base-10 exponentials and logarithms of IEEE 754-2019 9.2, including their forms near zero. Each function has the `f_ctx` and `try_f_ctx` shape described at the start of this section."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1810
msgid "Compute $2^x - 1$, $10^x - 1$, $\\log_2(1 + x)$ and $\\log_{10}(1 + x)$ as specified by IEEE 754-2019 9.2. These forms avoid the cancellation in `exp2(x) - 1` and the rounding of `1 + x` near zero. Each function has the `f_ctx` and `try_f_ctx` shape described at the start of this section."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1826
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 specifies the special values. For all four functions, $f(\\pm 0) = \\pm 0$ and $f(+\\infty) = +\\infty$. For `exp2m1` and `exp10m1`, $f(-\\infty)=-1$. For `log2p1` and `log10p1`, $f(-1)=-\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`; their domain is $[-1, +\\infty]$, so $x < -1$, including $-\\infty$, is invalid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1832
msgid "The exact results are $2^n - 1$ and $10^n - 1$ for integer $n$, and the integer $k$ when $1 + x = 2^k$ or $1 + x = 10^k$. Under IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2, these results have preferred exponent 0. Thus, in decimal64, `exp2m1_ctx(4)` returns `15`, `exp2m1_ctx(-1)` returns `-0.5`, `exp10m1_ctx(-2.0)` returns `-0.99`, and `log2p1_ctx(-0.50)` returns `-1`, all without flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1838
msgid "A context that cannot hold an exact result rounds it and returns its flags. For example, `log2p1_ctx(32767)` at precision 1 returns `2E+1`, a midpoint, with `inexact`. The identity $\\operatorname{log2p1}(1)=1$ is exact, while $\\operatorname{log10p1}(1)=\\log_{10}2$. The rule $f(1)=+0$ of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 applies only to `log`, `log2` and `log10`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1844
msgid "Results below the subnormal range are rounded, never replaced, so they keep the sign of $x$: `exp2m1_ctx(-1E-500)` returns `-0E-398` in decimal64 and `-1E-398` when rounding toward $-\\infty$. The same restricted contexts as `exp_ctx` return `not_computed`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1866
msgid "Compute the reciprocal square root, compound and real-power operations of IEEE 754-2019 9.2. `rsqrt_ctx` returns $1/\\sqrt{x}$, `compound_ctx` returns $(1 + x)^n$ for an `Int32` $n$, and `powr_ctx` returns $x^y = e^{y \\ln x}$. Each operation has the `f_ctx`/`try_f_ctx` shape described at the start of this section."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1880
msgid "`rsqrt_ctx` accepts $x$ in $[0, +\\infty]$. A negative input, including $-\\infty$, is invalid; $\\pm 0$ returns $\\pm\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`, and $+\\infty$ returns $+0$ (IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1). When $x = 2^{2i}5^{2j}$, the result is exact and has the preferred exponent $-\\lfloor Q(x)/2 \\rfloor$ (IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2). In decimal64, `rsqrt_ctx(4.00)` returns `0.5`, `rsqrt_ctx(1E+2)` returns `0.1`, and `rsqrt_ctx(1E-400)` returns `1E+200`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1887
msgid "`compound_ctx(x, n)` follows these special cases from IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1891
msgid "`compound(x, 0)` for $x \\ge -1$ or a quiet NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1892
msgid "`compound(x, n)` for $x < -1$, including when $n = 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1892
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1022
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1185
msgid "invalid"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1893
msgid "`compound(-1, n)` for $n < 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1893
msgid "$+\\infty$ with `division_by_zero`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1894
msgid "`compound(-1, n)` for $n > 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1896
msgid "`compound(+∞, n)` for $n > 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1897
msgid "`compound(+∞, n)` for $n < 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1899
msgid "An exact result has preferred exponent $\\lfloor n \\min(0, Q(x)) \\rfloor$ (IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2). `compound_ctx(0.10, 2)` returns `1.2100`, and `compound_ctx(0.00, 3)` returns `1.000000`. Every `Int32` value of $n$ is accepted, including the `Int32` minimum $-2^{31}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1904
msgid "`powr_ctx(x, y)` is $e^{y \\ln x}$, so its domain excludes $x < 0$ (invalid, also when $y$ is a quiet NaN). It returns a quiet NaN for a NaN operand even when $y = \\pm 0$, is invalid for `powr(±0, ±0)`, `powr(+∞, ±0)` and `powr(1, ±∞)`, and otherwise equals `pow_ctx` on $|x|$ (results, cohort and flags): `powr_ctx(-0, 3)` is `0` where `pow_ctx` gives `-0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1928
msgid "Compute the trigonometric functions of $x$ and $\\pi x$. Each has the `f_ctx`/`try_f_ctx` shape described at the start of this section."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1948
msgid "Compute the inverse trigonometric functions. `atan2(y, x)` returns the angle of the point $(x, y)$. Each has the `f_ctx`/`try_f_ctx` shape described at the start of this section."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal.md:1964
msgid "Compute the inverse circular functions measured in half-revolutions: $\\operatorname{asin}(x)/\\pi$, $\\operatorname{acos}(x)/\\pi$, $\\operatorname{atan}(x)/\\pi$ and $\\operatorname{atan2}(y, x)/\\pi$. The ordinate is the receiver of `atan2pi_ctx`, as in `y.atan2pi_ctx(x, ctx)`, and its result is in $[-1, 1]$ (IEEE 754-2019 9.2). Each operation has the `f_ctx` and `try_f_ctx` shapes described at the start of this section."
msgstr ""

