#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:1
msgid "decimal_checked API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:5
msgid "`decimal_checked` provides `DecimalChecked`, an immutable pipeline state for IEEE 754 decimal arithmetic with [`decimal`](decimal.md). It stores the current value, one `DecimalContext`, the flags raised by the latest step, the flags accumulated over all steps, and an optional `ArithmeticError`. Each operation applies the matching `Decimal::*_ctx` operation under the stored context. IEEE exceptional results (infinities, NaNs, rounded values) remain values and only set flags; only failure to certify an elementary function records an error and stops the pipeline. The [tutorial](../tutorial/decimal_checked.md) shows typical pipelines; the [design page](../design/decimal_checked.md) models the state as a writer monad over the flag monoid and proves that the accumulated flags are the union of the per-step flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:20
msgid "Add both packages to your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:29
msgid "The examples use the aliases `@decimal_checked.` and `@decimal.`, and list flags with this helper:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:47
#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:52
msgid "The state type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:51
msgid "`DecimalChecked` stores the state of an IEEE decimal pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:59
msgid "Write a state as $\\sigma = (v, c, r, F, \\varepsilon)$: value $v$, context $c$, raised flags $r$ of the latest step, accumulated flags $F$, and error $\\varepsilon$ (absent or one `ArithmeticError`). Every operation returns a new state; nothing is mutated."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:68
msgid "`from_decimal(value, context)` rounds a value into the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:74
msgid "It applies `Decimal::apply_ctx`, which rounds the value to the context's precision and exponent range, and records the resulting flags as both raised and accumulated."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:80
msgid "`parse(source, context)` converts a decimal string under the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:86
msgid "It uses `Decimal::from_string_ctx`. Extra digits are rounded (`inexact`); an invalid string gives a NaN value with `invalid_operation` raised, not an error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:92
msgid "Convert a MoonBit number into the pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:101
msgid "`from_int` and `from_bigint` parse the integer's decimal string, so an integer longer than the precision is rounded with flags. `from_double` first converts the binary value to a decimal of 17 significant digits and `from_float` to 9 digits (enough to identify the binary value), then rounds into the context with `from_decimal`. So `from_double(0.1, decimal64)` is `0.1000000000000000` with `inexact` raised."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:108
msgid "This is a conversion in two roundings, not the correctly rounded value of the binary number. With a context precision above 17 (decimal128) the result keeps only 17 digits: `from_double(0.1, decimal128)` is `0.10000000000000001`, not the exact `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827`, and its flags describe only the second rounding. With a precision below 17 the half modes can round twice (a 17-digit midpoint created by the first rounding). For the exact binary value use `from_decimal(Decimal::from_double(x, precision=p), context)` with `p = 767`, which holds every `Double` exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:143
msgid "Read the components of the state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:152
msgid "`raised()` is $r$, the flags of the latest successful step only. `flags()` is $F$, the bitwise OR of the flags of every successful step since construction or the last `clear_flags`. After an error, `value` keeps the value reached before the failing step. So do `raised` and `flags` for a method without a pipeline operand. A method with pipeline operands (the binary methods and `fma`) also takes their flags: `flags` gains theirs, and `raised` is theirs when an operand holds the error, otherwise the earlier `raised` joined with theirs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:162
msgid "`result` returns the value with the accumulated flags, or the error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:168
msgid "`result()` returns $\\mathrm{Err}(\\varepsilon)$ when an error is recorded and $\\mathrm{Ok}((v, F))$ otherwise. It is the only method that returns the value with accumulated flags; `value()` and `flags()` remain available after an error, and there is no separate `outcome()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:175
msgid "Test for and read the recorded error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:183
msgid "Controlling the state"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:187
msgid "`clear_flags()` resets both $r$ and $F$ to the empty flag set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:193
msgid "The value, context and error stay unchanged. The method also applies to a state with an error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:198
msgid "Switch to a new context and round the current value into it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:204
msgid "`with_context(context)` reapplies the value with `apply_ctx` under the new context; the new flags become $r$ and are ORed into $F$. On a state with an error it returns the state unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:210
msgid "`apply()` rounds the current value into the stored context and records its flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:237
msgid "Operations that cannot fail"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:241
msgid "These methods apply the corresponding IEEE decimal operation to the current value, and to the pipeline operands of the methods that take them, under the stored context of the left pipeline. A right operand is a `DecimalChecked`: an error of the left pipeline stays, an error of the right one is taken before the operation runs (the value of the left pipeline is kept), and the conditions of the right pipeline join `raised` and `flags`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:248
msgid "`DecimalChecked::from_decimal(x, ctx)` lifts a plain value by applying the context. A signaling NaN is quieted and raises `invalid_operation` during this lift. To use the operand exactly, as IEEE 754-2019 5.1 does (operands exact, one rounding of the result), lift it in a context wide enough to hold it, for example `decimal128`; lifting it in the context of the left pipeline rounds it first and then the result, two roundings. At three digits, half-even, `1.00 + 0.24500001` is `1.25` with the operand lifted in a wide context, and `1.24` with the operand lifted at three digits (it becomes `0.245` with `inexact`, and `1.00 + 0.245` is a tie that rounds to even); each is right for what was asked."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:276
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:682
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1858
msgid "Method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:278
msgid "$\\lvert v\\rvert$ rounded into the context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:279
msgid "$v \\circ y$ correctly rounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:280
msgid "$v \\cdot m + a$ with one rounding"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:281
msgid "$\\sqrt{v}$ correctly rounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:282
msgid "$v$ rounded to the exponent of `q`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:283
msgid "the number $10^q$ for the exponent $q$ of $v$ (IEEE 754-2019 5.3.2)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:284
msgid "IEEE remainder $v - d \\cdot n$, $n$ = $v/d$ rounded to nearest, ties to even"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:285
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 9.6 `minimumNumber`/`maximumNumber`: a quiet NaN loses to a number; a signaling NaN gives the number and raises `invalid_operation`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:286
msgid "adjacent representable value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:288
msgid "The General Decimal Arithmetic `min` and `max` operations are in `decimal_gda`, not this IEEE wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:291
msgid "An operation on a state with an error returns it unchanged. Otherwise the context operation produces $(v', r') = \\mathrm{op}(v, c)$ and the new state is $(v', c, r', F \\lor r', \\text{none})$ for a method without a pipeline operand. For the binary methods and `fma`, whose operands have raised flags $r_o$ and accumulated flags $F_o$ (joined over the operands of `fma`), it is $(v', c, r' \\lor r_o, F \\lor r' \\lor F_o, \\text{none})$. An invalid operation returns NaN with `invalid_operation`; division by zero returns an infinity with `division_by_zero`; overflow returns an infinity or the largest finite value with `overflow` and `inexact`, as in IEEE 754 decimal arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:321
msgid "Operations that can record an error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:325
msgid "Apply the certified `Decimal::try_*_ctx` function with the same name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:369
msgid "The results follow `decimal`, including its exact result for some non-integral powers before refinement; see [elementary functions](decimal.md#elementary-functions). For example, $0.0016^{0.25} = 0.2$ is exact. `atan2` with zero or infinite operands returns IEEE values; an `atan2` of $+\\infty$ and 1 returns $\\pi/2$, inexact. Domain violations also return IEEE values with flags: `ln` of a negative number returns NaN with `invalid_operation`, and `tanpi(0.5)` returns an infinity with `division_by_zero`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:378
msgid "These methods record an error only when `try_*_ctx` returns `Err` with a `CertificationFailure`, meaning the correctly rounded result could not be certified within the refinement budget. In that case the new state is $(v, c, r, F, \\mathrm{Some}(e))$; the value and flags from before the step remain. For `powr`, `pow`, `hypot`, `atan2` and `atan2pi`, which take a pipeline operand, the flags of the operand still join `raised` and `flags` as described under Observation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:386
msgid "[!IMPORTANT] As an implementation limit (`DEC_MAX_MATH` in decNumber, required by the decTest suite and permitted by the GDA Invalid context condition), these operations require, by default, context precision and exponent limits within 999,999. A context with `e_max` above 999,999, or with precision of 1,000 digits or more and no explicit `e_max`, returns NaN with `not_computed`; the `try_` forms return `Err` with a certification failure. Use `decimal32()`, `decimal64()`, `decimal128()` or an explicit `e_max` no greater than 999,999. `pow` and `pown` with an integral exponent, and `pow` with exponent `0.5`, are not restricted in `e_max`, but like the others they are held to the context's `elementary_precision_limit` (999,999 digits by default; `with_elementary_precision_limit(None)` removes it)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1
msgid "decimal_gda API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:5
msgid "`decimal_gda` implements the General Decimal Arithmetic Specification 1.70 (GDA 1.70) by M. F. Cowlishaw. It has three parts:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:8
msgid "A `Decimal` is a sign, a decimal coefficient of any length and a decimal exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:10
msgid "A `GdaContext` carries the precision, the rounding, the exponent limits, clamping, the switch between extended and subset arithmetic, the flags and the enabled traps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:13
msgid "Every GDA operation is a pure function that returns a `GdaOutcome`: the defined result, the next context and the conditions the operation raised."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:16
msgid "The [tutorial](../tutorial/decimal_gda.md) shows the package in use; the [design page](../design/decimal_gda.md) derives the rules stated here."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:19
msgid "The package does not depend on the IEEE 754 package `decimal`. It publishes the GDA layer only (`Decimal`, `GdaContext`, the `Gda*` types and the functions named after the GDA operations) and adapters to the `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` and `Luna-Flow/luna-generic` traits. The engine the functions are built on, which keeps no flags, is private to the package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:25
msgid "Throughout this page a finite value is written $(-1)^s \\cdot c \\cdot 10^{q}$, with coefficient $c \\ge 0$ and exponent $q$. Its *adjusted exponent* is $\\hat q = q + \\operatorname{digits}(c) - 1$, the exponent of its leading digit. For a context with precision $p$, $E_{\\mathrm{tiny}} = E_{\\min} - p + 1$ is the smallest exponent a result may have; with clamping, $E_{\\mathrm{top}} = E_{\\max} - p + 1$ is the largest."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:42
msgid "The examples on this page call the package through the alias `@decimal_gda.`, and the trait adapters through `@lf_arith.` for `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`. Several examples define a local helper `d(s)`, which parses a literal with `Decimal::from_string` and keeps its exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:48
msgid "The `Decimal` value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:52
msgid "`Decimal` is an immutable GDA number: a finite number or a special value (an infinity, a quiet NaN or a signaling NaN)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:62
msgid "A finite number keeps its exponent, so `2.50` and `2.5` are distinct members of the same *cohort*: they compare equal numerically and differ in `compare_total`, `same_quantum` and printing. Zero is signed. A NaN carries a sign and a non-negative integer payload. A payload of 0 stands for a NaN without diagnostic information and is printed as `NaN`; GDA 1.70, page 11 (Special values) describes the payload as a positive integer when a NaN has one, and pages 18 and 19 give the string form as `NaN` with optional digits that have no leading zeros."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:69
msgid "Every value stores a precision, the precision of the value (`precision()`). It is the precision of the context or constructor that produced it. GDA operations ignore the precision of the value and use the precision of the context instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:74
msgid "The coefficient is stored as a persistent decimal limb array, so a value can be shared freely."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:79
msgid "Return $+0$, $-0$ and $1$, each with exponent 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:87
msgid "`precision` (default 34) is the precision of the value: the number of digits the coefficient may have, from 1 to 999,999,999 (the implementation limit on the precision of a context). A precision outside that range aborts, and the message names the function and the argument; no constructor replaces it by 1. The same check applies to every constructor that takes a `precision`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:95
msgid "Build the special values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:104
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:165
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:219
#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:56
msgid "Call"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:108
msgid "a positive quiet NaN with payload 0, which stands for a NaN without diagnostic information"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:110
msgid "A payload has at most `precision - 1` digits, because GDA 1.70, page 11 (Special values) says that a payload is no larger than the coefficient can represent less one digit. A negative payload, or one that is too wide for the `precision` given, aborts; at precision 1 only the payload 0 exists."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:115
msgid "An arithmetic operation that consumes a signaling NaN raises `InvalidOperation` and replaces it by the quiet NaN with the same sign and payload."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:120
msgid "`gda_value_e_max` and `gda_value_e_tiny` are the limits of the exponents a finite `Decimal` can hold."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:128
msgid "Limit on"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:130
msgid "the adjusted exponent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:131
msgid "the exponent of the least digit; it is the $E_{\\mathrm{tiny}}$ of the [working context of the plain operators](#decimaladd-decimalsub-decimalmul-decimaldiv-decimalneg), whatever the precision"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:133
msgid "A finite value, a zero included (it has one digit), is stored only if $q \\ge$ `gda_value_e_tiny` and $\\hat q \\le$ `gda_value_e_max`, where $q$ is its exponent, $c$ its coefficient and $\\hat q = q + \\operatorname{digits}(c) - 1$ its adjusted exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:138
msgid "The constructors `make`, `try_make` and `from_string` reject a value outside the limits. The operations keep the values they produce inside, by rounding, underflow or overflow, so every `Decimal` you can obtain satisfies the condition. This is the invariant behind `x + 0 == x` and `x * 1 == x` for every stored finite value (see [the plain operators](#decimaladd-decimalsub-decimalmul-decimaldiv-decimalneg))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:145
msgid "A string outside the limits is read with a context when a rounded value is wanted: [`to_number`](#to_number) rounds, overflows and underflows by the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:151
msgid "`make(c, e, p)` returns $c \\cdot 10^{q}$, where $q$ is `e`, as a value of `p` digits of coefficient, the way the to-number conversion of GDA 1.70, page 21 (to-number) does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:160
msgid "The sign comes from the sign of `c`. The exponent is kept when `c` has at most `p` digits. A longer coefficient is rounded once, in `mode` (default `HalfEven`, one of the eight rounding algorithms of a context), to `p` digits, and the rounded coefficient is not reduced."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:172
msgid "To remove trailing zeros use `Decimal::normalized` or `Decimal::trim`. No flags are reported."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:175
msgid "After the rounding, the value must be inside the implementation limit on the exponents of a stored value ([`gda_value_e_max`, `gda_value_e_tiny`](#gda_value_e_max-gda_value_e_tiny)). An exponent below `gda_value_e_tiny`, or an adjusted exponent above `gda_value_e_max` (a carry of the rounding into the next power of ten included), is never replaced by a zero or an infinity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:184
msgid "exponent or adjusted exponent outside the limits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:184
