#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:432
msgid "`normalized` rounds half to even to the precision of the value (see [`Decimal`](#decimal)) and reduces the result, removing all trailing zeros: `7.50` becomes `7.5` and `1200` becomes `1.2E+3`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:435
msgid "`trim` removes only fractional trailing zeros and never makes the exponent positive: `7.50` becomes `7.5` and `1200` stays `1200`. A zero becomes `0` with exponent 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:438
msgid "`with_precision(p, mode)` rounds to `p` digits with the rounding algorithm `mode`, removes trailing zeros and sets the precision of the value. A special value only gets the new precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:441
msgid "None of the three reports flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:443
msgid "A carry of the rounding at the top of the [implementation limit on the exponents of a stored value](#gda_value_e_max-gda_value_e_tiny) follows the Overflow rule (GDA 1.70, page 53 (Overflow)) with that limit as the largest adjusted exponent, so the result is never a finite value above the limit. For example, `9999E+2147483643` rounded to 2 digits is `inf` in the rounding algorithms that round away from zero or to nearest. In the algorithms that round toward zero it is `9.9E+2147483646`, the largest finite value of that width."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:452
msgid "`identical` tests whether two values are the same datum."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:458
msgid "It is true for two values with the same class, sign, coefficient and exponent, the same precision and, for NaNs, the same payload and the same kind (quiet or signaling). It is `compare_total` being 0 together with equal precision; GDA defines `compare_total` without a precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:462
msgid "`2.5` and `2.50` are equal by `==` and not identical. `0` and `-0` are equal and not identical. Two NaNs with the same payload are identical, although `==` and `compare` treat every NaN alike."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:465
msgid "The precision of the value is part of the relation because [the plain operators](#decimaladd-decimalsub-decimalmul-decimaldiv-decimalneg) round to the larger precision of their operands. `1` with precision 3 and `1` with precision 34 are equal and have `compare_total` 0, but divided by `3` they give `0.333` and `0.3333333333333333333333333333333333`. Two identical values give the same result in every function."
msgstr ""

