#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:490
msgid "`GdaContext` is the immutable GDA context. It holds an arithmetic policy, the flags of the context and the enabled traps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:499
msgid "The policy is the precision $p \\ge 1$, the rounding (a `GdaRoundingMode`), $E_{\\min} \\le E_{\\max}$, `clamp` and `extended`. The flags are a `GdaFlags` value that operations only ever enlarge; the traps are a `GdaTrapSet`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:503
msgid "Neither the flags nor the traps influence the numerical result of an operation. They decide the next context and whether the outcome is `Completed` or `Trapped`, as [Trap selection](#trap-selection) states."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:509
msgid "Build a context with an empty set of flags. `new` aborts on an invalid parameter; `try_new` takes the same parameters and returns a `Result`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:517
msgid "The defaults are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:529
msgid "The parameters must satisfy the rules below. `try_new` returns `Err(domain_error)` naming the invalid parameter, and `new` aborts with the same message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:532
msgid "`precision` is within `1..=999_999_999`, `e_max` within `0..=999_999_999` and `e_min` within `-999_999_999..=0`. These implementation limits are those of decNumber's `decContext.h`; they keep $E_{\\mathrm{tiny}}$, $E_{\\mathrm{top}}$ and exponent arithmetic within `Int`. GDA 1.70 itself fixes no limit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:536
msgid "`e_min` is `-e_max`, `1 - e_max` (the IEEE 754 rule) or `-e_max - 1`. GDA 1.70, page 10 (Abstract representation of numbers) requires the magnitudes of $E_{\\min}$ and $E_{\\max}$ to differ by at most one, so that the range of results is balanced. A range such as `e_min=-50, e_max=60` is rejected. The same passage requires `Elimit` to exceed five times the maximum coefficient length when there is one. The context has no maximum coefficient length, and 636 rows of the pinned `official` and `official0` decTest corpora (525 and 111) run with an `e_max` below four times the precision, so the library does not enforce that requirement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:544
msgid "`clamp` and `extended` are not parameters of the context in the GDA text. They are decNumber extensions that the library keeps, and the official decTest files use both."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:547
msgid "`clamp=true` is the fold-down of the Clamped condition (GDA 1.70, page 51 (Exceptional conditions)). It limits exponents to $E_{\\max} - p + 1$, as the interchange formats do."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:549
msgid "`extended=false` selects the GDA subset arithmetic of Appendix A (GDA 1.70, page 55 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset); footnote 43: a parameter of the context \"might\" select it). In subset arithmetic, every operation that takes a context rounds an operand longer than $p$ digits first (GDA 1.70, page 56 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset)), which raises `LostDigits` when it is inexact; so `reduce(1234567891)` at precision 9 raises `LostDigits` and `next_toward(1234567891, 0)` is `1.23456788E+9`. The logical operations, the total orders and the classifications do not round their operands. Special values cannot be parsed, `divide` and `power` remove fractional trailing zeros, and `fma` is invalid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:557
msgid "The result of every operation that takes a context obeys three rules (GDA 1.70, page 55 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset)): a zero is $+0$ with exponent 0; a result below $E_{\\min}$ (a subnormal result) is $+0$ with exponent 0 and the flags `Underflow`, `Subnormal`, `Inexact` and `Rounded` (and `Clamped`, where the operation raised it before, as `exp` does); and a result with a positive exponent has exponent 0 when it then has at most $p$ digits. So `reduce` of `1200` is `1200`, `scaleb(12, 2)` is `1200`, `remainder(1.4E+3, 5E+2)` is `400`, `round_to_integral_value(1.2E+3)` is `1200`, `sqrt(1E+2)` is `10`, `power(-0, 3)` is $+0$ and `next_toward(0, 1)` is $+0$ with `Underflow`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:565
msgid "The copy operations (`copy`, `copy_abs`, `copy_negate`, `copy_sign`) take no context and are outside the rules: `copy_negate(0)` is $-0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:567
msgid "`quantize` and `rescale` keep the exponent of a zero and a positive exponent (`quantize` is the exception of page 55, `rescale` its former name): `quantize(-0, 1E+2)` is `0E+2`. `to_number` keeps a positive exponent, and reads a zero as $+0$ with exponent 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:570
msgid "Appendix A does not name `reduce`, `rescale`, `scaleb`, `shift`, `rotate`, `remainder`, `remainder_near`, the two round-to-integral operations or `next_toward`; the rules apply to them as to every operation. decNumber 3.68 gives other results for some of them ([conformance](../conformance/decimal_gda.md#subset-arithmetic-and-operations-appendix-a-does-not-name))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:575
msgid "[!NOTE] `exp`, `ln`, `log10`, `power` with a non-integer exponent other than 0.5 and `power` with an integer exponent above 999,999,999 or below $-1\\,999\\,999\\,997$ accept only a context with $p \\le 999\\,999$, $E_{\\max} \\le 999\\,999$ and $E_{\\min} \\ge -999\\,999$; with the default exponent range they return a NaN and raise `InvalidContext`, as [the implementation limit of these operations](#implementation-limit-of-exp-ln-log10-and-power) states."
msgstr ""

