msgid "rounds towards zero unless that leaves a last digit of 0 or 5, in which case it rounds away from zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:722
msgid "The [design page](../design/decimal_gda.md#rounding-to-precision) gives each algorithm as a formula."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:725
msgid "Signals, flags and traps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:729
msgid "`GdaSignal` names the eight signals of GDA 1.70 and the Lost digits signal of its Appendix A. Each has a flag in the flags of a context and a trap-enabler."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:747
msgid "The specification has eight signals (GDA 1.70, page 15 (flags and trap-enablers)): `Clamped`, `DivisionByZero`, `Inexact`, `InvalidOperation`, `Overflow`, `Rounded`, `Subnormal` and `Underflow`. `LostDigits` is the ninth, raised only in subset arithmetic (GDA 1.70, page 56 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:752
msgid "Four conditions that the specification says signal invalid-operation are not signals: conversion syntax, division impossible, division undefined and invalid context (GDA 1.70, pages 51 and 52 (Exceptional conditions)). They are conditions of `GdaFlags`, and raising any of them raises the signal `InvalidOperation`; [`GdaFlags::contains`](#gdaflagsnew-gdaflagscontains-gdaflagscombine) states how."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:761
msgid "`GdaFlags` is a set of conditions. It records both the conditions raised by one operation and the flags of a context. It holds the thirteen conditions the library raises, which are the twelve conditions of the Exceptional conditions section (GDA 1.70, pages 51 to 53) other than Insufficient storage, and Lost digits of Appendix A. Keeping the conditions apart is explained on the [design page](../design/decimal_gda.md#keep-the-detailed-invalid-conditions)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:773
msgid "The fields are private. Each of the 13 conditions is read with a method of the same name, such as `raised().inexact()` or `flags().division_by_zero()`. The only public constructor is `new`, so sets are built only with `new` and `combine`. `GdaTrapSet` reads the same way, with one method for each of the nine signals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:781
msgid "`new` is the empty set, `combine` the field-wise union, and `contains` the membership test of a *signal*, with the GDA invalid-operation grouping. The accessor methods read one *condition* each."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:791
msgid "`combine` is associative, commutative and idempotent, with `new()` as identity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:793
msgid "`contains(s)` reads the field for `s`, except that `contains(InvalidOperation)` is true when any of `invalid_operation`, `conversion_syntax`, `division_impossible`, `division_undefined` or `invalid_context` is set. Use `contains(InvalidOperation)` to ask whether the signal was raised."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:798
msgid "The accessor `invalid_operation()` reads the plain Invalid operation condition only, and the plain condition is not raised with every one of the four. A division undefined or impossible raises it; a conversion syntax error or an invalid context does not. This is as in decNumber, whose status word the decTest rows describe."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:806
msgid "`GdaTrapSet` is the set of enabled trap-enablers, one for each of the nine signals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:818
msgid "Build and query trap sets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:826
msgid "`new` enables no trap. `with_signal(s)` enables one trap, or disables it with `enabled=false`. `contains(s)` reads the trap-enabler of `s`. The trap of `InvalidOperation` fires for every condition that signals it, as [Trap selection](#trap-selection) states."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:833
msgid "`GdaOutcome[T]` is the result of every GDA operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:842
msgid "Both variants carry the GDA-defined result, the next context and the conditions raised by this operation. `Trapped` also names the trap that fired."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:845
msgid "Outside the package a caller can `match` on the enum and use the accessors, but only the GDA operations, `GdaOutcome::completed` and `GdaOutcome::absorb` construct an outcome. `GdaOutcome::resume_defined` turns a trapped outcome into its defined result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:852
msgid "Read the fields that both variants share, without a `match`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:860
msgid "`value` is the defined result, also for `Trapped`. `next_context` is the context with the updated flags. `raised` is the conditions of this operation only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:866
msgid "Build the outcomes a pipeline needs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:873
msgid "`completed(v, c)` is the outcome of an operation that raised nothing: `v` and `c` unchanged, empty `raised`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:876
msgid "`a.absorb(b)` keeps the value, the context (traps included) and the trap of `a`. The conditions that `b` raised join `raised` and the flags of the next context. In the flags, `invalid_operation` is set when one of the [four detailed invalid conditions](#gdaflagsnew-gdaflagscontains-gdaflagscombine) is among them. When `a` completed and `b` trapped, the result is `Trapped` with the signal of `b`. The traps of `a` are not tested against the conditions of `b`. [`GdaDecimalChecked`](decimal_gda_checked.md) uses `absorb` for the right operand of a binary operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:887
msgid "Turns a `Trapped` outcome into a `Completed` one; it is the only way to do so."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:893
msgid "`Completed` is returned unchanged. `Trapped(s, v, C, R)` becomes `Completed(v, C, none)`, where `C` is the next context that the outcome carries (not the input context of [Trap selection](#trap-selection)) and `none` is an empty `raised`; this is the GDA rule for continuing after a trap with the defined result. `GdaDecimalChecked::resume_defined` calls it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:898
msgid "Trap selection"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:900
msgid "A GDA function ends in the same three steps. Let $R$ be the conditions the operation raised and $C$ the input context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:903
msgid "If $R$ is empty the outcome is `Completed(v, C, none)`: the same context comes back."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:905
msgid "Otherwise the next context $C'$ is $C$ with flags $C.\\mathrm{flags} \\cup R$, where the `invalid_operation` flag is also set whenever $R$ contains one of the four detailed invalid conditions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:908
msgid "The trapped signal is the first $s$ in the order below with `raised.contains(s)` and `traps.contains(s)`: InvalidOperation, DivisionByZero, Overflow, Underflow, Subnormal, Inexact, Rounded, Clamped, LostDigits. If there is one the outcome is `Trapped(s, v, C', R)`, otherwise `Completed(v, C', R)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:914
msgid "Because `contains(InvalidOperation)` covers the detailed invalid conditions, the one `InvalidOperation` trap catches all of them, and the trapped signal is `InvalidOperation` whichever condition raised it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:938
msgid "GDA operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:940
msgid "Every function in this section takes its operands (a string for `to_number`) and a `GdaContext` and returns a `GdaOutcome` built by the [trap selection](#trap-selection) rule, except the four `try_` functions, which return it in a `Result`. A signaling-NaN operand raises `InvalidOperation` and yields the corresponding quiet NaN; a quiet NaN operand propagates without raising anything (the first NaN operand wins). In subset contexts (`extended=false`) finite operands longer than the precision are rounded first (see [`GdaContext::new`](#gdacontextnew-gdacontexttry_new)). Unless stated otherwise the result is the exact result, correctly rounded, and when it is exact it has the *ideal exponent* listed for the operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:952
msgid "`to_number` converts a string to a `Decimal` by the GDA to-number conversion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:958
msgid "The literal keeps its exponent unless it must be rounded to the precision or clamped to the exponent range. Overflow and underflow are decided from the exact exponent of the text, however large."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:962
msgid "Malformed text gives a quiet NaN with `ConversionSyntax`. In subset contexts infinities and NaNs are also a conversion-syntax error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:967
msgid "Round one operand to the context: `plus` is $0 + x$, `minus` is $0 - x$, `abs` is $|x|$, and `apply` is the plain conversion of a value to the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:977
msgid "The ideal exponent is that of the operand."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:979
msgid "A zero result takes its sign from the GDA rule for a zero sum. `plus(-0)` and `minus(0)` are $-0$ under `Floor` and $+0$ under every other rounding algorithm, while `plus(0)`, `minus(-0)` and `abs` of either zero are $+0$. In a subset context every zero result is $+0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:984
msgid "In an extended context `apply` keeps the sign of a zero; it does not round subset operands first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:988
msgid "Perform the basic arithmetic operations; `fma(a, b, c)` is $a \\times b + c$ with a single rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:999
msgid "The ideal exponent of each operation, with $q_1$ and $q_2$ the exponents of the first and second operand:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1002
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1049
msgid "Ideal exponent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1006
msgid "$q_1 - q_2$ (an inexact quotient has a full $p$-digit coefficient)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1007
msgid "the `add` rule applied to the exact product and the addend"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1009
msgid "An exact zero sum is $-0$ when both operands are negative, $+0$ when both are positive, and otherwise $+0$, or $-0$ under `Floor`. For `subtract` the operands are the first operand and the negated second; in a subset context a second operand longer than $p$ digits is first rounded with its own sign and then negated ([#761](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/761)), so `subtract(0, 12)` at precision 1 under `Floor` is `-1E+1`. So `add(0, -0)` is `0` under `HalfEven` and `-0` under `Floor`, while `add(0, 0)` is `0` under every rounding algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1018
msgid "The special cases:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1023
msgid "$\\pm\\infty$ with `DivisionByZero`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1024
msgid "NaN with `DivisionUndefined`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1025
msgid "a zero with exponent $E_{\\mathrm{tiny}}$ and `Clamped`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1027
msgid "An invalid result raises `InvalidOperation`; the conditions that count as that signal are listed under [`GdaFlags::new`](#gdaflagsnew-gdaflagscontains-gdaflagscombine). `fma` is invalid in a subset context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1032
msgid "A far smaller addend is absorbed as a sticky unit below every rounding boundary of the sum, and a result is rounded to the subnormal grid only when its exact result is below $10^{E_{\\min}}$, so every one of these operations rounds once. At precision 7, `add(1598618.5, 1E-20)` is `1598619`. In decimal32, `multiply(3.000001E-45, 1.500001E-45)` is `4.500005E-90` and `divide(1, 1.9999999999998E+101)` is `1E-101`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1041
msgid "Divide to an integer quotient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1049
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1858
msgid "Returns"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1052
msgid "$x - Q y$ (the sign of $x$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1053
msgid "$x - n y$, where $n$ is $x / y$ rounded to the nearest integer, ties to even"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1055
msgid "When $Q$ (or $n$) needs more than $p$ digits the result is NaN with `DivisionImpossible`. A zero divisor gives `DivisionByZero` (`divide_integer` of a nonzero number) or `DivisionUndefined` ($0 / 0$). A zero divisor or an infinite dividend makes both remainders invalid. A finite dividend with an infinite divisor is its own remainder."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1061
msgid "In an extended clamped context (see [`GdaContext::new`](#gdacontextnew-gdacontexttry_new)), a zero quotient keeps its exact sign and is folded to $E_{\\max}-p+1$ when needed, raising `Clamped` without raising `Rounded` or `Inexact`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1068
msgid "Round a value to a prescribed exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1075
msgid "`quantize(x, y)` returns the value of $x$ with the exponent $q_y$ of `y`, rounded with the rounding of the context (raising `Rounded`, and `Inexact` when digits are lost). `rescale(x, n)` does the same with exponent $n$, where $n$ must be an integer value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1080
msgid "In a subset context a second operand with more than $p$ digits is rounded to $p$ digits first (raising `LostDigits` when non-zero digits go), and the quantum is the rounded operand: at precision 1, `quantize(0, 12)` is `0E+1` and `rescale(0, 12)` is `0E+10`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1085
msgid "The result is invalid when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1087
msgid "the target exponent lies outside $[E_{\\mathrm{tiny}}, E_{\\max}]$ ($[E_{\\min}, E_{\\max}]$ in subset contexts),"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1089
msgid "the result coefficient would need more than $p$ digits, or"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1090
msgid "exactly one operand is infinite."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1092
msgid "Two infinities give the infinity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1094
msgid "A subnormal result raises `Subnormal` (and `Inexact` and `Rounded` when digits are lost) but never `Underflow`: in `decimal32`, `quantize(1.55E-100, 1E-101)` is `1.6E-100` with `Subnormal`, `Inexact` and `Rounded`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1100
msgid "Round to an integer with the rounding of the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1107
msgid "A value with exponent $\\ge 0$ is already an integer and is returned unchanged, even when it is longer than $p$ digits (a `clamp` context still folds its exponent down)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1111
msgid "A value with negative exponent is quantized to exponent 0 at a working precision of $\\max(p, \\text{its digit count})$, as decNumber does, so the integral part is never rounded to $p$ digits: at precision 3, `12345.6` gives `12346`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1116
msgid "`round_to_integral_exact` raises `Inexact` and `Rounded` when digits are dropped and `round_to_integral_value` never does. In a subset context the operand is first rounded to $p$ digits, with `LostDigits`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1134
msgid "Implementation limit of `exp`, `ln`, `log10` and `power`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1136
msgid "`exp`, `ln`, `log10`, `power` with a non-integer exponent other than 0.5 and `power` with an integer exponent above 999,999,999 or below $-1\\,999\\,999\\,997$ accept a context only if $p \\le 999\\,999$, $E_{\\max} \\le 999\\,999$ and $E_{\\min} \\ge -999\\,999$. Beyond a limit they raise `InvalidContext`; with the default exponent range they return NaN and raise `InvalidContext`. `power` returns a result that it decides before this check: $0^y$ for a non-integer $y$, a base `1` or `-1` (not `1.00`) with an integer $y$, and a power of ten with an integer exponent whose result overflows or underflows. A negative base with a non-integer exponent is invalid (`InvalidOperation`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1145
msgid "Limit"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1145
msgid "Applies to"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1147
msgid "`exp`, `ln`, `log10`, `power` with a non-integer exponent other than 0.5, and `power` with an integer exponent above 999,999,999 or below $-1\\,999\\,999\\,997$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1148
msgid "a nonzero operand has at most 999,999 digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1149
msgid "the adjusted exponent of a nonzero operand is at most 999,999 and at least $-1\\,999\\,997$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1149
msgid "`exp`, `ln`, `log10` (`power` gives `InvalidOperation` there, as [`power`](#power) states)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1151
msgid "This is an implementation limit. The operation text of GDA 1.70 states no such bound, and the limit is permitted by its Invalid context condition, which applies when the precision \"exceeds the capability of the underlying concrete representation\" (GDA 1.70, page 52 (Exceptional conditions)). The value 999,999 is the `DEC_MAX_MATH` of decNumber. The [design page](../design/decimal_gda.md#correctly-rounded-exp-ln-log10-and-non-integer-power) gives the reason."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1160
msgid "Return the correctly rounded square root, exponential, natural logarithm and base-10 logarithm of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1170
msgid "They round the result with the half-even algorithm, whatever the rounding of the context. In a subset context an operand longer than $p$ digits is first rounded with the rounding algorithm of the context, not with the half-even one ([#762](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/762)): `sqrt(0.999)` at precision 1 under `Down` is `0.9`. Every finite result not listed as exact is inexact with $p$ digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1176
#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1183
msgid "Operand"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1178
msgid "a perfect square"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1178
msgid "exact, with the ideal exponent $\\lfloor q/2 \\rfloor$, where $q$ is the exponent of the operand"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1181
msgid "a power of ten $10^k$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1181
msgid "the integer $k$, rounded if $k$ has more than $p$ digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1185
msgid "a negative number"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1186
msgid "$-\\infty$; invalid in a subset context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1188
msgid "all four"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1190
msgid "An invalid result raises `InvalidOperation`; the conditions that count as that signal are listed under [`GdaFlags::new`](#gdaflagsnew-gdaflagscontains-gdaflagscombine)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1193
msgid "`exp`, `ln` and `log10` are subject to the [implementation limit](#implementation-limit-of-exp-ln-log10-and-power). If the evaluation cannot decide the rounding within the [refinement limit](#gdacontextrefinement_limit-gdacontextwith_refinement_limit), the result is NaN with `InvalidOperation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1199
msgid "GDA 1.70 allows an inexact result of `exp`, `ln` and `log10` to be up to one unit in the last place from the correctly rounded one (GDA 1.70, page 30 (exp); page 31 (ln, log10)). The library returns the correctly rounded result, in a subset context as in the extended arithmetic (see the [conformance page](../conformance/decimal_gda.md#deviations-of-the-corpus))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1206
msgid "Return $x^y$ for `power(x, y)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1212
msgid "For an integer $y$, the result is computed by binary powering with $p + \\operatorname{digits}(|y|) + 2$ working digits (one fewer in subset contexts) and then rounded with the rounding of the context. An exact power that fits in $p$ digits is returned exactly, with exponent $y \\cdot q_x$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1217
msgid "For a non-integer $y$, $x$ must be positive (a negative base is invalid), and the result is certified to be correctly rounded *with the rounding of the context*. The case $y = 0.5$ is computed as a square root under the rounding of the context. The [implementation limit](#implementation-limit-of-exp-ln-log10-and-power) applies to a non-integer exponent other than 0.5. For a finite base, a non-integer exponent with a base whose adjusted exponent is above 999,999 or below $-1\\,999\\,997$, or an exponent whose adjusted exponent is below $-1\\,999\\,997$, and an integer exponent with an adjusted exponent above 999,999, give a NaN with `InvalidOperation`, not `InvalidContext`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1226
msgid "The special cases follow GDA:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1230
msgid "$1$; $0^0$ is invalid"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1231
msgid "a power of $\\pm\\infty$ or $\\pm 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1231
msgid "takes its sign from the parity of an integer exponent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1232
msgid "$1^y$, $y$ not an integer, or infinite"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1232
msgid "$1$ with `Inexact` and `Rounded`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1233
msgid "subset context, $0^0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1234
msgid "subset context, $0^{-n}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1234
msgid "invalid, instead of $\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1236
msgid "GDA treats every non-integer power as inexact, even when the real number $x^y$ is representable: `power(4, 1.5)` at precision 5 is `8.0000` with `Inexact` and `Rounded` in every rounding algorithm. Such cases, an exponent $a/b$ in lowest terms with $x$ a perfect $b$-th power, are decided before the refinement loop, because they lie on the boundary of a directed rounding cell or on a midpoint, which the loop cannot certify."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1242
msgid "GDA 1.70 allows an inexact `power` result to be up to one unit in the last place in error (GDA 1.70, page 35 (power)). The library always returns the correctly rounded result, so `Down`, `Floor` and `ZeroFiveUp` give `8.0000` where libmpdec gives `7.9999`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1248
msgid "Return the outcome of `exp`, `ln`, `log10` or `power`, or `Err` when the library could not certify the result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1258
msgid "Every case the specification defines is an `Ok` outcome exactly as the plain function returns it. These cases are domain errors, signaling NaNs, and a context beyond the supported exponent range, which `exp` and its siblings report as `invalid_context` (the [implementation limit](#implementation-limit-of-exp-ln-log10-and-power)). `Err` means that the refinement limit or a resource bound stopped the computation; the plain function then returns a NaN with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1267
msgid "Round a value to the context and remove its trailing zeros."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1273
msgid "A zero becomes $0$ with exponent 0, keeping its sign in extended contexts. In a clamped context (the parameter `clamp`, see [`GdaContext::new`](#gdacontextnew-gdacontexttry_new)) the exponent is not raised above $E_{\\max} - p + 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1279
msgid "`scaleb` scales a value by a power of ten, and `logb` returns its adjusted exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1286
msgid "`scaleb(x, n)` returns $x \\cdot 10^n$ by adding $n$ to the exponent. The operand $n$ must be an integer with exponent 0 and $|n| \\le 2(E_{\\max} + p)$; otherwise the result is invalid. The result then goes through the shared finalization:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1292
msgid "a coefficient longer than $p$ digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1292
msgid "rounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1293
msgid "an overflow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1293
msgid "$\\pm\\infty$ or the largest finite number, by the rounding algorithm"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1293
msgid "in decimal32 under `Down`, `scaleb(9E+96, 1)` is `9.999999E+96`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1294
msgid "a zero result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1294
msgid "clamped into the exponent range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1294
msgid "`scaleb(0E+300, 400)` in decimal64 is `0E+369`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1296
msgid "`logb(x)` returns $\\hat q$ as an integer. `logb(0)` is $-\\infty$ with `DivisionByZero`, and `logb(±∞)` is $+\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1301
msgid "Return the representable number adjacent to a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1309
msgid "`next_plus` returns the smallest representable number greater than $x$ in the context, and `next_minus` the largest smaller one. From a zero they step to $\\pm 10^{E_{\\mathrm{tiny}}}$, and past the largest finite number they reach $\\pm\\infty$. Neither raises flags for finite results (GDA 1.70, page 34); in a subset context a step below the normal range gives $+0$, since a subnormal result is not permitted, and still no flag ([#770](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/770))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1315
msgid "`next_toward(x, y)` steps from $x$ towards $y$, returning $x$ (with the sign of $y$ for zeros) when they compare equal. When the step leaves the normal range, it raises `Overflow`, or `Underflow` and `Subnormal`, together with `Inexact` and `Rounded` (page 35: the flags of the sum with an infinitesimal); in a subset context the step below the normal range gives $+0$ with `Underflow`, `Subnormal`, `Inexact` and `Rounded`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1323
msgid "Compute the digit-wise and, or, exclusive or and inversion of logical operands (GDA 1.70, page 41 (Miscellaneous operations)). A logical operand is a non-negative integer with exponent 0 whose digits are all 0 or 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1333
msgid "Each operand is read as exactly $p$ digits: a shorter operand is padded with leading zeros, and only the low $p$ digits of a longer one are used. `logical_invert` inverts all $p$ digits. Any other operand makes the result invalid (an [`InvalidOperation`](#gdaflagsnew-gdaflagscontains-gdaflagscombine) condition)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1341
msgid "Move the coefficient digits of $x$ by $n$ places within a window of $p$ digits (GDA 1.70, page 49 (shift); page 47 (rotate))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1349
msgid "$n$ must be an integer with exponent 0 and $|n| \\le p$, otherwise the result is invalid. A positive $n$ moves digits to the left. `shift` drops the digits that leave the window and fills with zeros; `rotate` moves them round to the other end. The exponent and sign are unchanged, and an infinite $x$ is returned unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1357
msgid "Compare two values by value or in the GDA total order (GDA 1.70, page 42 (compare-total))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1366
msgid "An operand is a NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1368
msgid "the decimal $-1$, $0$ or $1$, by value ($-0 = +0$, $2.50 = 2.5$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda.md:1368
