msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:100
msgid "An invalid string gives NaN with `conversion_syntax`; because GDA treats conversion syntax as an invalid-operation condition, a context that traps `InvalidOperation` (such as `GdaContext::basic_default()`) traps it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:108
msgid "These methods return the wrapped outcome and its components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:118
msgid "`value()` is the defined result, also when trapped. `context()` is the next context, including its updated flags and its traps. `raised()` holds the signals of the latest operation only. `flags()` is `context().flags()`, the sticky union of everything raised since the context's flags were last cleared; whenever any invalid-operation condition (`conversion_syntax`, `division_impossible`, `division_undefined`, `invalid_context`) is raised, the flags also get `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:128
msgid "These report whether a trap fired and which signal it was."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:137
msgid "`error()` is the trapped signal or `None`; `result()` is `Ok(value)` for a completed pipeline and `Err(signal)` for a trapped one (`value()` still gives the defined result of a trapped pipeline). The error type is `GdaSignal`, not `ArithmeticError`: a trap is the failure channel of this tier, and the signal says which of the nine signals it was. These are the names `DecimalChecked` uses. When several raised signals are trapped at once, the reported one is the first in the priority order `InvalidOperation`, `DivisionByZero`, `Overflow`, `Underflow`, `Subnormal`, `Inexact`, `Rounded`, `Clamped`, `LostDigits`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:146
msgid "Recovery"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:150
msgid "`resume_defined()` continues a trapped pipeline with its defined result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:156
msgid "On `Trapped(signal, value, context, raised)` it returns `Completed(value, context, GdaFlags::new())`: the value and the context (with its flags, which already contains the trapped signal) are kept, the trap marker and the latest-step flags are dropped. On a completed pipeline it does nothing. Traps stay enabled in the context, so the same condition traps again if it recurs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:193
#: manual/api/frontend/itl_expr.md:119
#: manual/conformance/interval.md:61
msgid "Operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:195
msgid "Every operation method applies the `decimal_gda` function of the same name to the current value under the stored context; `remainder` is the GDA `remainder` (truncating) and `remainder_near` the nearest one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:199
msgid "On `Completed(v, c, _)` the method returns the outcome of `@decimal_gda.op(v, …, c)`; on `Trapped` it returns the state unchanged. The other operands (`other`, `multiplier`, `addend`, `exponent`, `quantum`, `divisor`, `target`, `count`) are pipelines too, so that the conditions of the pipeline that produced them are not lost. Rules for a binary operation `a.op(b)`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:204
msgid "If `a` is trapped, the result is `a`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:205
msgid "Otherwise, if `b` is trapped, no operation runs: the result keeps the value and the context of `a`, the conditions of `b` join `raised` and the flags, and the trap of `b` is the trap of the result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:208
msgid "Otherwise the operation runs under the context of `a`, and the conditions that `b` raised join `raised` of the result and the flags of its context. The traps of `a` are not tested against the conditions of `b`: `b` decided its own."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:212
msgid "`fma` applies the same rules to its two operands. The GDA operation computes the result under `c`'s precision, rounding, exponent limits, clamping and extended mode; if it raises no signal the context is passed on unchanged with empty `raised`; otherwise the raised signals are merged into the flags of the next context and, if one of them is enabled in `c.traps()`, the outcome is `Trapped`. The mathematical functions `exp`, `ln`, `log10` and `power` with a non-integer exponent need precision and exponent limits within $\\pm 999\\,999$; otherwise they return NaN with `invalid_context`, which an `InvalidOperation` trap catches."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:222
msgid "The operations that return a number are all here: `apply`, `plus`, `minus`, `abs`, `add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, `divide`, `divide_integer`, `fma`, `remainder`, `remainder_near`, `power`, `sqrt`, `exp`, `ln`, `log10`, `quantize`, `rescale`, `reduce`, `round_to_integral_exact`, `round_to_integral_value`, `scaleb`, `shift`, `rotate`, `logb`, `logical_and`, `logical_or`, `logical_xor`, `logical_invert`, `next_minus`, `next_plus`, `next_toward`, `compare`, `compare_signal`, `max`, `min`, `max_magnitude` and `min_magnitude`. The functions of `decimal_gda` that return a `GdaOutcome[String]`, `GdaOutcome[Bool]` or `GdaOutcome[Int32]` (`class_name`, `is_normal`, `is_subnormal`, `compare_total`, `compare_total_magnitude`) and the `try_` forms are called on `value()` and `context()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:259
msgid "These round or change the sign of the current value: `apply` is the GDA conversion to the context, `plus` is $0 + x$, `minus` is $0 - x$ and `abs` is $|x|$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:272
msgid "These combine the current value with the pipelines `b` and `c`; `fma(b, c)` is $v \\times b + c$ with one rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:285
msgid "These are the GDA elementary functions of the current value; `power(y)` is $v^y$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:298
msgid "These set the exponent, take a remainder or remove trailing zeros. The other operations of the list above have the shape of these: one pipeline for a unary one and `(Self, Self) -> Self` for a binary one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:310
msgid "These step to an adjacent representable value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:1
msgid "def API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:5
msgid "`def` is the shared vocabulary of the `floating` packages. It defines the sign classification `Sign`, the ten-valued IEEE class `IeeeClass`, the four-way comparison result `PartialOrder`, the `Floating` trait implemented by the scalar representations, four generic predicates built on that trait, and re-exports the context, rounding, classification and error types of [Luna-Flow/arithmetic](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/) so that callers need only one import. It contains no arithmetic. The [tutorial](../tutorial/def.md) shows the vocabulary in use; the [design page](../design/def.md) states the trait laws and explains why `PartialOrder` is not a total order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:18
msgid "Add the package to your `moon.pkg`, next to the representation you use:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:27
msgid "The examples on this page are complete tests. They call the package as `@def.`, the representations as `@binary.`, `@decimal.`, `@decimal_gda.` and `@interval.`, and `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` as `@lf_arith.`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:31
msgid "Sign and comparison results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:35
msgid "`Sign` classifies the sign returned by [`Floating::signum`](#floatingsignum)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:48
msgid "negative value, including $-\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:49
msgid "either signed zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:50
msgid "positive value, including $+\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:53
msgid "`Sign` does not distinguish $-0$ from $+0$; use the concrete package (for example `Binary::is_negative_zero`) when the sign bit of a zero matters. `Sign` is also the payload of `SemanticScalar::Infinity` in the [`semantic`](semantic.md) package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:70
msgid "`IeeeClass` is the ten-valued `class` of IEEE 754-2019 clause 5.7.2. Unlike `FpClass`, it separates the two NaNs, the sign of every class and the normal, subnormal and zero cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:90
msgid "`Binary::class_of` and `Decimal::class_of` return it. Normal and subnormal depend on the format's $e_{\\min}$, so these methods take the context that fixes the bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:106
msgid "`PartialOrder` records the result of an IEEE 754 comparison. Exactly one of the four mutually exclusive relations *less*, *equal*, *greater* and *unordered* holds between two floating-point data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:122
msgid "at least one NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:123
msgid "equal numeric values, including $-0$ and $+0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:124
msgid "one numeric value is less than the other"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:124
msgid "`Less` or `Greater`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:126
msgid "`Binary::compare_quiet` and `Binary::compare_signaling` in [`binary`](binary.md) return this type with the comparison flags. It is a value type only; `def` does not perform comparisons itself. The [design page](../design/def.md#partialorder-is-the-ieee-four-way-relation) explains why the type has four results instead of a `Compare` instance."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:149
msgid "The `Floating` trait"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:153
msgid "`Floating` is the observation and re-precision interface shared by the scalar representations in this repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:166
msgid "`Floating` is implemented only by `@binary.Binary`, `@decimal.Decimal` and `@decimal_gda.Decimal`. `@interval.Interval` does not implement it (see [Intervals are not `Floating`](../design/def.md#intervals-are-not-floating)). Implementing it for a type outside this module is not supported or covered by the compatibility promise because methods may be added to the trait in a minor release. It is declared `pub(open)` only because implementations are in other packages of this module; the [design page](../design/def.md#why-floating-is-declared-open) gives the reason. Since the MoonBit 0.10 migration, trait methods are not promoted automatically, so generic code calls them in the qualified form `@def.Floating::classify(x)`; the concrete types also provide inherent methods with the same names (`x.classify()`). The [design page](../design/def.md#the-floating-laws) lists the laws every implementation is expected to satisfy."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:182
msgid "`classify` returns the class of a value: `Finite`, `Infinity` or `NaN`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:188
msgid "For the scalar types this is the IEEE class with signaling and quiet NaNs merged. The operation is total and never aborts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:193
msgid "`signum` returns the sign classification of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:201
msgid "`Binary`, both `Decimal`s"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:201
msgid "value $< 0$, including $-\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:201
msgid "$\\pm 0$ and every NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:201
msgid "value $> 0$, including $+\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:205
msgid "`precision` returns the working precision stored on the value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:211
msgid "It counts significant bits for `Binary` and significant decimal digits for both `Decimal` types. Every constructor requires a precision of at least $1$ and aborts otherwise, so the result is always at least $1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:217
msgid "`with_precision(x, p)` returns `x` re-expressed at precision $q = p$. `Binary` and `@decimal.Decimal` abort when $p < 1$ or $p$ exceeds `binary_precision_max` or `decimal_precision_max`, and `@decimal_gda.Decimal` uses $q = \\max(1, p)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:226
msgid "For a finite scalar, the value is rounded to $q$ significant digits of its radix, to nearest with ties to even (IEEE 754-2019 4.3.1). The trait method has no rounding parameter, because it cannot return an error; the inherent `with_precision` of each scalar type takes a rounding mode. No context exponent bounds apply and no flags are reported (use the `*_ctx` APIs of the concrete package for both). Each type keeps the range of its stored values. For `Binary` a result whose exponent leaves $[1 - e_{\\max}, e_{\\max}]$ with $e_{\\max} = 2^{30} - 1$ (`binary_value_e_max`, `binary_value_e_min`) overflows to an infinity or to the largest finite value, or underflows, as in IEEE 754. The decimal types behave alike at their stored ranges: the exponent of the least digit is at least `decimal_value_e_tiny` or `gda_value_e_tiny` ($-2147483645$) and the adjusted exponent is at most `decimal_value_e_max` or `gda_value_e_max` ($2147483646$). Zeros keep their sign. Infinities and NaNs keep their class, sign and payload and only change the stored precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:244
msgid "`normalized` returns the canonical representative of a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:250
msgid "For `Binary` this is the representation with an odd coefficient (or zero); for both `Decimal` types it removes trailing zeros of the coefficient, so `1.500` becomes `1.5` (the cohort changes, the value does not) and `-0.000` becomes `-0`. Non-finite scalars are returned unchanged. On the scalar types `normalized` keeps the value and is idempotent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:305
msgid "Generic predicates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:309
msgid "These functions test the class of any `Floating` value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:327
msgid "Exactly one of the first three is true for every value. `is_zero` is true for both signed zeros and false for NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:341
msgid "Re-exported types"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:343
msgid "`def` re-exports these types with `pub using`, so `@def.RoundingMode` and `@lf_arith.RoundingMode` name the same type and no conversion is needed. Their definitions and full semantics are documented by [Luna-Flow/arithmetic](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:350
msgid "`ArithmeticContext` is the precision, rounding direction and optional exponent bounds passed to the contextual traits of Luna-Flow/arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:357
msgid "`BinaryContext` and `DecimalContext` are built from it by their `from_arithmetic_context` constructors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:362
msgid "`ArithmeticError` is the structured error of every `Result`-returning (`*_checked`, `try_*`) API and of the checked wrapper packages; `ArithmeticErrorKind` is its kind."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:371
msgid "The kinds are `DivisionByZero`, `ParseError`, `DomainError`, `FormatError`, `UnsupportedOperation`, `UnorderedComparison` and `CertificationFailure(detail)`; `ArithmeticError` has one `is_*` predicate per kind and a `message`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:378
msgid "These types are the payload of an `ArithmeticError` of kind `CertificationFailure`: an elementary function whose correctly rounded result could not be certified within its refinement budget."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:388
msgid "The detail records the operation, the stage and reason, the target precision, and the final working precision and refinement count (see `certified_failure` in the [internal API](internal.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:394
msgid "`FpClass` is the result of `Floating::classify`: `Finite`, `Infinity` or `NaN`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:403
msgid "`RoundingMode` lists the five rounding directions that an `ArithmeticContext` carries: `ToNearestEven`, `TowardZero`, `TowardPositive`, `TowardNegative` and `AwayFromZero`. The inherent `with_precision` methods and the concrete constructors take the rounding type of their own package (`BinaryRoundingMode`, `DecimalRoundingMode`, `GdaRoundingMode`) and the trait method `Floating::with_precision` takes none; `AwayFromZero` has no `BinaryRoundingMode` or `DecimalRoundingMode` counterpart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:417
msgid "`BigInt` is the arbitrary-precision integer of `moonbitlang/core/bigint`, used for coefficients and exact values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/def.md:438
msgid "These methods are the derived `Eq` instance, promoted explicitly so that `a.equal(b)` remains available; prefer `==` and `!=`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:1
msgid "differential API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:5
msgid "`differential` is the executable of the seeded random differential campaign (`random-diff`). It reads a generated corpus of MPFR (binary, IEEE `decimal`) or libmpdec (`decimal_gda`) rows, validates them against `floating` in batches through the [mare_mark](https://lunaflow.cn/en/mare_mark/) runner and prints the append-only JSONL record of the run on standard output. The package has no public MoonBit items; this page documents its command line and its record. The [tutorial](../tutorial/differential.md) shows a campaign end to end and the [design page](../design/differential.md) explains why the harness validates batches and how a failing batch is minimized. The row formats and the oracle programs are described in [`testdata/binary/README.md`](../../../testdata/binary/README.md) and [`testdata/decimal/README.md`](../../../testdata/decimal/README.md) (IEEE `decimal`) and [`testdata/decimal_gda/README.md`](../../../testdata/decimal_gda/README.md) (`decimal_gda`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:21
msgid "`differential` is an executable package (`pkgtype(kind: \"executable\")`) and cannot be imported. It builds for native, JavaScript, Wasm and LLVM; it is excluded from Wasm-GC because the mare_mark runner uses `moonbitlang/async`, which has no Wasm-GC runtime. It is only the entry point: its `moon.pkg` imports the library [`differential/harness`](differential/harness.md), which holds the behaviour described below, and the environment access of the core library:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:45
msgid "the corpus: `mpfr-random-v1` rows, `mpfr-decimal-random-v2` rows or a `.decTest` file whose header says `libmpdec-random-v1`; the format is detected from the text"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:46
msgid "cases validated together; default 1000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:47
msgid "the mare_mark run seed, below 2^31; it seeds the balanced block order and is recorded in every failure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:48
msgid "provenance id written into the environment snapshot and the summary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:49
msgid "the backend name recorded in the environment snapshot (it does not select the backend: the binary you run does)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:50
msgid "the command stored in each `validation_failure` for `mare-mark replay`; default `python3`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:51
msgid "a leading argument of that command, repeatable; the harness appends `--target T` and the failing case id or `first..last` range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:53
msgid "Arguments before the first `--option` are ignored, because a Node.js or Wasm host puts the program or script path first. Every option takes one value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:56
msgid "Exit status: `0` when every batch agreed with its oracle; `1` when a batch failed or a case was not executable; `2` for a usage error, an unreadable file or a file that is not a random differential corpus."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:60
msgid "The record"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:62
msgid "The harness writes mare_mark JSONL (`mmka_1`) to standard output, one event per line, nothing else:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:65
#: manual/design/interval.md:702
msgid "When"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:67
#: manual/api/differential.md:69
msgid "once per batch"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:67
msgid "`status` (`valid`, `invalid` with a `reason`, `infrastructure_failure`), `operands` = `[first id, last id, case count]`, `scale` = the corpus header line (family, seed, range, oracle version), `dataset_id` = the batch number"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:68
msgid "once per invalid batch"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:68
msgid "the same fields plus `seed`, the `shrink_path` (each step of the delta debugging, as an id range), `minimal_input` (the failing case, as its row) and the `replay_command` with its arguments"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:69
msgid "the timing of the batch; one iteration per block"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:70
msgid "last"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:70
msgid "`validation_count`, `passed_count`, `failed_count`, the protocol and the environment"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:72
msgid "The protocol is fixed: `MultipleDatasetsSingleMeasurement`, no warm-up, one iteration per batch, `EveryDataset` validation, balanced blocks seeded by `--seed`. Timing is a by-product; correctness is the verdict."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:76
msgid "A case is identified by its id, `<seed>.<family>.<index>` for MPFR rows and `d<seed>.<family>.<index>` for libmpdec rows. The id is all that `tools/random_differential.py replay` needs, because both generators are pure functions of seed, family and index."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:81
msgid "Replaying a failure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:83
msgid "The `validation_failure` event carries the replay command, so the record can be replayed by the mare_mark command line:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential.md:91
msgid "The command it prints is `python3 tools/random_differential.py replay --target native <id or range>`, which regenerates the rows and runs them through the frontends. Without mare_mark, run that command directly or `just random-diff replay <id>`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/harness.md:1
msgid "differential/harness API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/harness.md:5
msgid "`differential/harness` holds the behaviour of the seeded random differential executable: it parses the command line, splits a corpus into batches, runs each batch through the matching frontend under the mare_mark runner and writes the JSONL record. The package `differential` is only the entry point that calls `run` with `@env.args()`. The command line and the workflow are documented on the [`differential` page](../differential.md); the [tutorial](../../tutorial/differential/harness.md) shows what the package is for and the [design page](../../design/differential/harness.md) explains the split."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/harness.md:23
msgid "The package needs `moonbitlang/async` and is built for native, JavaScript, Wasm and LLVM, not for wasm-gc."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/harness.md:34
msgid "`run(args)` takes the program arguments (the program name, and under Node.js the script path too, come first). It exits the process with `2` for a bad command line or corpus and with `1` when a batch disagrees with its oracle; otherwise it returns after the record is written."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:1
msgid "differential/interval_mpfr API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:5
msgid "`differential/interval_mpfr` is the test side of the untimed differential stage of `Interval` against MPFR (`tools/interval_diff.py`). It is a test-only package and exports no MoonBit items. Its one test reads a file of operand rows and the oracle file written by `tools/interval_mpfr_oracle.c`, runs every row on `Interval` (`add`, `sub`, `mul`, `div`, `sqr`, `sqrt`, `fma`, `exp`, `ln`) and compares both bounds of the result, and the operands as the library read them, with the oracle's, as canonical text; for `exp` and `ln` the result must enclose the oracle's and not be wider than 1,024 units of the last place. The [tutorial](../../tutorial/differential/interval_mpfr.md) shows how to run it and the [design page](../../design/differential/interval_mpfr.md) explains the comparison."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:9
msgid "The package is test-only and exports no items, so there is nothing to import from it. Its tests import the packages under test and the file and environment access of the runner:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:22
msgid "Environment and output"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:24
msgid "Name"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:26
msgid "the row file (`id op size class operand...`, an operand is `lo:hi`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:27
msgid "the oracle file (`id lo hi flags echo lohex hihex`), same ids in the same order"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/differential/interval_mpfr.md:29
msgid "Without the two variables the test does nothing. It prints `INTERVALDIFF_FAIL id op size class expected lo hi got lo hi` for a differing row, `INTERVALDIFF_UNDECIDED id` for a row the oracle marked undecided, and `INTERVALDIFF_SUMMARY rows=N failed=K undecided=U`; it fails when any row differs. `tools/interval_diff.py run` reads these lines."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/doc_examples.md:1
msgid "doc_examples API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/doc_examples.md:5
msgid "`doc_examples` is a test-only package. Its only source is `tooling/src/doc_examples/README.mbt.md`, whose `moonbit check` blocks are compiled and run as tests; it exports no MoonBit items. The blocks are short, executable versions of the main workflows of the public packages (binary contexts and interchange, GDA sticky status, IEEE decimal flags, intervals and decorations, checked pipelines, semantic comparison, `numeric_expr`, and the four conformance frontends). See the [tutorial](../tutorial/doc_examples.md) and the [design page](../design/doc_examples.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/doc_examples.md:16
msgid "Nothing can be imported from `doc_examples`. Its `moon.pkg` imports the packages its examples use, for tests only:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/doc_examples.md:38
#: manual/api/interval.md:277
#: manual/conventions.md:180
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/doc_examples.md:40
msgid "The README holds eleven `moonbit check` tests, one per workflow: binary context and interchange, GDA sticky status, IEEE decimal status, interval and decorated semantics, checked pipelines, the semantic projection, `numeric_expr` callbacks, and one inline document for each of the GDA, ITL, MPFR and TestFloat frontends."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:1
msgid "frontend/gda_expr API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:5
msgid "`frontend/gda_expr` reads General Decimal Arithmetic test files (the `.decTest` format of Cowlishaw's decimal specification) and executes their rows against `decimal_gda`. Parsing turns every row into a `GdaCase` with its context and a [`numeric_expr`](../numeric_expr.md) expression; execution evaluates the expression and compares the result and the status flags with the row. The package does no file or process IO; the command-line runner is [`cli/gda_expr_cli`](../cli/gda_expr_cli.md). The [tutorial](../../tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md) shows the workflow and the [design page](../../design/frontend/gda_expr.md) specifies the row mapping and the pass rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:18
