msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:1
msgid "frontend/mpfr_expr API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:5
msgid "`frontend/mpfr_expr` parses reference data produced with GNU MPFR and executes it against `binary` (the `mpfr-decimal-random-v2` rows against `decimal`). Seven formats are supported: MPFR's square-root `data_check` files, integer-power rows, an elementary-function matrix with exception flags, two corpora for decimal character conversion (`mpfr_strtofr` and `mpfr_get_str`), and the seeded random differential rows (`mpfr-random-v1`) and their decimal counterpart (`mpfr-decimal-random-v2`). The package does no IO; the runner is [`cli/mpfr_expr_cli`](../cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md). See the [tutorial](../../tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md) for the workflow and the [design page](../../design/frontend/mpfr_expr.md) for the pass rules."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:25
msgid "The examples call it through the alias `@mpfr_expr`; rows are written as text, so `binary` does not need to be imported."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:28
msgid "Common row syntax"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:30
msgid ""
"All seven parsers split the text at `\\n"
"`, trim each line, and skip empty lines and lines starting with `#`. Fields are separated by spaces, tabs or carriage returns. Line numbers start at 1. A rounding field (in every format but `mpfr-decimal-random-v2`, and absent from the `shortest` rows of `mpfr-decimal-output-v1`) is one of"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:34
msgid "Field"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:42
msgid "and a number is read by `@binary.Binary::from_hex` (`0x…p…` with a hexadecimal significand that may have a point, an optional sign, `inf`, `nan`, `snan`, in any case) at the precision named for that field, except where a format below names another precision (the operands of the power, elementary and `mpfr-random-v1` formats; the input of `mpfr-strtofr-v1` is decimal text and the operands of `mpfr-decimal-random-v2` are decimal numbers); a significand with more bits than that precision is rounded to nearest-even. Each parser returns `Ok(document)` when every line is valid and otherwise `Err` with one `ParseDiagnostic` per invalid line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:51
msgid "Square-root data"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:55
msgid "`parse_sqrt_data(source, text)` parses rows of five fields: `input_precision output_precision rounding input expected`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:62
msgid "The input is read at `input_precision` bits and the expected value at `output_precision` bits. Row ids are `sqrt:LINE`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:67
msgid "`execute_sqrt_data(document)` computes `input.sqrt_ctx(ctx)` for every row, where `ctx` is `BinaryContext::new(precision=output_precision, rounding~)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:74
msgid "A row passes when the result is identical (`Binary::identical`) to the expected `Binary`. Flags are not compared."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:79
msgid "`MpfrDocument` is a parsed square-root file; `MpfrCase` is one of its rows and has no public methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:94
msgid "`source` returns the name given to the parser and `case_count` the number of rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:102
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:812
#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:82
msgid "Integer powers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:106
msgid "`parse_pow_data(source, text)` parses rows of ten fields."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:112
msgid "The fields are `precision rounding input_coefficient_hex input_exponent2 input_negative exponent expected_coefficient_hex expected_exponent2 expected_negative inexact`, where the two numbers are $(-1)^{\\text{negative}} \\cdot \\text{coefficient} \\cdot 2^{\\text{exponent2}}$, the signs and `inexact` are `0` or `1`, and `exponent` is the integer power. The input is read at 512 bits and the expected value at `precision` bits. Row ids are `pow:LINE`. The coefficient fields are bare hexadecimal digits; the parser builds `0xCOEFFpEXP` from them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:123
msgid "`execute_pow_data(document)` computes `input.pown_ctx(exponent, ctx)` with `ctx = BinaryContext::new(precision=precision, rounding~)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:130
msgid "A row passes when the result is identical (`Binary::identical`) to the expected value, the inexact flag equals the row's flag, and no underflow, overflow, division by zero or invalid flag is raised."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:136
msgid "`MpfrPowDocument` is a parsed power file; `MpfrPowCase` is one row and has no public methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:151
#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:216
msgid "These methods return the name given to the parser and the number of rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:162
msgid "`parse_elementary_data(source, text)` parses rows of ten fields: `op precision rounding x y n expected inexact invalid divbyzero`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:171
msgid "`op` is one of `exp`, `exp2`, `exp10`, `expm1`, `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `ln`, `log2`, `log10`, `log1p`, `log2p1`, `log10p1`, `sqrt`, `rsqrt`, `rootn`, `pown`, `compound`, `pow`, `powr`, `hypot`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `sinpi`, `cospi`, `tanpi`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `asinpi`, `acospi`, `atanpi`, `atan2pi`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `tanh`, `asinh`, `acosh`, `atanh`. `x` and `y` are operands read at 512 bits, or at four bits per character of the token when that is more (`y` is `-` when unused), `n` is the integer argument of `rootn`, `pown` and `compound`, `expected` is read at `precision` bits, and the last three fields are `0` or `1`. Row ids are `op:LINE`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:181
msgid "A row of the two-operand functions `pow`, `powr`, `hypot`, `atan2` and `atan2pi` whose `y` is `-` is the diagnostic `\"invalid MPFR elementary field\"`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:186
msgid "`execute_elementary_data(document)` evaluates every row with the checked `try_*_ctx` method of `Binary` for its operation (`sqrt_ctx` and `pown_ctx` for `sqrt` and `pown`) in `BinaryContext::new(precision=precision, rounding~)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:196
msgid "A row passes when the result compares equal to the expected value (`compare == 0`, so every NaN equals every NaN) with the same sign when it is zero, the inexact, invalid and division-by-zero flags equal the row's flags, and neither underflow nor overflow is raised. If a `try_*_ctx` method returns `Err` (for example a certification failure) the row fails with the message `\"certification failure for MPFR differential case\"`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:206
msgid "`MpfrElementaryDocument` is a parsed elementary file."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:223
msgid "Decimal character conversion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:227
msgid "`parse_strtofr_data(source, text)` parses rows of seven fields: `precision rounding input expected inexact overflow underflow`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:234
msgid "`input` is a decimal literal kept as text, `expected` is the `mpfr_strtofr` result read at `precision` bits, and the last three fields are `0` or `1`. Row ids are `strtofr:LINE`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:240
msgid "`execute_strtofr_data(document)` evaluates `Binary::from_string_ctx(input, ctx)` with `ctx = BinaryContext::new(precision=precision, rounding~)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:247
msgid "A row passes when the result compares equal to the expected value, with the same sign (including zero) and class, the inexact, overflow and underflow flags equal the row's flags, and neither invalid nor division by zero is raised. A literal rejected by the parser fails the row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:254
msgid "`MpfrStrtofrDocument` is a parsed file; the methods return the name given to the parser and the number of rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:268
msgid "`parse_decimal_output_data(source, text)` parses two row kinds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:274
msgid "`fixed digits precision rounding value expected_digits expected_exponent10 inexact` (eight fields) is a value printed with `digits` significant digits: `expected_digits` is the digit string and `expected_exponent10` the decimal exponent of its first digit. `shortest precision value expected_digit_count` (four fields) is the number of significant digits of the shortest round-trip form. Values are read at `precision` bits. Row ids are `fixed:LINE` and `shortest:LINE`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:284
msgid "`execute_decimal_output_data(document)` runs `Binary::to_decimal_string_ctx` for a `fixed` row and `Binary::to_shortest_string` for a `shortest` row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:291
msgid "A `fixed` row passes when the printed digits, the exponent and the sign match and the inexact flag equals the row's flag (no overflow or underflow). A `shortest` row passes when the text has exactly `expected_digit_count` significant digits (leading zeros and the trailing zeros of positional notation are not counted) and `Binary::from_string(text, precision~)` returns the same value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:309
msgid "Seeded random rows"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:311
msgid "`mpfr-random-v1` rows come from `tools/generate_mpfr_random_oracle.c`: one row per case, with precisions up to 10,000, five rounding directions, an optional exponent range with its tininess rule, up to three exact operands and the MPFR result with its flags. The full field list is in [`testdata/binary/README.md`](../../../../testdata/binary/README.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:327
msgid "Parses the text (the first argument names the source). Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped. Every other line must have thirteen fields, a known operation (`add sub mul div sqrt fma strtofr getstr shortest` or one of the elementary names), a rounding of `n na z u d`, a positive precision, an exponent range that is either `- - -` or `emin emax` with `emin = 1 - emax` and an `emax` that `BinaryContext::try_new` accepts for the precision, then `a` (tininess after rounding) or `b` (before rounding), exact operands (`[-]0x<hex>p<exp>`, `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`, `-`) and `flags` equal to `-` or a subset of `xouvz`. Operands and results are read at least at the row's precision, because reading a value below the smallest subnormal of a lower precision would flush it to zero. Each bad row is one diagnostic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:345
msgid "Runs every row with the row's `BinaryContext` (bounded when the row has an exponent range, the context of the precision alone otherwise, with `e_max` 1073741823 and gradual underflow) and passes a row only when the value and the flags agree. Values are compared with their sign for zeros and infinities; a NaN result must be a quiet NaN. A domain error of the elementary functions (`ArithmeticError::is_domain_error`) counts as the invalid operation: a quiet NaN with flag `v`; any other error fails the row with the error message. The row id is the first field of the row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:353
msgid "Seeded random decimal rows"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:355
msgid "`mpfr-decimal-random-v2` rows come from `tools/generate_mpfr_decimal_random.py`: one row per case for the IEEE decimal elementary functions (`exp exp2 exp10 expm1 exp2m1 exp10m1 ln log2 log10 log1p log2p1 log10p1 rsqrt sin cos tan sinpi cospi tanpi asin acos atan asinpi acospi atanpi sinh cosh tanh asinh acosh atanh pow powr hypot atan2 atan2pi rootn pown compound`), with a decimal context (precision and exponent range of an IEEE format, one of the five IEEE rounding directions: `half_even`, `half_up`, `down`, `ceiling`, `floor`), exact decimal operands and the expected value with its flags. The expected value is the rounding of a rigorous 768-bit MPFR enclosure, never one MPFR result, and a row exists only when both enclosure endpoints round alike and the rounded value lies outside the enclosure (the true value is not representable), so exact results are not sampled by this corpus."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:376
msgid "Parses the text (the first argument names the source). Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped. Every other line must have ten fields: an identifier, a known operation, a rounding of `half_even half_up down ceiling floor` (the five rounding-direction attributes of IEEE 754-2019 4.3), a positive precision, an integer `emax` that forms a valid `DecimalContext` (the IEEE format of the precision and `emax`: `emin = 1 - emax`, tininess before rounding), one operand, a second operand or `-` (required by `pow`, `powr`, `hypot`, `atan2` and `atan2pi`), an integer (`rootn`, `pown` and `compound`; `0` otherwise), the expected value and `flags` equal to `-` or a subset of `xouvz` (inexact, overflow, underflow, invalid operation, division by zero) in that order. Every digit written in an operand or the expected value is kept. Each bad row is one diagnostic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:395
msgid "Runs every row through the `try_*_ctx` form of the operation with the row's context, so a certification failure fails the row instead of becoming a quiet NaN. A row passes only when the result has the expected value *and* cohort (`total_order_compare` equal, so `1.0` and `1.00` differ) and the flags agree. The row id is the first field of the row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:403
msgid "`MpfrDecimalRandomDocument` is a parsed `mpfr-decimal-random-v2` corpus (IEEE `decimal` elementary functions against MPFR); `source` is the label it was parsed from, `case_count` the number of rows and `case_ids` their ids in order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:418
msgid "`MpfrRandomDocument` is a parsed `mpfr-random-v1` corpus (binary operations against MPFR) with the same three accessors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:427
msgid "`case_ids` lists the row ids in order, which the differential harness uses to name the failing case of a batch."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:430
msgid "Diagnostics and results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:434
#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:225
msgid "`ParseDiagnostic` is one invalid line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:445
#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:236
msgid "These methods return the name given to the parser, the 1-based line number and the message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:454
msgid "Messages name the problem, for example `\"MPFR pow row must contain ten fields\"`, `\"invalid MPFR pow field\"` or `\"invalid MPFR elementary expected value\"`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:460
msgid "`CaseResult` is the outcome of one row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:470
msgid "These methods return the row id, whether the row passed, and a message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:478
msgid "The message is empty for a pass and otherwise shows the expected and the actual value (hexadecimal for the binary formats, except digit text and exponent for `mpfr-decimal-output-v1` and for the `getstr` and `shortest` rows of `mpfr-random-v1`; decimal text for the decimal formats) and the compared flags where the format compares flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:485
msgid "`RunSummary` aggregates the rows of one document."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:495
msgid "These methods return the number of rows, and how many passed and failed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:505
msgid "`results` returns a copy of the per-row results; `success` is the verdict."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:512
msgid "Every row is executed, so $\\text{total} = \\text{passed} + \\text{failed}$; `success` is true when no row failed. `results` returns the results in row order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:535
msgid "These methods compare location and message and render a diagnostic for `Debug`. Use `==`, `!=` and `debug_inspect` in new code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:1
msgid "frontend/testfloat_expr API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:5
msgid "`frontend/testfloat_expr` parses Berkeley TestFloat test vectors and executes them against `binary` in the binary16, binary32, binary64 and binary128 interchange formats. A `TestFloatSpec` describes the function, rounding mode, tininess rule and exactness that produced a vector file; `parse_testfloat` reads the vectors and `execute_document` runs them. The package does no IO; the runner is [`cli/testfloat_expr_cli`](../cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md). The [tutorial](../../tutorial/frontend/testfloat_expr.md) shows the workflow and the [design page](../../design/frontend/testfloat_expr.md) specifies the pass rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:17
msgid "Add the package to the `import` block of your `moon.pkg`; import `binary` as well when you compare the format or rounding mode of a specification:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:27
msgid "The examples use the aliases `@testfloat_expr` and `@binary`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:33
msgid "`TestFloatOperation` lists the TestFloat functions the executor supports."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:60
msgid "TestFloat name"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:60
msgid "`binary` method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:66
msgid "`to_integral_exact_ctx` if exact, else `to_integral_value_ctx`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:67
msgid "`to_int_ctx`, `to_int64_ctx`, `to_uint_ctx`, `to_uint64_ctx` with `exact~`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:68
msgid "`convert_format` (operand in the format of the function name, result in `format`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:74
msgid "`TestFloatSpec` is the configuration of one TestFloat run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:85
msgid "`TestFloatSpec::parse(function_name, rounding, tininess?, exact?)` builds a specification from TestFloat names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:92
msgid "`function_name` is `FORMAT_OPERATION`: the part before the first `_` is the format (`f16`, `f32`, `f64`, `f128`) and the rest is the operation (`add`, `mulAdd`, `to_ui64`, `to_f32`, `le_quiet`, …). Names are matched case-insensitively with `-`, `_` and spaces removed. TestFloat functions outside the supported operations, such as `i32_to_f64` or `f64_to_i32_r_minMag`, are rejected; `f64_to_f32` is the conversion of a binary64 operand to binary32 (the first column is read in the format of the name's prefix, the result column in the destination format). `rounding` is one of `rnear_even`, `rnear_maxMag`, `rminMag`, `rmin`, `rmax` (also accepted without the leading `r`, or as the IEEE names `roundTiesToEven`, `roundTiesToAway`, `roundTowardZero`, `roundTowardNegative`, `roundTowardPositive`). `tininess` is `\"after\"` (default) or `\"before\"` (also `tininessAfter`, `tininessBefore`). `exact` (default `false`) selects TestFloat's `-exact` variants of `roundToInt` and the integer conversions. Errors are `\"unsupported TestFloat function name …\"`, `\"… format …\"`, `\"… operation …\"`, `\"… rounding mode …\"` and `\"… tininess mode …\"`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:129
msgid "These accessors return the parsed configuration."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:140
msgid "`function_name` is the name as given. `exact` is stored for every operation but only changes the result of `roundToInt` and the four integer conversions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:148
msgid "`parse_testfloat(source, text, spec)` parses the vectors of one function."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:154
msgid "Lines are trimmed; empty lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped. A vector has $k + 2$ white-space separated fields, where $k$ is the operand count of the operation: $k$ operand encodings, the expected result and a two-digit hexadecimal flag mask. Operand encodings are hexadecimal bit patterns of `spec.format()`. The expected result is an encoding for arithmetic operations and the format conversions (in the destination format), a hexadecimal integer of at most 16 digits for the integer conversions, and `0` or `1` for comparisons. A wrong field count gives `\"unexpected TestFloat field count\"`, an unreadable field `\"invalid TestFloat hexadecimal field\"`. The result is `Ok` only when no line is invalid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:168
msgid "`TestFloatDocument` is a parsed vector file."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:178
msgid "These methods return the name given to the parser, the specification, a copy of the vectors in file order, and their number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:190
msgid "`TestFloatCase` is one vector."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:200
msgid "These methods return the id `FUNCTION:LINE`, for example `f64_mulAdd:17`, and the 1-based line number of the vector in the parsed text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:249
msgid "`execute_document(document, options?)` executes the selected vectors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:255
msgid "Vector $k$ (counting from 0 in file order) is executed when $k \\bmod n = i$ for shard count $n$ and index $i$. Operands are decoded to `Binary` and the operation runs in `spec.format().context(rounding~, tininess~)`. The vector passes when:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:260
msgid "**arithmetic:** the result, packed into the bit fields of the result format without rounding, has exactly the expected bit pattern, or the expected result is any NaN and the actual result is a quiet NaN; and the SoftFloat mask of the operation's flags equals the expected mask. A NaN result against a non-NaN expected column, and a result that is not a number of the format, fail with `\"result not in the format: …\"`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:266
msgid "**integer conversion:** if the expected mask has the invalid bit `10`, the conversion must report invalid (`None`) and its value field is ignored; otherwise the conversion must return the expected integer (signed results compared as two's-complement bit patterns); in both cases the flag masks must be equal;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:271
msgid "**comparison:** the boolean and the flag mask are equal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:273
msgid "Every selected vector is executable; there are no skipped vectors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:277
msgid "`RunOptions` selects a shard."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:287
msgid "`RunOptions::new(shard_count?, shard_index?)` builds the options."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:293
msgid "Defaults: one shard, index 0. `RunOptions::new` aborts unless `shard_count > 0` and `0 <= shard_index < shard_count`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:298
msgid "These accessors return the shard count and index."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:309
msgid "`CaseResult` is the outcome of one vector."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:319
msgid "These methods return the vector id, whether it passed, and a message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:327
msgid "Messages: empty for a pass; `\"value mismatch: expected HEX, actual HEX\"` (`expected a NaN` instead of the first HEX when the column is a NaN), `\"integer mismatch: …\"`, `\"comparison mismatch: …\"`, `\"result not in the format: …\"`, or `\"flags mismatch: expected M, actual M\"` (masks in decimal)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:335
msgid "`RunSummary` aggregates a run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:345
msgid "These methods return the number of vectors in the document, the number this shard executed, and how many of those passed and failed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:357
msgid "`results` returns a copy of the results in vector order; `success` is the verdict."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:365
msgid "`total_cases` is the number of vectors in the document, `selected_cases = passed_cases + failed_cases` the number executed by this shard, and `success` is true when none failed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:371
msgid "`arms` returns the names of the operations that ran in this process, in the order they first ran; the set is process-global, so a summary also lists the operations of earlier runs in the same process. Each operation of the TestFloat frontend is a function that records its own name when it executes; the `--json` summary of the command-line runner prints the set as `arms`. The verification tools key a task by the code those operations reach."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:388
msgid "`equal` compares constructors; `to_repr` renders the constructor name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:398
msgid "`equal` compares all six fields, including `function_name` as written, so `f64_mul` and `F64_MUL` give unequal specifications with the same behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:409
msgid "`equal` compares location and message; `to_repr` renders both."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal.md:1
msgid "internal API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal.md:5
msgid "`internal` holds exact integer and dyadic helpers for the numeric cores: powers of 5 and 10, digit counting, removal of trailing factors, integer division with an explicit rounding mode, a decimal string splitter, dyadic enclosures and the refinement budget of the certified elementary functions. It is an internal package: it cannot be imported from outside `Luna-Flow/floating`, and its interface may change without notice. The [design page](../design/internal.md) proves the rounding and enclosure invariants; the [tutorial](../tutorial/internal.md) shows how the helpers combine."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal.md:15
