#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:1
msgid "internal/conformance API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:5
msgid "`internal/conformance` is the shared result model of the conformance frontends: source locations, per-case results with a disposition, run summaries with fixed counting rules, and deterministic shard selection. `frontend/gda_expr`, `frontend/itl_expr`, `frontend/mpfr_expr`, `frontend/testfloat_expr` and `internal/runner_cli` build on it and wrap its types in their own public types. It is an internal package: code outside the `Luna-Flow/floating-tools` module cannot import it, and its interface may change without notice. See the [tutorial](../../tutorial/internal/conformance.md) and the [design page](../../design/internal/conformance.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:25
msgid "The examples call the package as `@conformance.`. The manual's example checker has no alias for internal subpackages, so they are marked `nocheck`; each one is a copy of behaviour checked by the package's own tests or verified by hand against the source."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:30
#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:28
msgid "Source locations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:34
msgid "`SourceLocation` is a position in a named source."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:45
msgid "`SourceLocation::new(source, line, column?)` creates a location."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:51
msgid "`column` defaults to `1`. Line and column are clamped to at least `1`, so a location always points at a real character position."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:56
msgid "These accessors return the components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:73
msgid "Case results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:77
msgid "`CaseDisposition` classifies a selected case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:89
msgid "`Executable`: the case was run; its `passed` flag is meaningful."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:90
msgid "`Diagnostic(reason)`: the case is not a valid test of this kind (for example a placeholder row)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:92
msgid "`Legacy(reason)`: the case belongs to an older version of the corpus format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:93
msgid "`Unsupported(reason)`: the case is valid but the implementation does not provide the feature."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:98
msgid "`CaseResult` is the outcome of one case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:109
msgid "`CaseResult::new(id, disposition, passed, message?)` builds a result; `CaseResult::executable(id, passed, message?)` is `new` with `Executable`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:117
msgid "`message` defaults to `\"\"`. No consistency check is made between `disposition` and `passed`; summaries only read `passed` for executable results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:123
msgid "These accessors return the stored fields."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:132
msgid "Sharding"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:136
msgid "`ShardSpec` is a validated pair (shard count, shard index)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:147
msgid "`ShardSpec::try_new(count, index)` validates and builds a shard; `ShardSpec::new` aborts with the same message instead of returning `Err`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:155
msgid "Errors: `\"shard count must be positive\"` when `count <= 0`, and `\"shard index must be within shard count\"` unless `0 <= index < count`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:160
msgid "These accessors return the count $n$ and the index $i$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:169
msgid "`shard.selects(k)` is true when the case with ordinal `k` belongs to this shard, that is when $k \\bmod n = i$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:176
msgid "Ordinals are expected to be non-negative. MoonBit's `%` keeps the sign of the dividend, so a negative ordinal $k$ is selected by shard 0 when $n$ divides $k$ and by no shard otherwise (`ShardSpec::new(3, 0).selects(-3)` is `true`, `selects(-1)` is false for every index)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:191
msgid "Run summaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:195
msgid "`RunSummary` counts the results of one run or shard."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:206
msgid "`RunSummary::from_results(total, results)` counts `results` and records `total` as the number of cases before shard selection."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:213
msgid "Each result is counted once: an `Executable` result as passed or failed according to `passed()`, any other result as skipped and in its own disposition counter. The results are copied."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:219
msgid "`RunSummary::merge(parts)` combines shard summaries."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:225
msgid "All counters are summed except `total_cases`, which is the maximum over the parts; results are concatenated in the order of `parts`. An empty array gives an empty summary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:231
msgid "These accessors return the run-level counters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:241
msgid "`total_cases` is the caller's total (the maximum over merged parts); `selected_cases` is the number of results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:246
msgid "These accessors return the skip counters, in total and per disposition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:257
msgid "`results()` returns a copy of the results in counting order; `success()` is the verdict, and `success_with_counts` is the verdict that also states how many skipped, diagnostic, legacy and unsupported cases there must be."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:267
msgid "The identities"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:277
msgid "hold for every summary built by `from_results` or `merge`. `success()` is `failed_cases() == 0`; skipped cases never make it false, and a summary with no executable case (including `merge([])`) is a success. Frontends that need a stricter verdict (`itl_expr` also fails on diagnostics) add their own rule. `success_with_counts` is `success()` and exactly the four stated counts: a corpus or phase with a known number of skipped cases uses it, so that a row that silently turns into a skipped or unsupported case fails the run instead of passing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:303
msgid "All five types derive `Eq` and `Debug`; the promoted methods below compare all fields and render values. Use `==`, `!=` and `debug_inspect` in new code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:322
msgid "Two dispositions with the same constructor and different reasons are different."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/conformance.md:349
msgid "Summary equality includes the order of the result list, so a merged summary equals the serial summary only in its counters, not as a value (see the [design page](../../design/internal/conformance.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/decimal_kernel.md:1
msgid "internal/decimal_kernel API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/decimal_kernel.md:5
msgid "`internal/decimal_kernel` holds the integer arithmetic on decimal coefficients that `decimal` (IEEE 754) and `decimal_gda` (General Decimal Arithmetic) both need: a persistent coefficient type, addition, subtraction, comparison, shifts by powers of ten, digit counts, trailing-zero trimming, division by a limb, and the multiplication and division algorithms with their per-target thresholds. Every function is an exact function of its arguments. The package raises no condition and has no notion of a rounding mode, a context or a flag; those belong to the standard-specific finalizers of the two decimal packages. It is an internal package: code outside `Luna-Flow/floating` cannot import it, and neither decimal package exposes its types. See the [tutorial](../../tutorial/internal/decimal_kernel.md) and the [design page](../../design/internal/decimal_kernel.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/decimal_kernel.md:47
msgid "A non-negative integer, either `Small(UInt64)` for values below $10^{18}$ or `Limbs(units, digits)`: little-endian base-$10^9$ limbs without a leading zero limb, with the digit count cached. The value is persistent; no function mutates the limbs of an operand. The constructors are public because the two decimal packages pattern-match on them in a few fast paths; prefer the functions below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/decimal_kernel.md:55
msgid "The eight rounding directions (`HalfEven`, `HalfUp`, `HalfDown`, `Down`, `Ceiling`, `Floor`, `Up`, `ZeroFiveUp`) as a plain enum. It is a direction, not a status: each decimal package converts its own rounding-mode type into it with a total match, and the functions in the rounding family answer only whether a kept magnitude grows by one unit in the last place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/internal/decimal_kernel.md:63
msgid "A sign and a `DecCoeff` magnitude, for algorithms whose intermediate values can be negative (Toom-3, the Newton reciprocal). Zero is never negative. The type is `pub`: its fields can be read, but a value is built only by this package."
msgstr ""

