msgid "`json_stringify(value)` prints a value as compact JSON with escaped strings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1
msgid "interval API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:5
msgid "`interval` is interval arithmetic over `Binary` endpoints. An `Interval` is a closed real interval $[\\underline{x}, \\overline{x}]$, possibly unbounded, or the empty set; every operation returns an interval that contains all exact results of the operation on points of its operands (the *inclusion property*). `DecoratedInterval` adds the decorations of IEEE 1788-2015, `IntervalContext` rounds results into a target binary format and reports `IntervalFlags`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:12
msgid "Despite the name, the stored representation is the endpoint pair, not a midpoint and a radius: `Interval::from_midpoint_radius(center, radius)` builds an interval from the midpoint–radius view, and `midpoint()` and `radius()` read it back as IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8 defines them. The [tutorial](../tutorial/interval.md) shows typical use; the [design page](../design/interval.md) derives the formulas and proves the inclusion property. The fallible pipeline wrapper is [`interval_checked`](interval_checked.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:21
msgid "Conventions used on this page:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:23
msgid "$\\operatorname{RD}_p$ and $\\operatorname{RU}_p$ round toward $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$ to $p$ significant bits. \"Rounded outward\" means the lower endpoint is rounded with $\\operatorname{RD}_p$ and the upper with $\\operatorname{RU}_p$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:27
msgid "The *precision* of an interval is a tag in bits. Operations round their result outward to the larger precision of their operands; elementary functions use the precision of their argument."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:30
msgid "The exponent range of `Binary` endpoints is the implementation range of `binary`: leading-bit exponents up to $e_{\\max} = 2^{30} - 1$, and nonzero magnitudes down to $2^{e_{\\min} - p + 1}$ with $e_{\\min} = 1 - e_{\\max} = -(2^{30} - 2)$. Use an `IntervalContext` to impose a narrower range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:34
msgid "The *precision* of an interval is between 1 and `binary_precision_max` (268,435,456) bits, the limits of a `BinaryContext`. A constructor that returns a value aborts on a precision outside them and names the limits; the `try_` constructors and the checked tier return an error. A precision is never replaced silently (before 0.12 a precision below 1 became 1)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:39
msgid "*Empty* is the empty set, *Entire* is $(-\\infty, +\\infty)$. An unbounded interval stores $-\\infty$ and/or $+\\infty$ as endpoints; these mean that the set is unbounded on that side, never that it contains an infinite value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:42
msgid "The few inputs for which the code currently loses part of the input set, widens without need, or rounds or flags a context result incorrectly are noted where they occur, with their tracking issue, and collected under [Known limitations](../design/interval.md#known-limitations) on the design page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:50
msgid "Add the package and the endpoint package to your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:60
msgid "The examples refer to the packages as `@interval.`, `@binary.` and `@lf_arith.` (for `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`, which defines `ArithmeticError`, `RoundingMode` and the enclosure traits). They use two helpers: `iv(lo, hi)` builds the 53-bit interval with integer endpoints, and `fmt(x)` prints the endpoints as six-digit decimals, the lower one rounded down and the upper one rounded up, so the text still encloses the stored set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:97
msgid "Types"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:101
msgid "`Interval` is a closed interval with `Binary` endpoints and a precision tag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:110
msgid "A non-empty value satisfies $\\underline{x} \\le \\overline{x}$, has no NaN endpoint, never has $\\underline{x} = +\\infty$ or $\\overline{x} = -\\infty$, and has precision $\\ge 1$. The empty set is a separate state. The fields are private; construct values with the functions in [Construction](#construction). `==` is the `equal` of IEEE 1788-2015 Table 10.3: it compares the sets, not the representation; see [`Interval::equal`](#intervalsubset-intervalinterior-intervalequal-intervaldisjoint) and [`Interval::identical`](#intervalidentical)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:121
msgid "`DecoratedInterval` is an `Interval` paired with an IEEE 1788 [`Decoration`](#decoration), or the special value NaI (not an interval)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:130
msgid "The decoration is kept canonical: an empty interval always carries `Trv`, an unbounded interval never carries `Com`, and only `DecoratedInterval::nai` carries `Ill`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:136
msgid "`Decoration` is the IEEE 1788 decoration of a decorated interval, ordered `Ill < Trv < Def < Dac < Com`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:150
msgid "For a decorated result $(\\boldsymbol{y}, d)$ of a function $f$ evaluated on a box $\\boldsymbol{x}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:155
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is non-empty and bounded, $f$ is defined and continuous on $\\boldsymbol{x}$, and $\\boldsymbol{y}$ is bounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:156
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is non-empty, $f$ is defined on $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and its restriction to $\\boldsymbol{x}$ is continuous"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:157
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is non-empty and $f$ is defined on $\\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:158
msgid "nothing is known"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:159
msgid "the value is NaI"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:161
msgid "Each property implies the ones below it, which is why the decorations are totally ordered; `Decoration` derives `Compare` in that order, so `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` are the comparisons of decorations of IEEE 1788-2015 11.5.3. `Show` prints the lower-case IEEE 1788 names `com`, `dac`, `def`, `trv`, `ill`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:168
msgid "`OverlapState` is the result of `overlap`: the IEEE 1788 classification of the relative position of two intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:193
msgid "The constructors are the 16 states of IEEE 1788-2015 Table 10.7 (10.6.4), with the standard's names in upper camel case: `BothEmpty`, `FirstEmpty`, `SecondEmpty`, `Before`, `Meets`, `Overlaps`, `Starts`, `ContainedBy`, `Finishes`, `Equals`, `After`, `MetBy`, `OverlappedBy`, `StartedBy`, `Contains` and `FinishedBy`. There is no 17th state: the decorated `overlap` has no value for NaI (11.7.2) and returns `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:202
msgid "`IntervalContext` describes a target binary format of IEEE 754-2019 3.3: a precision in bits and the exponent range $[1 - e_{\\max}, e_{\\max}]$ (`e_min` is derived, not a parameter)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:212
msgid "$e_{\\min}$ and $e_{\\max}$ follow IEEE 754: a finite nonzero value $v$ with $2^{e} \\le |v| < 2^{e+1}$ is in range when $e \\le e_{\\max}$, and it is normal when $e \\ge e_{\\min}$. See [Contexts and flags](#contexts-and-flags)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:218
msgid "`IntervalFlags` records the conditions raised while rounding an interval into an `IntervalContext`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:228
msgid "`inexact` is set when an endpoint (or, for `midpoint_ctx`, the midpoint) changed; `overflow` when it was beyond $e_{\\max}$; `underflow` when it is tiny and inexact, as IEEE 754 defines it (see [`Interval::apply_ctx`](#intervalapply_ctx)). The fields are private; each flag is read with the accessor method of the same name, listed under [`IntervalFlags::new`](#intervalflagsnew-intervalflagscombine-intervalflagsinexact-intervalflagsoverflow-intervalflagsunderflow)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:238
msgid "`Interval::from_midpoint_radius` builds the hull in the format of $p$ bits of the interval $[c - r, c + r]$ from a center and a radius (IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.7). `Interval::try_from_midpoint_radius` returns the same interval, or an `ArithmeticError` where `from_midpoint_radius` aborts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:247
msgid "The default precision is the larger of the precisions of `center` and `radius`. With $p$ that precision, the stored interval is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:254
msgid "the exact $c - r$ rounded down and the exact $c + r$ rounded up, once each, in the exponent range of the library: both ends are numbers of the format and the interval is the tightest one of the format that contains $[c - r, c + r]$ (see the [design page](../design/interval.md#from-midpointradius-to-endpoints)). `from_midpoint_radius` aborts, and `try_from_midpoint_radius` returns a domain error, when `center` or `radius` is not finite or when `radius` is negative, and when `precision` is outside `1..=binary_precision_max`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:261
msgid "When $c$ already has at most $p$ bits and $r = 0$, the result is the singleton $\\{c\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:263
msgid "`Interval::from_string` and `DecoratedInterval::from_string`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:265
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 `textToInterval` (10.5.8, 12.12.7, 13.2): the interval literal `s`, as the hull of its value at `precision` bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:274
msgid "The portable literals of 12.11 are accepted, with the case ignored and no whitespace except the spaces and tabs the grammar allows inside the brackets:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:279
msgid "number literals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:280
msgid "inf-sup"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:280
msgid "`[1.5, 2.5]`, `[-Inf, 2/3]`, `[0x1.3p-1,]` (the omitted bound is infinite), `[,]` (Entire)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:281
msgid "point"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:282
msgid "uncertain `m?rve`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:282
msgid "`3.56?1` ($[3.55, 3.57]$), `3.56?1e2` ($[355, 357]$), `3.560?2u`, `-10?` (half an ulp), `-10??u` ($[-10, +\\infty]$), `-10??` (Entire)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:283
msgid "special"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:284
msgid "decorated"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:286
msgid "The bounds are parsed exactly (the decimal, hexadecimal and rational value, the ulp of the last place of an uncertain form) and rounded outward: the lower bound down and the upper bound up. The comparison $l \\le u$ is decided from the exact values, so a literal is never rejected or emptied by rounding; a decimal exponent or a binary exponent beyond the range is decided from the exponent and the digits without forming the value (a lower bound above the range is the largest finite float and an upper bound is $+\\infty$; below the smallest stored value they are 0 and the smallest positive stored value). Only two finite bounds of different radices that agree to 64 bits and have an exponent beyond 65,536 cannot be ordered; then (12.12.7 case (b), `PossiblyUndefinedOperation`) the result contains both bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:295
msgid "`Err` is the failed call of the standard (the result Empty for a bare literal, NaI for a decorated one, and the exception `UndefinedOperation`). It is an `ArithmeticError` of the parse kind whose message names the rule that was broken (for example \"the lower bound is greater than the upper bound (l <= u is required)\", \"rational literal: the denominator must be a positive natural number\", \"the decoration com needs a bounded interval (11.4)\"). A decoration or `[nai]` in a bare literal is such a failure for `Interval::from_string`. `DecoratedInterval::from_string` accepts a bare literal (decorated by `newDec`, so `com` for a bounded interval and `dac` otherwise), `bare_dec`, and `[nai]`, which is `Ok(NaI)`; a decoration not permitted for the value (11.4: only `trv` for Empty, `com` only for a bounded interval) is an `Err`, and `com` becomes `dac` if the rounding of a bounded literal overflowed. A string longer than `interval_literal_max_length` (65,536 characters) is not parsed: 12.11.5 allows an implementation to \"restrict support of some input strings (too long strings or strings with a rational number literal)\" and then requires that the constructor \"shall always return a Level 2 interval (possibly Entire in this case) that contains the Level 1 interval\", so the result is Entire (decorated: `dac`, or the `trv` or `def` the string ends with)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:324
msgid "`Interval::from_bounds` and `Interval::try_from_bounds`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:326
msgid "`Interval::from_bounds` builds the interval $[\\underline{x}, \\overline{x}]$ from its endpoints, rounded outward to the requested precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:334
msgid "The default precision is the larger of the endpoint precisions. Infinite endpoints build unbounded intervals: `from_bounds(-inf, +inf)` is Entire. The inputs are invalid when an endpoint is NaN, the lower endpoint is $+\\infty$, the upper endpoint is $-\\infty$, or $\\underline{x} > \\overline{x}$; `from_bounds` aborts and `try_from_bounds` returns a domain error. There is no way to build Empty from bounds; use `Interval::empty`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:351
msgid "`Interval::exact` and `Interval::try_exact`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:353
msgid "`Interval::exact` builds the singleton $\\{x\\}$ of a finite `Binary`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:360
msgid "The default precision is that of `x`. When `x` has more significant bits than the requested precision, the singleton is rounded outward to the two-point interval $[\\operatorname{RD}_p(x), \\operatorname{RU}_p(x)]$. A non-finite `x` aborts `exact` and makes `try_exact` return a domain error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:365
msgid "`Interval::from_int` and `Interval::from_coefficient`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:367
msgid "`Interval::from_int` and `Interval::from_coefficient` build an enclosure of an integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:375
msgid "The precision of `from_int` is required and counts bits (it was 53 by default). `from_coefficient` takes a non-negative `BinCoeff` magnitude and a separate sign. The integer is converted to a `Binary` exactly and then passed to `exact`, so the result is the singleton $\\{n\\}$ when $n$ fits in $p$ bits and the two-point interval $[\\operatorname{RD}_p(n), \\operatorname{RU}_p(n)]$ otherwise. Either way it contains $n$. Pass a precision at least as large as the bit length of the integer to get a singleton."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:383
msgid "`Interval::from_double`, `Interval::try_from_double`, `Interval::from_float` and `Interval::try_from_float`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:385
msgid "These functions build the singleton of the exact binary value of a `Double` or `Float`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:395
msgid "Default precisions are 53 and 24. The conversion to `Binary` is exact; a smaller requested precision rounds the singleton outward. NaN and infinities abort the plain forms and are domain errors for the `try_` forms. The result encloses the binary value, not the decimal literal it was written as: `from_double(0.1)` does not contain $1/10$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:401
msgid "`Interval::entire` and `Interval::empty`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:403
msgid "`Interval::entire` returns Entire and `Interval::empty` returns Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:410
msgid "The default precision is 53."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:412
msgid "Observers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:414
msgid "`Interval::lower_bound` and `Interval::upper_bound`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:416
msgid "`lower_bound` and `upper_bound` are `inf` and `sup` of IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8: they return the endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:424
msgid "For an unbounded side the endpoint is an infinity. For Empty they return $+\\infty$ and $-\\infty$ respectively, so test `is_empty` first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:427
msgid "The sign of a zero follows 12.12.8: in a format with a signed zero a Level 1 value of 0 is returned as $-0$ by `lower_bound` and as $+0$ by every other function of the subclause (`upper_bound`, `midpoint`, `radius`, `width`, `magnitude`, `mignitude` and their contextual forms). So the lower bound of `[0, 1]` is $-0$ and the upper bound of `[-1, 0]` is $+0$, whatever sign of zero the interval was built from; `[0, 0]` has the representative $(-0, +0)$. An interval is a set: the sign of a stored zero is not part of it. `Binary`'s `==` agrees with `compare` (`-0 == +0`); `identical` tells the two zeros apart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:435
msgid "Every endpoint is a number of the format of $p$ bits (at most $p$ significant bits, on the grid of $p$ bits, the band below the range floor included) or an infinity, and its label, `endpoint.precision()`, is the precision $p$ of the interval. `Binary`'s `==` agrees with `compare` and ignores the label (`Binary::identical` compares it), so `x.lower_bound() == y` holds for a value `y` of the same number; `Interval`'s own `==` and `identical` compare the endpoints by value and never read a label. The sign of a stored zero is not part of the interval: the accessors decide it, as the paragraph above says."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:443
msgid "`Interval::midpoint` and `Interval::radius`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:445
msgid "`midpoint` is `mid` and `radius` is `rad` of IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8 (the names `mid`, `rad`, `wid`, `inf`, `sup`, `mag` and `mig` are spelled out here; the standard's short names would sit next to `Binary::inf`, so the long names are a documented exception)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:455
msgid "Both are total; none aborts. `midpoint` returns NaN for Empty, 0 for Entire, the largest finite number of the format (the interval's precision, the widest exponent range of `Binary`) with the sign of the unbounded side for a half-bounded interval, and otherwise the exact midpoint rounded to nearest (ties to even) at the interval's precision. `midpoint_ctx` is the same observer for a given `IntervalContext` as the result format. `radius` returns NaN for Empty, $+\\infty$ for every unbounded interval, and otherwise the smallest number $r$ of the precision with $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq [m - r, m + r]$, where $m$ is the value `midpoint` returns: the larger of $m - \\underline{x}$ and $\\overline{x} - m$, rounded up. This is measured from the rounded midpoint, not from the exact center, so `[midpoint - radius, midpoint + radius]` always contains the interval (before 0.12 the radius was the half-width and could miss a bound by up to half an ulp). For $[1, 1 + 3 \\cdot 2^{-52}]$ at 53 bits the midpoint is $1 + 2^{-51}$ and the radius $2^{-51}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:472
msgid "`width` returns $\\operatorname{RU}_p(\\overline{x} - \\underline{x})$, an upper bound of the exact width. It is NaN for Empty and $+\\infty$ for unbounded intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:476
msgid "`Interval::magnitude` and `Interval::mignitude`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:478
msgid "`magnitude` returns $\\sup\\{|\\xi| : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}\\}$ and `mignitude` returns $\\inf\\{|\\xi| : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:486
msgid "For a non-empty interval,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:498
msgid "because $|\\xi|$ is convex, so its maximum over an interval is at an endpoint, and it is monotone on each side of 0. Both are exact. Empty gives NaN for both (12.12.8); `magnitude` of an unbounded interval is $+\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:517
msgid "`precision` returns the precision tag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:525
msgid "These predicates test the shape of the set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:534
msgid "`is_common_interval` holds when the interval is non-empty with two finite endpoints (IEEE 1788-2015 10.6.3). `is_singleton` holds when $\\underline{x} = \\overline{x}$. To ask whether an interval contains 0, use `is_member` with a zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:539
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:505
#: manual/performance/binary.md:31
#: manual/performance/binary.md:65
#: manual/performance/binary.md:77
#: manual/performance/binary.md:95
#: manual/performance/binary.md:110
#: manual/performance/binary.md:132
