#: manual/performance/binary.md:296
#: manual/performance/binary.md:310
msgid "Precision"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:543
msgid "`with_precision` re-rounds an interval to a new precision without shrinking it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:550
msgid "The endpoints are rounded outward: the lower bound toward $-\\infty$ and the upper bound toward $+\\infty$, because outward rounding is the only direction that keeps the result an enclosure; there is no rounding-mode argument. Empty stays Empty with the new precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:555
msgid "[!NOTE] The result is the tightest representable enclosure of the input, so widening the precision is exact and re-rounding at an unchanged precision is an identity. The center–radius rebuild this function used to perform is not an identity: if the exact center $(\\underline{x} + \\overline{x})/2$ needs more than $p$ bits, both endpoints move outward by the rounding error of the center, so at 53 bits $[1, 1 + 2^{-52}]$ became $[1 - 2^{-52}, 1 + 2^{-52}]$. `normalized`, `convex_hull` with an Empty operand, the checked capabilities (`div_checked`, `pow_nat_checked`, `pow_int_checked`) and the `pown` method of `interval_checked` round endpoints the same way and do not widen a representable interval either."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:588
msgid "`normalized` normalizes the two endpoints (`Binary::normalized` removes trailing zero bits) and stores them at the interval's precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:595
msgid "The stored endpoints are already representable at that precision, so the result is the same set as the input: `normalized` of $[1, 1 + 2^{-52}]$ at 53 bits is $[1, 1 + 2^{-52}]$. Empty stays Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:599
msgid "Set operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:601
msgid "`Interval::intersection` and `Interval::convex_hull`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:603
msgid "`intersection` returns $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap \\boldsymbol{y}$ and `convex_hull` returns the smallest interval containing $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cup \\boldsymbol{y}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:611
msgid "For non-empty operands both are computed by endpoint `max`/`min` ($[\\max(\\underline{x}, \\underline{y}), \\min(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})]$ and $[\\min(\\underline{x}, \\underline{y}), \\max(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})]$), exact apart from the final outward rounding to the larger precision. Disjoint intervals intersect to Empty, and an Empty operand makes the intersection Empty. When one operand of `convex_hull` is Empty, the result is the other operand at the larger of the two precisions, which is exact (see the note under [`Interval::with_precision`](#intervalwith_precision))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:620
msgid "`Interval::cancel_plus` and `Interval::cancel_minus`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:622
msgid "`cancel_minus(x, y)` is the IEEE 1788 cancellative subtraction: in exact arithmetic it returns the interval $\\boldsymbol{z}$ with $\\boldsymbol{y} + \\boldsymbol{z} = \\boldsymbol{x}$, which undoes a previous sum; `cancel_plus(x, y)` is `cancel_minus(x, -y)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:632
msgid "For bounded operands `cancel_minus` is $[\\operatorname{RD}(\\underline{x} - \\underline{y}), \\operatorname{RU}(\\overline{x} - \\overline{y})]$. Indeed $\\boldsymbol{y} + \\boldsymbol{z} = [\\underline{y} + \\underline{z}, \\overline{y} + \\overline{z}]$, so $\\boldsymbol{y} + \\boldsymbol{z} = \\boldsymbol{x}$ forces $\\underline{z} = \\underline{x} - \\underline{y}$ and $\\overline{z} = \\overline{x} - \\overline{y}$, and this is an interval exactly when $w(\\boldsymbol{x}) \\ge w(\\boldsymbol{y})$. Because of the outward rounding, the computed $\\boldsymbol{z}$ satisfies $\\boldsymbol{y} + \\boldsymbol{z} \\supseteq \\boldsymbol{x}$. When $w(\\boldsymbol{y}) > w(\\boldsymbol{x})$, when an operand is unbounded, or when only $\\boldsymbol{y}$ is empty, the result is Entire. Empty $\\boldsymbol{x}$ with bounded or empty $\\boldsymbol{y}$ gives Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:659
msgid "Relations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:661
msgid "All relations are set relations of IEEE 1788; none of them is a total order. They never abort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:666
msgid "`is_member` tests whether a point belongs to the interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:672
msgid "It returns false for Empty and for a non-finite point (an unbounded interval contains all sufficiently large reals, but not $\\pm\\infty$ or NaN). The trait method `@lf_arith.Contains::contains` instead takes two intervals and tests inclusion; see [Enclosure relations](#arithmeticcontains-arithmeticoverlaps-arithmeticdefinitelylt-arithmeticdefinitelyle-arithmeticmaybeeq)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:680
msgid "These test inclusion, inclusion in the interior, equality and disjointness of sets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:690
msgid "`x.subset(y)` is $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq \\boldsymbol{y}$, that is $\\underline{y} \\le \\underline{x}$ and $\\overline{x} \\le \\overline{y}$. `x.interior(y)` is $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq \\operatorname{int} \\boldsymbol{y}$: each endpoint comparison is strict, except that two equal infinite endpoints count as interior (the interior of $[a, +\\infty)$ is $(a, +\\infty)$, so Entire is interior to itself). Empty is a subset of, interior to and disjoint from every interval. `equal` is the method of the `Eq` implementation, so `x == y` is `x.equal(y)`: the two intervals are the same set (two empty intervals are equal), whatever their precision tags and the representation of their endpoints (IEEE 1788-2015 12.3 and 12.12.9)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:703
msgid "`identical` compares the datum, not only the set (12.3): the same set and the same precision tag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:710
msgid "The same interval at 24 and at 113 bits is `==` and not `identical`; use `identical` where the precision matters, for example to check that a result has the precision that was asked for. Two empty intervals are identical when their precisions are equal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:715
msgid "The same holds for any other part of the format of an interval that a later release adds (the optional exponent range of the 754-conforming types, issue 232): the format is not part of `==` or of `compare`; only `identical` may look at it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:721
msgid "These are the order relations of IEEE 1788."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:730
msgid "For non-empty operands:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:732
msgid "Relation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:732
msgid "Set definition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:734
msgid "$\\forall\\xi\\,\\exists\\eta: \\xi \\le \\eta$ and $\\forall\\eta\\,\\exists\\xi: \\xi \\le \\eta$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:734
msgid "$\\underline{x} \\le \\underline{y}$ and $\\overline{x} \\le \\overline{y}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:735
msgid "the same with $<$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:735
msgid "$\\underline{x} < \\underline{y}$ (or both $-\\infty$) and $\\overline{x} < \\overline{y}$ (or both $+\\infty$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:739
msgid "With Empty: `less` and `strict_less` hold only when both are Empty; `precedes` and `strict_precedes` hold when either is Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:744
msgid "`overlap` classifies the relative position of two intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:750
msgid "The result is one of the sixteen states of [`OverlapState`](#overlapstate); it is computed from the comparisons of the four endpoints, testing equal endpoints first, so a singleton that coincides with an endpoint of the other interval gives `Starts`, `Finishes`, `StartedBy` or `FinishedBy` rather than `Meets` or `MetBy`, as IEEE 1788 requires."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:776
msgid "The four basic operations return the outward-rounded hull of $\\{\\xi \\circ \\eta : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}, \\eta \\in \\boldsymbol{y}\\}$; they are also available as the operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:787
msgid "The result precision is the larger operand precision; an Empty operand gives Empty. Endpoint formulas:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:798
msgid "with $0 \\cdot \\infty$ taken as 0 in $S$ (a zero endpoint times an unbounded side contributes 0). The sums and products are formed exactly and rounded once. For bounded operands the signs select the products that can be extremal: two when at most one operand has 0 in its interior, four (the minimum of $\\underline{x}\\,\\overline{y}$ and $\\overline{x}\\,\\underline{y}$, the maximum of $\\underline{x}\\,\\underline{y}$ and $\\overline{x}\\,\\overline{y}$) when both operands have 0 in their interior. The design page proves the [sign-case table](../design/interval.md#endpoint-formulas)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:807
msgid ""
"Division follows IEEE 1788: $\\boldsymbol{x} / \\boldsymbol{y}$ is the hull of $\\{\\xi/\\eta : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}, \\eta \\in \\boldsymbol{y}, \\eta \\n"
"e 0\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:810
msgid "Divisor $\\boldsymbol{y}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:812
msgid "$[\\operatorname{RD}\\min Q, \\operatorname{RU}\\max Q]$ over the endpoint quotients $Q$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:813
#: manual/api/interval.md:1887
msgid "Empty"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:814
msgid "Entire, unless $\\boldsymbol{x} = \\{0\\}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:815
msgid "$[0, \\overline{y}]$ or $[\\underline{y}, 0]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:815
msgid "half-unbounded (see below), or Entire when $\\underline{x} < 0 < \\overline{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:817
msgid "When the divisor touches 0 at one end, the quotient is unbounded on one side: for example $[1, 2]/[0, 4] = [1/4, +\\infty)$ and $[-2, -1]/[0, 4] = (-\\infty, -1/4]$. A dividend equal to $\\{0\\}$ gives $\\{0\\}$ for any divisor other than $\\{0\\}$. Each selected quotient is computed by one directed division at the result precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:837
msgid "`Interval::neg` and `Interval::abs`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:839
msgid "`neg` returns $[-\\overline{x}, -\\underline{x}]$ and `abs` returns $\\{|\\xi| : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:847
msgid "`abs` is $\\boldsymbol{x}$ when $\\underline{x} \\ge 0$, $-\\boldsymbol{x}$ when $\\overline{x} \\le 0$, and $[0, \\operatorname{mag}\\boldsymbol{x}]$ otherwise. Both are exact. `neg` is also the unary operator `-`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:853
msgid "`recip` returns $1/\\boldsymbol{x}$ with the division rules above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:859
msgid "`Interval::sqr` and `Interval::pown`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:861
msgid "`sqr` returns $\\{\\xi^2\\}$ and `pown` returns $\\{\\xi^n\\}$ for an integer exponent $n$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:869
msgid "Unlike `x * x`, these use the same point twice, so `[-1, 2].sqr()` is $[0, 4]$ (while `x * x` is $[-2, 4]$): `sqr` is $[\\operatorname{mig}(\\boldsymbol{x})^2, \\operatorname{mag}(\\boldsymbol{x})^2]$. `pown` evaluates the monotone pieces of $\\xi^n$ at the endpoints with directed rounding: odd positive powers are increasing; even positive powers decrease then increase, with minimum 0 when $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{x}$; negative powers have a pole at 0. `pown(x, 0)` is $\\{1\\}$ for every non-empty `x`; Empty stays Empty. `pown(x, n)` with $n < 0$ returns Empty for $\\boldsymbol{x} = \\{0\\}$, a half-unbounded interval when 0 is an endpoint, and for 0 in the interior Entire (odd $n$) or $[\\min(\\underline{x}^n, \\overline{x}^n), +\\infty)$ (even $n$). As a shortcut, $n < -4096$ with 0 in the interior returns Entire even for even $n$; this is a valid but not tight enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:896
msgid "`fma(x, y, z)` returns an enclosure of $\\{\\xi\\eta + \\zeta\\}$ with a single outward rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:903
msgid "The exact product bounds are computed as for `mul` and added to the endpoints of $\\boldsymbol{z}$ before the final rounding, so the result is never wider than `x * y + z`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:907
msgid "`Interval::min` and `Interval::max`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:909
msgid "`min` and `max` return $\\{\\min(\\xi, \\eta)\\}$ and $\\{\\max(\\xi, \\eta)\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:917
msgid "`min` is $[\\min(\\underline{x}, \\underline{y}), \\min(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})]$ and `max` is the analogue with `max`: $\\min$ is increasing in each argument. An Empty operand gives Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:923
msgid "The integer functions of IEEE 1788-2015 Table 9.1. `sign` is $-1$, $0$ or $1$ by the sign of the point; `ceil` and `floor` give the smallest integer not less and the largest integer not greater than the point; `trunc` the nearest integer toward zero; `round_ties_to_even` and `round_ties_to_away` the nearest integer, a tie going to the even integer or away from zero (the names are the 754 `roundTiesToEven` and `roundTiesToAway`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:939
msgid "Each function $f$ is nondecreasing, so the result is $[f(\\underline{x}), f(\\overline{x})]$. The values are integers (or $-1$, $0$, $1$), so the result is exact: nothing is rounded, the precision tag is kept, and a zero bound is stored as $+0$. The function of $\\pm\\infty$ is $\\pm\\infty$ (for `sign`, $\\pm 1$). An Empty operand gives Empty. There is no `try_` form: no input fails."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:961
msgid "`Interval::sqr_rev`, `Interval::abs_rev`, `Interval::pown_rev` and `Interval::cosh_rev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:963
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4 and Table 10.1. Each is the natural reverse extension: the hull of $\\{\\, x \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\varphi(x) \\in \\boldsymbol{c} \\,\\}$, where $\\boldsymbol{c}$ is the receiver and the prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$ is the optional last argument (default Entire). The hull is taken after meeting the preimage with $\\boldsymbol{x}$, not before: for $\\boldsymbol{c} = [1, 4]$, `sqr_rev` is $[-2, 2]$ and with the prior $[0, 1.2]$ it is $[1, 1.2]$, while $[-2, 2] \\cap [0, 1.2] = [0, 1.2]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:978
msgid "`pown_rev(c, p, x?)` is the standard's `pownRev(c, x, p)`: the power comes before the optional prior. The functions:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:981
msgid "Preimage of $\\boldsymbol{c}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:983
msgid "$\\pm\\sqrt{\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap [0, +\\infty)}$, the endpoints of the roots rounded outward"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:984
msgid "$\\pm(\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap [0, +\\infty))$, exact"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:986
msgid "$\\mathbb{R}$ if $1 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$, else empty ($x^0 = 1$ for every $x$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:987
msgid "`pown_rev`, $p$ even and positive"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:988
msgid "`pown_rev`, $p$ odd and positive"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:988
msgid "$\\sqrt[p]{\\boldsymbol{c}}$, one interval ($p = 1$: $\\boldsymbol{c}$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:989
msgid "`pown_rev`, $p$ even and negative"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:989
msgid ""
"$x \\n"
"e 0$ with $x^p \\in \\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (0, +\\infty)$: $\\pm$ the $\\lvert p\\rvert$-th root of $1/\\boldsymbol{c}$, the upper end of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ bounding $\\lvert x\\rvert$ from below and the lower end from above"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:990
msgid "`pown_rev`, $p$ odd and negative"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:990
msgid "the positive part of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ gives positive $x$ and the negative part gives negative $x$, each by $\\sqrt[p]{\\cdot}$ with the pole 0 excluded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:992
msgid "An Empty $\\boldsymbol{c}$, or a $\\boldsymbol{c}$ outside the range of the function (negative for `sqr_rev`, below 1 for `cosh_rev`), gives Empty. The root endpoints are directed roundings of exact points, so the results are the tightest in the type. For a negative power the value $x = 0$ is not in the domain, so the component that ends at 0 (when $\\boldsymbol{c}$ is unbounded above for an even $p$, or reaches the pole) is open there: with $\\boldsymbol{c} = [1, +\\infty)$ and $p = -2$ the set is $[-1, 0) \\cup (0, 1]$ and the prior $[0, 0]$ gives Empty. The result precision is the largest of the operands'."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1012
msgid "`Interval::sin_rev`, `Interval::cos_rev` and `Interval::tan_rev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1014
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4 and Table 10.1. Each is the hull of $\\{\\, x \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\varphi(x) \\in \\boldsymbol{c} \\,\\}$ for the receiver $\\boldsymbol{c}$ and the optional prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$ (default Entire)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1024
msgid "The preimage is a periodic union of intervals. With $\\boldsymbol{c}' = \\boldsymbol{c} \\cap [-1, 1]$, $A = \\arcsin \\underline{c'}$ and $B = \\arcsin \\overline{c'}$, the preimage of `sin_rev` is $\\bigcup_k [A + 2\\pi k, B + 2\\pi k] \\cup [\\pi - B + 2\\pi k, \\pi - A + 2\\pi k]$; `cos_rev` and `tan_rev` are analogous (`tan_rev`: $\\bigcup_k (\\arctan \\underline{c} + \\pi k, \\arctan \\overline{c} + \\pi k)$, a bound at $\\pm\\infty$ giving the pole $\\pm\\pi/2 + \\pi k$, which is not in the domain). With the default prior the result is Entire for every $\\boldsymbol{c}$ that meets the range of the function; $\\boldsymbol{c}$ outside $[-1, 1]$ gives Empty for `sin_rev` and `cos_rev`, and $\\boldsymbol{c} \\supseteq [-1, 1]$ (`tan_rev`: $\\boldsymbol{c} = $ Entire) returns the prior itself. With a bounded prior only the first and the last piece that meet it matter, so the cost does not depend on how many periods the prior spans."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1036
msgid "The result is the hull of the true set in the type: every comparison of a prior end with a piece end is decided exactly, and an end of the result that comes from a piece is the exact directed rounding of that end (`RD` for a lower end, `RU` for an upper end). This is the tightest result, and it can be narrower than a reference value that leaves an ulp of slack. For example `sin_rev([0, 0], x = [RU(pi), 7])` is $[RD(2\\pi), RU(2\\pi)]$ and `sin_rev([0, 0], x = [RU(pi), 6])` is Empty. How the ends are decided is on the [design page](../design/interval.md#reverse-sine-cosine-and-tangent). The one limit is the forward trigonometric one: an end of the prior whose reduction needs more than 8,000,000 working bits ($\\max(0, k) + p + 96$ for the adjusted exponent $k$ of the end) is kept as it is (valid, not tight), decided before any work is done, and a prior whose finite ends are all beyond it is returned at once. For a prior end of magnitude $2^e$ inside the limit the cost grows with $e$ like the cost of the forward functions of the [trigonometric functions section](#intervalsin-intervalcos-intervaltan-intervaltry_sin-intervaltry_cos-intervaltry_tan), the working precision following $e$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1072
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 Table 10.1. `b.pow_rev1(c, x?)` is the hull of $\\{\\, x \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\exists\\, \\beta \\in \\boldsymbol{b} : x^\\beta \\text{ is defined and in } \\boldsymbol{c} \\,\\}$ for the optional prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$ (default Entire); the receiver is the exponent interval $\\boldsymbol{b}$. $x^\\beta$ is defined for $x > 0$ and for $x = 0$ with $\\beta > 0$ (value 0)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1081
msgid "For $x > 0$, $x^\\beta \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ means $\\beta \\ln x \\in \\ln(\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$, so the set is $\\exp(L /_{\\text{set}} \\boldsymbol{b})$ with $L = \\ln(\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$, the set division of [`mul_rev`](#intervalmul_rev_to_pair-and-intervalmul_rev) in logarithm coordinates, plus the point 0 when $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ and $\\boldsymbol{b}$ has a positive point. Every end of a piece is $c_\\text{end}^{1/\\beta_\\text{end}}$, 0, 1 or infinite. The ends that are not attained are open: 0 (from $\\ln x \\to -\\infty$, unless the zero part closes it) and 1 (the limit of a quotient by an unbounded $\\boldsymbol{b}$); a prior that only touches such an end does not meet the set. For example `pow_rev1([1, +inf), [2, +inf))` is $(1, +\\infty)$, so with the prior $[0, 1]$ the result is Empty, and `pow_rev1([-1, 1], [0.5, 2])` is $(0, +\\infty)$, so with the prior $[0, 0]$ it is Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1092
msgid "The result is the hull in the type: every comparison of a prior end with a piece end is exact and an end written to the result is the exact directed rounding of the piece end. The exact value of $c^{1/\\beta}$ is classified from the structure of $c$ and $\\beta$ (no squaring): it is a float candidate (dyadic), a rational that is not a float (for example $9^{1/(-2)} = 1/3$), or irrational, and the last is decided by a loop that raises the working precision as `sin_rev` does. The [design page](../design/interval.md#reverse-power-with-an-interval-exponent) has the classes, the budgets and their cost. An end of the set above the largest finite float is written as $+\\infty$ when it is an upper end and as the largest finite float of the result precision when it is a lower end; an end below the smallest positive stored value is written as 0 when it is a lower end and as the smallest positive stored value when it is an upper end; in between, including the subnormal range, the end is the usual directed rounding. An exponent of magnitude $2^{10^9}$ is decided at once because the sign of $\\ln(c)/\\beta$ is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1119
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 Table 10.1. `a.pow_rev2(c, x?)` is the hull of $\\{\\, \\beta \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\exists\\, a_0 \\in \\boldsymbol{a} : a_0^\\beta \\text{ is defined and in } \\boldsymbol{c} \\,\\}$ for the optional prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$ (default Entire); the receiver is the base interval $\\boldsymbol{a}$ and the unknown is the exponent. $a_0^\\beta$ is defined for $a_0 > 0$ and for $a_0 = 0$ with $\\beta > 0$ (value 0)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1129
msgid ""
"For $a_0 > 0$, $a_0^\\beta \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ means $\\beta \\ln a_0 \\in \\ln(\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$, so the set is $L /_{\\text{set}} M$ with $L = \\ln(\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$ and $M = \\ln(\\boldsymbol{a} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$, the set division of [`mul_rev`](#intervalmul_rev_to_pair-and-intervalmul_rev) in logarithm coordinates, plus $(0, +\\infty)$ when $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{a}$ and $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ (the base 0). The base 1 is the branch \"$0$ in the divisor\": if $1 \\in \\boldsymbol{a}$ and $1 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ every exponent qualifies, and if $\\boldsymbol{a} = [1, 1]$ and $1 \\n"
"otin \\boldsymbol{c}$ there is none. Every end of a piece is $\\ln(c_\\text{end}) / \\ln(a_\\text{end})$, 0 or infinite. The ends that are not attained are open: the infinities, and 0 when it is the limit of a quotient by an unbounded $M$ (and $0 \\n"
"otin L$); a prior that only touches such an end does not meet the set. For example `pow_rev2([2, +inf), [2, 4])` is $(0, 2]$, so with the prior $[-3, 0]$ the result is Empty, while with $\\boldsymbol{c} = [1, 4]$ the end 0 is attained and the result is $[0, 0]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1142
msgid "The result is the hull in the type: every comparison of a prior end with a piece end is exact and an end written to the result is the exact directed rounding of the piece end. The value $r = \\ln(C)/\\ln(A)$ of an end is classified from the structure of $A$ and $C$: it is rational iff $A$ and $C$ are multiplicatively dependent, decided by the multiplicative Euclidean algorithm on the odd parts (an inexact division proves independence). A rational is a float iff its reduced denominator is a power of two, and otherwise it is a rational that is not a float (for example $\\ln 3 / \\ln 27 = 1/3$); every other end is irrational and is decided by a loop that raises the working precision as `sin_rev` does. The [design page](../design/interval.md#reverse-power-with-an-interval-base) has the classes, the budgets and their cost. A base one unit from 1 gives a huge quotient, and the first working precision follows its magnitude."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1166
msgid "`Interval::atan2_rev1` and `Interval::atan2_rev2`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1168
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 Table 10.1, equations (6) and (7) of 10.5.4. `b.atan2_rev1(c, x?)` is the hull of $\\{\\, y \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\exists\\, b_0 \\in \\boldsymbol{b} : \\operatorname{atan2}(y, b_0) \\text{ is defined and in } \\boldsymbol{c} \\,\\}$: the receiver is the abscissa interval $\\boldsymbol{b}$ and the unknown is the ordinate $y$. `a.atan2_rev2(c, x?)` is the hull of $\\{\\, x_0 \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\exists\\, a_0 \\in \\boldsymbol{a} : \\operatorname{atan2}(a_0, x_0) \\text{ is defined and in } \\boldsymbol{c} \\,\\}$: the receiver is the ordinate interval $\\boldsymbol{a}$ and the unknown is the abscissa $x_0$. The optional prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$ defaults to Entire."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1181
msgid "$\\operatorname{atan2}(y, x)$ is defined on the plane minus the origin, with the range $(-\\pi, \\pi]$ (Table 9.1, footnote f): the angle $\\pi$ is attained on the half-line $y = 0$, $x < 0$, and $-\\pi$ is never attained. With $\\Theta = \\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (-\\pi, \\pi]$ the set is the projection of the sector $\\{\\, r(\\cos t, \\sin t) : r > 0,\\ t \\in \\Theta \\,\\}$ on the axis of the unknown, cut by the strip of the given operand. The [design page](../design/interval.md#reverse-arctangent-of-two-arguments) has the case table by angle region and by the sign of the operand, the open ends, the branch cut and the origin. The set is empty when $\\boldsymbol{c}$ has no point in $(-\\pi, \\pi]$ (for example $[4, 5]$), when an operand or the prior is Empty, and for the abscissa $\\{0\\}$ (Rev1) or the ordinate $\\{0\\}$ (Rev2) when $\\boldsymbol{c}$ misses the angles $\\pm\\pi/2$ (Rev1) or $0$ and $\\pi$ (Rev2); the origin is never part of the set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1191
msgid "The result is the hull in the type: every comparison of a prior end with a piece end is exact, and an end written to the result is the exact directed rounding of the piece end. The exact ends are 0 and the infinities; every other end is $b_\\text{end} \\tan(c_\\text{end})$ (Rev1) or $a_\\text{end} / \\tan(c_\\text{end})$ (Rev2) of nonzero floats, which is irrational (the tangent of a nonzero algebraic number is transcendental), so it differs from every float and a loop that raises the working precision decides it, as in `sin_rev`. Whether an end of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ is below or above a multiple of $\\pi/2$ is decided exactly ($\\pi$ is irrational, so a float is never equal to such a multiple)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1212
msgid "`Interval::mul_rev_to_pair` and `Interval::mul_rev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1214
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.5 and Table 10.1. For intervals $\\boldsymbol{b}$ and $\\boldsymbol{c}$ the set"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1221
msgid "has zero, one or two connected components, and is not always closed ($[1, 1] /_{\\text{set}} [1, +\\infty) = (0, 1]$). `mul_rev_to_pair` returns the closures of the components, ordered, as a pair; `mul_rev` returns the hull of the part of the set that lies in the prior interval `x`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1231
msgid "`mul_rev_to_pair(b, c)` is `(Empty, Empty)` when there is no component, `(u, Empty)` for one, and `(u, v)` with $u < v$ for two. The two closures can share an endpoint: $[1, 1] /_{\\text{set}} \\text{Entire} = (-\\infty, 0) \\cup (0, +\\infty)$ gives `([-inf, 0], [0, +inf])`. An Empty operand gives `(Empty, Empty)`. The receiver is $\\boldsymbol{b}$ and the argument is $\\boldsymbol{c}$, as in the standard's `mulRevToPair(b, c)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1238
msgid "`mul_rev(b, c, x?)` is $\\operatorname{hull}\\{x \\in \\boldsymbol{x} \\mid \\exists y \\in \\boldsymbol{b} : x y \\in \\boldsymbol{c}\\}$ with the default $\\boldsymbol{x} = $ Entire. The hull is of the set and not of its closure, and not the intersection of `x` with the hull of the set: with $\\boldsymbol{b} = [-1, 1]$, $\\boldsymbol{c} = [1, 1]$ and $\\boldsymbol{x} = [0, 0.5]$ the result is Empty, whereas the hull of the set is Entire. An endpoint 0 that is the unattained limit of a quotient by an unbounded $\\boldsymbol{b}$ counts as open, so `mul_rev([1, +inf), [1, 1], x=[-1, 0])` is Empty (the set is $(0, 1]$) and with $\\boldsymbol{x} = [0, 0.5]$ it is $[0, 0.5]$. The endpoints of the components are quotients rounded outward, so the results are the tightest in the type. The result precision is the largest of the operands'."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1267
msgid "Each function $f$ returns an interval containing $f(\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap D_f)$, where $D_f$ is the domain of $f$; points of $\\boldsymbol{x}$ outside $D_f$ are ignored and the result is Empty when $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap D_f$ is empty. The result precision is the argument's precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1273
msgid "Functions come in two forms, and there is one kernel. The `try_` form (`try_exp`, …) evaluates each endpoint with the corresponding `binary` `try_*_ctx` function rounded toward $-\\infty$ or $+\\infty$, and returns `Err(ArithmeticError)` with a certification-failure detail naming the operation, stage and reason when that evaluation runs out of its work budget. The total form (`exp`, `sin`, …) is the `try_` form with a fallback: it returns the same interval whenever the `try_` form succeeds, and otherwise the range of the function over its domain (the fallbacks are listed below), which is a valid but wide enclosure. A total form never aborts, at any precision the type accepts; a domain error is not a failure (the result is Empty), so an argument outside the domain and a certification failure give different results. Nothing is computed twice: the total form calls the `try_` form once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1289
msgid "`sqrt` returns $\\sqrt{\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap [0, +\\infty)}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1295
msgid "The endpoints are the downward and upward square roots at the interval's precision. Empty when $\\overline{x} < 0$. There is no `try_` form: square root never fails."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1301
msgid "These return enclosures of $e^{\\xi}$, $2^{\\xi}$, $10^{\\xi}$ and $e^{\\xi} - 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1315
msgid "All four are increasing, so the result is $[\\operatorname{RD} f(\\underline{x}), \\operatorname{RU} f(\\overline{x})]$. For $\\xi \\ge 2^{30}$ the enclosure of all four is $[\\text{largest finite}, +\\infty)$; for $\\xi \\le -2^{30}$ that of `exp` and `exp10` is $[0, \\text{smallest positive}]$, and that of `expm1` is $[-1, -(1 - 2^{-p})]$, as $e^{\\xi} - 1$ lies just above $-1$. The first is valid because $e^{2^{30}}$ exceeds $2^{e_{\\max}+1}$, and the second because $e^{-2^{30}}$ is below the smallest positive `Binary` at every precision. `exp2` and `exp10` return exact powers for integer endpoints whose power is representable at the interval's precision (`exp2` of $\\{10\\}$ is $\\{1024\\}$, `exp10` of $\\{3\\}$ is $\\{1000\\}$). For `exp2` this includes the subnormal numbers: the result at an integer endpoint $n$ is the exact power $\\{2^n\\}$ down to $n = e_{\\min} - p + 1$, the exponent of the smallest positive number of precision $p$, and $[0, \\text{smallest positive}]$ only below that. An integer endpoint whose power is below the smallest positive number or above the largest finite number keeps the outward-rounded enclosure, so at $p = 53$ `exp2` of $\\{-1073742000\\}$ is $[0, \\text{smallest positive}]$ and of $\\{2^{30}\\}$ is $[\\text{largest finite}, +\\infty)$. The fallback of the total forms is $[0, +\\infty)$ for `exp`, `exp2` and `exp10` and $[-1, +\\infty)$ for `expm1`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1338
msgid "These return enclosures of $\\ln \\xi$, $\\log_2 \\xi$, $\\log_{10} \\xi$ and $\\ln(1 + \\xi)$ over their domains $(0, \\infty)$ and $(-1, \\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1352
msgid "When the interval reaches the domain boundary ($\\underline{x} \\le 0$, or $\\underline{x} \\le -1$ for `log1p`) the lower endpoint is $-\\infty$; when it lies entirely outside the domain ($\\overline{x} \\le 0$, or $\\overline{x} < -1$ for `log1p`) the result is Empty. `log10` returns exact integers at endpoints $10^k$ that are numbers of the precision ($k \\le 22$ at 53 bits). The fallback of the total forms is Entire (an endpoint at or below the domain boundary is decided before the kernel runs and is not a failure)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1378
msgid "`Interval::pow` and `Interval::try_pow`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1380
msgid "`pow(x, y)` returns an enclosure of $\\{\\xi^{\\eta} : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}, \\eta \\in \\boldsymbol{y}\\}$ over the IEEE 1788 domain of `pow`: $\\xi > 0$, or $\\xi = 0$ and $\\eta > 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1389
msgid "Negative parts of the base are ignored (use `pown` or `rootn` for negative bases). Since $\\xi^\\eta = e^{\\eta \\ln \\xi}$ and $\\eta \\ln \\xi$ is bilinear in $(\\ln \\xi, \\eta)$, the extrema over a box with $\\underline{x} > 0$ are at the four corners. When the base reaches 0, $\\xi^\\eta \\to 0$ for $\\eta > 0$ and $\\to +\\infty$ for $\\eta < 0$, so the result is extended by 0 or $+\\infty$ accordingly. The code also adds the value 1 when the base interval contains 1 or the exponent interval contains 0; it already lies between the corner values, so this changes nothing but is harmless. $0^{\\eta}$ for $\\eta \\le 0$ is excluded, so `pow([0, 0], y)` is Empty when $\\overline{y} \\le 0$. The result precision is the larger operand precision. A corner whose power is representable at the result precision, such as $16^{3/4} = 8$, gives that power exactly. The total form falls back to $[0, +\\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1403
msgid "`Interval::rootn` and `Interval::try_rootn`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1405
msgid "`rootn(x, n)` returns an enclosure of the real $n$-th roots $\\{\\xi^{1/n}\\}$: for even $n$ over $\\xi \\ge 0$, for odd $n$ over all reals, and for negative $n$ the reciprocal of the root."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1414
msgid "`rootn(x, 0)` is Empty; `try_rootn(x, 0)` is a domain error. `rootn(x, 1)` is `x` and `rootn(x, 2)` is `sqrt`. The total form is `try_rootn` with a fallback ($[0, +\\infty)$ for an even degree, Entire for an odd one). For negative $n$, `try_rootn` returns Entire when an odd root's argument contains 0. Exact roots are returned exactly for every degree, so `try_rootn({8}, -3)` is $\\{1/2\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1434
msgid "`Interval::hypot` and `Interval::try_hypot`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1436
msgid "`hypot` returns an enclosure of $\\{\\sqrt{\\xi^2 + \\eta^2}\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1443
msgid "The function is increasing in $|\\xi|$ and $|\\eta|$, so the result is $[\\operatorname{RD}\\operatorname{hypot}(\\operatorname{mig}\\boldsymbol{x}, \\operatorname{mig}\\boldsymbol{y}), \\operatorname{RU}\\operatorname{hypot}(\\operatorname{mag}\\boldsymbol{x}, \\operatorname{mag}\\boldsymbol{y})]$, evaluated on the endpoints of `abs(x)` and `abs(y)`. The total form falls back to `sqrt(sqr(x) + sqr(y))`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1451
msgid "These return enclosures of $\\sin$, $\\cos$ and $\\tan$ over the interval (in radians)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1463
msgid "Both forms evaluate the `binary` functions at the endpoints (a point interval) or at the endpoints and the critical points. A critical point $k\\pi/2$ inside the interval contributes the extremum $\\pm 1$ of `sin`/`cos` (`sin` reaches $+1$ at $k \\equiv 1$ and $-1$ at $k \\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$, `cos` reaches $+1$ at $k \\equiv 0$ and $-1$ at $k \\equiv 2$); for `tan` an odd multiple of $\\pi/2$ inside the interval (a pole) makes the result Entire. The set of candidate indices $k$ is computed from the enclosure of $\\pi$ and can only be too large, which widens but never invalidates the result. Unbounded arguments give $[-1, 1]$ (Entire for `tan`). When $\\max(0, k) + p + 96$ exceeds 8,000,000, with $k$ the adjusted exponent of the larger endpoint magnitude, the `try_` forms return a resource-limit error without evaluating; the total forms return $[-1, 1]$ (Entire for `tan`), the same fallback they use when the certification budget (12 work precisions) is exhausted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1478
msgid "These return enclosures of $\\sin \\pi\\xi$, $\\cos \\pi\\xi$ and $\\tan \\pi\\xi$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1489
msgid "Here the critical points are the exact half-integers $k/2$, located from the dyadic endpoints without approximating $\\pi$. `tanpi` returns a half-unbounded interval when a pole is exactly an endpoint and no other pole lies in the interval (for example $[1/2, 1]$ gives $(-\\infty, 0]$, whose upper bound is 0, read as $+0$ by `upper_bound` (IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8); $\\tan \\pi$ is $-0$ in IEEE 754-2019 9.2, but the sign of a stored zero is not part of the interval), Empty for the singleton of a pole, and Entire when a pole lies inside. Unbounded arguments and fallbacks give $[-1, 1]$ (Entire for `tanpi`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1514
msgid "These return enclosures of $\\arcsin$, $\\arccos$ (over $[-1, 1]$) and $\\arctan$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1526
msgid "`asin` and `atan` are increasing and `acos` is decreasing, so only endpoints are evaluated. `atan` of an infinite endpoint is $\\pm\\pi/2$. The fallback of the total forms is $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$ for `atan` and `asin` and $[0, \\pi]$ for `acos`, with $\\pi$ taken from a constant of at most 64 bits and rounded outward to the interval's precision, so the fallback itself cannot fail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1532
msgid "`Interval::atan2` and `Interval::try_atan2`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1534
msgid ""
"`y.atan2(x)` returns an enclosure of the angles $\\operatorname{atan2}(\\eta, \\xi) \\in (-\\pi, \\pi]$ of the points $(\\xi, \\eta) \\n"
"e (0, 0)$ of the box $\\boldsymbol{x} \\times \\boldsymbol{y}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1543
msgid "The receiver is the ordinate. The result is the hull of the angles at the four corners and at the points where the box meets the axes. When the box crosses the branch cut ($\\underline{x} < 0$ and $\\underline{y} < 0 \\le \\overline{y}$) the result is $[-\\pi, \\pi]$. The box $\\{(0, 0)\\}$ gives Empty. The total form falls back to $[-\\pi, \\pi]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1551
msgid "These return enclosures of the hyperbolic functions and their inverses over their domains ($[1, \\infty)$ for `acosh`, $(-1, 1)$ for `atanh`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1569
msgid "`cosh` has its minimum 1 at 0; the others are monotone. `atanh` of an interval reaching $\\pm 1$ is unbounded on that side, and an interval whose only point in the domain's closure is $1$ or $-1$ gives Empty. The fallback of the total forms is Entire for `sinh`, `asinh` and `atanh`, $[-1, 1]$ for `tanh`, $[1, +\\infty)$ for `cosh` and $[0, +\\infty)$ for `acosh`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1575
msgid "The total forms keep their relative accuracy for a tiny argument, since they are the `binary` kernels: at 53 bits `sinh` of $\\{2^{-300}\\}$ is the two-ulp interval $[2^{-300}, (1 + 2^{-52})\\,2^{-300}]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1591
msgid "Contexts and flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1593
msgid "`IntervalContext::new` and `IntervalContext::try_new`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1595
msgid "`IntervalContext::new` builds a context from a precision and the largest exponent `e_max`; `e_min` is `1 - e_max` (IEEE 754-2019 3.3)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1603
msgid "The defaults are precision 53 and, for `e_max`, the implementation limit `binary_value_e_max` of `binary`, the same as `BinaryContext::new` given a precision alone (so `IntervalContext::new()` is not binary64: use `binary64()` for that). The legality of the pair is decided by `BinaryContext::try_new`, so `binary` and `interval` have one definition of a format: the precision is between 1 and `binary_precision_max` and `e_max` is at least 1. An illegal pair aborts `new`, naming the parameter, and is a domain error for `try_new`. Before 0.12 `e_min` was a parameter, any pair with `e_min <= e_max` was accepted, and the precision had no upper limit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1613
msgid "`IntervalContext::with_precision` and `IntervalContext::with_e_max`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1615
msgid "These return the same format with another precision, or another largest exponent (and `e_min = 1 - e_max`); they abort when the result is not legal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1625
msgid "These return the contexts of the IEEE 754 interchange formats: binary16 ($p = 11$, $[-14, 15]$), binary32 ($p = 24$, $[-126, 127]$), binary64 ($p = 53$, $[-1022, 1023]$) and binary128 ($p = 113$, $[-16382, 16383]$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1638
msgid "These return the parameters of a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1648
msgid "`IntervalFlags::new` returns the flags with nothing raised; `combine` is their union; the accessors read the fields."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1661
msgid "`apply_ctx` rounds an interval outward into a context and reports the flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1667
msgid "Each finite nonzero endpoint is rounded outward to the context precision. An endpoint whose rounded exponent exceeds $e_{\\max}$ overflows: it becomes $\\mp\\infty$ if it is a negative lower or a positive upper endpoint, and the largest finite value of the right sign otherwise (a positive lower endpoint becomes the largest finite value, which is still below it). An endpoint below the normal range is then rounded outward on the subnormal grid $2^{e_{\\min} - p + 1}\\mathbb{Z}$, so a tiny positive upper endpoint becomes the smallest subnormal rather than 0. Rounding twice in the same direction equals rounding once onto the coarser grid, so the endpoints are the directed roundings of the stored ones. Zeros and infinities are kept. Empty gives Empty with no flags. The result has the context precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1679
msgid "`underflow` follows IEEE 754 with tininess detected after rounding: it is raised when an endpoint is inexact and its rounding to $p$ bits with an unbounded exponent range lies below $2^{e_{\\min}}$, whichever of the two steps lost the bits. With $p = 4$ and $e_{\\min} = -2$, the lower endpoint $2^{-3}(1 + 2^{-10})$ becomes $2^{-3}$, which is already on the subnormal grid, and raises both `inexact` and `underflow`. An exact tiny endpoint raises nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1687
msgid "There is no `add_ctx` or `exp_ctx`: the operation and the context are composed, `(x + y).apply_ctx(ctx)`. When the operands' precision is at least the context precision, the double rounding gives the same endpoints as a single outward rounding into the context (see the [design page](../design/interval.md#contexts-and-double-rounding))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1695
msgid "`midpoint_ctx` is the IEEE 1788 `mid` operation with the context as the result format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1702
msgid "Following IEEE 1788-2015 §12.12.8, Empty gives NaN, Entire gives $+0$, an interval unbounded only above gives the largest finite value of the context, $(2 - 2^{1-p})\\,2^{e_{\\max}}$, and one unbounded only below gives its negative; none of these raises a flag. A bounded interval gives its exact center rounded once to nearest (ties to even) onto the context's grid, including the subnormal grid $2^{e_{\\min} - p + 1}\\mathbb{Z}$, so with $p = 4$, $e_{\\min} = -2$ the center $2^{-6} + 2^{-20}$ gives $2^{-5}$. A zero center gives $+0$. `inexact` is set when the result differs from the center and `underflow` when it is also tiny, as for `apply_ctx`. A center that rounds beyond $e_{\\max}$ overflows to an infinity of its sign with `overflow` and `inexact`: rounding to nearest maps it to $\\pm\\infty$ in IEEE 754, and IEEE 1788 uses that rounding for the bounded case. Only an interval whose endpoints are themselves outside the context's range can do this."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1732
msgid "Decorated intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1734
msgid "A decorated operation computes the bare result and the decoration $d = \\min(d_1, \\dots, d_k, d_f)$, where $d_i$ are the operand decorations and $d_f$ is the decoration of the operation on these operands (`Com` when the function is defined and continuous on the whole input box, `Trv` when the input leaves the domain, as listed below). The minimum is then made canonical (Empty → `Trv`, unbounded with `Com` → `Dac`). Any NaI operand gives NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1743
msgid "`DecoratedInterval::new` decorates a bare interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1749
msgid "The default decoration is `Com`. `new` is `setDec` of IEEE 1788-2015 11.5.2: the decoration `Ill` gives NaI for any interval, empty or not (at the precision of the interval, so the result equals `DecoratedInterval::nai`); otherwise the decoration is made canonical, so `new(entire())` is decorated `Dac` and `new(empty())` is decorated `Trv`, as in IEEE 1788 `newDec`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1755
msgid "`DecoratedInterval::nai` and `DecoratedInterval::is_nai`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1757
msgid "`nai` returns NaI, the result of an invalid decorated construction; `is_nai` tests for it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1765
msgid "NaI has decoration `Ill` and an Empty interval (default precision 53), but it is not Empty: `is_empty` is false for NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1768
msgid "`DecoratedInterval::interval` and `DecoratedInterval::decoration`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1770
msgid "These return the bare interval and the decoration."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1779
msgid "These predicates apply the bare predicate to the interval and return false for NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1792
msgid "These relations apply the bare relation to the intervals and return false when an operand is NaI, except `identical`, which is the equality of datums (IEEE 1788-2015 12.3): it is true for two NaI and false for NaI against any other value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1810
msgid "`overlap` returns `None` when an operand is NaI, which has no value for a non-interval-valued operation (IEEE 1788-2015 11.7.2), and `Some(state)` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1816
msgid "These apply the bare operation and always lower the decoration to `Trv` (set operations are not point functions, so no property of a function can be claimed for their results)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1829
msgid "The arithmetic operations apply the bare operation; their operation decoration is `Com` except where the table says otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1848
#: manual/api/interval.md:1885
msgid "Operation decoration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1850
msgid "`Trv` when the divisor contains 0"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1851
msgid "`Trv` when $n < 0$ and $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1852
msgid "identity (IEEE 1788 `pos`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1853
msgid "others"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1857
msgid "The decorated elementary functions apply the bare total form (there are no decorated `try_` forms) and lower the decoration when the input leaves the domain of the function, as listed in the table at the end of this group."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1861
msgid "These are the decorated square root, exponentials, logarithms, powers and `hypot`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1881
msgid "The interval part is the bare result. The operation decoration is the strongest decoration of 11.2 for the function over the input box, and the result is the minimum of it and the input decoration (11.6, equation 18):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1885
msgid "Input box $\\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1888
msgid "the function is not constant on $\\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1889
msgid "constant, and $\\boldsymbol{x}$ unbounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1890
msgid "constant, bounded, and $\\boldsymbol{x}$ contains a point of discontinuity of the function on the real line"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1891
msgid "constant, bounded, and no such point in $\\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1893
msgid "The functions take integer values, so their restriction to $\\boldsymbol{x}$ is continuous exactly when it is constant (`dac` asks for continuity of the restriction, `com` for continuity at each point of $\\boldsymbol{x}$). The points of discontinuity are the integers for `ceil` and `floor`, the nonzero integers for `trunc`, $0$ for `sign`, and the half-integers for the two roundings. For example `ceil` of the box $[1.1, 2.0]$ is $[2, 2]$ with `Dac`: the restriction is constant, but $2$ is a point of discontinuity of `ceil`. `floor` of the same box is $[1, 2]$ with `Def`. NaI gives NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1911
msgid "`DecoratedInterval::sqr_rev`, `DecoratedInterval::abs_rev`, `DecoratedInterval::pown_rev` and `DecoratedInterval::cosh_rev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1913
msgid "Reverse-mode operations have the trivial decorated version of 11.7.1: NaI if any input (the interval or the prior) is NaI, else the bare result decorated `Trv`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1923
msgid "`DecoratedInterval::sin_rev`, `DecoratedInterval::cos_rev` and `DecoratedInterval::tan_rev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1925
msgid "The trivial decorated version of 11.7.1: NaI if any input (the interval or the prior) is NaI, else the bare result decorated `Trv`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1936
msgid "The trivial decorated version of 11.7.1: NaI if any input is NaI, else the bare result decorated `Trv`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1942
msgid "`DecoratedInterval::mul_rev_to_pair` and `DecoratedInterval::mul_rev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:1944
msgid ""
