#: manual/api/interval.md:2062
msgid "`Show` writes an interval as the portable inf-sup literal of IEEE 1788-2015 13.3 (`intervalToText`): the text `from_string` reads."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2077
msgid "A nonempty bounded or half-bounded interval prints as `[l, u]`, Empty as `[empty]`, Entire as `[entire]`; a decorated interval appends `_` and the decoration, and NaI prints as `[nai]`. Each bound is a decimal number rounded outward (the lower bound down, the upper bound up), so the text is a literal of an interval that contains the value (13.3). The digits are the fewest that keep the bound within one unit in the last place of the stored bound at the precision of the interval, so `from_string(text, precision=x.precision())` contains `x` and is wider by at most one unit in the last place at each end. A bound with a binary exponent beyond 4096 in magnitude is written in hexadecimal (`0x3p1073741000`) because its decimal expansion needs a power of ten of that size; a hexadecimal bound is exact and is a valid literal. A zero bound is written `0`, without a sign, whatever sign it has: the literal of $[0, 0]$ does not carry the signs that `lower_bound` and `upper_bound` return ($-0$ and $+0$, 12.12.8), and 13.3 does not ask for them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2100
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015 13.4 `intervalToExact` and `exactToInterval`: a text that names the interval itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2110
msgid "The text is `[empty]`, `[entire]` or `[l, u]` with the bounds written as hexadecimal numbers (`0x1fffffp-3`; `inf` and `-inf` for an infinite bound), and for a decorated interval `_` and the decoration (`[nai]` for NaI). The bounds are the stored bounds, so `from_exact_string(x.to_exact_string(), precision=x.precision())` is `identical` to `x` (no signed zero is written, 13.4). `from_exact_string` reads the text exactly and never widens it. The bounds of the text must be numbers of the format of `precision` bits, as every stored bound is: a bound with more than `precision` significant bits (`0x1.00000000000001p0` has 57 bits, with any number of trailing zero digits, and is read at `precision=57` and refused at 53), a bound beyond the exponent range of the library, or finer than the grid of `precision` bits below the range floor is refused with a parse error that names the reason, and so is a decimal or malformed bound and a lower bound above the upper bound. Which bounds are representable at all is decided by the converter of IEEE 754-2019 5.12.3 (`Binary::from_hex_ctx` reports `inexact`), not by counting digits of the text, so a fractional mantissa (`0x1.8p0`, `0x.8p1`), leading and trailing zero digits and upper case are read. Before 0.12 a wider bound was accepted and kept with the label of its own bit count, because the type could hold such ends; it cannot any more."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2138
msgid "The midpoint-radius view, `midpoint +/- radius`: the two functions of IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8 ([`Interval::midpoint` and `Interval::radius`](#intervalmidpoint-and-intervalradius)), numbers of the format of the interval, written in the `Binary` notation (`3p-1` is $3 \\cdot 2^{-1}$). The radius is taken about the midpoint and covers both bounds, so the printed pair encloses the interval. Before 0.12 `Show` printed the view; since then it is for reading, not a literal (`from_string` does not read it), and its cost does not depend on the exponent range: an interval whose bounds have exponents a billion apart is written with numbers of $p$ bits. An interval with an infinite bound prints as `[l, u]` of binary numbers, Empty as `[empty]`; a decorated interval appends `_` and the decoration. Before the change of 0.12 the text printed the exact center and half-width, and a number of about 65,000 bits for bounds more than 65,536 places apart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2157
msgid "These are the `Eq` and `Debug` methods of the structures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2171
msgid "These are the derived `Eq` and `Debug` methods of the enums."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2182
msgid "The `Eq` implementations of `Interval` and `DecoratedInterval` are the `equal` relation of IEEE 1788-2015 (12.12.9), not equality of representation: `==` compares sets, ignoring precision tags, and on decorated intervals it is false whenever one operand is NaI, so NaI is not equal to itself (as NaN is not), and decorations are ignored. `identical` is the equality of the datum. `to_repr` gives the structural `Debug` form. The `Eq` of `Decoration` and `OverlapState` is the equality of the variants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2192
msgid "`Interval` implements the enclosure relation traits of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2202
msgid ""
"`Contains::contains(x, y)` is `y.subset(x)` (set inclusion, not the point-taking method). `Overlaps::overlaps(x, y)` is $\\underline{x} \\le \\overline{y} \\wedge \\underline{y} \\le \\overline{x}$, that is $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap \\boldsymbol{y} \\n"
"e \\emptyset$, and `MaybeEq::maybe_eq` is the same relation read as \"the two unknown points may be equal\". `DefinitelyLt::definitely_lt(x, y)` is $\\overline{x} < \\underline{y}$ and `DefinitelyLe::definitely_le(x, y)` is $\\overline{x} \\le \\underline{y}$: they hold when the order holds for every pair of points. All four are false when an operand is Empty. This differs from `disjoint`, `precedes` and `strict_precedes`, which are true for an Empty operand (IEEE 1788-2015 Table 10.4): `overlaps` is false where `disjoint` is true, and `definitely_lt` is false where `strict_precedes` is true, so a `true` answer of the traits is always backed by at least one pair of points. These are the generic interface of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`; the interval package spells the standard relations `disjoint`, `precedes` and `strict_precedes`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2220
msgid "`Interval` implements `DivChecked`, `PowNatChecked` and `PowIntChecked`; their methods are promoted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval.md:2232
msgid "Only `ctx.precision` is used: the operands are re-rounded to it with `with_precision`, the operation is applied, and the result is re-rounded outward (see [`Interval::with_precision`](#intervalwith_precision)). At the operands' own precision the result is that of the plain operation; at a lower precision it can be wider. They always return `Ok`. `div_checked` follows the division rules above (a divisor containing 0 gives an unbounded result, not an error). `pow_int_checked` uses `pown`. `pow_nat_checked` uses binary powering by repeated interval multiplication, which treats the factors as independent: for an argument containing 0 its result is wider than `pown`. An Empty base gives Empty for every exponent, including 0, as `pown` does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval_checked.md:1
msgid "interval_checked API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/interval_checked.md:5
msgid "`interval_checked` provides `IntervalChecked`, a closed wrapper around `Result[Interval, ArithmeticError]` for interval computations. Construction validates its inputs, every operation passes on the first error, and the interval operations of [`interval`](interval.md) are applied when all operands are successes. A valid but uninformative enclosure (the empty set, the whole line) is a success, not an error. The wrapper stores no decorations and no `IntervalFlags`. The [tutorial](../tutorial/interval_checked.md) builds pipelines; the [design page](../design/interval_checked.md) explains which outcomes are errors and why."
msgstr ""

