#: manual/conformance/binary.md:1
msgid "binary conformance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:3
msgid "This page records the binary floating-point semantic and test boundary of the current source (the `Unreleased` section of the changelog). It records finite results for named corpora, inputs, and contexts, together with the cases they do not cover."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:7
msgid "Normative and research sources"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:9
msgid "IEEE, *IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic*, IEEE 754-2019: interchange formats, rounding-direction attributes, NaNs, infinities, signed zeros, exceptions, and tininess detection."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:12
msgid "S. Fousse, G. Hanrot, V. Lefèvre, P. Pélissier, and P. Zimmermann, [MPFR: A Multiple-Precision Binary Floating-Point Library with Correct Rounding](https://doi.org/10.1145/1236463.1236468), ACM TOMS 33(2), 2007. MPFR supplies the exact-result-then-one-rounding model used for arbitrary precisions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:16
msgid "John Hauser's Berkeley [SoftFloat](https://www.jhauser.us/arithmetic/SoftFloat.html) and [TestFloat](https://www.jhauser.us/arithmetic/TestFloat.html), release 3e, provide independently generated IEEE result and flag vectors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:20
msgid "Mathematical model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:22
msgid "A finite, nonzero `Binary` denotes the dyadic real"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:26
msgid "where $s$ is 1 for a negative value and 0 otherwise, $c$ is the non-negative `coefficient` represented by `BinCoeff`, and $q$ is its `exponent2`. The finite coefficient is normalized by removing factors of two. The sign of zero remains observable. Infinity, qNaN, sNaN, NaN payload, and NaN sign are explicit states rather than sentinel finite values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:32
msgid "For `add_ctx`, `sub_ctx`, `mul_ctx`, `div_ctx`, `sqrt_ctx`, and `pown_ctx`, the contextual operations follow this sequence:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:35
msgid "Resolve IEEE special cases before finite arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:36
msgid "Compute the exact finite result. Addition, subtraction, and multiplication use exact dyadic arithmetic; division uses an exact integer quotient/remainder decision; square root uses an exact integer square root and an exact comparison with the rounding midpoint. Integer powers certify a directed enclosure with a Ziv loop and use the exact power if certification does not succeed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:41
msgid "Round the exact result once, in the requested direction, directly to the coarser of the precision grid and the subnormal grid of the exponent range. This avoids double rounding; overflow is decided from the rounded value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:44
msgid "Derive `BinaryFlags` from the mathematical result: inexact, underflow, overflow, division-by-zero, and invalid-operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:47
msgid "This order matters. For example, binary16 `0x0400 * 0x3BFF` produces `0x0400` with `inexact | underflow`. After-rounding tininess is determined from the precision-rounded unbounded value, not only from the final normal interchange encoding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:51
msgid "No operation branches on test identifiers, test values, or corpus format. The corpus interpreter is an adapter around the public contextual operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:54
msgid "Interchange and context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:56
msgid "`BinaryInterchange` decodes and encodes IEEE binary16, binary32, binary64, and binary128 bit patterns. `BinaryContext` carries precision, rounding direction, paired exponent limits, and before- or after-rounding tininess detection. Encoding and contextual arithmetic return status flags. Ordinary operators use a nearest-even context determined by their precision alone and intentionally do not expose those flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:62
msgid "In the TestFloat adapter, results are compared by class only when the expected result is a NaN. Non-NaN encoded bits and all exception bits must match exactly. The class comparison reflects IEEE's permitted choice of a newly generated NaN payload. The implementation itself preserves the selected input NaN's sign and payload and quiets signaling NaNs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:67
#: manual/conformance/decimal.md:39
#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:11
#: manual/conformance/interval.md:7
msgid "Clause traceability"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:69
msgid "This table maps IEEE 754-2019 clauses, cited by bare number, to the `binary` API and to the evidence that supports each status. A row is `conforming` when the operation exists and the cited evidence exercises it. It is `partial` when part of the clause is missing or the evidence is thin (the cell says which); `not implemented` and `not applicable` otherwise. The `Level` column is `required` for clauses stated with “shall”, `recommended` for those stated with “should” and for the optional parts, and `optional` for features a flavor may leave out. Evidence without a corpus name is a unit test in the repository; “no external corpus” marks operations that no independent oracle checks."
msgstr ""

