msgid "convertFormat evidence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:110
msgid "TestFloat covers `f16`, `f32`, `f64`, and `f128` converted to every other format: 52,632 level-1 vectors with five rounding directions and both tininess rules for narrowing pairs. Package tests are `library/src/binary/convert_format_wbtest.mbt` and `library/src/binary/ieee754_wbtest.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:115
msgid "Comparison-predicate evidence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:117
msgid "The API comprises `compare_quiet`, `compare_signaling`, `compare_quiet_equal`, `compare_signaling_equal`, `compare_quiet_less`, `compare_signaling_less`, `compare_quiet_less_equal`, `compare_signaling_less_equal`, `compare_quiet_unordered`, and the fifteen predicates in `order_predicates.mbt`, 22 in all, as listed in Tables 5.1 and 5.2. `library/src/binary/order_predicates_wbtest.mbt` tests every predicate against every relation, value, and flags. The TestFloat gate covers eq, le, lt, eq_signaling, le_quiet, and lt_quiet; `library/src/binary/order_wbtest.mbt` provides additional package tests."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:125
msgid "Class-predicate evidence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:127
msgid "`library/src/binary/class_of_wbtest.mbt` tests all ten classes in binary16, binary32, binary64, and binary128, including the smallest and largest subnormal and normal values in both signs. `library/src/binary/contextual_traits_wbtest.mbt` covers contextual traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:131
msgid "Hexadecimal-conversion evidence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:133
msgid "`library/src/binary/hex_grammar_wbtest.mbt` tests every production of the IEEE 754-2019 5.12.3 grammar and the 5.12.1 words, accepted and rejected, through both entry points, and checks a 4,000-value round trip. The `from_hex_ctx` tests contain 5,095 rows across all five rounding directions, both tininess rules, binary16, binary32, binary64, binary128, two toy formats, and the implementation range. They cover midpoints, subnormals, and the overflow threshold against exact rational arithmetic in `library/src/binary/hex_ctx_wbtest.mbt` and `hex_ctx_rows_wbtest.mbt`; `tools/generate_hex_ctx_rows.py` generates the rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:141
msgid "Mathematical-function evidence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:143
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 9.2 functions covered are `exp`, `exp2`, `exp10`, `expm1`, `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `ln`, `log2`, `log10`, `log1p`, `log2p1`, `log10p1`, `hypot`, `rsqrt`, `rootn`, `pown`, `compound`, `pow`, `powr`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `sinpi`, `cospi`, `tanpi`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `asinpi`, `acospi`, `atanpi`, `atan2pi`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `tanh`, `asinh`, `acosh`, and `atanh`, through their `*_ctx` and `try_*_ctx` forms. The MPFR elementary fixture (1,740 rows) is the corpus evidence. The package tests include `library/src/binary/elementary_certified_wbtest.mbt` and `library/src/binary/tanh_saturation_issue185_wbtest.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:152
msgid "Tests of range-limit decisions cover exact subnormal powers of two for `exp2` and `pow`, `pown` tininess, the `expm1` overflow window, and all elementary exponentials against an independent reference. The tests are `library/src/binary/exp2_subnormal_wbtest.mbt`, `pow_exact_subnormal_wbtest.mbt`, `pown_subnormal_tininess_wbtest.mbt`, `expm1_overflow_window_wbtest.mbt`, and `underflow_siblings_wbtest.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/binary.md:158
msgid "The `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `log2p1`, and `log10p1` tests cover special values of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1, exact cases, and thresholds in `library/src/binary/pm1_functions_wbtest.mbt`. The `rsqrt`, `compound`, and `powr` tests cover special values of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1, exact cases, the tiny-argument rule, and agreement with `pow` in `library/src/binary/rsqrt_compound_powr_wbtest.mbt`. The `asinpi`, `acospi`, `atanpi`, and `atan2pi` tests cover special values and exact constants of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1; anchors at ±1/2 and ±1 for huge, tiny, and extreme-ratio arguments; tiny arguments at the implementation subnormal limit; and agreement with the result at p + 150. They are in `library/src/binary/pi_inverse_wbtest.mbt`. The corresponding range-boundary sweep uses six contexts. It covers underflow thresholds, the anchors ±1/2 and ±1 for huge and tiny arguments and extreme ratios, and inputs next to ±1. `atan2pi` rows whose operands differ by more than 100,000 binades are not oracle rows (MPFR is too slow). Seeded random and targeted boundary campaigns are listed in the corpus table."
msgstr ""

