#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:40
msgid "In a subset context (`extended=false`) the result of every operation of `decimal_gda` that takes a context obeys the three result rules of GDA 1.70, page 55 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset), which the page states for any operation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:42
msgid "a result whose coefficient is zero has sign 0 and, unless the operation is quantize, exponent 0;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:43
msgid "a subnormal result is not permitted: it is an Underflow, and the result is the zero of the subset, with `Underflow`, `Subnormal`, `Inexact` and `Rounded` as `multiply` has always given (an operation that raised `Clamped` before keeps it: `exp` raises it as well); `next_plus` and `next_minus` are the exception, they set no flag for a numeric operand (page 34) and give `0` without flags ([#770](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/770)), while `next_toward` raises the four flags, as the sum with an infinitesimal of page 35 does;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:44
msgid "after the rounding of its result (unless the operation is quantize), a positive exponent is reduced to 0 when the coefficient then has at most precision digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:46
msgid "The library applies the rules in one step, the last of every operation in a subset context ([#754](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/754)); an extended context does not reach it. `add` and `subtract` obeyed the zero and the exponent rule before; a subnormal result is now refused for them too (`add(1E-9, -1E-99)` at precision 3, round-down, Emin -9 was `9.9E-10` and is `0`). The copy operations (`copy`, `copy_abs`, `copy_negate`, `copy_sign`) take no context and are outside the rules: `copy_negate(0)` is `-0`. The one exception is `quantize`, and with it `rescale`, the former name of the operation (GDA 1.70, page 37 (quantize), note 2): they keep the exponent of a zero and a positive exponent, and a zero is still of sign 0. Appendix A does not name `reduce`, `rescale`, `scaleb`, `shift`, `rotate`, `remainder`, `remainder_near`, the two round-to-integral operations or `next_toward`; the rules say \"after any operation\", so they apply to these operations too. The to-number conversion and the parsing keep a positive exponent, as page 55 states the rule for operations; a zero they read is `+0` with exponent 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:48
msgid "`reduce`, `round_to_integral_value` and `sqrt` obey the rules too (`reduce(1200)` is `1200`, `round_to_integral_value(1.2E+3)` is `1200`, `sqrt(1E+2)` is `10`), and so does `round_to_integral_exact`. 64 rows of the pinned subset corpus (`dectest0`) expect decNumber's result for them and are recorded as deviations ([Deviations of the corpus](#deviations-of-the-corpus)). The corpus has no row in a subset context for `scaleb`, `shift`, `rotate`, `next_toward` or `round_to_integral_exact`: their results rest on the text of page 55 alone."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:50
msgid "Every operation that takes a context also rounds an operand longer than the precision first and raises `LostDigits` when non-zero digits go (GDA 1.70, page 56 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset), \"Operation differences\", first item; [#759](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/759)). `apply`, `reduce`, the two round-to-integral operations, `next_plus`, `next_minus`, `next_toward`, `scaleb`, `shift` and `rotate` did not before: `next_toward(1234567891, 0)` at precision 9 was `1.23456789E+9` and is `1.23456788E+9`, with `LostDigits`, as in decNumber 3.68; `shift(1234567891, 0)` was `1234567891` and is `1.23456789E+9`. decNumber 3.68 does not round the operand of `shift` and `rotate`, and for `next_toward` it gives other results when the operand or the target is longer than the precision (40 rows of the sampled matrix of the decNumber comparison, recorded in the tests; the library follows the text). Where a second operand exists it is rounded too, as it is for `add`. `compare_total` and `compare_total_magnitude` round their operands like `compare`; the second drops the sign first, as page 43 defines it through copy-abs, so in `Floor` and `Ceiling` `compare_total_magnitude(-12, 1E+1)` at precision 1 rounds 12 and not -12 ([#763](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/763)). The logical operations, `logb` and the classifications are not arithmetic on the value and keep their operands."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:52
msgid "What Appendix A defines differently is not changed: the rounding of the operands first, the trailing zeros that `divide` and `power` remove, and the power rules of pages 56 and 57."
msgstr ""

