#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:69
msgid "In a subset context the second operand of `quantize` is rounded to precision digits before use, like every operand (GDA 1.70, page 56 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset), \"Operation differences\", first item), and the exponent of the quantum is the exponent of the rounded operand: `quantize(0, 12)` at precision 1 is `0E+1`, and `quantize(0, 96)` is `0E+2`, because 96 rounds to `1E+2` ([#764](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/764)). The second operand of `rescale`, the former name of `quantize` (page 37, note 2), is an operand with a coefficient too: it is rounded first, and the rounded integer is the power of ten of the quantum, so `rescale(0, 12)` at precision 1 is `0E+10`. The first operand is rounded by the rule above, and an extended context uses the second operand as it stands (page 36). decNumber 3.68 (`DECSUBSET=1`) takes the quantum from the unrounded operand and gives `0` and `0E+12`. In a comparison with decNumber 3.68 over 127,296 calls of the two operations the library gives the same value and flags in all 63,648 extended calls, and in a subset context the rule changes the result in 7,908 of the 63,648 calls, and every changed result is the one decNumber 3.68 gives for the rounded operand; the 12 rows of the sampled matrix of the comparison that differ by it are recorded in the tests. The corpus has no row for `quantize` in this case; its 3 rows for `rescale` are recorded as deviations ([Deviations of the corpus](#deviations-of-the-corpus))."
