#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:122
msgid "Deviations of the corpus"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:124
msgid "The pinned corpora are decNumber's test suite. A row is recorded in `testdata/decimal_gda/deviations.tsv` when its result token differs from the value this library returns. There are three kinds of row. The first kind rests on the tolerance of the mathematical functions: decNumber's mathematical functions use the tolerance the specification gives: of `exp`, `ln` and `log10` the specification says that an inexact result \"will be rounded using the round-half-even algorithm\" and \"should be correctly rounded, but may be up to 1 ulp ... in error\" (GDA 1.70, pages 30 and 31). The library returns the correctly rounded value in both arithmetics. Where a row of the corpus holds the other value, one unit in the last place away and inside the tolerance, the row is recorded with the corpus value, the held value, the value of decNumber 3.68 and what the held value rests on: the corpus value is not wrong, the row is recorded because the two texts differ. The stage judges the row against the held value, reports it as the verdict `deviation`, and fails it if the result token of the row stops being the recorded corpus value. Nothing is reported upstream. There is one row of this kind: `official0/ln0.decTest` row `ln116` (`ln 0.99999991000000405` at 17 digits, subset arithmetic) expects `-8.9999999999999879E-8`, decNumber's value, where the correctly rounded result is `-8.9999999999999878E-8` (the exact value is `-8.99999999999998784999918E-8`). The extended row `official/ln.decTest` `lnx116` lists the correctly rounded value, and decNumber itself returns the other one for it, as it does for `lnx732`. Every other row of `exp`, `ln`, `log10` and `squareroot` of the two corpora (4,816 and 3,652 rows of the supported operations) equals the correctly rounded result of CPython `decimal` in its own context, apart from rows that depend on the exponent range of CPython or on the subset rules that CPython does not have."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:149
msgid "The second kind of row, 64 rows, follows from rule P of page 55 (GDA 1.70, page 55 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset)): in a subset context a positive exponent is reduced to 0 when the coefficient then has at most precision digits, after any operation. The corpus holds decNumber's results for `reduce`, `tointegral` and `squareroot`, which keep the exponent, and 64 rows of `official0` differ from the library by that rule alone, with the same number and the same flags: 47 of `reduce0.decTest` (`red060` to `red144`), 9 of `squareroot0.decTest` (`sqtx2207` to `sqtx2847`) and 8 of `tointegral0.decTest` (`int067` to `int090`). `reduce 10.0` expects `1E+1` and the library gives `10`. Each is recorded in `testdata/decimal_gda/deviations.tsv` with the corpus value, the held value, the value of decNumber 3.68 (equal to the corpus value) and the decision that the library follows the text of page 55 for every operation; the corpus value is not wrong, and nothing is reported upstream."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:151
msgid "The third kind, 3 rows (`res397` to `res399` of `rescale0.decTest`), follows from the rounding of the second operand of `rescale` (GDA 1.70, page 56 (Appendix A, The X3.274 subset), first item of \"Operation differences\"): `rescale 222 +2.000000001` at precision 9 has the second operand `2.00000000` after rounding, an integer, and the library gives `2E+2` with `Inexact`, `Lost_digits` and `Rounded`; the corpus value is decNumber's, which refuses the unrounded operand as not being an integer (`Invalid_operation`, no value). The row is recorded with the held value `2E+2` and, in the eighth column of `deviations.tsv`, the held flags, which the stage judges instead of the flags of the row. The corpus value is not wrong, and nothing is reported upstream."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:153
msgid "Known deviations outside the corpus"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:155
msgid "A passing corpus is a finite claim. Review of the implementation found these behaviours, none of which has a pinned row:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:158
msgid "Integer `power` is not correctly rounded, as the specification allows. In the nearest rounding modes the result is within about 0.55 units in the last place of the exact value (0.6 for negative exponents). In the directed modes it is within one unit in the last place of the correctly rounded result, and up to 1.03 units from the exact value (measured on 8,320 random cases in both arithmetics against Python `decimal`; no case at two or more units)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:164
msgid "An exponent of `power` with more than nine digits is not computed: the result is NaN with Invalid operation, the limit of the decNumber design (the specification states none). Python `decimal` (libmpdec) and the IEEE `decimal` package compute these powers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:168
msgid "In the directed rounding modes an inexact `power` can differ from libmpdec in the last digit, because the specification allows an inexact `power` to be up to one unit in the last place from the correctly rounded result and libmpdec is not correctly rounded there either."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:172
msgid "`power` of a zero base and a negative exponent is an infinity with no condition raised, as in libmpdec: the Division by zero condition of the specification applies when \"the dividend was not zero\". The IEEE `decimal` package signals divideByZero for it (IEEE 754-2019 §9.2.1)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:177
msgid "Isolation and native benchmark"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:179
msgid "The dependency scan `tools/check_imports.py` requires `decimal_gda` to contain no import of the IEEE `decimal`, and `decimal` none of `decimal_gda`; `decimal_gda_checked` and `frontend/gda_expr` import `decimal_gda` and no IEEE `decimal`, which no scan enforces. IEEE tests and its independent conformance corpus are run separately."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:185
msgid "The quick benchmark builds only the current engine in an isolated snapshot and runs three native samples per cell. It reports arithmetic, parser, context, and elementary timing observations without treating performance as a conformance requirement or comparing against a historical adapter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/decimal_gda.md:199
msgid "See [the decimal_gda data guide](../../../testdata/decimal_gda/README.md) for manifests, filters, phases, JSON output, and failure triage."
msgstr ""

