#: manual/design/bench.md:236
msgid "**Discarding outliers before averaging.** Any fence needs a tuning constant and silently changes the sample; the median makes it unnecessary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:238
msgid "**Unpaired comparison of two sample sets.** Drift between the measurement of $B$ and $C$ would enter the difference directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:240
msgid "**Normal-theory intervals.** They assume symmetric errors that timing data does not have."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:245
msgid "No timing, process control or file output: Maremark's runner and `tools/benchmark.py` do that."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:247
msgid "No absolute performance targets; thresholds apply to relative differences."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:248
msgid "Performance results never change correctness claims, and the benchmarks run only on the native target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:1
msgid "bench/binary design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:5
msgid "Measure the cost of each layer of the binary stack separately, on inputs where all layers must produce the same result, so that a change in one layer shows up as a change in one reported percentage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:11
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:11
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:11
msgid "For one dataset let $k_j$, $c_j$ and $f_j$ be the per-call times of the kernel, core and checked paths in block $j$ (a case without a kernel path reports only the second quantity). The reported quantities are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:21
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:21
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:21
msgid ""
"the relative median paired overheads of one layer over the layer below. The median of the differences is not the difference of the medians in general ($\\operatorname{med}(c - k) \\n"
"e \\operatorname{med}(c) - \\operatorname{med}(k)$), so `core_pct` is not $100\\,(\\operatorname{med} c / \\operatorname{med} k - 1)$; it is the quantity whose uncertainty the paired bootstrap describes. The estimators, the pairing and the bootstrap interval are derived in the [bench design](../bench.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:31
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:31
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:28
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:31
msgid "Workloads"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:38
msgid "Arithmetic inputs are fixed bit patterns with the top and bottom bits set; the precision is the sum of the operand bit lengths plus 4, and for `div` the dividend is the product of the two patterns, so every result is exact and every path computes the same number. Elementary inputs are 1.25, 0.5 and 0.25 at the dataset precision, each inside the domain of the functions it is used with."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:40
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:39
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:36
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:39
msgid "Correctness oracle"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:42
msgid "Arithmetic outputs are checked against the exact `BigInt` result, and elementary outputs against the core result (so the checked path must agree with the core). An output that disagrees with the oracle is counted as a failure and the performance test asserts that there is none, so a comparison is never between paths that compute different things."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:46
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:45
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:42
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:45
msgid "Exact inputs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:48
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:47
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:44
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:47
msgid "Exact results make the arithmetic work identical across paths: no path can win by rounding earlier, and the measured difference is the cost of representation, context handling and checking."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:54
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:53
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:50
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:53
msgid "Plan tests compile every specification in ordinary test runs, so the benchmarks cannot rot unnoticed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:56
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:55
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:52
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:55
msgid "Performance tests assert `failed_count == 0`: every output matched its oracle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:58
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:57
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:54
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:57
msgid "Seeds are fixed (`20260715`), so the measurement order and the bootstrap resamples are reproducible."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:63
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:62
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:59
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:62
msgid "**Random inputs per sample.** They would mix input variance into timing variance; fixed patterns per dataset isolate the cost of the code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:65
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:64
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:61
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:64
msgid "**Only end-to-end timings.** A single number cannot tell whether a regression is in the kernel, the core or the checked wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:70
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:69
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:66
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:69
msgid "Native target only; no claims for other targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:71
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:70
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:67
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:70
msgid "No public API; the package exists for `tools/benchmark.py`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/binary.md:72
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:71
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:68
#: manual/design/bench/interval.md:71
msgid "Measurements are evidence for the performance pages, never for correctness."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:1
msgid "bench/decimal design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:5
msgid "Measure the cost of each layer of the IEEE decimal stack separately, on inputs where all layers must produce the same result, so that a change in one layer shows up as a change in one reported percentage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:37
msgid "Inputs are fixed digit patterns; the context precision is the sum of the operand digit counts plus 4, and for `div` the dividend is the product of the two patterns, so every result is an exact integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:41
msgid "Outputs are checked against the exact `BigInt` result (numerically, with `compare`). An output that disagrees with the oracle is counted as a failure and the performance test asserts that there is none, so a comparison is never between paths that compute different things."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:1
msgid "bench/decimal_gda design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:5
msgid "Measure the cost of each layer of the GDA decimal stack separately, on inputs where all layers must produce the same result, so that a change in one layer shows up as a change in one reported percentage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:11
msgid "For one dataset let $c_j$ and $f_j$ be the per-call times of the core and checked paths in block $j$. This suite has no kernel path, so it reports one quantity,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:19
msgid "the relative median paired overhead of the checked wrapper over the core functions. The median of the differences is not the difference of the medians in general, so `full_pct` is not $100\\,(\\operatorname{med} f / \\operatorname{med} c - 1)$; it is the quantity whose uncertainty the paired bootstrap describes. The estimators, the pairing and the bootstrap interval are derived in the [bench design](../bench.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:34
msgid "The left operand is a fixed digit pattern of the dataset length, the right operand 2 and the addend 3; the context has precision $\\max(9, 2d + 4)$ for $d$ digits and exponent limits $\\pm 999999$, so no operation rounds or traps. `parse` reads the pattern text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:38
msgid "Outputs are checked against the core result with structural equality, so the checked path must return the identical decimal, exponent included. An output that disagrees with the oracle is counted as a failure and the performance test asserts that there is none, so a comparison is never between paths that compute different things."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:2
msgid "bench/external design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:6
msgid "Put a number on how far `floating` is from the established C libraries, on the same machine, the same operands and the same measurement protocol, so that a statement such as \"N times slower than MPFR at 512 bits\" can be checked and tracked from release to release. Benchmarks that compare `floating` with its own earlier versions cannot say that."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:14
msgid "Let $t_F$ and $t_R$ be the time per operation of `floating` and of the reference library for one case (operation and precision). The number the project is judged by is the ratio of the *net* times, which are the times of the operation without the cost of the benchmark loop around it. The method is fixed in advance and versioned (`net-ratio-v1`); a change of any number below is a new method version."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:21
msgid "**Pairs.** Each side is timed in pairs of blocks, in one process: a block of the case, $x_{r,j}$, and a block of its null, $y_{r,j}$ (the same loop without the library call, see below), both in ns per operation, each with its own calibrated batch of about 20 ms, run back to back with the order inside the pair alternating. Here $r$ is the round and $j$ the confirmatory pair of the round ($R = 7$ rounds of $B = 12$ pairs, after 3 exploratory pairs per round that are discarded by position). The difference of a pair is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:33
msgid "The two blocks of a pair are 40 ms apart, so a drift of the machine that is slower than that cancels in $d$; it does not cancel in $x$ or $y$ alone."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:36
msgid "**Net time and its interval.** The net time of a side is the median of all its differences,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:43
msgid ""
"Blocks of one round come from one process and share memory layout, frequency state and system noise, so they are not independent. The interval of $\\n"
"u$ is a two-level percentile bootstrap that keeps the pairs intact: a replicate draws $R$ rounds with replacement, then draws the $B$ pairs of each chosen round with replacement, and $\\n"
"u^*$ is the median of the $RB$ drawn differences; the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles of 2000 seeded replicates are the interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:50
msgid ""
"**Ratio.** $\\hat\\rho = \\n"
"u_F / \\n"
"u_R$. The two sides run in different processes and share no structure, so their replicates are drawn independently, $\\rho^*_b = \\n"
"u^*_{F,b} / \\n"
"u^*_{R,b}$ (with $\\rho^*_b = +\\infty$ when $\\n"
"u^*_{R,b} \\le 0$). Replicate $b$ of one side is divided by replicate $b$ of the other only to form the distribution of the ratio; it is not a pairing. The seed of every draw is derived from the run seed, the case, the side and the purpose, so two sides never share a stream, even when their data are identical. The interval $[\\rho_{lo}, \\rho_{hi}]$ is the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles of the $\\rho^*_b$. The raw ratio of the 0.11.0 method, the median of the case blocks over the median of the case blocks, is kept beside it, with its interval from a bootstrap that pairs the rounds of the two sides."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:62
msgid "**Criterion.** A case meets the target when $\\rho_{hi} \\le 3$. This is a one-sided 97.5% statement made from a two-sided 95% interval, so it is conservative. A resolved case that does not meet it is marked borderline when $\\rho_{lo} \\le 3 < \\rho_{hi}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:67
msgid ""
"**Unresolved.** A case is reported as unresolved, and never as met, when (1) the 2.5% quantile of $\\n"
"u^*_R$ is at most zero, or $\\n"
"u_R \\le 0$ (the reference net is not distinguishable from zero), (2) $\\n"
"u_F \\le 0$, or (3) $\\rho_{hi} / \\rho_{lo} > 1.25$ (the interval is too wide; in the 0.11.0 summary the raw interval has $\\rho_{hi}/\\rho_{lo}$ of 1.02 at the median case and 1.07 at the worst). The raw ratio decides such a case and is printed as \"met (raw)\" or \"not met (raw)\"; it is never counted among the met cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:75
msgid ""
"**Flags.** The null loop scales with the clock, so the null blocks are a thermometer. With $n_r$ the median of the null blocks of round $r$, a side is flagged `d` (drift) when $\\max_r |n_r / \\operatorname{med}_r n_r - 1|$ exceeds 5%. A case is flagged `o` (order) when the mean of $d$ over the pairs where the null ran first differs from the mean over the pairs where it ran second by more than 5% of $\\n"
"u$. Flags are reported; they remove no data and change no verdict by themselves (a drift between rounds widens the interval, and an interval above the width bound makes the case unresolved)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:84
msgid "A raw cell is flagged as noisy when the half-width of its median interval exceeds 5% of the median or when the medians of the rounds spread (maximum minus minimum) by more than 10% of the pooled median, on either side."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:88
msgid "Nothing is removed from the data after it is measured: not by hand, not by a rule that looks at the result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:93
msgid "One operand file, read by both sides"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:95
msgid "A seeded generator (Python's `random.Random` seeded by a string, stable across versions) writes `operands-per-case = 8` operand sets for every case. Binary operands are exact `±0x<mantissa>p<exponent>` values with exactly the case precision in mantissa bits and the leading bit set; decimal operands are exact `±<coefficient>E<exponent>` values with exactly the case digit count. Parsing is therefore exact on both sides and needs no rounding mode, and both sides time the same values. Magnitudes are chosen so that no operation takes a special-case shortcut: the leading-bit exponent of `add`, `mul`, `div` and `sqrt` operands lies in $[-8, 8]$, `exp` and `sin` arguments have $|x| \\in [0.25, 4)$, `ln` arguments lie in $[2^{-3}, 2^4)$; for decimal the magnitudes are $[10^{-2}, 10^{3})$ for arithmetic, $[0.1, 10)$ for `exp` and $[0.1, 10^3)$ for `ln` and `sqrt`. Every operand is a different random mantissa, so no result repeats and nothing can be answered from a cache of results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:110
msgid "Oracle: the reference result of the same operands"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:112
msgid "The C harness prints the correctly rounded MPFR (round to nearest even) and libmpdec (`HalfEven`, `allcr = 1`) results for every operand to a reference file. The `floating` benchmark reads that file as its oracle, so Maremark validates every `floating` output against the reference library before it is timed. A mismatch is reported (it would be a correctness finding) and the case is still timed, so the table never silently compares different results. The elementary functions of both libraries are correctly rounded, so for them, too, equal precision implies equal results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:121
msgid "Same protocol on both sides"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:123
msgid "The C harness implements the Maremark protocol literally: warm-up passes until both a count and a time are reached, the calibration rule that grows the batch towards a target batch time, then exploratory and confirmatory blocks of a fixed number of passes. The numbers (warm-up, batch target, block counts) are passed to both sides from one place. Only confirmatory blocks enter the statistics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:130
msgid "Alternating sides, shuffled cases"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:132
msgid "Each round runs the C harness and the `floating` test as two processes, the first side alternating from round to round (seeded AB/BA), with one shuffled case order per round shared by both sides, so a case occupies different times of the run in different rounds. Seven rounds give each case seven process-level measurements per side to bootstrap over; between-process variation (core placement, allocator layout, other load) is larger than the variation between blocks of one process, and the two-level bootstrap accounts for it. Before each side starts the runner waits until other processes use under 150% CPU and records the load it saw."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:142
msgid "What is not in the timed region"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:144
msgid "Operand parsing, context and `mpfr_t` / `mpd_t` initialisation, result buffers (MPFR and libmpdec write into preallocated results; `floating` writes into a preallocated array of results), and printing are outside. What stays inside is the cost of the library's own API: for MPFR and libmpdec the call and any internal temporaries, for `floating` the construction of an immutable result value, which is how the library is used. That difference is inherent in the two designs and is part of what the ratio reports."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:152
msgid "The null of a case: the same loop without the library call"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:154
msgid "The null removes everything the library needs to do and nothing else, so that the net time is what the library call costs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:157
msgid "**`floating`.** A second implementation of the same case, run by Maremark next to the operation (the blocks of the implementations are interleaved, each implementation has its own calibration). Its closure has the shape of the operation closures, an indirect call with the same arguments returning its first one, and it loads from `expected` instead of the operands so that its output validates against the reference. It keeps the closure call, the array loads, the reference-counted store of a result and Maremark's per-pass work. It removes the library call and the allocation, construction and release of a result. The operation of a case is a closure chosen at run time by the operation name, and the generated C calls it through the closure object (`f->code(f, a, b)`, checked on the release build). A top-level function with a known body would be called directly and could be inlined, which would make the null cheaper than the harness part of the case and the net larger, so the null closure is held in a `Ref` and is called through the closure object the same way (checked in the generated C as well)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:172
msgid "**MPFR and libmpdec.** `pass_null` runs the loop of the case with the same operand and result accesses (the three operand pointers of every row, and the context and the status of the decimal calls) handed to an empty `asm` statement, so the compiler can neither drop the accesses nor fuse the loop. For binary the null computes the three row addresses (`a[i]`, `b[i]` and `r[i]` are addresses into arrays of `mpfr_t`); for decimal it loads three pointers from the pointer arrays, and takes the address of the context and of the status. That is exactly what `pass` does before the call."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:181
msgid "What the null cannot remove, and what the net therefore still contains:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:183
msgid "The call itself: argument set-up and return of `mpfr_add` are in the net of the reference; the direct call into the library is in the net of `floating`, while the closure that reaches it is in the null. The closure belongs to the harness, the library call to the library."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:187
msgid "Cache, branch-predictor and allocator state differ between a loop with and a loop without the call, so the net is an approximation of \"the operation alone\", not an identity. Its error is of the order of the variation of the null and is the same for every build of the library, so comparisons between builds are not affected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:192
msgid "Per-pass and per-block costs (the pair of clock reads, `Bench::keep`) are in both blocks and cancel in the difference; they are not removed from the individual times."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:195
msgid "The release of the previous result happens in the next call: in the null `out[i]` holds a shared value, in the case it frees an allocation. That cost is caused by the operation and stays in the net."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:199
msgid "A regression of the time on the number of operations per block was considered and not adopted: it removes per-pass costs but not the per-operation cost of the harness (the closure call, the array traffic, the reference loop), which is the largest part of the floor, so a null would still be needed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:204
msgid "Configurations of the floating side"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:206
msgid "The floating side is built and published in named configurations, recorded as `floating_configuration` in the protocol block of the summary and in the names of the published files: `native` (the native limb primitives where the build has them), `native-portable` (the portable path forced), and the runtimes `wasm` and `js`. The target of the baseline has to be reached by the portable path as well. The runner passes the name and the environment variables that select a configuration (`--configuration`, `--moon-env KEY=VALUE`); how the portable path is selected is defined by the kernel work, not by the benchmark. One summary, one samples file and one section of the manual page exist per configuration."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:216
msgid "Runtimes other than native"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:218
msgid "`--target native|wasm|js` chooses the `moon` target the floating side is built and run for (`wasm` runs under `moonrun`, `js` under node; the versions are recorded in `meta.runtime`). The reference side is always the native C process of MPFR and libmpdec, run in every round of the same run, so the number of a cell on another runtime is the library on that runtime against the native reference. That mixes two differences, the library and the runtime. The second ratio separates them: the net time of `floating` on the runtime over the net time of the same case in `native-portable`, which runs the same code path. It is computed at publishing time from the two samples files of the same release (they must have read the same operand file and used the same protocol; each has its own configuration in the seed of every draw, so the two resamplings are independent) and has an interval and no verdict: it is a diagnostic of the cost of the runtime. `wasm-gc` is not a target: the benchmark driver uses `moonbitlang/async`, which has no wasm-gc runtime (the module initialiser of that package casts between two struct types of the runtime's scheduler and the runtime rejects the module at validation; a reduced reproduction with that package alone shows it, and nothing is filed)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:236
msgid "**The criterion of a configuration** is one row of a table in the tool (`CRITERIA` in `tools/external_baseline.py`): which ratio is judged and two bounds, the target and the limit. Every configuration is judged on the net ratio to the native reference process (MPFR, libmpdec); for `wasm` and `js` that is the ratio of this library on the runtime to the native reference. The upper end of the 95% interval decides:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:243
msgid "configuration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:243
msgid "target"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:243
msgid "limit"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/external.md:243
msgid "met"
