#: manual/design/binary.md:137
#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:122
msgid "Rounding functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:139
msgid "For $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ let $x^- = \\max\\{y \\in F : y \\le x\\}$ and $x^+ = \\min\\{y \\in F : y \\ge x\\}$, extending $F$ by $\\pm\\infty$ beyond $\\pm\\Omega$ for the moment. The five rounding directions of `BinaryRoundingMode` are the maps $\\mathbb{R} \\to F \\cup \\{\\pm\\infty\\}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:153
msgid "Two properties of every $\\circ$ above carry most of the proofs on this page:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:159
msgid "(R1) holds because $x^- = x^+ = x$ on $F$. (R2) holds because each $\\circ(x)$ is one of the two neighbours of $x$ chosen by a rule that only moves from $x^-$ to $x^+$ as $x$ increases through a cell $[x^-, x^+]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:163
msgid "The standard error model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:165
msgid "Let $u = 2^{-p}$ be the unit roundoff. Take $x$ with $2^{t} \\le |x| < 2^{t+1}$, $t \\ge e_{\\min}$ (the normal range) and $|x|$ below the overflow threshold of the rounding direction (otherwise the result is an infinity and no relative bound holds). The points of $F$ in that binade are spaced $2^{t-p+1}$ apart, and $2^{t+1}$ belongs to $F$ or is the overflow point, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:179
msgid "where RN is RNE or RNA. Writing $\\circ(x) = x(1 + \\delta)$ gives the standard model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:186
msgid "The nearest bound sharpens to $|\\delta| \\le u/(1+u)$ by dividing by $|\\circ(x)|$ instead of $|x|$.[^higham] Below $2^{e_{\\min}}$ the spacing is the constant $\\eta$, so the error is absolute: $|\\operatorname{RN}(x) - x| \\le \\eta/2$ and $|\\operatorname{RD}(x) - x| < \\eta$. Both regimes together give the model with an underflow term,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:196
msgid "for nearest rounding ($2u$ and $\\eta$ for directed rounding). Addition and subtraction never need $\\epsilon$: if $a, b \\in F$ then both are integer multiples of $\\eta$, so is $a \\pm b$, and a multiple of $\\eta$ below $2^{e_{\\min}}$ lies in $F$. Hence a subnormal sum is exact, which is why gradual underflow keeps $a - b = 0 \\iff a = b$.[^kahan]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:202
msgid "N. J. Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd ed., SIAM 2002, §2.2; D. Goldberg, \"What every computer scientist should know about floating-point arithmetic\", *ACM Computing Surveys* 23(1), 1991."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:207
msgid "J.-M. Muller et al., *Handbook of Floating-Point Arithmetic*, 2nd ed., Birkhäuser 2018, §2.1 and §4.3."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:210
msgid "Correct rounding is a stronger property than the model: the result is the one point $\\circ(f(x))$, not merely some point within $u$. Every value `binary` returns is that point, so the model above holds for every operation, including the elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:217
msgid "Round once from exact data"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:219
msgid "**Problem.** A result must equal $\\circ(r)$ for the exact real $r$, but $r$ may need far more bits than $p$ (a product of two $p$-bit numbers has $2p$), or infinitely many (a quotient, a square root, $e^x$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:223
msgid "**Options.** Compute in a wider format and round again, as hardware without FMA does; or keep a fixed number of guard bits; or decide the rounding from exact information."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:227
msgid "**Choice.** Every operation computes an exact description of $r$ and calls one finalizer. For dyadic results (sum, difference, product, `fma`, `scaleb`, `remainder`, conversion) the description is the exact integer magnitude $m$ and exponent $e$ with $r = \\pm m 2^{e}$. The finalizer picks the shift"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:237
msgid "the larger of the precision shift and the shift onto the subnormal grid, and splits $m 2^{-\\sigma} = q + f$ with $q = \\lfloor m 2^{-\\sigma} \\rfloor$ and $0 \\le f < 1$ into three items of data:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:245
msgid "These are the classical round and sticky bits, read from the coefficient by `test_bit` and `ctz` without building any shifted copy. They determine every rounding direction: with $q_0$ the last bit of $q$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:249
msgid "Direction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:249
msgid "increment $q$ when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:251
msgid "RNE"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:252
msgid "RNA"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:253
msgid "RZ"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:253
#: manual/design/binary.md:2015
#: manual/design/binary.md:2017
#: manual/design/decimal.md:199
msgid "never"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:254
msgid "RU"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:255
msgid "RD"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:257
msgid ""
"*Derivation.* $f > \\frac12 \\iff g \\wedge t$, $f = \\frac12 \\iff g \\wedge \\n"
"eg t$, and $f > 0 \\iff g \\vee t$. RNE rounds the magnitude up when $f > \\frac12$, or when $f = \\frac12$ and $q$ is odd; that is $(g \\wedge t) \\vee (g \\wedge \\n"
"eg t \\wedge q_0) = g \\wedge (t \\vee q_0)$. The directed rows round the magnitude up exactly when $f > 0$ and the direction points away from zero for the sign $s$. Inexactness is $g \\vee t$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:264
msgid "**Why.** Because $\\sigma$ already includes the subnormal shift, a tiny result is rounded once, directly from $m$, to the grid $\\eta \\mathbb{Z}$. Rounding first to $p$ bits and then to the subnormal grid would be a double rounding: a value just above a midpoint of the coarse grid can be pushed onto that midpoint by the first rounding and then rounded the wrong way by the second. A carry out of $q$ (when $q + 1 = 2^p$) only raises $\\operatorname{top}$ by one; the result is re-normalized and the overflow test below is applied to the rounded value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:273
msgid "**The three paths of the finalizer, and why the normalization chain is skipped.** `binary_round_dyadic` takes one of three paths. (1) *Exact exit:* if $\\operatorname{bits}(m) \\le p$ and $e_{\\min} \\le \\operatorname{top} \\le e_{\\max}$ (the context range, clamped to the implementation range), then $r = m 2^{e}$ is representable: $e \\ge e_{\\min} - p + 1$, so the shift $\\sigma$ is $0$, nothing is rounded and no flag is raised; this holds for every context alike. (2) *Precision path:* if the shift onto the subnormal grid is not larger than the precision shift, the precision rounding already uses the final $\\sigma$, so its result is the final result and the rounding is done once; its top bit is also the \"rounded top\" that the tininess rule after rounding and the overflow test need. (3) *Range path:* otherwise the value is split again at the range shift, and the precision rounding is kept only to supply the rounded top. In all three paths the stored value is built by `binary_finite_in_range`: the coefficient has at most $p$ bits (a carry out of $q$ gives a power of two, which is even and is stripped to $1$), its top bit is at least $e_{\\min} - p + 1$ and at most $e_{\\max}$ (overflow is tested first), so the general normalization has nothing left to do except stripping trailing zeros: the implementation-range underflow, the re-rounding of excess bits and the overflow test of `normalize_finite` cannot fire. The tests keep copies of the old core and compare value and flags on random corpora and at the exponent limits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:275
msgid "Finishing a wide result in place"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:277
msgid "**Problem.** The plain `add`, `mul`, `div` and `sqrt` of operands wider than one word end in a rounding to nearest even. Done on the whole coefficient, it copied the kept bits into a new array, incremented it, and shifted it a second time to strip the trailing zero bits: three passes over the result and an array of the same size each time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:279
msgid "**Choice.** The fast path hands the finisher the magnitude as a limb array $W$ (little-endian, the top limbs may be zero), the exponent of bit 0, the precision, a sticky flag and the rounding direction. With $b = \\operatorname{bits}(W)$ and $\\sigma = b - p$, the finisher reads the round bit, the last kept bit and (only when the decision needs it, and stopping at the first non-zero limb) the lower bits, decides the increment with the table above, and finds the position $q$ of the lowest set bit of the rounded value without forming it. Let $K = \\lfloor W / 2^{\\sigma} \\rfloor$ be the kept bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:281
msgid "No increment: $q$ is the position of the lowest set bit of $W$ at or above $\\sigma$, and the coefficient is $W \\gg q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:282
msgid "Increment: $K + 1$ has as many trailing zeros as $K$ has trailing ones, say $t$, and $(K + 1) \\gg t = (K \\gg t) \\mid 1$ because bit $t$ of $K$ is zero. So $q$ is the position of the lowest zero bit of $W$ at or above $\\sigma$ (at most $b$, since bit $b$ is zero) and the coefficient is $(W \\gg q) \\mid 1$; when every kept bit is set, $q = b$ and the coefficient is $1$, which is $2^p$ stripped. No carry is ever propagated."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:284
msgid "The coefficient is then $W \\gg q$ written into the same array: the destination index never exceeds the source index, so one forward loop suffices, and the result keeps the array. The sticky flag stands for \"the exact value is $W$ plus an amount in $(0, 1)$ units of bit 0\". It is allowed only when $b > p$, and then it changes the decision only through $t$, because the discarded part $d + \\varepsilon$ with integer $d < 2^{\\sigma}$ and $0 < \\varepsilon < 1$ is below, at or above the half $2^{\\sigma - 1}$ exactly as $d$ is, except that $d = 2^{\\sigma - 1}$ is above it. This is what lets a caller truncate a quotient, a square root or an aligned sum at $p + 2$ bits and pass the rest as one bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:286
msgid "**Why.** The result array is the only allocation besides the value itself, and the rounding is one probe of a few limbs plus one shifting loop. The finisher takes the direction as a parameter so that the contextual operations can use it; the plain operators pass round to nearest even. The tests keep the previous finisher and the contextual rounding as references and compare value, sign, exponent and precision bit for bit in every direction, on ties, on all-ones carries and on cancelled leading limbs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:288
msgid "**Who owns the array.** The product (exact or short), the scaled and the short quotient and the packed square root of the coefficient kernel return a heap coefficient whose array nothing else refers to (the contract is stated in the kernel design page), so the fast paths of `mul`, `div` and `sqrt` hand it to the finisher as it is and it is rounded in place, with no copy. A coefficient of at most 128 bits has no array and takes the copying path; so does the rare division through a general quotient and remainder, whose array is not covered by the contract. The tests run each operation twice on the same operands, on a value with itself and on a copy of it, and check that no operand word changed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:290
msgid "Division from quotient and remainder"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:292
msgid "**Problem.** $a/b$ with $a = c_a 2^{e_a}$, $b = c_b 2^{e_b}$ is a rational $N/D \\cdot 2^{e}$ ($N = c_a$, $D = c_b$, $e = e_a - e_b$) whose binary expansion is usually infinite."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:296
msgid "**Choice.** First the exact leading exponent: with $k = \\operatorname{bits}(N) - \\operatorname{bits}(D)$, $\\lfloor \\log_2 (N/D) \\rfloor$ is $k$ if $N \\ge D 2^{k}$ and $k - 1$ otherwise, one integer comparison. That fixes the target exponent $\\tau = \\max(\\operatorname{top} - p + 1,\\ e_{\\min} - p + 1)$, and then one integer division"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:307
msgid "gives $q$ directly, and the remainder gives the rounding data: $f = r/D$, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:314
msgid "No approximation of $N/D$ is involved; the rounding is decided by the sign of $2r - D$. When $e - \\tau < 0$ the shift is moved to the denominator, and a quotient below one unit is decided by comparing $2N$ with $D 2^{\\tau - e}$ without forming it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:319
msgid "Square root from an integer root and a midpoint test"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:321
msgid "**Problem.** $\\sqrt{c 2^{e}}$ is irrational unless $c 2^{e}$ is a square."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:323
msgid "**Choice.** The leading exponent is $\\lfloor \\operatorname{top}(x)/2 \\rfloor$ (floor division), which fixes $\\tau$ as above. Write the radicand as $X = c\\, 2^{e - 2\\tau}$, so $\\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{X}\\, 2^{\\tau}$. An exact integer square root gives $s = \\lfloor \\sqrt{X} \\rfloor$ with remainder $X - s^2$; the root is exact iff the remainder is zero. Otherwise the rounding data come from the midpoint $s + \\frac12$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:334
msgid ""
"an exact comparison of integers (with the power of two moved to the other side when $X$ is fractional). So $g = [4X \\ge (2s+1)^2]$ and $t = \\n"
"eg\\text{exact} \\wedge [4X \\n"
"e (2s+1)^2]$. When $X$ is an integer the right side is odd and the left even, so the root is never exactly a midpoint; this is the classical fact that $\\sqrt{x}$ of a $p$-bit number is never a $(p+1)$-bit midpoint.[^muller-sqrt] The equality branch is still kept, because an operand with more bits than the context precision can make $\\sqrt{X}$ an exact midpoint (for example $\\sqrt{9/4} = 3/2$ rounded to one bit)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:344
msgid "Muller et al., *Handbook of Floating-Point Arithmetic*, §5.3 and §7.6."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:347
msgid "Exponent range, overflow and underflow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:349
msgid "**Overflow** is decided on the rounded value: if the rounded result has $\\operatorname{top} > e_{\\max}$, the operation overflows, which is IEEE 754's \"after rounding\" rule (clause 7.4). From the rounding table this happens at the thresholds"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:362
msgid "because a value in $(\\Omega, \\Omega + \\frac12\\operatorname{ulp})$ rounds to nearest down to $\\Omega$, while the tie $\\Omega + \\frac12\\operatorname{ulp}$ goes to the even neighbour $2^{e_{\\max}+1}$, which lies outside $F$. An overflowing result is $\\pm\\infty$ for the first two groups and $\\pm\\Omega$ for the third, always with `overflow` and `inexact`. In binary16, $\\Omega = 65504$ and $\\frac12\\operatorname{ulp}(\\Omega) = 16$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:380
msgid "**Tininess.** A nonzero result is tiny when it lies strictly between $\\pm 2^{e_{\\min}}$. IEEE 754-2019 (clause 7.5) allows two readings, and `TininessDetection` selects one:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:388
msgid "where $\\circ_{p,\\infty}$ rounds to $p$ bits with an unbounded exponent range. The finalizer computes $\\operatorname{top}(r)$ exactly and, for the after-rounding rule, a second split of the same magnitude at the precision shift alone. The two readings differ only for $r$ just below $2^{e_{\\min}}$ that rounds up to it at $p$ bits: for binary16, $r = 2^{-14} - 2^{-27}$ is tiny before rounding but rounds at 11 bits to $2^{-14}$, which is not tiny."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:395
msgid "**Underflow flag.** Under default exception handling the underflow flag is raised for a tiny result only when it is also inexact. The finalizer returns no flags at all for an exact result, so an exact subnormal (for example any subnormal difference, as shown above) raises nothing. The `binary16` product $2^{-14} \\times (1 - 2^{-11})$ shows the full rule: the exact value $2^{-14} - 2^{-25}$ is tiny under both readings, lies halfway between two subnormals of spacing $2^{-24}$, rounds to the even neighbour $2^{-14}$, the smallest normal, and raises `underflow` and `inexact`, although the encoded result `0x0400` is normal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:421
msgid "**Far below the range.** A result certainly smaller than $\\eta/2$ in magnitude (decided from exponent bounds without forming it, for example $2^{-10^9} \\cdot 2^{-10^9}$) rounds to $\\pm 0$ or $\\pm\\eta$ according to the direction, with `underflow` and `inexact`. A result certainly above the range goes to the overflow result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:427
msgid "**Exact results in the subnormal range.** An elementary function whose exact result is the dyadic $m \\cdot 2^{n}$ ($2^{n}$ for `exp2` of an integer, the exact power of `pow` with a dyadic result) passes it to the finalizer as the pair $(m, n)$. It does not build a `Binary` first: a `Binary` made from $(1, n)$ at one bit has its smallest value at $2^{e_{\\min}}$, so $2^{n}$ with $n < e_{\\min}$ was flushed to zero before any context could round it, although it is exactly representable at $p \\ge 2$. The after-rounding rule uses $\\circ_{p,\\infty}$ with no exponent range at all, not a context given by its precision alone, which still has gradual underflow at the implementation limit $2^{e_{\\min}}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:438
msgid "**One layer for the new recommended functions.** The defects of the range audit (an exact power of two flushed by a one-bit `Binary::make`, a shortcut returning before a signaling NaN is seen, a context given by its precision alone used as a range-free rounding) were each a pattern that a function could write again. `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `log2p1` and `log10p1`, and the families that follow in [#235](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/235), therefore take four decisions from one internal file (`elementary_range.mbt`): the NaN and pole results (`binary_nan_unary`, `binary_pole_result`, a signaling NaN first); an exact result rounded as a dyadic by the finalizer, never built as a `Binary` (`binary_integer_result`, `binary_pow2_minus_one_integer`); the overflow decision of $b^x - 1$ from certified bounds of $\\log_2 b$ (`binary_positive_exp_minus_one_overflow`, including the window $x \\log_2 b \\in [e_{\\max} + 1, e_{\\max} + 2)$ and, for base 2, $x > e_{\\max} + 1$ with $x - (e_{\\max} + 1) \\ge 2^{-(e_{\\max} + 1) + 1}$); and one Ziv driver (`binary_ziv_round`). For an integer $n$ with $n \\ge p + 2$ the value $2^n - 1$ lies in the open interval between the midpoint under $2^n$ and $2^n$, where every value rounds alike in each direction, so a representative of the same binade stands for it and the huge integer is never formed; below $p + 2$ bits it is exact or a tie and is rounded as it is."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:458
msgid "`rsqrt`, `compound` and `powr` (second family of [#235](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/235)) add no new rounding logic: each reduces to an operation that already owns the exact cases and the range decision, after the special values of §9.2.1. $1/\\sqrt{x}$ is `rootn(x, -2)`, $x^y$ for $x > 0$ is `pow`, and $(1 + x)^n$ is `pown` of the exact sum $1 + x$ whenever that sum fits in $2^{26}$ bits. The one decision of its own is the tiny rule of `compound`: for $|n x| < 2^{-(p + 9)}$ the exact value is $1 + \\delta$ with $|\\delta| < 2^{-(p+8)}$, far inside the window around $1$ bounded by the breakpoints $1 - 2^{-(p+1)}$ and $1 + 2^{-p}$, so the result is $1$ or its neighbour by direction alone and equals the rounding of the surrogate $1 \\pm 2^{-(p+3)}$, which `binary_round_dyadic` takes through the same finalizer as any exact value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:471
msgid "`asinpi`, `acospi`, `atanpi` and `atan2pi` (third and last family of [#235](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/235)) are $\\operatorname{asin}(x)/\\pi$, $\\operatorname{acos}(x)/\\pi$, $\\operatorname{atan}(x)/\\pi$ and $\\operatorname{atan2}(y, x)/\\pi$: the existing certified enclosures of the angle divided by a certified enclosure of $\\pi$, rounded by the Ziv driver. By Niven's theorem the only exact (dyadic) values are the constants of §9.2.1 and $\\operatorname{atanPi}(\\pm 1) = \\pm 1/4$, $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(\\pm a, \\pm a) = \\pm 1/4, \\pm 3/4$, which are rounded as dyadics; $1/6$, $1/3$ and $2/3$ are rational but not breakpoints, so the loop terminates on them. The range hazards are anchors, decided before the loop by `binary_tiny_argument_result` from a certified distance: $\\operatorname{atanPi}(x) = \\pm(1/2 - \\delta)$ with $\\delta < 2^{-t}$ for $|x| \\ge 2^t$, $t \\ge p + 8$; $\\operatorname{acosPi}(x) = 1/2 \\mp \\delta$ with $\\delta < |x|/2$ for a tiny $|x|$; and $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(y, x)$ at $\\pm 1/2$ ($D \\ge p + 8$) and $\\pm 1$ ($D \\le -(p + 8)$, $x < 0$), where $D$ is the difference of the leading-bit exponents, so that the ratio $y/x$ (whose exponent can be $\\pm 2^{31}$) is never formed. For $x > 0$ and a ratio so small that $t/\\pi$ lies below a quarter of the smallest subnormal the result is the tiny nonzero result of the direction. Everything else is the enclosure $\\operatorname{atan}(t)/\\pi$ (or $1 - \\operatorname{atan}(t)/\\pi$) at a working precision above the operands'."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:492
msgid "Handover: the range layer and how to add a function"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:494
msgid "The recommended functions of §9.2 were added in three families on one layer (`library/src/binary/elementary_range.mbt`) and the helpers it builds on (`binary_round_dyadic` and `binary_round_precision_only` in `context_arithmetic.mbt`; `binary_certified_inexact_target_rounding`, `binary_inexact_inner_endpoint` and `binary_tiny_argument_result` in `elementary_certified.mbt`). Their purpose is to make the defect class of the range audit ([#271](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/271) to [#316](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/316)) impossible to write again: `Binary::make(c, e, w)` at a width $w$ flushes every value below $2^{e_{\\min} - (w - 1)}$, so a result or a helper value must never be built that way; exact results are rounded as dyadics, and a value that clusters at a representable anchor is decided by its certified distance to it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:507
msgid "The layer exposes (all internal):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:509
msgid "name"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:509
msgid "use"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:511
msgid "NaN operand: quiet NaN, `invalid` for a signaling one; call first"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:512
msgid "$\\pm\\infty$ with `divideByZero`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:513
msgid "an integer result rounded as a dyadic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:514
msgid ""
"$2^n - 1$ for any integer $n \\n"
"e 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:515
msgid "$-1 + \\varepsilon$ decided by direction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:516
msgid "certain overflow of $b^x - 1$ from certified $\\log_2 b$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:517
msgid "the Ziv driver: `bounds_at(work)` returns a certified enclosure of an inexact result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:518
msgid "an exact value, both tininess rules, overflow, subnormals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:519
msgid "the $p$-bit rounding with no range (for after-rounding tininess)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:520
msgid "a result within $2^{scale}$ of a representable anchor, above or below it, decided by direction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:521
msgid "a nonzero result below a quarter of the smallest subnormal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:523
msgid "To add a function: (1) write the special-value table of §9.2.1 and Table 9.1 from the PDF text and quote it in the file header and the tests; (2) check a NaN first (`binary_nan_unary`), a signaling NaN before any shortcut; (3) list the exact results (by Niven-type arguments or direct algebra) and return them through `binary_round_dyadic`; (4) find the anchors and the ranges that cluster (a tiny or huge argument, an extreme ratio) and decide them with `binary_tiny_argument_result` with a proved bound on the distance; (5) write the enclosure for `binary_ziv_round`, with the working precision at least the operand precision plus 16 and the operation known to be inexact; (6) add the `try_*_ctx`, `*_ctx` and plain forms, the `BinaryChecked` wrappers and the `mpfr_expr` operation; (7) test against a model that does not use the code under test (an exact rational, an anchor table, the result at precision $p + 150$), at the subnormal limit of the implementation and of a few bounded formats, in every rounding direction and both tininess modes; (8) add the MPFR operation to `tools/generate_mpfr_random_oracle.c` as a new family appended after the existing operations (the rows of older families stay byte for byte), generate range boundary rows in `tools/range_boundary_rows.py`, run 1,000,000 native and 100,000 cases on each other backend and the boundary sweep, and add a few boundary rows to `testdata/binary/random-regressions.txt`; (9) document the function, its evidence and, for a defect of the oracle, the finding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:544
msgid "The oracle is not the specification. Findings about MPFR 4.2.2 (Homebrew bottle, arm64) made while doing this, each documented in `testdata/binary/README.md`: `mpfr_sqrt` returns a wrong ternary value or a result on the wrong side for some odd-exponent operands near the output precision (the generator checks every `sqrt` row with an exact product and draws again); `mpfr_compound_si` is not correctly rounded for a negative $n$ in the directed modes nor near the ends of the exponent range (the generator rounds the exact rational with `mpfr_set_q`; the library was right in every one of 2,017 rows proved by independent rational arithmetic); `mpfr_rec_sqrt(-0)` is $+\\infty$ by MPFR's own convention where §9.2.1 says $-\\infty$ (the expected value follows the standard); `mpfr_atan2pi` takes time that grows with the exponent difference of its operands (not a wrong answer: rows over 100,000 binades apart are not generated)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:558
msgid "Signed zeros and NaNs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:560
msgid "An exact zero sum $a + b = 0$ with $a, b$ of opposite sign is $+0$ in every direction except RD, where it is $-0$; $(-0) + (-0) = -0$ (clause 6.3). Products and quotients take the exclusive-or of the signs. A NaN operand yields the first NaN operand, quieted, with its sign and payload (clause 6.2.3 allows any input NaN), and `invalid_operation` is raised exactly when an operand is a signaling NaN or the operation is invalid on its own ($\\infty - \\infty$, $0 \\cdot \\infty$, $0/0$, $\\infty/\\infty$, $\\sqrt{x<0}$, $\\operatorname{remainder}(\\infty, y)$, $\\operatorname{remainder}(x, 0)$). Flags are values: `combine` is the bitwise OR, so the flags of a computation form a commutative idempotent monoid and can be accumulated in any order, which a global sticky register cannot offer to concurrent code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:573
msgid "The elementary functions take the signs of their special values from IEEE 754-2019 §9.2.1, which reads the sign of a zero operand: for example $\\operatorname{atan2}(\\pm 0, -0) = \\pm\\pi$ but $\\operatorname{atan2}(\\pm 0, +0) = \\pm 0$, $\\operatorname{tanpi}(n)$ has the sign of $\\operatorname{sinpi}(n)/\\operatorname{cospi}(n)$, and $(-0)^y$ and $(-\\infty)^y$ are negative exactly for an odd integral $y$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:580
msgid "Classes of a value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:582
msgid "IEEE 754-2019 clause 5.7.2 defines `class(x)` with ten values, which the `def` package spells as `IeeeClass`. Zeros, infinities and NaNs are read from the stored class and sign (a signaling NaN is the flag the value keeps). The normal/subnormal split is the one rule of clause 3.4: a nonzero finite $x$ is normal when $2^{e_{\\min}} \\le |x|$, that is when the exponent of its leading bit is at least $e_{\\min}$, and subnormal otherwise. A `Binary` stores no $e_{\\min}$ (it has only a precision), so `class_of`, `is_normal` and `is_subnormal` take the `BinaryContext`, the same way `Decimal` takes a `DecimalContext`. The leading-bit exponent is computed from the coefficient's bit length and the exponent, so the test is exact and independent of the precision; no rounding happens and no flag is raised. `is_finite`, `is_infinite` and `is_nan` need no context. `class_of` is a total function: the ten classes partition every `Binary`, and `class_name` of `decimal_gda` is the text of the same partition for its `Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:597
msgid "Format conversion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:599
msgid "`convert_format` is `apply_ctx` in the destination format's context, so the conversion inherits the one rounding step of the dyadic finalizer: a destination with at least the precision and range of the source is exact (the value needs at most $p_{\\text{source}} \\le p_{\\text{destination}}$ bits and its exponent is inside the wider range), and a narrower one rounds once, with the flags of clause 7. The only extra step is the payload of a NaN: `apply_ctx` keeps the whole payload of a `Binary` only for a context of at least the same precision (a NaN of precision $p$ has $p - 2$ payload bits, so every operation and constructor that delivers a NaN at another precision truncates, 6.2.3), and a format holds $p - 2$ payload bits below the quiet bit, so the payload is masked to its low bits, which makes the result the value that decoding `to_interchange` returns and keeps the clause 6.2.3 round trip (narrower to wider to the same narrower format) exact. The independent oracle is Berkeley TestFloat: all twelve pairs among binary16, 32, 64 and 128 in the gate, five rounding directions and both tininess rules for the narrowing pairs; a widening pair does not round, so it is one task."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:616
msgid "Far-apart operands in addition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:618
msgid "**Problem.** $2^{10^9} + 2^{-10^9}$ is exact as a dyadic number, but forming it needs a two-billion-bit coefficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:621
msgid "**Choice.** When the leading exponents differ by more than $p + 3$, the sum is formed on the grid with unit $\\upsilon = 2^{e(\\text{high}) - p - 3}$, where $e(\\text{high})$ is the exponent of the last bit of the larger operand, so $\\upsilon \\le 2^{\\operatorname{top}(\\text{high}) - p - 3}$. The larger operand $H\\upsilon$ is exact on that grid; of the smaller operand only the integer part $L$ of its value in units $\\upsilon$ enters, and if anything below $\\upsilon$ was discarded the magnitude becomes $2(H \\pm L) + 1$ (for subtraction $2(H - L - 1) + 1$) in units of $\\upsilon/2$, that is, one sticky bit at the half unit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:631
msgid "**Why it is exact for rounding.** The result has $\\operatorname{top} \\ge \\operatorname{top}(\\text{high}) - 1$, because the smaller operand is below $2^{\\operatorname{top}(\\text{high}) - p - 2}$. The last retained bit of the rounded result therefore has position at least $\\operatorname{top}(\\text{high}) - p$ (more on the subnormal grid), and its round bit at least $\\operatorname{top}(\\text{high}) - p - 1$. The true sum and the substitute both lie strictly inside the same open interval $\\bigl((H \\pm L)\\upsilon, (H \\pm L + 1)\\upsilon\\bigr)$ (or are equal when nothing was discarded), and that interval lies between two consecutive multiples of $\\upsilon \\le 2^{\\operatorname{top}(\\text{high}) - p - 3}$, at least two bit positions below the round bit. So $q$ and $g$ agree, and $t$ is set in both cases exactly when something nonzero was discarded. The argument does not need the larger operand to have at most $p$ bits. For subtraction, $H - (L + \\varepsilon) = (H - L - 1) + (1 - \\varepsilon)$ with $0 < 1 - \\varepsilon < 1$, so the same substitution applies to the borrowed form. The complete argument, including the subnormal shift, is in the attachment below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:649
msgid "One aligned pass for the sum of wide operands"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:651
msgid "**Problem.** The plain `add` of two coefficients wider than one word aligned each operand with a shift into a new array, added or compared and subtracted into a third, and then rounded: about ten passes over arrays of the size of the sum and six allocations, although the result needs $p$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:653
msgid "**Choice.** Let $X$ be the operand with the larger top bit $t$ and $Y$ the other, $e_X, e_Y$ the exponents of their lowest bits, and $K = p + 3$. The sum is formed in a window of $n = \\lfloor (t - L) / 64 \\rfloor + 1$ limbs whose bit 0 has weight $2^{L}$, with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:659
msgid "So $n \\le \\lfloor (p + 3) / 64 \\rfloor + 1$, whatever the exponent gap. One loop over the limbs reads both operands through their shifts (the word and bit shift are computed once) and writes $X \\pm Y$ once. $X$ is never cut ($e_X \\ge t - p > t - K$). When $Y$ reaches below the window, its dropped part is non-zero, because stored coefficients are odd (bit 0 of $Y$ is dropped), and that is the sticky flag; when $Y$ lies entirely below, none of its limbs is read. For a subtraction with a cut operand the loop starts with a borrow of one: $|X| - |Y| = (X - Y_t - 1) + (1 - f)$ with $Y_t$ the integer part and $0 < f < 1$, as in the previous section. The window then holds the exact result, or a truncated one with the sticky flag, which the finisher of the previous section rounds in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:661
msgid "**Why the window is enough.** If $Y$ is cut, then $e_Y < t - K$, so its top is below $t - K + p = t - 3$ and the gap between the tops is at least $4$. Then for an addition $W \\ge 2^{K-1}$ has at least $p + 3$ bits, and for a subtraction $|X| - |Y| > 2^{t-1} - 2^{t-4} \\ge 2^{t-2}$, so $W = X - Y_t - 1$ has at least $K - 1 = p + 2$ bits. By the argument of the finisher (the discarded part is $d + \\varepsilon$ with integer $d$ and $0 < \\varepsilon < 1$), $p + 1$ bits and the sticky flag decide every rounding direction. If $Y$ is not cut the result is exact. When the tops differ by at most one, the exact difference fits the window ($\\min(e_X, e_Y) \\ge t - 1 - p$), and any number of leading limbs may cancel: the finisher scans down to the top limb. For equal tops the difference is formed as $\\text{left} - \\text{right}$ and negated in place when the final borrow is set, so no comparison pass is needed; an all-zero window is the exact zero, which the general path signs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:663
msgid "**Boundary.** The operands must satisfy the same range test as the other coefficient paths; the former limit on the exponent gap is gone, because a far operand costs only the sticky flag. The tests keep the previous path and compare bit for bit with it and with `add_ctx` and `sub_ctx`, in the sizes, gaps, cancellations and ties listed in the pull request."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:665
msgid "Fused multiply-add"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:667
msgid "`fma_ctx` forms the product $c_x c_y 2^{e_x + e_y}$ exactly as a value whose precision equals its own bit length, and passes it to the addition finalizer, so $x y + z$ is rounded once (clause 5.4.1). The difference from two roundings is the point of the operation: for $a = \\operatorname{RN}(0.1)$ in binary64, the rounding error $a \\cdot a - \\operatorname{RN}(a \\cdot a)$ is lost by `mul_ctx` followed by `sub_ctx` (the second operation sees two equal numbers) but `fma_ctx(a, a, -RN(a*a))` returns it exactly, $-8.33\\ldots \\cdot 10^{-19}$. That the result is exact is Dekker's theorem: the error of a rounded-to-nearest product is itself in $F$ when no underflow occurs (precisely, when $e_a + e_b \\ge e_{\\min} - p + 1$ for the exponents of the last bits of $a$ and $b$, so the exact product lies on the grid of $F$).[^dekker] If the product exponent leaves the `Int` range, the product either certainly overflows, or it is so small that it acts as a sticky bit next to a nonzero addend; the code places a single bit $p + 9$ positions below the addend's last bit, which by the far-operand argument above rounds identically. With a zero addend such a product is the tiny result of the underflow rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:685
msgid "T. J. Dekker, \"A floating-point technique for extending the available precision\", *Numerische Mathematik* 18, 1971; Muller et al., §4.4."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:689
msgid "IEEE remainder is exact"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:691
msgid ""
"**Claim.** If $x, y \\in F$ (same precision $p$, same range) and $y \\n"
"e 0$, then $r = x - n y$ with $n = \\operatorname{RNE}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(x/y)$ lies in $F$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:695
msgid "**Proof.** Write $x = M_x 2^{q_x}$, $y = M_y 2^{q_y}$ with $|M_x|, |M_y| < 2^p$ and $q_x, q_y \\ge e_{\\min} - p + 1$. By the choice of $n$, $|r| \\le |y|/2$. If $n = 0$ then $r = x$. Otherwise $|x/y| \\ge \\frac12$, so $|r| \\le |y|/2 \\le |x|$. Now $r$ is an integer multiple of $2^{\\min(q_x, q_y)}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:701
msgid "If $q_x \\ge q_y$: $r = k 2^{q_y}$ with $|k| 2^{q_y} \\le |M_y| 2^{q_y}/2$, so $|k| < 2^{p-1}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:703
msgid "If $q_x < q_y$: $r = k 2^{q_x}$ with $|k| 2^{q_x} \\le |x| = |M_x| 2^{q_x}$, so $|k| < 2^{p}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:706
msgid "In both cases $|k| < 2^p$, the exponent is at least $e_{\\min} - p + 1$, and $|r| \\le \\max(|x|, |y|) \\le \\Omega$, so $r \\in F$. $\\square$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:709
msgid "The implementation never forms $n$, which can have $2^{30}$ bits. With $X = |x| 2^{-m}$, $Y = |y| 2^{-m}$, $m = \\min(q_x, q_y)$ (integers), it computes $X \\bmod 2Y$ by modular exponentiation of $2^{q_x - q_y}$ when $q_x > q_y$. Writing $X = Q (2Y) + R$, $0 \\le R < 2Y$, gives $\\lfloor X/Y \\rfloor = 2Q + [R \\ge Y]$, so $R$ alone yields both $X \\bmod Y$ and the parity of $\\lfloor X/Y \\rfloor$, which is all the ties-to-even choice of $n$ needs. The exact $r$ then goes through the usual finalizer; by the claim, no rounding happens for operands of the context's format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:719
msgid "Neighbours, scaling and integral values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:721
msgid "`next_up_ctx(x)` adds a positive step $2^{\\pi - 2}$ to $x$ and rounds toward $+\\infty$, where $\\pi = \\min(e_{\\min} - p,\\ e(x),\\ \\operatorname{top}(x) - p)$. Every gap between consecutive points of $F$ next to $x$ is at least $2^{\\min(e_{\\min} - p + 1,\\ \\operatorname{top}(x) - p)}$ and $x$ itself is a multiple of $2^{e(x)}$, so $x < x + 2^{\\pi - 2} < x^{+}$ for $x \\in F$, and by the definition of RU the result is the least point of $F$ above $x$. The same argument works for an $x$ with more than $p$ bits, which is why such operands are accepted. The flags of this internal addition are discarded, because `nextUp` is quiet (clause 5.3.1) even when it steps from $\\Omega$ to $+\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:732
msgid "`scaleb_ctx(x, n)` (with `n` an integer-valued `Binary`) is the finalizer applied to $(c, e + n)$: exact in the normal range, correctly rounded with underflow and overflow outside it. `logb_ctx` returns $\\operatorname{top}(x)$, which is exact and correct for subnormal $x$ because $\\operatorname{top}$ is computed on the integer coefficient. The integral roundings use the same round and sticky bits with $\\sigma = -e$ (the bits below the binary point); `to_int_ctx` and its siblings round first and then compare the integer with the target range, reporting `invalid_operation` instead of returning an implementation-defined sentinel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:742
msgid "Decimal conversion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:744
msgid ""
"**Parsing.** `from_string_ctx` reads $D \\cdot 10^{k}$ exactly ($D$ an integer without trailing zeros). For $|k|$ up to $\\max\\bigl(400, \\lfloor (3n + p)/2 \\rfloor + 64\\bigr)$ ($n$ the number of digits) it is rounded exactly: $D \\cdot 5^{k} \\cdot 2^{k}$ by the dyadic finalizer for $k \\ge 0$, and $D / 5^{|k|} \\cdot 2^{k}$ by the division finalizer for $k < 0$. Beyond that bound it uses directed enclosures $[\\operatorname{RD}_w(D)\\operatorname{RD}_w(10^k), \\operatorname{RU}_w(D)\\operatorname{RU}_w(10^k)]$ at a working precision $w$, doubled until both ends round to the same value with the same flags. The bound makes the loop terminate. The rounding breakpoints of a direction are the points of $F$ (directed modes) or the midpoints between them (nearest modes); both are dyadic with at most $p + 1$ significant bits. For $k \\ge 0$ the odd part of $D 10^{k}$ is a multiple of $5^{k}$, and $5^{k} > 2^{2.32 k} > 2^{p+2}$ beyond the bound, so the value is no breakpoint: $k > \\lfloor (3n+p)/2 \\rfloor + 64 > (p+2)/2.32$. For $k < 0$, $|k| > 1.5n$ gives $5^{|k|} > 10^{n} > D$, so $5^{|k|} \\n"
"mid D$ and $D/10^{|k|}$ is not even dyadic. The points where a flag changes (the overflow threshold, $2^{e_{\\min}}$ for tininess, the points of $F$ for inexactness) are dyadic with at most $p + 1$ significant bits as well. A value that is none of these has a positive distance to all of them, and the enclosure, whose relative width is $O(2^{-w})$, eventually lies strictly between two of them, so some $w$ certifies it; with no count limit (`BinaryContext::with_refinement_limit(None)`) the loop ends by itself once $w$ passes the work cap below. Values whose binary logarithm is certainly beyond the range, estimated with $\\log_2 10$ and a safety margin, overflow or underflow without any arithmetic, so `1e100000000` costs nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:771
msgid "**Hexadecimal parsing.** The value of a hexSequence is the integer of its hex digits times a power of two, so it is exactly a dyadic rational and no enclosure is needed: `from_hex_ctx` parses once (the grammar function is shared with `from_hex`) and hands the integer and the binary exponent to the same dyadic finalizer as the arithmetic operations, which applies the rounding direction, the exponent range, subnormals and the tininess rule, and returns the flags. A binary exponent beyond the 32-bit range is saturated: the value is then above $2^{2^{31}-1}$ or below $2^{-2^{31}+b}$ ($b$ the number of bits of the integer), outside every exponent range, so it overflows or underflows exactly as the saturated one does. `from_hex` keeps its own range check (a parse error beyond the 32-bit range) and its results for every in-range sequence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:784
msgid "**Fixed digits.** `to_decimal_string_ctx(x, d)` needs $\\operatorname{round}(x / 10^{E - d + 1})$ with $E = \\lfloor \\log_{10}|x| \\rfloor$. $E$ starts from $\\lfloor \\operatorname{top}(x) \\log_{10} 2 \\rfloor$, which is within one of the answer, and is corrected by comparing $|x|$ with $10^{E+1}$, first through directed bounds of the power and, when they straddle, exactly. The quotient is formed exactly as an integer division when its operands have at most $2^{20}$ bits, so ties and exact results are recognised; otherwise directed enclosures are widened until both ends round to the same integer, which terminates because such a quotient is neither an integer nor a half-integer. A carry into a new leading digit ($9.99 \\to 10.0$) increments $E$ and repeats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:796
msgid "**Shortest output.** Let $I(x)$ be the set of reals that round to $x$ under RNE in the context; it is an interval containing $x$. For each digit count $n$, the two $n$-digit decimals next to $x$ (truncated and rounded away from zero) are candidates, and a candidate is accepted when parsing it returns $x$, that is when it lies in $I(x)$. Acceptance is monotone in $n$: if the $n$-digit truncation $d_n$ lies in $I(x)$, the $(n+1)$-digit truncation satisfies $d_n \\le d_{n+1} \\le x$ (in magnitude), so it lies in the interval too, and likewise for the upper neighbour. So the least accepted $n$ is found by bisection on $[1, \\lceil p \\log_{10} 2 \\rceil + 2]$, an upper bound at which a candidate is always accepted.[^shortest] If both candidates are accepted, the nearer one is chosen, then the even one, which is the nearest decimal of that length. For binary64 this reproduces the host formatter on every value tested."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:810
msgid "The digit count $\\lceil p \\log_{10} 2 \\rceil + 1$ suffices for round trip (Matula 1968; Goldberg 1991, Theorem 15); the extra digit is a margin for the bisection's upper end."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:816
msgid "**Problem.** For $f = \\exp, \\ln, \\sin, \\ldots$ the value $f(x)$ is transcendental and must be rounded correctly without knowing it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:819
msgid "**Options.** Fixed polynomial approximations with a proven error bound (fast, but tied to one precision); Ziv's strategy of evaluating with an a priori error bound and retrying at a higher precision when the rounding is ambiguous;[^ziv] or interval evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:824
msgid ""
"**Choice.** A Ziv loop whose error bound is not estimated but computed: every elementary function evaluates an enclosure $[L, U] \\n"
"i f(x)$ in which every internal operation is rounded downward for $L$ and upward for $U$ at a working precision $w$. If"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:833
msgid "the common value is returned, otherwise $w$ grows. **The value is sound by (R2):** $L \\le f(x) \\le U$ implies $\\circ(L) \\le \\circ(f(x)) \\le \\circ(U)$, so equal ends force $\\circ(f(x)) = \\circ(L)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:837
msgid "The flags need a separate argument. Overflow and tininess (under either rule) depend only on $|r|$ through a threshold, so on one side of zero they are monotone: if $L$ and $U$ (which have the same sign once they round to the same nonzero value) agree, every $r \\in [L, U]$ agrees with them. Inexactness is not monotone: it is false exactly on $F$. If $L < U$ round to the same value $v$ with `inexact`, then $f(x)$ is inexact *unless* $f(x) = v \\in F$. The loop cannot tell these apart, so the flags are right only if every input whose exact result lies in $F$ is caught before the loop. That is why the exact cases below are filtered, and why an exact case that is missed shows up as a spurious `inexact` (nearest rounding) or as a budget failure (directed rounding, where $L$ and $U$ round to different neighbours of $v$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:850
msgid "The enclosures come from series with rigorous tails and from monotone reductions. For $\\exp$ on $[0, 1/8]$, the terms $t_k = x^k/k!$ satisfy $t_{k+1}/t_k = x/(k+1) \\le 1/8$, so after the last summed term $t_n$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:858
msgid "and the code stops once $t_n < 2^{-(w+8)}$ and adds $2 t_n$ (rounded upward) to the upper sum; the lower sum of positive terms is already a lower bound. Larger arguments are halved $r$ times and the result squared $r$ times, which is monotone on positive numbers and therefore keeps the enclosure; $e^{-x} = 1/e^{x}$ handles negative arguments. For $\\ln v$ with $v \\in [1, 2]$ the series is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:869
msgid "with tail $\\sum_{k \\ge m} z^{2k+1}/(2k+1) \\le \\frac{z^{2m+1}}{2m+1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - z^2}$, the bound the code adds. The trigonometric functions reduce $x$ by an enclosure of $\\pi/2$ (the cached binary-splitting $\\pi$, see \"Where $\\pi$ comes from\" below) at $w \\ge p + \\max(0, \\operatorname{top}(x) + 1) + 96$ bits, so the quadrant $k = \\operatorname{round}(x / (\\pi/2))$ is the same integer at both ends of the enclosure; otherwise the attempt is repeated at a higher precision. This is the Payne–Hanek idea realised by brute precision instead of a stored table of $2/\\pi$; its cost grows with $\\log_2|x|$, so inputs needing more than 8,000,000 bits are refused with `ResourceLimit` rather than run for minutes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:880
msgid "**Budget.** The loop starts at $w_0 = p + 64$ (for some functions at the operand precision plus 16 when that is larger) and steps $w_{i+1} = w_i + \\max(32, \\lfloor w_i/2 \\rfloor)$, at most $L$ attempts, where $L$ is the refinement limit of the context (`BinaryContext::refinement_limit`, default $12$; a value below $1$ counts as $1$). For binary64 and $L = 12$ the sequence is $117, 175, 262, \\ldots, 10053$ bits, and a hard-to-round input that needs more than that fails with a `CertificationFailure` carrying the refinement count; `Some(n)` with a larger $n$ certifies it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:889
msgid "With $L$ = `None` the count is unbounded, but the loop is not: it stops, with the same `CertificationFailure`, when $w$ exceeds $\\max(8\\,000\\,000,\\ 256\\,w_0)$ bits (capped at $10^9$ so $w$ fits `Int32`), and a trigonometric argument that needs more than $8\\,000\\,000$ bits for its reduction is still refused with `ResourceLimit` whatever the limit. So `None` cannot allocate without bound: each step costs $O(M(w))$ for the working precision $w \\le$ the cap, and the number of steps is at most $\\log_{1.5}(\\text{cap}/w_0)$. A result that sits at distance $\\delta$ from a breakpoint needs $w \\approx \\log_2(1/\\delta)$, so the default $L = 12$ decides everything farther than about $2^{-10000}$ from a breakpoint at binary64 and `None` extends that to the cap. Functions without a context argument (the `Interval` enclosures) keep $12$. The exact fallback of the integer power `pown` (12 Ziv rounds, then an exact product) cannot fail and is not governed by the limit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:904
msgid ""
"**Termination.** Ziv's argument has two parts. First, $f(x)$ must not be a breakpoint of $\\circ$ (a point of $F$, a midpoint, or a flag threshold), all of which are dyadic. For a nonzero dyadic $x$, $e^{x}$, $\\sin x$, $\\cos x$, $\\tan x$, the hyperbolic functions, their inverses and $\\ln x$ ($x \\n"
"e 1$) are transcendental by Lindemann–Weierstrass. For $2^x$, $10^x$, $\\log_2 x$ and $\\log_{10} x$ irrationality is enough and elementary: $2^{a/b}$ with $b \\n"
"mid a$ is irrational, so $2^x$ is dyadic only for integral $x$, and $\\log_2 x = a/b$ forces $x = 2^{a/b}$, a power of two; likewise $10^x$ and $\\log_{10} x$ are dyadic only at integral $x \\ge 0$ and at $x = 10^k$. For the $\\pi$-scaled functions Niven's theorem shows that integral, half-integral and (for `tanpi`) quarter-integral arguments are the only dyadic exceptions.[^niven] For `pow` and `rootn` the exceptions are characterised under *Exact powers and roots* below. The exceptions are filtered before the loop: $e^0 = 1$, $\\ln 1 = 0$, $\\log_2 2^k = k$, $2^n$ for integral $|n| < 2^{31}$, $\\sin(\\pm 0)$, the $\\pi$-scaled cases, $10^n$ for integral $0 \\le n \\le \\max(4096, p)$ (beyond that the odd part $5^n$ has more than $p + 1$ bits, so $10^n$ is not a breakpoint), $\\log_{10} 10^n$ for every $n \\ge 1$, dyadic powers and exact roots, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:923
msgid "Second, the enclosure must shrink onto $f(x)$ fast enough. The series stop when the next term is below $2^{-(w+8)}$ or $2^{-(w+12)}$ in *absolute* value, which is fine for arguments of moderate size; the width then is $O(2^{-w})$ relative to $f(x)$, a non-breakpoint has a positive distance to every breakpoint, and some $w$ certifies it. Tiny arguments need the two measures under *Tiny arguments* below. How large $w$ must be is the table maker's dilemma: no useful a priori bound is known for arbitrary $p$, so the budget is a resource limit, not a correctness condition. When it runs out the `try_*` form reports a `CertificationFailure` with the stage, the reason and the last $w$, and the total forms return a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation`; neither returns an uncertified value. The pinned MPFR corpus never exhausts it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:936
msgid "**Exact powers and roots.** An integral exponent with $|y| < 2^{31}$ goes to `pown`. A larger integral $y$ needs no filter: for $x = c \\cdot 2^e$ with $c > 1$ odd, $c^{|y|}$ has more than $2^{31} > p + 1$ bits, and a power of two $2^{ey}$ lies outside every exponent range, so neither is a breakpoint. Every other exponent is $y = m / 2^k$ with $m$ odd and $k \\ge 1$. If $x^y$ is rational, so is $q = x^{|y|}$, and $x^{|m|} = q^{2^k}$; comparing the exponents of $2$ and of each odd prime gives $q = a \\cdot 2^f$ with $a$ odd, $e|m| = f \\cdot 2^k$ and $c^{|m|} = a^{2^k}$. Since $|m|$ is odd, $2^k \\mid e$ and every prime exponent of $c$ is divisible by $2^k$, so $c = r^{2^k}$ and $q = r^{|m|} \\cdot 2^{e|m|/2^k}$. Conversely these values are exact. So a rational `pow` result exists exactly under these two divisibility conditions; for $m > 0$ it is the dyadic $q$, and for $m < 0$ it is $1/q$, which is dyadic only when $r = 1$. Every other `pow` result is irrational. `binary_pow_exactness` computes the dyadic results and rounds them once, unless the odd part $r^m$ would need more than $2p + 64$ bits (then it has more than $p + 1$ significant bits and is not a breakpoint). The same argument with $1/|n|$ in place of $|y|$ shows that $\\operatorname{rootn}(x, n)$ is rational exactly when $|n| \\mid e$ and $c = r^{|n|}$; `rootn` returns $\\pm r \\cdot 2^{e/|n|}$ rounded once for $n > 0$, and for $n < 0$ the correctly rounded quotient $1 / (\\pm r \\cdot 2^{e/|n|})$, which division decides with the right `inexact` flag although it need not be dyadic. The perfect-power test has no size limit. It first takes square roots with remainder for every factor $2$ of the degree, stopping at the first nonzero remainder, and then the floor of the remaining odd root: by bisection while the root has at most 64 bits, otherwise by integer Newton steps $r \\leftarrow \\lfloor ((d-1) r + \\lfloor c / r^{d-1} \\rfloor) / d \\rfloor$ started just above the root, which decrease monotonically to $\\lfloor c^{1/d} \\rfloor$; one power $r^d$ then decides exactness. A coefficient with at most $d$ bits other than $1$ is rejected at once, because $r \\ge 2$ gives $r^d \\ge 2^d$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:966
msgid "A negative base with an integral exponent beyond the `pown` range is evaluated as $\\pm|x|^y$. Negation is an exact symmetry of $\\circ$ that swaps the two directed modes, $\\circ_{\\mathrm{RD}}(-v) = -\\circ_{\\mathrm{RU}}(v)$, so for an odd $y$ the code rounds $|x|^y$ with RD and RU exchanged and negates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:971
msgid "**Tiny arguments.** For small enough $|x|$ (about $2^{-p/2}$ for the odd functions, $2^{-p}$ for $e^x$) the result lies closer to a representable number $a$ than the target spacing: $a = x$ for the odd functions, $a = 1$ for $e^x$, $\\cos$, $\\cosh$. An enclosure whose series stops after its first term then has $a$ itself as one end ($U = x$ for $\\sin$, $L = 1$ for $e^x$), and that end rounds without `inexact` while the other end rounds with it, so the acceptance test alone would only pass once $w$ separates $f(x)$ from $a$, at $w \\approx \\log_2(1/|x|)$ or more. Two measures remove the dependence on $|x|$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:980
msgid ""
"*Inner endpoints.* Every result that reaches the loop is known not to be a breakpoint. For every function other than `pow` and `rootn`, `binary_certified_inexact_target_rounding` replaces an end $E$ that could be a breakpoint (an odd coefficient of at most $p + 1$ bits) by $E' = E \\pm 2^{e_E - s}$ toward the inside of the enclosure, with $s = \\max(1, p + 3 - \\operatorname{bits}(c_E))$. If the leading bit of $E$ is $2^t$, breakpoints near $E$ are at least $2^{t - p - 1}$ apart and the offset is at most $2^{t - p - 2}$, so no breakpoint lies in $(E, E']$. Since $f(x) \\n"
"e E$, either $f(x)$ lies beyond $E'$ or between $E$ and $E'$, and in the second case it shares the open cell between breakpoints with $E'$ and rounds like it. So $\\circ(E') = \\circ(U')$ with equal flags still forces $\\circ(f(x))$ and its flags, and the test passes as soon as the enclosure is narrower than the spacing, independently of $|x|$. `pow` uses inner endpoints whenever its exactness test (above) has shown that the result is irrational, not dyadic, or wider than $p + 1$ bits, so a tiny exponent with $x^y$ closer to $1$ than the target spacing is certified too ($2^{2^{-16000}}$ in binary128 is $1$ with `inexact`). The test leaves a few cases with a power-of-two base undecided (an exponent of magnitude at least $2^{31}$, or a scale outside the 32-bit range); those keep the plain acceptance test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1001
msgid "*Tiny-argument bounds.* Some enclosures (for $\\sinh$, $\\tanh$ via $e^x - e^{-x}$, and for $\\operatorname{asinh}$, $\\operatorname{atanh}$, $\\operatorname{asin}$, $\\tan$, `expm1`, `log1p`) straddle the neighbour of $a$ until $w \\approx 3\\log_2(1/|x|)$. For $|x| \\le 1/2$ the series give one-sided bounds with a known side: $0 < x - \\sin x$, $0 < \\tan x - x$, $0 < \\operatorname{asin} x - x$, $0 < \\sinh x - x$, $0 < x - \\tanh x$, $0 < x - \\operatorname{asinh} x$, $0 < \\operatorname{atanh} x - x$, each at most $|x|^3$ (for $x > 0$, mirrored for $x < 0$), and $0 < 1 - \\cos x$, $0 < \\cosh x - 1$, $0 < \\operatorname{expm1}(x) - x$, $0 < x - \\operatorname{log1p}(x)$, each at most $x^2$. With $|x| < 2^T$, `binary_tiny_argument_result` uses the enclosure $[a, a + 2^{\\sigma}]$ or $[a - 2^{\\sigma}, a]$ with $\\sigma = 3T$ (respectively $2T$), raised to at least $t_a - p - 8$, when $\\sigma < t_a - p - 3$ ($2^{t_a}$ the leading bit of $a$), and rounds it with inner endpoints. The condition implies $|x| < 2^{-(p+3)/2} \\le 1/2$, so the bounds apply, and the enclosure is at most a quarter of the breakpoint spacing at $a$ wide. When $a$ is a breakpoint, both ends lie in the cell next to $a$ and the result is decided before the loop; otherwise (an argument wider than $p + 1$ bits) the loop runs as usual. With both measures `sin` and `atan` of $2^{-6000}$ at 53 bits and $e^{2^{-20000}}$ are certified in every rounding mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1022
msgid ""
"`rootn` keeps the plain test, and `pow` uses it only for the few results its exactness test cannot classify. For `rootn` it suffices: its enclosure is $e^{\\ln(x)/n}$ at a working precision above the operand precision $q$, and $|\\ln x| / |n| > 2^{-(q + 31)}$ for $x \\n"
"e 1$ and $|n| \\le 10^9$, so an end at $1$ disappears after an attempt or two. `pow` evaluates $e^{y \\ln x}$, where $y$ can be arbitrarily small: an exponent so small that $x^y$ lies closer to $1$ than the target spacing gives an enclosure with $L = 1$ at every affordable $w$. That is why `pow` moves such an end inward once its exactness test has excluded a breakpoint, as described under inner endpoints; for example $2^{2^{-16000}}$ in binary128 is then $1$ with `inexact`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1033
msgid "I. Niven, *Irrational Numbers*, 1956, Corollary 3.12: if $r$ is rational and $\\sin(\\pi r)$ is rational, then $\\sin(\\pi r) \\in \\{0, \\pm\\frac12, \\pm 1\\}$; similarly for $\\cos$, and $\\tan(\\pi r) \\in \\{0, \\pm 1\\}$. The value $\\pm\\frac12$ needs $r$ with denominator 6 or 3, which is not dyadic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1039
msgid "A. Ziv, \"Fast evaluation of elementary mathematical functions with correctly rounded last bit\", *ACM TOMS* 17(3), 1991; for interval evaluation see W. Tucker, *Validated Numerics*, Princeton 2011, and F. Johansson, \"Arb: efficient arbitrary-precision midpoint-radius interval arithmetic\", *IEEE Trans. Computers* 66(8), 2017."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1045
msgid "**Integer powers.** `pown_ctx` computes $x^n$ by an addition chain at $w = p + \\operatorname{bits}(n) + \\operatorname{bits}(p) + 4$ bits with round-to-nearest. Each chain step $a_k = a_i + a_j$ multiplies two approximations; if $x^{a} \\prod (1 + \\delta)^{E(a)}$ describes the error structure, then $E(a_k) = E(a_i) + E(a_j) + 1$ and $E(1) = 0$, so by induction $E(a) \\le a - 1$. The computed value is therefore $x^n (1 + \\theta)$ with $|\\theta| \\le \\gamma_{n-1} = (n-1)u_w/(1 - (n-1)u_w)$ in Higham's notation, which is below $2^{\\operatorname{bits}(n)}$ units in the last place of the $w$-bit result. The code uses the radius $2^{\\operatorname{bits}(n) + 2}$ units (one more factor of two for a negative power, whose reciprocal adds $n$ further factors), builds the interval, and accepts when both ends round alike, by the same (R2) argument. When 12 attempts (with $w$ doubled between them) do not certify, the exact power $c^n 2^{ne}$ is formed and rounded, so the result is correctly rounded in every case. A power certainly outside the range is decided first from certified $\\log_2$ bounds; powers of two and powers whose exact value fits in $p$ bits are computed exactly. The Ziv path therefore only sees $c^n$ with $c > 1$ odd and more than $p$ bits, or $1/c^n$, which is not dyadic: neither is in $F$, so the `inexact` it reports is right (the flag argument above). A $c^n$ with exactly $p + 1$ bits is a midpoint; the enclosure then never certifies under nearest rounding and the exact fallback decides it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1067
msgid "**hypot.** `hypot` forms $a^2 + b^2$ exactly from the coefficients and takes one correctly rounded square root, so it needs no loop; the cost is the alignment shift between the two squares. For $|a| \\ge |b| > 0$, $|a| < \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\le |a| + b^2 / (2|a|)$. Write $|a| = c \\cdot 2^e$ with leading bit $2^t$ and $g = 2^{\\min(e,\\, t - p - 1)}$: $|a|$ and every rounding breakpoint near it are multiples of $g$, so when $b^2 < 2|a|g$ no breakpoint lies in $(|a|, |a| + b^2/(2|a|)]$ and every such $b$ gives the same rounded result and flags. $|b| < g/8$ is enough, and `binary_hypot_negligible_operand` then replaces $b$ by $g/8$, which keeps the shift at about twice the operand and target precisions however far apart the exponents are ($\\operatorname{hypot}(1, 2^{-600000}) = 1$ with `inexact`). A shift above $10^6$ bits is still refused as a `certification_failure`; after the replacement it needs a precision near $500\\,000$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1081
msgid ""
"**`atan`, `asin`, `acos` and `atan2`: one evaluation chain for the pair.** The enclosure of $\\operatorname{atan}$ over a pair $[a, b]$ is computed by one chain, not by one chain per endpoint. $\\operatorname{atan}$ is odd and increasing, so $L = \\operatorname{lower}(\\operatorname{atan}\\,a)$ and $U = \\operatorname{upper}(\\operatorname{atan}\\,b)$ enclose $\\operatorname{atan}$ on the whole pair. For $0 \\le a \\le b$ and $b > 1/2$ the reduction is $\\operatorname{atan}\\,x = 2 \\operatorname{atan}\\, r(x)$ with $r(x) = x / (1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2}) = \\tan(\\operatorname{atan}(x)/2)$, which is increasing on $x \\ge 0$ (it is a composition of increasing maps). Hence $[r^-(a), r^+(b)] \\n"
"i r(x)$ for every $x \\in [a, b]$, where $r^-(a)$ divides $a$ rounded down by an upper bound of $1 + \\sqrt{1 + a^2}$ and $r^+(b)$ divides $b$ rounded up by a lower bound of $1 + \\sqrt{1 + b^2}$; the bounds of the denominator are directed squares, sums and square roots, each monotone on nonnegative numbers. The reduced pair is again nonnegative and ordered, so the recursion is a chain (two levels at most, because $r(x) < 1$ and $r(1) < 1/2$), and the Taylor series runs once, with its lower track on the lower end and its upper track on the upper end. A negative pair $[a, b]$ is $-[-b, -a]$; a pair that contains zero is widened to $[-m, m]$ with $m = \\max(-a, b)$, which contains it. The old code evaluated the series once for $|x| \\le 1/2$, twice up to $4/3$ and four times above (a lower and an upper call per reduction level); a point argument now costs one series at every size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1104
msgid "**Large arguments.** The square $x^2$ of the reduction leaves the exponent range for $|x| \\ge 2^{2^{29}}$, so for a leading bit of $x$ of at least $2w + 2$ the reduction is formed without it: with $y = 1/x$, $r(x) = 1 / (y + \\sqrt{1 + y^2})$ and $1 + y \\le y + \\sqrt{1 + y^2} \\le 1 + y + y^2$, hence $1/(1 + y + y^2) \\le r(x) \\le 1/(1 + y)$, evaluated with directed reciprocals of $x$; the relative gap is below $2y \\le 2^{-2w-1}$. An infinite end of a pair (a quotient of `atan2` that overflowed) is bounded by $\\pi/2$ rounded in the matching direction. The test `atan_single_evaluation_wbtest.mbt` covers $3 \\cdot 2^e$ for $e$ on both sides of $2^{29}$ and near $2^{30}$, both signs and three rounding directions, against $\\pi/2$ from the cached constant; the public results equal those of the previous code there."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1117
msgid "For $\\operatorname{asin}$ the pair is the ratio $f(x) = x / \\sqrt{1 - x^2}$, increasing on $(-1, 1)$. With $\\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\in [\\rho^-, \\rho^+]$ on the operand pair (directed roots of directed $1 - x^2$), the lower end is $a / \\rho^-$ if $a < 0$ and $a / \\rho^+$ if $a \\ge 0$, the upper end is $b / \\rho^-$ if $b \\ge 0$ and $b / \\rho^+$ if $b < 0$, which replaces the eight directed quotients and the search for their minimum and maximum. For $\\operatorname{atan2}$ and $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}$ the ratio $y / x$ of a rectangle with $x$ of one strict sign takes its extremes at the corners selected by the signs of the ends of $y$ (four directed divisions replace the eight of the previous search). The soundness of the new evaluation is tested against the previous functions, kept as test-only copies, evaluated at 128 more bits (`atan_single_evaluation_wbtest.mbt`); the Ziv loops take the same number of attempts on that corpus. A pair that is wide because the operand is (`asin` close to $1$) can come out up to about 1.4 times wider than before, because the alternating-series bracket pairs the lower track with the terms of the other end; narrow pairs differ by a few units in the last place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1134
msgid "**The arctangent leaf in fixed point.** On $[0, \\tfrac12]$ the series is evaluated as $\\arctan x = x - R(x)$ with $R(x) = x^3/3 - x^5/5 + x^7/7 - \\cdots$, increasing in $x$, so on a pair $[a, b]$ the enclosure is $[\\,a - R^{+},\\ b - R^{-}\\,]$ with $R^{-} \\le R(a)$ and $R(b) \\le R^{+}$. $R$ is computed with integers of $F = w + q + 20 + \\operatorname{bits}(w)$ fractional bits, where $q = -\\operatorname{top}(b)$: a lower chain $P^{-}_k \\le a^{2k+1} 2^F$ by floors and an upper chain $P^{+}_k \\ge b^{2k+1} 2^F$ by ceilings (each step multiplies by a floor or ceiling of the square and shifts, and a floor of a product of lower bounds, or a ceiling of a product of upper bounds, keeps the bound), then $T^{-}_k = \\lfloor P^{-}_k / (2k+1) \\rfloor$ and $T^{+}_k = \\lceil P^{+}_k / (2k+1) \\rceil$ by a division by a single word. For terms decreasing in size the partial sum after an odd number of terms is at least $R$ and after an even number at most $R$; with $A = \\sum_{k \\text{ odd}} T^{+}_k$, $B = \\sum_{k \\text{ even}} T^{-}_k$, $C = \\sum_{k \\text{ odd}} T^{-}_k$ and $D = \\sum_{k \\text{ even}} T^{+}_k$ this gives $R^{+} = A - B$ (odd partial sum, upper terms added, lower terms subtracted) and $R^{-} = C' - D$ (even partial sum, $C'$ without the last odd term), and the loop stops at an odd $k$ whose $T^{+}_k$ is below two units. Every floor or ceiling loses at most one unit and $x^2 \\le \\tfrac14$, so the chain errors stay below two units, each term is within three, and the whole enclosure within $1.5 F$ units of $2^{-F}$, which the 20 and $\\operatorname{bits}(w)$ guard bits cover relative to $x \\ge 2^{-q}$. For $q \\ge w/2 + 8$ the series is not run: $0 \\le R(x) \\le x^3/3$ and $x^3/3 < x\\, 2^{-w-14}$, so the enclosure is $[\\,a - b^3/3,\\ b\\,]$. Where the previous floating-point leaf ran four rounded `Binary` operations per term, a term is now two integer products and two single-word divisions: instructions retired for `atan`, `asin`, `acos` and `atan2` fall by 2.1 to 2.5 times at 53, 128 and 512 bits. The tests keep the floating-point leaf as a copy and check that the new enclosure contains the old one at 128 more bits (points, neighbouring and wide pairs, a pair starting at zero) and at both sides of the threshold $q = w/2 + 8$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1136
msgid "**Where $\\pi$ comes from.** The binary-splitting $\\pi$ of this package is cached per working precision. `Binary` $+\\infty$ `.try_atan_ctx` returns the correctly rounded $\\pi/2$ from that cache, so `decimal` and `interval` obtain $[\\operatorname{RD}(\\pi), \\operatorname{RU}(\\pi)]$ from two calls (rounding downward and upward) and a scaling by $2$, which is exact: the same interval, bit for bit, as $4\\,[\\operatorname{RD}(\\operatorname{atan} 1), \\operatorname{RU}(\\operatorname{atan} 1)]$, since scaling by a power of two commutes with directed rounding. No public function was added for it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1145
msgid ""
"**`sinh`, `cosh` and `tanh` from one `exp`.** With $[L, U] \\n"
"i e^{x}$ from one kernel call and $0 < L$, the enclosure of $e^{-x}$ is $[1/U, 1/L]$ with directed divisions (the reciprocal is decreasing); if $L$ is not positive (an underflowed enclosure) the kernel is called on $-x$ as before. $\\sinh$ and $\\cosh$ then combine the four ends as before. For $\\tanh = s / c$ with $0 < c_0 \\le c \\le c_1$ and $s \\in [s_0, s_1]$ the quotient is increasing in $s$ and, for $s \\ge 0$, decreasing in $c$ (the reverse for $s < 0$), so the extremes are $s_0 / c_1$ if $s_0 \\ge 0$ and $s_0 / c_0$ otherwise (lower end), $s_1 / c_0$ if $s_1 \\ge 0$ and $s_1 / c_1$ otherwise (upper end): two divisions instead of eight. At arguments where $e^{|x|}$ leaves the exponent range the enclosures are the same as before ($\\sinh$, $\\cosh$) or $[0, \\infty]$ instead of a NaN end ($\\tanh$), and the public results did not change in the tests at those arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1147
msgid "**Corners by sign.** The product of two intervals $[a_0, a_1] \\cdot [b_0, b_1]$ is no longer the minimum and maximum of four rounded products. With $a$ nonnegative, the smallest $a b$ over the box is $a_0 b_0$ if $b_0 \\ge 0$ and $a_1 b_0$ otherwise (take the smallest $b$, then the $a$ that suits its sign), and the largest is $a_1 b_1$ if $b_1 \\ge 0$ and $a_0 b_1$ otherwise; a nonpositive $a$ is $-|a|$ and a mixed $a$ with a sign-definite $b$ swaps the roles; two mixed intervals still take the four corners. The quotient by a positive interval $[d_0, d_1]$ has the lower end $n_0 / d_1$ if $n_0 \\ge 0$ and $n_0 / d_0$ otherwise, and the upper end $n_1 / d_0$ if $n_1 \\ge 0$ and $n_1 / d_1$ otherwise. Directed rounding is monotone, so the rounded exact extreme equals the extreme of the rounded corners. One thing is not monotone: the sign of a zero end depends on which of several equal candidates the old code kept, so when an end is zero the old four-candidate form is used and the results stay bit-identical."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1149
msgid "**Trigonometric functions: no reduction near zero, one series for a point.** If both ends of the argument enclosure lie strictly inside $(-\\pi/4, \\pi/4)$ (the lower bound of $\\pi/4$ is half the lower bound of $\\pi/2$, exact), then $x / (\\pi/2) \\in (-\\tfrac12, \\tfrac12)$ at both ends, the nearest integer is $0$ for both, the product $0 \\cdot \\pi/2$ is $0$ and the reduced argument is the input itself; the division by $\\pi/2$ and the multiplication back are skipped, and the result is the one of the general path. When the reduced pair is a point (equal ends) the series is evaluated once and its two ends are used for both bounds, as evaluating twice gives the same value; `tan` takes the quotient corner by the sign of the cosine enclosure (a negative cosine uses $n / d = (-n) / (-d)$). The same point shortcut is used in `exp2`, `exp10`, `pow`, `rootn`, `log1p`, `asinh`, `acosh`, `atanh`, `expm1`-based `exp2m1` and `exp10m1`, and `compound`: `binary_bounds_at_ends` takes the lower end of the evaluation at the lower argument and the upper end of the evaluation at the upper argument, and evaluates once when the two arguments are equal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1151
msgid "**Trigonometric functions: one series at the midpoint of a reduced interval.** When the argument needs the reduction, the reduced enclosure $[\\ell, u]$ of $x - n \\pi/2$ has two distinct ends (it comes from both bounds of $\\pi$), and the enclosure of the series used to be the lower end of the series at $\\ell$ and the upper end of the series at $u$: two series evaluations. Sine and cosine are 1-Lipschitz ($|\\sin'| = |\\cos| \\le 1$, $|\\cos'| = |\\sin| \\le 1$). Take any value $m$ (here the midpoint $(\\ell + u)/2$ rounded to nearest at the working precision) and $h = \\max(u - m,\\ m - \\ell)$ with both differences rounded upward; then every $x \\in [\\ell, u]$ has $|x - m| \\le h$, so $|f(x) - f(m)| \\le h$ and $f(x) \\in [f(m)_{\\mathrm{low}} - h,\\ f(m)_{\\mathrm{high}} + h]$ with the two sums rounded outward, where $[f(m)_{\\mathrm{low}}, f(m)_{\\mathrm{high}}]$ is the series enclosure at $m$. Soundness does not need $m$ to lie in $[\\ell, u]$, since $h$ covers both distances whatever $m$ is. The widened enclosure is then clamped to what is known of the function: the range bounds $-1$ and $1$ for both functions (and the cosine keeps its upper end $1$ when $0 \\in [\\ell, u]$), and, for a sine whose reduced interval has ends of one sign, the sign (the lower end is at least $0$ when $\\ell > 0$, the upper end at most $0$ when $u < 0$). The clamp matters by this rule: a widened end that crosses a bound the true value sits on, or within the directed-rounding unit of at every work precision, never converges, while one that crosses a bound the true value is strictly inside of converges as refinement shrinks $h$. The cosine of a tiny argument is $1 - x^2/2$, closer to $1$ than the work precision resolves, so the unclamped upper end $1 + h$ lies above the breakpoint $1$ for ever and a directed rounding never reaches one value (the first form of this change returned an error, a NaN from `cospi`, for such arguments). No other result is of that kind: the sine of a point interval is not widened (its upper end is the series' own), and for the distinct ends of `sinpi` and `tanpi` of a tiny argument the widened upper end exceeds the two-ended one by at most one unit of the work precision, which is the strictly inside kind. No two-ended fallback remains. The endpoint $1$ is then decided by `binary_inexact_inner_endpoint`, inside `binary_certified_inexact_target_rounding` (the step of every refinement loop of these functions): the result is known not to be representable, so an enclosure end that is a rounding breakpoint of the target format is moved strictly inside, and $[1 - d, 1]$ certifies a directed rounding; the end $1 + h$ of the first form has far more bits than a breakpoint, so that step did not apply to it. The same holds for $-1$, which only the quadrant maps produce (as the negation of the capped cosine upper end). Near the bottom of the exponent range the ends and $h$ are rounded to the quantum of the work precision (a value of width $w$ stores nothing below $2^{e_{\\min} - (w - 1)}$); while at least 64 bits of the product lie above the quantum, $h$ is a few quanta against a midpoint of many bits and the lower end of the sine stays positive, and where fewer remain (the smallest subnormal of a wide precision used at a narrow one) the widened lower end can reach $0$ or below and is clamped at $0$, as the two-ended lower end was at most the quantum. One series is evaluated instead of two, the half-width enters the radius as an upper bound, and nothing else changes: `tan` takes the quotient of the two enclosures as before, so it needs no derivative bound of its own, and the widened cosine enclosure still excludes $0$ wherever the two-ended one did (the reduced argument is within $\\pi/4$, so the cosine is at least $0.7071$ and $h$ is a few units of the working precision). The new enclosure contains the exact value and is at most $u - \\ell$ wider on each side than the two-ended one (where the derivative is small the two-ended enclosure was narrower than the interval); $u - \\ell$ is a few units of the working precision (for a huge argument the ends are first rounded outward to the series precision, so it is of the order of the series error already in the enclosure), so the uniqueness test of the final rounding and the refinement loop are unchanged and can only retry when the result lies within $u - \\ell$ of a rounding boundary. The tests keep the two-ended function and check that the new enclosure lies inside the old one widened by $2 (u - \\ell)$ plus a few units, that both contain the enclosure at a working precision 256 bits higher, that `tan` declines exactly where the old one does, and a named test of an argument with an exponent of a few thousand. A sweep of `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `sinpi`, `cospi` and `tanpi` of $\\pm 2^e$ and $\\pm 3 \\cdot 2^e$ for $e$ from $-60$ down to the bottom of the range, of the smallest subnormals of each tested precision and the quanta above them, and of floats just above, at and below multiples of $\\pi/2$, in the five rounding directions at precisions $2$, $3$, $24$, $53$ and $113$, pins every value and flag to the two-ended form, requires no NaN, and fails if a case takes more refinement rounds than the two-ended form plus one. Why the first form passed its tests: the random operands (1.26 million) and the huge-argument test never ran a directed rounding on a result within one unit of the range bound, so the enclosure above $1$ never mattered; a reviewer of a change that widens an enclosure should ask for the directed-mode sweep at the range bounds first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1153
msgid "**`rootn` and `rsqrt` by one integer root.** For $2 \\le |n| \\le 32$ (within the gate described below) the result is rounded in one step, without a Ziv loop. For a positive $x = c 2^{e}$ with $2^t \\le x < 2^{t+1}$, the top bit of $x^{1/n}$ is $k = \\lfloor t/n \\rfloor$ ($t + 1 \\le n (k + 1)$ because $t < n (k + 1)$ are integers); the top bit of $x^{-1/n}$ is $-k - 1$, or $-\\lceil t/n \\rceil$ when $c = 1$ ($x = 2^t$ exactly), by the same inequality on $1/x$. With $T$ the exponent of the last place of the rounded result (the subnormal grid included), take $g = T - 1$ (half a unit) and the integer $y = \\lfloor x 2^{-n g} \\rfloor$ (for $x^{1/n}$) or $\\lfloor 2^{-n g} / x \\rfloor$ (for $x^{-1/n}$); since $\\lfloor v^{1/n} \\rfloor = \\lfloor \\lfloor v \\rfloor^{1/n} \\rfloor$ for real $v \\ge 0$ (an integer $r$ has $r^n \\le v$ exactly when $r^n \\le \\lfloor v \\rfloor$), $r_2 = \\lfloor y^{1/n} \\rfloor$ (the integer square root kernel for $n = 2$, the integer Newton root otherwise) is $\\lfloor |x|^{\\pm 1/n} / 2^{g} \\rfloor$. The retained part is $r_2 \\gg 1$, the round bit is the low bit of $r_2$, and the sticky bit is \"the root is not exactly $r_2 2^{g}$\", which holds unless $y$ was exact (no dropped bits, no remainder) and $y = r_2^n$: a boundary of any rounding direction is a multiple of half a unit, so a root strictly between $r_2$ and $r_2 + 1$ is on the same side of every boundary as $r_2$ with the sticky bit set. `binary_round_pack` then decides the result and the flags as for the square root, including the subnormal range and the tininess rule after rounding (a second root on the unbounded grid when it differs). The integer has about $n (p + 2)$ bits and the Newton steps of its root raise a number of that size to the power $n - 1$, so the path is used only where it is measurably faster than the certified `exp(ln(x) / n)` loop, which gets the fast `exp` and `ln` kernels from about 1500 bits on. Instructions retired, loop divided by integer root: for $n \\le 12$ the integer root wins at every precision measured (53 to 32768 bits) by 2.4 to 300 times; for $13 \\le n \\le 32$ it wins by 6.7 to 49 times up to 1024 bits, but at 2048 bits $n = 16$ gains only 1.6 times, $n = 20$ 1.1 times, and $n = 24$ and $n = 32$ are 1.3 and 2.2 times slower. The gate is therefore $|n| \\le 12$, or $|n| \\le 32$ with context precision at most 1024 bits, and the loop elsewhere; the tests compare the public result with the loop on both sides of each bound. The exact roots of perfect powers are still decided before either path. Instructions retired per call (53, 128, 512 bits): `rsqrt` 57, 23 and 35 times fewer, `rootn` with $n = 3$ 2.1, 3.6 and 11 times fewer."
msgstr ""

