#: manual/design/binary.md:1159
msgid "`sin`, `cos` and `tan` of an argument $x$ with $2^{k-1} \\le |x| < 2^k$ reduce $x$ modulo $\\pi/2$, which cancels the top $k$ bits of $x$: the reduction needs $\\pi$ to $p + k + 96$ bits for a result of $p$ bits. The reduced argument lies in $(-\\pi/4, \\pi/4)$ and the series needs only the precision of the result, so for $k > 256$ the enclosure of the reduced argument is rounded outward to $p + 96$ bits before the series (the reduction and the series are separate precisions). The working precision of the reduction is limited to 8,000,000 bits, so that every argument of the decimal operand range ($10^{999999} < 2^{3321929}$) is evaluated; a larger work size is a `ResourceLimit` certification failure. The `decimal` driver passes a huge integer operand to `Binary` exactly (an integer $c \\cdot 10^e$ is a dyadic rational) at the result precision, and `interval` evaluates a point interval by two directed evaluations without the rational index computation."
