#: manual/design/binary.md:1203
msgid "Asymptotically fast elementary kernels (implemented, with open items)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1205
msgid "*Status: design and results for [#18](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/18). The constants (`pi`, `ln 2`, `ln 10` by binary splitting), the `exp` enclosure (bit-burst, and by tables at medium precision), the `ln` enclosure (Newton step and arithmetic-geometric mean) and the `sin` and `cos` enclosures are implemented and merged; `tan` keeps the series. The measured figures are baselines or results of the implemented part; the figures marked \"projection\" come from a cost model fitted to them and are replaced by measurements as each step lands. The final measurement of the merged kernels is in \"Measured result\" below. All arguments in this section are paper arguments; none is mechanized.*"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1216
msgid "**Problem.** Every enclosure above is built from a Taylor or artanh series whose terms are $w$-bit numbers, so one evaluation costs $O(w / \\log w)$ full multiplications, that is $O(w\\, M(w) / \\log w)$. At $w \\approx 8200$ bits on the native target `exp` takes 17.8 ms, `ln` 164 ms and `sin` 64 ms (before the fixed-point `ln` and `sin` branches), while the $8192 \\times 8192$-bit product takes 43 µs: `exp` is about 400 products. The constants are worse when cold: `pi` takes 742 ms, `ln 2` 297 ms and `ln 10` 146 ms at 8192 bits, which a call without a cached constant pays in full (MPFR on the same machine: 0.09 ms for `pi`, 0.29 ms for `ln 2`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1226
msgid "**Options.** (1) Binary splitting of the series of a rational argument, $O(M(w) \\log^2 w)$. (2) Bit-burst: split the argument into chunks of doubling length, evaluate the exponential of each chunk by a series with few terms (the later the chunk, the smaller it is), multiply the results: $O(M(w) \\log^2 w)$ with binary splitting inside and, with the plain fixed-point series inside, one cheap unbalanced product per term (measured: $8192 \\times 64$ bits 1.8 µs, $\\times 1024$ bits 6.9 µs, $\\times 8192$ bits 43 µs). (3) Newton iteration for `ln` on top of `exp`. (4) The arithmetic-geometric mean (AGM) for `ln` and `pi`, $O(M(w) \\log w)$ with large constants. (5) More halving and squaring: the squarings cost one full product each and lose a bit each, so it does not scale."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1239
msgid "**Choice.** Keep the Ziv loop, its working precisions and the exact cases untouched (see [Certified elementary functions](#certified-elementary-functions)). Replace only the function that produces the enclosure $[L, U]$ for a given $w$, and only from a per-target precision threshold upward. Every new kernel is a pure function $(x, w) \\mapsto [L, U]$ of exactly the type the loop already consumes (`binary_exp_bounds`, `binary_ln_bounds`, `binary_trigonometric_bounds`, and the constants), so `exp2`, `exp10`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `pow`, `rootn` and the logarithm family inherit the speed-up, and the recommended functions of [#235](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/235) (`compound`, `powr`, `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `log2p1`, `log10p1`, `rSqrt`, the $\\pi$-scaled inverses) are written on the same layer. A kernel returns `None` for any precondition it cannot verify, and the caller then runs the existing path, so correctness never depends on the new code covering a case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1254
msgid "*Integer enclosures.* All new kernels work on non-negative integers in units of $2^{-F}$ with $F = w + g$, using only `Coeff` operations (`mul`, `add`, `sub_checked`, shifts, `div_rem_checked`, `compare`, `bit_length`), which exist on every target, so the same source runs on native, LLVM, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript. A quantity $E$ is carried as a pair $(\\ell, u)$ of integers with the invariant $\\ell \\le E \\cdot 2^{F} \\le u$. Every step produces a new pair from the old ones by monotone operations on non-negative numbers, so the invariant is proved step by step, and the final width $u - \\ell$ is a computed integer, not an estimate. The last step converts to `Binary::make_coeff(coeff, exp, w, mode=RoundTowardNegative / RoundTowardPositive)`, which is what the existing fixed-point series already does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1267
msgid "**The `ln` series loop on limb arrays.** The fixed-point series of `ln` ($\\ln v = 2 \\sum_k P(k) / (2k + 1)$ with $P(0) = Z$, $P(k) = \\lfloor P(k-1) Q / 2^F \\rfloor$, the terms $T(k) = \\lfloor P(k) / (2k + 1) \\rfloor$ and the stop at $P(k) < 2^6$) computed each term with four `Coeff` operations that each allocate a result (the product, the shift, the quotient with its remainder, the sum): about four allocations and 3,700 instructions per term at 576 working bits. On the targets with limb rows the loop keeps `P`, `Q`, the product, the quotient and the sum in five limb arrays allocated once per call. A term is the exact product by `bin_limbs_mul_1` and `bin_limbs_addmul_1` (so the same integer as `P(k-1) Q`), the floor by $2^F$ as one copy pass with the shift $F = 64 w + r$ (limb offset $w$, bit offset $r$, the case $r = 0$ apart), the quotient by the odd divisor in place from the top limb, and the sum with a carry. The divisor is at most $20001$ (the loop stops after 10000 terms), so the running remainder $\\rho < 2^{15}$ and $\\rho \\cdot 2^{32} + h < 2^{47}$ for a half limb $h$: a limb divides by two hardware divisions of its 32-bit halves, no 128-bit helper. Every integer equals the `Coeff` form's, so the bounds and their error analysis (above) are unchanged bit for bit; the JavaScript target keeps the `Coeff` form, since a host `BigInt` has no limb rows. The tests keep the `Coeff` loop as the reference and compare the sum and the term count for every $F$ from 60 to 700 (so every residue of $F$ modulo 64, in particular $F + 1$ a multiple of 64 and one below and above it, at one to eleven limbs), for terms whose product has a zero top limb, and the bounds of the whole function on exact values at 380 working precisions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1269
msgid "**Shared fixed-point toolkit.** The kernels below share one private layer, `elementary_toolkit.mbt` (issue [#368](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/368)); each piece states its error bound once, and the kernel sections add only what is specific to their series. The extraction is a pure refactor: every kernel returns the same enclosure $(L, U)$ as before, bit for bit and `None` in the same cases, which `elementary_toolkit_digest_wbtest.mbt` checks with one digest per kernel and working precision (a corpus around every per-target threshold, the multiples of $\\ln 2$ and $\\pi/2$, tiny and large arguments) against a table generated on the kernels before the extraction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1280
msgid "*Directed conversion* (`fixed_scaled_floor`, `fixed_scaled_exact`, `fixed_shift`, `fixed_enclosure`). A fixed-point integer $N$ stands for $N / 2^{F}$. `fixed_scaled_floor` rounds toward $-\\infty$ and loses less than one unit; `fixed_scaled_exact` is `None` unless the scaling is exact; `fixed_enclosure` turns an integer pair $(\\ell, u)$ into floats rounded outward (lower end `RoundTowardNegative`, upper end `RoundTowardPositive`), swapping the ends for a negative result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1287
msgid "*Bounded cache* (`BoundedCache[V]`: `find`, `insert`, capacity as a parameter, oldest entry dropped first). It replaces the three ad-hoc caches (constants, 6 entries per constant; `exp` tables, 24 sets; factorial coefficients, 64 entries). A cached value is a pure function of its key, so a hit returns the value a recomputation returns."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1292
msgid "*Term-by-term stage* (`fixed_series_step`, `fixed_exp_series`). With $y = N / 2^{s} < 1$, $t_0 = 2^{F}$ and $t_i = \\lfloor \\lfloor t_{i-1} N / 2^{s} \\rfloor / i \\rfloor$, the exact term $\\tau_i = 2^{F} y^i / i!$ satisfies $t_i \\le \\tau_i < t_i + 3$ (the deficit $d_i$ obeys $d_i < d_{i-1} y / i + 1/i + 1$). `fixed_exp_series` sums until a term is below $2^{8}$ and returns the sum, the last term and its index; the exact value is within $t_n + 3n + 3$ above the sum. The fixed `exp` stage and the bit-burst `exp` stage use it with their own slack constants (4 and 3), and the burst `sin`/`cos` stage uses the step for the real and imaginary parts of $e^{iy}$. `binary_ln_unit_fixed_bounds` and `binary_sin_cos_fixed_series` stay apart: their recurrences are in $z^2$ and $x^2$ with different divisors and different tail proofs (an artanh series with ratio $z^2 \\le 1/9$, an alternating series), not the recurrence above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1305
msgid "*Binary splitting* (`SplitState`, `split_combine`, `split_combine_t`). A state on $[from, to)$ is $(p, q, q\\_shift, t)$ with $p = \\prod p(k)$, $q = \\prod q'(k)$, the power of two of $q(k) = q'(k) 2^{e(k)}$ kept apart in $q\\_shift = \\sum e(k)$, and $t = q\\, 2^{q\\_shift} \\cdot$ (partial sum), merged as $t = t_{\\mathrm{left}}\\, q_{\\mathrm{right}} 2^{q\\_shift_{\\mathrm{right}}} + p_{\\mathrm{left}}\\, t_{\\mathrm{right}}$. The constants (artanh and Chudnovsky series) use $q\\_shift = 0$, the series of $e^{N/2^s}$ uses $q\\_shift = s$ per index from 1 (`split_exp_state`). All of it is exact integer arithmetic, so the partial sum is the exact rational $t / (q\\, 2^{q\\_shift})$ and the error bound of a kernel is the bound of its omitted tail alone. The complex stage of `sin`/`cos` (`SplitComplexState`) keeps its own type because it carries two components and rotates the right component by $i^{\\mathrm{leaves}_{\\mathrm{left}}}$ before the merge; it shares `split_combine_t` and the products of $p$, $q$ and $q\\_shift$. `fixed_split_terms` gives the number of terms for which the omitted tail of $\\sum N^k / (k!\\, 2^{sk})$ is below two units (the proof is in its comment)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1322
msgid "*Rectangular splitting* (`rect_plan`, `rect_powers`, `rect_sum`, `rect_inverse_factorials`). For a series $\\sum_{i<N} c_i r^i$ with nonnegative integer coefficients, $r = a / 2^{F} < 2^{-k}$, $k \\ge 4$, and $N = b m$ terms, the powers $p_j = \\lfloor p_u p_v / 2^{F} \\rfloor$ ($u + v = j$) satisfy $p_j \\le 2^{F} r^j < p_j + 2$. With $P_\\beta = \\sum_{j<m} c_{\\beta m + j} p_j$, $\\mathrm{Acc}_{b-1} = P_{b-1}$ and $\\mathrm{Acc}_\\beta = P_\\beta + \\lfloor p_m \\mathrm{Acc}_{\\beta+1} / 2^{F} \\rfloor$, the sum satisfies $\\mathrm{Acc}_0 \\le 2^{F} \\sum c_i r^i$ and its deficit $\\varepsilon_\\beta$, in the units of the common denominator of the $c_i$, obeys $\\varepsilon_\\beta < 2 \\sum_j c_{\\beta m + j} + 2\\,\\mathrm{Acc}_{\\beta+1} / 2^{F} + 1 + r^m \\varepsilon_{\\beta+1}$. The coefficient sequence is the parameter: `rect_inverse_factorials` gives $c_i = N!/i!$ for $e^r$ (deficit below 8 units of $N!$, the constant 10 of `binary_exp_taylor_fixed`); the next kernels pass $c_i = D/(2i+1)$ with a common multiple $D$ (the odd reciprocals of $\\ln$ and $\\arctan$) or $N!/(2i)!$ and $N!/(2i+1)!$ (sine and cosine, whose alternating signs the caller splits into a positive and a negative series in $r^2$ with nonnegative coefficients). The block size minimizes a cost estimate (`rect_block_size`) and never changes a result bound; `rect_factorial_terms` is the term count for factorial decay."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1342
msgid "*Reduction by $\\ln 2$* (`fixed_exp_reduce`, `fixed_exp_finish`), the integer reduction and the closing units of the bit-burst and the table `exp`, described in the next two sections, and the chunk schedule of the bit-burst (`fixed_burst_chunks`), used by the `exp` and the `sin`/`cos` kernels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1348
msgid "The guard-bit constants (`binary_exp_fixed_guard_bits` 16, `binary_exp_table_guard_bits` 16, `binary_exp_burst_guard_bits` 48 and the 64 extra bits of the `sin`/`cos` burst) stay separate on purpose: they are tuned per kernel (they set the width of the final enclosure, which the width checks compare with the working precision), and changing one changes the enclosure, which this refactor must not do. The mutation hooks (`BurstMutation`, `BurstLnMutation`, `BurstTrigMutation`) stay in the kernels, since they name the kernel's own proof obligations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1357
msgid ""
"**`exp`, by ln 2 reduction and bit-burst.** For finite $x \\n"
"eq 0$ with $|x| < 2^{30}$ (larger magnitudes are decided before the loop or fall back), let $n = \\lfloor x / \\ln 2 \\rfloor$, found from the cached enclosure of $\\ln 2$ at $G = F + \\operatorname{bits}(n) + 2$ bits. Let $L = \\lfloor 2^{G} \\ln 2 \\rfloor / 2^{G}$ (a dyadic lower bound with $0 \\le \\ln 2 - L \\le 2^{1-G}$) and $R = x - nL$, computed exactly. If $R < 0$ or $R \\ge 1$ the integer $n$ is corrected by one and $R$ recomputed. Put $R_t = \\lfloor 2^{F} R \\rfloor / 2^{F}$ and $\\delta = (x - n \\ln 2) - R_t$. Then $-|n|\\,2^{1-G} \\le \\delta \\le 2^{-F}$ for $n \\ge 0$ (mirrored for $n < 0$), so $|\\delta| \\le 2^{-F}$ by the choice of $G$, and since $0 \\le R_t < 1$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1373
msgid "The factor $e^\\delta$ costs at most $2 \\cdot 3 = 6$ units at the end (because $e^{R_t} < 3$), and no sign handling or reciprocal is needed because $R_t \\ge 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1377
msgid "Write $R_t = \\sum_j y_j$ with $y_j = N_j / 2^{b_{j+1}}$ the bits of $R_t$ at positions $(b_j, b_{j+1}]$ after the binary point, $0 = b_0 < b_1 < \\dots$, and $b$ growing geometrically (factor about 4, first chunk about 16 bits; the constants are tuned per target). Then $y_j < 2^{-b_j}$, $N_j < 2^{b_{j+1} - b_j}$, and $e^{R_t} = \\prod_j e^{y_j}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1383
msgid "*Stage kernel.* For $y = N / 2^{s} < 1$ let $t_0 = 2^{F}$ and $t_i = \\lfloor \\lfloor t_{i-1} N / 2^{s} \\rfloor / i \\rfloor$ (the second floor is absent for $i = 1$), and let $\\tau_i = 2^{F} y^i / i!$ be the exact terms. By induction $t_i \\le \\tau_i$ (both floors round down), and the deficit $d_i = \\tau_i - t_i$ satisfies $d_1 < 1$ and $d_i < (d_{i-1}\\, y + 1)/i + 1$, hence $d_2 < 2$ and $d_i < 4/3 + 1 < 3$ for $i \\ge 3$ (this is the argument of the fixed-point series already in `elementary_fixed_exp.mbt`, with $y < 1$ in place of $y \\le 1/8$). Stop at the first $n$ with $t_n < 2^{8}$. For $n \\ge 1$ the ratio of consecutive exact terms is at most $y/(n+1) \\le 1/2$, so $\\sum_{i > n} \\tau_i \\le \\tau_n \\cdot \\frac{1/2}{1 - 1/3} < \\tau_n < t_n + 3$. With $S = \\sum_{i=0}^{n} t_i$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1401
msgid "Then $(\\ell, u) \\mapsto (\\lfloor \\ell\\, S / 2^{F} \\rfloor,\\; \\lceil u\\,(S + e_y) / 2^{F} \\rceil)$ folds the stage into the running product: $e^{y}$ and the partial product are in the stated intervals, both are non-negative, and multiplication is monotone on non-negative numbers, so the new pair satisfies the invariant. Each stage adds at most $e_y + 2$ units of relative width, so with at most $J$ stages the final width is below $J (2^{8} + 3 n_{\\max} + 5) + 6$ units, about $2^{15}$ units for $w = 8192$; $g = 48$ guard bits therefore keep the enclosure within $2^{-(w+6)}$ relative width, which is the bound the kernel checks (the current series gives about $2^{-(w+7)}$). The kernel computes the true width and returns `None` when it exceeds that bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1413
msgid "*One full product per chunk.* The pair $(\\ell, u)$ and the stage $[l, s]$ ($l \\le e^{y} \\cdot 2^F \\le s$, both in units of $2^{-F}$) are folded as $(\\lfloor \\ell\\, l / 2^F \\rfloor,\\; \\lfloor u\\, s / 2^F \\rfloor + 1)$. Put $w = u - \\ell$ and $r = s - l$. Both are short, and the kernel guarantees it: $r$ is below $2^{8} + 3 n + 5$ (the stage bound above), and $w$ never decreases from one fold to the next (each factor $l / 2^F \\ge 1$ since $e^{y} \\ge 1$), so $w$ is at most the final width, which the final check limits to $2^{g - 6} = 2^{42}$ units, one limb. Observed in 150 folds of the kernel at working precisions 2112 and 8256 bits (36 pseudo-random arguments with magnitudes from $2^{-20}$ to $2^{12}$): $w$ at most 9 bits and $r$ at most 8 bits. Then $u\\, s = \\ell\\, l + \\ell\\, r + w\\, s$ exactly, so the upper end is formed from the product $\\ell\\, l$ that the lower end already needs, plus two products by short numbers: one full product and two unbalanced ones instead of two full products. The integers are the same as before, so no error term arises and the width argument above is unchanged. The first chunk starts from $\\ell = u = 2^F$ and is the stage itself, $(l, s + 1)$, without a product."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1415
msgid "*Cost.* A stage with $m$-bit numerator at position $b$ needs $n_b \\approx F / (b + \\log_2 n_b)$ terms, each a product of the shrinking term by an $m$-bit integer, a shift and a division by a one-limb integer. For $w = 8192$ the first chunk (16 bits) needs about 880 terms, the chunk at 65 to 261 bits about 120, the chunk at 261 to 1045 bits about 31, and the last chunks fewer than 10. The early chunks have too many terms for the fixed-point loop; they are evaluated by **binary splitting** of $\\sum_{i<n} N^i / (i!\\, 2^{si})$ with exact integers $(P, Q, T)$, $T = T_L Q_R 2^{s\\,|R|} + P_L T_R$, $P = P_L P_R$, $Q = Q_L Q_R$. The enclosure then has no accumulated rounding: $\\ell = \\lfloor 2^{F} T / (Q 2^{sn}) \\rfloor$, $u = \\ell + 3$ once $n$ is large enough that $N^n 2^{F} < n!\\, 2^{sn}$ (an integer comparison on bit lengths), because then the tail is at most $2\\, \\tau_n < 2$ units. A stage is estimated (not yet measured) at 8 to 14 full products independent of $N$, a fixed-point stage with $n$ terms costs $n$ unbalanced products, so the crossover per stage is at $n$ of a few tens of terms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1431
msgid "*The merge of the tree computes the least that the integers need.* The values $(P, Q, T)$ are exact, so any way of forming them that gives the same integers gives the same enclosure and needs no new error bound; the tree of the stages of `exp` and of `sin` and `cos` forms them with less work than the textbook merge. (1) $P_L$ is only read when the node is a left child, and every node of one level has $N^{k}$ for the same few $k$ (the lengths of the ranges of a level differ by at most one), so $P$ is not carried: the merge reads $N^{\\ell}$, with $\\ell$ the number of indices $\\ge 1$ of the left range, from a table of the powers of $N$ that makes each power once as a square or as a product of two smaller ones (about $2 \\log_2 n$ powers for $n$ terms, 28 products for the 315 and 105 terms of the first two chunks at 2048 bits, against 418 products of $P$). (2) The shift by $s\\,|R|$ and the sum are one operation, `Coeff::add_shifted_left`, equal to the shift followed by the sum, in one limb array instead of two. (3) A node of two leaves, indices $k \\ge 1$, is written out: $Q = k (k+1)$, $T = N ((k+1) 2^{s} + N)$ (for `sin` and `cos` the real part is $-N N$ and the imaginary part $N (k+1) 2^{s}$), which replaces two leaf states and the general merge. The tests keep the old tree as a reference and compare the integers: equal for 390 combinations of numerator size ($1$ to $768$ bits), shift and term count, and the enclosures of the kernels are checked against the series at a working precision 256 bits higher."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1433
msgid "*Implementation (`elementary_burst_exp.mbt`).* The kernel `binary_exp_bounds_burst` follows the construction above with these choices: $g = 48$ guard bits; chunk boundaries at $16, 64, 256, \\dots$ (first chunk 16 bits, factor 4); a chunk is evaluated by binary splitting when its term count (from the integer bound on $2^F N^n < n!\\, 2^{sn}$, using $\\log_2 n! \\ge \\sum_{i \\le n} (\\operatorname{bits}(i) - 1)$) is at least 40, and by the fixed-point series otherwise; `ln 2` is requested at the next multiple of 64 above $G + 2$. The reduced argument comes from one Euclidean division of $x 2^G$ by $\\lfloor 2^G \\ln 2 \\rfloor$, so $0 \\le R < \\ln 2$ with no correction loop. The kernel declines (`None`, and the series runs) for $|x| < 2^{-65}$ or $|x| \\ge 2^{30}$, when $|n| \\ge 2^{29}$ (the result exponent would approach the limit of the exponent range), when $x$ has bits below $2^{-G}$, and when the computed width exceeds $2^{-(w+6)}$ of the value. (Below the table kernel's ceiling, next section, the table kernel is tried first.) It is used from a per-target working precision, kept with the coefficient thresholds: 1024 bits on Native and LLVM, 1088 on Wasm, 640 on Wasm-GC, 256 on JavaScript (the kernel is slower than the series below roughly 1000 bits on Native, 600 on Wasm-GC; on JavaScript it wins already at 256). The three chunk constants were swept on Native; in a repeat on a quiet machine the chosen point (16, factor 4, split at 40 terms) was the fastest at 8256 bits (1.5 ms against 1.6 to 2.3 ms for the other tried settings) and within about 15 % of the fastest at 2112 bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1455
msgid "The sweep was repeated after the binary-splitting trees and the fold became cheaper (36 settings, 1024 to 8192 bits of precision, `exp` and `sin`, three arguments per size, instructions by the two-count method). The constants (16, 4, 40) stay the best at 8192 bits, but below that a larger first chunk and fewer splitting stages win, and the spread between arguments at one setting is 25 to 30 percent, so a setting is judged by its ratio to the old one on the same argument. On Native the constants are therefore functions of the working precision `w`: for `w <= 3000` the first chunk is 24 bits, the factor 8 and the splitting starts at 80 terms; for `3000 < w <= 6000` the same with factor 4; above 6000 the constants above. Against (16, 4, 40) the instructions per call are 0.69 to 0.99 for `sin` and 0.81 to 0.98 for `exp` at working precisions up to 4160, and the two settings are the same code above 6000. The other targets keep (16, 4, 40) at every precision (not measured). A different chunking gives a different but valid enclosure, so the digest rows of the kernels pin the old constants explicitly and new rows pin the kernels with the constants of the working precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1457
msgid "The sweep for `exp` alone was repeated after the assembly limb rows became the Native default, in both native states (the default rows and `LUNA_FLOATING_LIMBS=c`), on five arguments per size (three random values in [1, 2), one near $2^{-9}$, one at 0.69) and by the same executable with explicit settings, so that the comparison is not across builds (two builds of identical code differ by up to 3.5 percent in instruction counts). The setting (24, 8, 80) loses 6 to 15 percent on random arguments from 2300 bits of precision on, although it gains up to 2048 bits, and (24, 4, 80) loses 4 to 8 percent up to 2300 bits; so `exp` has its own constants on Native: for `w <= 2200` (24, 8, 80), for `2200 < w <= 2700` (24, 8, 120), for `2700 < w <= 6000` (24, 4, 80), above 6000 (16, 4, 40). Against (16, 4, 40), no argument at any measured size (1024, 1536, 2048, 2304, 2560, 2816, 3072, 3584, 4096, 5120, 6144 bits) is above 1.00 in the default state or above 1.01 in the C state. `sin` and `cos` keep the constants above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1459
msgid "*Evidence for the `exp` kernel.* White-box tests (`elementary_burst_exp_wbtest.mbt`): the enclosure at $w$ contains the series enclosure at $w + 256$ for every argument the kernel accepts in a corpus of 224 values of 60 to 2000 bits with $|x|$ from $2^{-60}$ to $2^{30}$, and for arguments next to multiples of $\\ln 2$, to $2^{29} - 1$ and to $1 - 2^{-k}$; it is no wider than the series enclosure at $w$; and `exp_ctx` returns the same value and flags as the Ziv loop run on the series alone, in four rounding directions. Three deliberately wrong variants are caught by the containment test: a bias of 64 units in every stage, 40 terms fewer in the binary-splitting stages, and `ln 2` taken with 128 bits fewer for an argument near $2^{27}$. Two obligations are not exercised by such tests: the slack of the fixed-point stages and the units for $\\delta$ are dominated by the much larger worst-case terms of the other stages, so an enclosure without them still contains the value; their justification is the paper argument above, not the tests. MPFR 4.2.2 comparison: 35,040 rows (precisions 256 to 4096, four rounding directions, arguments from $2^{-60}$ to $2^{28}$ and next to multiples of $\\ln 2$) all match; the output is byte-identical on Native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript; the random differential run (`elem`) agrees on 100,000 cases on Native and 20,000 on each other target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1479
msgid "*Measured (native, release, this machine).* Kernel only, $w = p + 64$ bits, $x \\approx 0.7$: 20 ms to 1.97 ms at 8256 bits, 3.3 ms to 0.67 ms at 4160, 594 to 220 µs at 2112, 112 to 87 µs at 1088; below 1000 bits it is slower (37 to 41 µs at 576). End to end, `just bench external quick --ops exp` paired and alternating with `origin/main` (four rounds, the quiet ones last), floating time per call: 8192 bits 20.2 and 20.4 ms against 1.78 and 1.69 ms (paired ratios 9.1 to 12.1), 2048 bits 508 and 517 µs against 187 and 196 µs (2.1 to 2.7), and 53 and 512 bits unchanged within the noise of the machine (0.92 to 1.19). Against the stored MPFR reference of that tool the ratio of the 8192-bit call moves from about 95 to about 8 on the machine of the run (the machine reads about 1.4 times slower than the stored run, so the drift corrected values are about 66 and 5.6)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1492
msgid "*Ceiling.* At 8192 bits the kernel takes the time of 35 to 45 full-size products (43 µs each) and spends most of it in products and divisions of $w$-bit numbers. With a multiplication and division kernel three times faster, and everything else unchanged, a first estimate (not measured) is 0.6 to 0.8 ms at 8192 bits and 60 to 80 µs at 2048 bits, which is about 3 to 4 times the MPFR reference (213 µs and 17 µs). The remaining gap would be allocation and the small-integer steps of the fixed-point stages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1501
msgid "**`exp`, by tables and rectangular splitting (medium precision).** Below a per-target working precision (1536 to 3072 bits, see below) the enclosure of $e^x$ comes from `binary_exp_bounds_table` (`elementary_table_exp.mbt`), the scheme of Johansson, *Efficient implementation of elementary functions in the medium-precision range*, ARITH 22 (2015), arXiv:1410.7176, on the integer arithmetic of `Coeff`. Let $F = w + g$ rounded up to a multiple of 64 ($\\ge 128$; multiples of 256 above 1024), so $g \\ge 16$. The reduction to $R_t = \\lfloor 2^F R \\rfloor / 2^F \\in [0, \\ln 2)$ and the residual factor $e^{\\delta}$ are those of the bit-burst kernel above (3 units below, 6 above in units of $2^{-F}$, valid because $e^{R_t} < 3$), the very code of `fixed_exp_reduce` and `fixed_exp_finish` (toolkit piece 6). The Taylor series of the remainder is the rectangular splitting of toolkit piece 5 with the coefficients $N!/i!$; its error bound is stated there and not repeated below. Then, with $K = k_1 + k_2$ ($(k_1,k_2) = (7,7)$ for $F \\le 256$, $(6,6)$ up to 1024, $(6,5)$ above),"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1524
msgid "all three parts exact multiples of $2^{-F}$, so $e^{R_t} = T_1[j_1]\\, T_2[j_2]\\, e^{r}$ with $T_1[j] = e^{j/2^{k_1}}$ ($j \\le \\lfloor 2^{k_1} \\ln 2 \\rfloor$) and $T_2[j] = e^{j/2^{K}}$ ($j < 2^{k_2}$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1528
msgid "*Tables.* Built lazily, one set per value of $F$ (at most 24 sets are kept), each entry an integer enclosure $L \\le 2^{F} e^{j/2^k} \\le H$ taken from the certified kernels already in the library (`binary_exp_bounds_untabled`, the bit-burst or series path, at $F + 8$ bits): $L = \\lfloor 2^F \\cdot \\mathrm{low} \\rfloor$, $H = \\lfloor 2^F \\cdot \\mathrm{high} \\rfloor + 1$. A table is therefore as certified as the kernels it comes from, and a test recomputes every entry of two table sets from the series at $F + 256$ bits. The tables are not hard-coded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1536
msgid "*Taylor series of $r$.* Write $r = a / 2^F$, $0 \\le a < 2^{F-K}$ (so $r < 2^{-K}$, $K \\ge 11$ in the shapes above). The number of terms $N$ is the least with $K N + \\sum_{i \\le N} (\\operatorname{bits}(i) - 1) \\ge F + 1$; as $N! \\ge 2^{\\sum (\\operatorname{bits}(i)-1)}$ and the ratio of consecutive terms is below $1/2$, the tail satisfies $\\sum_{i \\ge N} r^i/i! \\le 2 r^N/N! \\le 2^{-F}$: at most one unit. The sum is evaluated by the rectangular (Paterson–Stockmeyer) splitting of toolkit piece 5, with $N$ rounded up to $N' = b m$, the integer coefficients $C_i = N'!/i!$ (computed once per $N'$) and one final division, $E = \\lfloor \\mathrm{Acc}_0 / N'! \\rfloor$; the powers $p_j$ and the recurrence for $\\mathrm{Acc}_\\beta$ are those defined there, with $e_j := 2^F r^j - p_j < 2$ (so $e_0 = e_1 = 0$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1548
msgid "*Error of the series.* The exact counterpart is $\\mathrm{Acc}^*_0 = N'!\\, 2^F \\sum_{i<N'} r^i/i!$, and every computed quantity is a lower bound of its exact counterpart. Let $\\varepsilon_\\beta = \\mathrm{Acc}^*_\\beta - \\mathrm{Acc}_\\beta$ in units of $N'!$. The deficit of $P_\\beta$ is $\\sum_{j\\ge 2} C_{\\beta m+j} e_j / N'! < 2 \\sum_{j \\ge 2} 1/(\\beta m + j)! \\le 2(e - 2) < 1.44$ ($e_0 = e_1 = 0$). In the Horner step the error of $p_m$ contributes $e_m \\mathrm{Acc}^*_{\\beta+1} / (2^F N'!) < 2 e^{r} < 3.4$ (because $\\mathrm{Acc}^*_{\\beta+1} \\le N'!\\, 2^F e^{r}$), the propagated error $p_m \\varepsilon_{\\beta+1} / 2^F < \\varepsilon_{\\beta+1}/16$, and the floor less than one unit. So $\\varepsilon_\\beta < 1.44 + 3.4 + 1 + \\varepsilon_{\\beta+1}/16$, whence $\\varepsilon < 6.2 \\cdot 16/15 < 7$ for every block. The final floor loses less than one more unit, and the tail at most one:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1565
msgid "*Assembly.* With the table entries (already in units of $2^{-F}$), $\\mathrm{low} = \\lfloor \\lfloor L_1 L_2 / 2^F \\rfloor E / 2^F \\rfloor - 3$ and $\\mathrm{high} = \\lfloor \\lfloor H_1 H_2 / 2^F \\rfloor + 1) (E + 10) / 2^F \\rfloor + 1 + 6$. All factors are positive, so the floor-and-add-one products are monotone enclosures; the final enclosure is then rounded outward to $w$ bits. The kernel declines (the older paths run) in the same cases as the bit-burst kernel ($|x| < 2^{-65}$, $|x| \\ge 2^{30}$, $|n| \\ge 2^{29}$, $x$ with bits below $2^{-G}$) and when the width exceeds $2^{-(w+6)}$ of the value. The width of the enclosure is a few dozen units of $2^{-F}$ (the series 10, the tables 2 each, the reduction 9, the products another factor up to 2), far below the $2^{F-w-6}$ units the check allows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1577
msgid "*Why results are bit-identical.* The kernel only supplies an enclosure of $e^x$ to the Ziv loop of `exp_ctx` and of the functions built on it (`exp2`, `exp10`, `expm1`, `pow`, `compound`, `powr`, the hyperbolic functions, the decimal elementary functions). Correct rounding is unique, so any valid enclosure gives the same value and flags; a different enclosure can only change how many precision steps the loop takes. The proof obligation is therefore exactly the validity of the enclosure, which the analysis above establishes and the white-box tests check against the series at $w + 256$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1586
msgid "*Evidence.* `elementary_table_exp_wbtest.mbt`: containment of the series enclosure at $w + 256$ for up to 256 arguments of $2^{-60}$ to $2^{30}$ at working precisions 53 to 1000, positive and negative; arguments equal to table nodes $j/2^{k_1}$, $j/2^K$ and one unit below and above them (remainder at or just off a table boundary); multiples of $\\ln 2$ up to $n = 10^6$; the Taylor stage on $a \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 2^{F-K} - 1, 2^{F-K-1}, \\text{random}\\}$ at $F$ from 80 to 2100 with its width at most 10 units; every entry of two table sets; declined arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1595
msgid "*Measured (release, this machine, kernel only, $x \\approx 0.7$).* Native: 53 bits 7 µs against 10 µs for the series; 100 bits 2.5 against 5.6; 300 bits 4.9 against 12.6 (bit-burst 24); 1000 bits 20 against 42 (burst 69); 2000 bits 94 against 111 (burst 143); 3000 bits 245 against 208. The crossovers (table kernel first up to 1792 bits on Native and LLVM, 2048 on Wasm, 1536 on Wasm-GC, 3072 on JavaScript) are per-target constants beside the bit-burst threshold. Above them the bit-burst kernel (from its own threshold) and the series are unchanged. The cost of the table kernel is dominated by a few dozen `Coeff` operations of 100 to 250 ns each at these sizes, and by the size of the integer coefficients $N'!/i!$ above 2000 bits, which is why it loses there."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1607
msgid "**`ln`, by verified Newton step.** Write $v = m\\, 2^{k}$ with $m \\in [2^{-1/2}, 2^{1/2})$, so $\\ln v = k \\ln 2 + \\ln m$. Take any dyadic $y_0$ with about $w/2 + 16$ bits (from the same function at half the precision, recursively, down to the existing series). Correctness does not depend on how good $y_0$ is, only the speed does. Let $[E_\\ell, E_u]$ be the enclosure of $e^{y_0}$ from the kernel above and $q_\\ell = m / E_u$, $q_u = m / E_\\ell$ (directed divisions). Since $\\ln$ is monotone, $\\ln m = y_0 + \\ln(m / e^{y_0}) \\in y_0 + [\\ln q_\\ell, \\ln q_u]$, and for $|u| \\le 1/2$ one has $u - u^2 \\le \\ln(1 + u) \\le u$, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1621
msgid "whenever $|q_\\ell - 1|, |q_u - 1| \\le 1/2$ (checked at run time, else `None`). If $|\\ln m - y_0| \\le 2^{-(w/2+8)}$ the quadratic term is below $2^{-(w+14)}$ and the width is that of $E$. Adding $k \\ln 2$ uses the exact integer product of $k$ with the dyadic bounds of $\\ln 2$. For $|m - 1| < 2^{-16}$ the existing artanh series has $O(w/16)$ terms and stays in use. The cost is that of one `exp` at $w$ plus the recursive call at about $w/2$ (about $1.5$ times one `exp` in total) plus two divisions. Projection at 8192 bits: 3 to 6 ms, against 164 ms today (41 ms projected for the fixed-point branch)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1631
msgid "*Implementation of the `ln` step (`elementary_burst_ln.mbt`).* `binary_ln_unit_burst_bounds` encloses $\\ln v$ for $1 + 2^{-16} < v \\le 2$ (the unit enclosure that `binary_ln_bounds` adds $k \\ln 2$ to). With $\\mathrm{inner} = w + \\max(0, 2 - \\operatorname{top}(v - 1))$ bits, so that an argument near 1 keeps $w$ significant bits of the result, $y_0$ is the lower end of the enclosure of $\\ln v'$ at $\\mathrm{inner}/2 + 16$ bits, where $v'$ is $v$ rounded to that many bits (the same function one level down, so the recursion ends in the series below the threshold); $[E_\\ell, E_u]$ is the `exp` kernel at $\\mathrm{inner} + 8$ bits; the two divisions and the final operations use $\\mathrm{inner} + 24$ bits with directed rounding, and the result is rounded outward to $w$ bits. It declines (and the series runs) for $v \\le 1 + 2^{-16}$ or $v > 2$, when $|q - 1| > 1/2$, and when the width of the result exceeds 8 units of $2^{-\\mathrm{inner}}$. The thresholds, kept with the other coefficient-kernel thresholds, are 1152 bits on Native and LLVM, 1280 on plain Wasm and on JavaScript and 576 on Wasm-GC (the kernel is slower than the fixed-point series below roughly 1000 bits on Native, 500 on Wasm-GC and 1200 on Wasm and JavaScript)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1649
msgid "*Evidence for the `ln` step.* White-box tests (`elementary_burst_ln_wbtest.mbt`): for every argument of a corpus of 158 values in $(1, 2]$ (random, near 1 down to $2^{-100}$, near 2, 3/2, 5/4) that the kernel accepts, the enclosure at $w$ contains the series enclosure at $w + 256$; it is no wider than the series enclosure at $w$; `ln_ctx` returns the same value and flags as the Ziv loop on the series alone in four rounding directions; and the kernel declines out of range. Two deliberately wrong variants are caught: a bias of 8 units in the lower end, and a poor start value (about $w/8$ bits) with the quadratic term dropped. A poor start value with the quadratic term kept only widens the enclosure and the test checks that it is still valid. One obligation is not reached by the tests: dropping the quadratic term with the real start value is not detected, because $u^2 \\approx 2^{-(w+26)}$ is below the width of the `exp` enclosure; its justification is the paper argument ($u - u^2 \\le \\ln(1+u)$ for $u \\ge -1/2$), not the tests. MPFR 4.2.2 comparison: 13,200 rows (precisions 256 to 4096, four rounding directions, arguments with binary exponents from $-1000$ to $10^5$, near 1 and near powers of two; the threshold was set to 256 for this run so that every row used the kernel) all match, and the output is byte-identical on Native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1669
msgid "*Measured for the `ln` step (native, release, this machine, on the 64-bit limbs, the Karatsuba and the division of #324, #326 and #328).* Kernel only, $w = p + 64$ bits, $v$ random in $[1, 2)$, against the fixed-point series of #319 (the machine was loaded, the ratios are the figure): 23.0 ms to 2.24 ms at 8256 bits (10.3 times), 3.27 ms to 0.75 ms at 4160 (4.4), 581 to 309 µs at 2112 (1.9), equal at 1024; slower below about 1000 bits (0.5 to 0.8 times at 384 to 768). End to end, `just bench external quick --ops ln` paired and alternating with `origin/main`, four rounds on a loaded machine (load average about 10), the floating time per call: 8192 bits 27.0, 25.6, 25.6 and 28.0 ms against 2.43, 2.40, 2.57 and 2.44 ms (paired ratios 11.1, 10.7, 10.0 and 11.5), 2048 bits 695, 565, 639 and 856 µs against 300, 295, 378 and 365 µs (2.3, 1.9, 1.7 and 2.35), 512 and 53 bits unchanged (0.98 to 1.07 at 53 bits, 0.99 to 1.36 at 512 bits, where the code path is the old one and the machine was noisy). The raw ratio to the stored MPFR medians (floating time of the day over the stored MPFR median) of the 8192-bit call goes from 221 to 243 to 21 to 22, and of the 2048-bit call from 37 to 57 to 20 to 25. MPFR re-measured on the machine at that time reads 1.9 to 2.0 times slower than the stored run at 8192 bits and 1.65 times at 2048 bits (the load), so the drift-corrected ratios are about 10.7 to 11.4 at 8192 bits and about 12 to 15 at 2048 bits; an earlier run on a quiet machine gave 11.5 to 13.6 at 8192 bits (on the previous division kernel)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1690
msgid "**`ln`, by the arithmetic-geometric mean.** For the outermost `ln` call (never for the constants, which the mean needs) and for $1 + 2^{-16} < v \\le 2$ the enclosure of $\\ln v$ is built from $\\ln s = \\pi / (2\\, \\mathrm{AG}(1, 4/s))$ up to a model term, with $s = v\\, 2^{m}$, $m = \\lceil p/2 \\rceil + 2$ (so $s > 2^{p/2 + 2}$ and $4/s^2 < 2^{-(p+2)}$), $\\ln v = \\ln s - m \\ln 2$.[^brent-agm] Every step below has a stated bound; none uses MPFR's constants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1692
msgid "*The argument.* $x = 4/s = (4/v)\\, 2^{-m}$: one division rounded to nearest at $p$ bits, relative error at most $2^{-p}$, then an exact shift. $\\mathrm{AG}$ is increasing in each argument and homogeneous of degree one (both maps $(a, b) \\mapsto ((a+b)/2, \\sqrt{ab})$ are), so for $\\theta \\ge 0$: $\\mathrm{AG}(1, x) \\le \\mathrm{AG}(1, x(1+\\theta)) \\le \\mathrm{AG}(1+\\theta, x(1+\\theta)) = (1+\\theta)\\, \\mathrm{AG}(1, x)$, and symmetrically for $\\theta < 0$: the relative error of $x$ moves $\\mathrm{AG}$ by at most as much."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1693
msgid "*The iteration.* $u' = \\sqrt{u v}$, $v' = (u+v)/2$ from $(x, 1)$, every product, square root and sum rounded to nearest at $p$ bits (relative error $\\delta = 2^{-p}$ each). The halving of the sum is exact and is made by changing the exponent of the already normalized coefficient, and the root is the correctly rounded one, taken from the limb-array kernel of the public square root (the context form gives the same value and costs 39 percent more at 2140 bits); neither changes a value, so the induction below is that of the plain iteration. With the exact sequence $(U_k, V_k)$ and $u_k = U_k (1 + a_k)$, $v_k = V_k (1 + b_k)$, $|a_k|, |b_k| \\le \\varepsilon_k$: the product has $(1+a)(1+b)(1+\\eta_1)$, the root halves the exponent of that factor and adds one rounding, the sum is bounded by $(1 + \\varepsilon)(1 + \\eta_3)$ and halving is exact, so $\\varepsilon_{k+1} \\le \\varepsilon_k + 2\\delta + O(\\delta\\, \\varepsilon_k)$, and $\\varepsilon_n \\le (3 n + 4)\\delta$ for $n < 64$ (MPFR's analysis of the same iteration, with the Higham calculus, gives $3n/2$; ours is looser by 2)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1694
msgid "*Stopping.* After each iteration stop when $|u - v| < 2^{\\operatorname{top}(v) - p + 3} \\le 16\\,\\delta v$ (tested on exponents; when the exponents of $u$ and $v$ differ by 2 or more the two differ by more than half of the larger, which is not below $16\\,\\delta v$, so the difference is formed only when they differ by at most 1). A tolerance of one unit would never stop: two nearest-rounded means can stay one unit apart. $\\mathrm{AG}(U_n, V_n)$ lies between $U_n$ and $V_n$, which differ by at most $16\\,\\delta v + 2 \\varepsilon_n v (1 + \\varepsilon_n)$, so $|\\mathrm{AG}(1, 4/s) - v_n| \\le e\\, v_n$ with $e = (9 n + 30)\\delta$ (the $+1$ from step 1 included). The code forms $v_n (1 \\pm e)$ with directed products."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1695
msgid "*The model term.* With $F = \\pi / (2\\, \\mathrm{AG}(1, 4/s))$ the lower end uses $\\ln s \\ge F (1 - 4/s^2)$ and the upper end $\\ln s \\le F$. Both follow from two equations of the NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, for $0 < k < 1$ and $k' = \\sqrt{1 - k^2}$: DLMF 19.8.5, $K(k) = \\pi / (2\\, \\mathrm{AG}(1, k'))$, and DLMF 19.9.2, $1 + k'^2/8 < K(k) / \\ln(4/k') < 1 + k'^2/4$. Put $k' = 4/s$ with $s > 4$ (so $0 < k' < 1$ and $\\ln(4/k') = \\ln s$): $F = K(k)$ by 19.8.5 and 19.9.2 reads $(1 + 2/s^2) \\ln s < F < (1 + 4/s^2) \\ln s$. Hence $\\ln s < F$, and $\\ln s > F / (1 + 4/s^2) > F (1 - 4/s^2)$ since $1/(1 + x) > 1 - x$ for $x > 0$. The same inequality gives the lower bound $F - \\ln s > 2 \\ln s / s^2$ as a by-product. The range is $s > 4$, and the kernel's $s > 2^{p/2 + 2}$ with $p \\ge 64$ satisfies it. Numerically (exact decimal arithmetic, $s = 2^k$, $k = 1 \\ldots 40$) the ratio $(F - \\ln s) / (4 \\ln s / s^2)$ is between 0.532 and 0.964, inside the interval $(0.5, 1)$ that 19.9.2 predicts (the values for $k = 1, 2$, that is $s \\le 4$, are also inside, though DLMF does not cover them). The code subtracts $F\\, 2^{-(p-2)}$, sixteen times the term $4/s^2 < 2^{-(p+2)}$ that the lemma needs, and the error margin $e F$ of step 3 is more than a hundred times that term: the factor is slack, not a need, and is kept as it is."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1696
msgid "*The constants and the result.* $\\pi_l \\le \\pi \\le \\pi_u$ at $p + 8$ bits and $\\ln 2$ at $p + 16$ bits (the cached enclosures), $F_l = \\pi_l / (2 v_n (1 + e))$ and $F_u = \\pi_u / (2 v_n (1 - e))$ by directed divisions, $m \\ln 2$ by directed products with the integer $m < 2^{\\lceil \\log_2 p \\rceil}$, and the subtraction directed. Cancellation: $F$ and $m \\ln 2$ are about $0.35 p$ and the result about $2^{\\mathrm{lead}}$ with $\\mathrm{lead} \\ge -16$, so about $\\log_2(0.35 p) + 16 \\le 28$ bits are lost; they are paid once in the working precision $p = w + (2 - \\mathrm{lead}) + \\operatorname{bits}(w) + 16$ (no restart, as MPFR does). The width of the result is at most $2^{-(w + 3)}$ by this choice and is checked (as in the Newton step): a wider result returns `None` and the Newton step or the series runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1698
msgid "*Cost.* $n$ iterations of one product, one square root and one sum: $n = 18$ at 2112 bits and 22 at 8256 bits (exact decimal AGM of $(1, 4/2^{p/2+1})$, 14 at 512), about $2 \\log_2 p - 4$. Per call: $n$ products, $n$ square roots, 3 divisions (one for $4/v$, two for $F$), about 5 products by short numbers, the two cached constants. In multiplication units ($M$ of the same side, the 0.11.0 baseline) this is about 66 at 2048 bits and 61 at 8192 against 178 and 133 for the Newton step and 65 and 55 for MPFR: the count equals MPFR's, so the ratio to MPFR stays at the ratio of the product and square-root kernels (about 4)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1700
msgid "*Choice of path.* The mean costs the same for every argument in $(1 + 2^{-16}, 2]$, the series of an argument $1 + d$ with $|d| \\approx 2^{-k}$ needs about $p/(2k)$ terms: they meet at $k \\approx 16$ at 2048 bits, which is the existing bound `binary_ln_burst_near_one_bits`, so the argument ranges of the Newton step and of the mean are the same, and below $1 + 2^{-16}$ the series remains. The threshold `binary_ln_agm_min_precision` (native: 1152 working bits, per target; it was 640 before the series ran on limb arrays, which made the series cost 0.27 to 0.39 of its former cost at 117 to 576 working bits) is chosen from the instruction counts below and from a re-measurement on both limb-row tiers (the series below 768 working bits, the Newton step from 768 to 1151, the mean from 1152; `binary_ln_entry_min_precision` is 768); the other targets are not measured and keep the kernel off (the constant is $2^{30}$ there), so their results are unchanged. The Newton step stays for working precisions below the threshold and as the fallback of the width check."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1702
msgid "*Result* (native, instructions retired per call, Newton step before and mean after):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1704
msgid "`ln` precision (bits)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1704
msgid "Newton step"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1704
msgid "mean"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1704
msgid "ratio"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1713
msgid "The enclosure is verified by the same tests as the Newton step (containment of the exact value shown by the series at 256 bits more, no wider than the Newton enclosure, declined outside its range, mutated kernels caught). The results of `ln`, `log2`, `log10` and the inverse hyperbolic functions are correctly rounded from either enclosure, so they do not depend on which one ran."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1715
msgid "**`sin` and `cos`, by complex bit-burst.** With $H = \\lfloor 2^{G} \\pi/2 \\rfloor / 2^{G}$, $G = F + \\operatorname{bits}(n) + 2$, the quadrant integer $n = \\operatorname{round}(x / H)$ and $R = x - nH$ (exact, $|R| \\le 0.8$ after a correction of $n$), the true reduced argument $r = x - n \\pi/2$ satisfies $|r - R| \\le |n|\\,2^{1-G} \\le 2^{-F}$. Sine and cosine are 1-Lipschitz, so $\\sin r \\in \\sin R \\pm 2^{-F}$ and likewise for the cosine; truncating $|R|$ to $F$ bits costs one more unit. The quadrant $n \\bmod 4$ maps $(\\sin r, \\cos r)$ to the result, as in `binary_reduced_trigonometric_bounds`. For $y = N/2^s < 1$ the real and imaginary parts of the series of $e^{iy}$ are the even and odd terms of the same sequence $t_i$ as above, with signs $(-1)^{\\lfloor i/2 \\rfloor}$, so a stage costs the same as an `exp` stage and gives both $\\cos y$ and $\\sin y$. The deficits satisfy the same bound $d_i < 3$, and each of the two alternating series has terms that decrease in size ($y^2 / ((i+1)(i+2)) < 1$), so the tail after $t_n < 2^{8}$ is at most $t_n + 3$ in size and the enclosure is $S_{\\mathrm{cos}}$ or $S_{\\mathrm{sin}} \\pm (t_n + 3n + 3)$. Stages are folded by rotation, $(C, S) \\leftarrow (C c - S s,\\; S c + C s)$, with four products and interval bounds on non-negative integers; all partial angles lie in $[0, 0.8]$, so all four factors are non-negative and the difference of bounds is checked to be non-negative. Because the merge costs four full products per stage, the stages are fewer than for `exp` (factor 4 to 8 between chunk boundaries), and the last stage computes only the requested one of the two values. For $|R| < 2^{-16}$ the existing series stays. Projection at 8192 bits: 4 to 6 ms, against 64 ms today (16 ms projected for the fixed-point branch); `tan` and the $\\pi$-scaled functions follow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1741
msgid "*Implementation of the `sin` and `cos` step (`elementary_burst_trig.mbt`).* `binary_trig_bounds_burst` (hooked into `binary_trigonometric_bounds` for `sin` and `cos`; `tan` keeps the series) follows the construction above for $2^{-64} \\le |x| < 2^{4096}$. With $F_0 = w + 112$ and $G = F_0 + \\max(1, \\operatorname{top}(x)) + 2$, the nearest integer $n$ to $|x| / H$, $H = \\lfloor 2^{G} \\pi/2 \\rfloor / 2^{G}$, comes from one Euclidean division of integers, $R = |x| - nH$ is exact with $|R| \\le H/2$, and the true reduced argument $r = |x| - n\\pi/2$ satisfies $|r - R| \\le n\\, 2^{1-G} \\le 2^{-(F_0+1)}$. The working scale is $F = w + 48 + \\mathrm{lz}$ with $\\mathrm{lz} = \\max(0, 2 - \\operatorname{top}(R))$ (declined above 64, so $R$ near a multiple of $\\pi/2$ keeps $w$ significant bits of a sine), and $R_t = \\lfloor 2^{F} |R| \\rfloor / 2^{F}$ satisfies $|r - \\operatorname{sign}(R) R_t| \\le 1.5 \\cdot 2^{-F}$, so by the Lipschitz bound the sine and cosine of $r$ are within 2 units of those of $\\operatorname{sign}(R) R_t$. $R_t$ is cut into chunks at 16, 64, 256, ... bits as for `exp`. A chunk is a *ball*: a midpoint and a radius in units of $2^{-F}$. The fixed-point stage sums the even terms of the series of $e^{iy}$ for the cosine and the odd ones for the sine, with signs $+ - + - \\dots$ in each, from the same sequence $t_i$ as in the `exp` stage (deficit below 3); each of the two alternating series decreases in size, so each sum is within $3n + t_n + 3$ of its value. The binary-splitting stage keeps a complex state: the leaf of an index $i \\ge 1$ is $(0, N)$ (the factor $i N$), $p = N^{\\mathrm{leaves}}$, $q = \\prod i$, and $t = t_L q_R 2^{s\\,\\mathrm{len}_R} + i^{\\mathrm{leaves}_L} p_L t_R$, where multiplying by $i^m$ only permutes and negates the two components; the partial sums are exact rationals, the floors lose less than 1 unit, and the omitted terms are below 2 units, so each part is a ball of radius 2. The product of two balls with midpoints $a, b$ and radii $\\alpha, \\beta$ has midpoint $\\lfloor a b / 2^F \\rfloor$ and radius $\\lceil (a \\beta + b \\alpha + \\alpha \\beta)/2^F \\rceil + 2$, and the stages are folded by rotation, $C' = C c - S s$ and $S' = S c + C s$ (four ball products; the first stage is not multiplied at all, and the last fold forms only the component the result reads, two ball products), with the midpoint of the difference required to be non-negative. The quadrant $n \\bmod 4$ and the signs of $x$ and $R$ select which component is the result. It declines for $|x| < 2^{-64}$, $|x| \\ge 2^{4096}$, when the reduced argument is smaller than $2^{-62}$, and when the width exceeds $2^{-(w+5)}$ of the value. The thresholds are 1536 bits on Native and LLVM, 2112 on plain Wasm, 640 on Wasm-GC and 256 on JavaScript (the kernel is slower than the series below roughly 1500 bits on Native, 2000 on Wasm and 600 on Wasm-GC; on JavaScript it wins from 256)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1781
msgid "*The binary-splitting stages by the real series of the sine.* A stage whose chunk has at least `split_min_terms` terms no longer evaluates the complex series $e^{iy}$, $y = N/2^s < 1$ (a tree with two signed components and five products per node), but the real series $\\sin y = y\\, S(y^2)$, $S(z) = \\sum_{k \\ge 0} (-z)^k / (2k+1)!$, as a tree over the indices $k = 0 \\ldots n'-1$ with $p(k) = -N^2$, $q(k) = 2k (2k+1)\\, 2^{2s}$ ($k \\ge 1$) and a signed $T$, merged exactly as the tree of $e^{y}$ (powers of $N^2$ from the power table, sign $(-1)^{\\ell}$ from the parity of the left leaves, the signed merge of `split_combine_t`). The exact partial sum is $T / (Q\\, 2^{q_{\\mathrm{shift}}})$ and the sine is $N T / (2^{s}\\, Q\\, 2^{q_{\\mathrm{shift}}})$, the same rational as the imaginary part of the complex tree over $2n'$ indices (the test compares the two as integers)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1783
msgid "*Term count.* Let $n$ be the term count of the exponential series from the integer bound of the `exp` stage: $2^F N^n < n!\\, 2^{sn}$, which follows from $F + m n \\le L(n) + s n$ with $m = \\operatorname{bits}(N)$ and $L(n) = \\sum_{i \\le n} (\\operatorname{bits}(i) - 1) \\le \\log_2 n!$. The bound holds for every larger count: going from $n$ to $n+1$ the left side grows by $m \\le s$ (since $N < 2^s$ gives $m \\le s$) and the right side by $\\operatorname{bits}(n+1) - 1 + s \\ge s$, so $F + m(n+1) \\le L(n+1) + s(n+1)$. The first omitted term of the sine series has the exponent $2n' + 1$, and with $n' = \\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ this is at least $n$, so that term is $y^{2n'+1} / (2n'+1)! < 2^{-F}$, below one unit. The series alternates and its terms decrease ($y^2 < 1$, the ratio of consecutive terms is $y^2 / ((2k+2)(2k+3)) < 1$), so the whole tail is below one unit in size; the floor of the quotient loses less than one unit; the ball $[q - 1, q + 3]$ (midpoint $q + 1$, radius 2, as for the complex stage) contains $\\sin y$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1785
msgid "*The cosine of the stage.* The fold needs the cosine too. Every stage satisfies $0 < y \\le \\pi/2$ (the chunks are parts of the reduced argument, at most $\\pi/4$), so $\\cos y = +\\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 y}$ without a sign question, and the root is well conditioned there ($\\sin y / \\cos y \\le 1$ for $y \\le \\pi/4$), so no bits are lost near $0$ (where $\\cos y \\approx 1$) and the argument never approaches $\\pi/2$. With the sine ball $[s_\\ell, s_h]$ in units of $2^{-F}$ (clamped to $s_\\ell \\ge 0$ and $s_h \\le 2^F$, both true for the sine of $y \\in (0, \\pi/2]$), the function $c(s) = \\sqrt{2^{2F} - s^2}$ decreases in $s$, so for every $s \\in [s_\\ell, s_h]$: $\\lfloor \\sqrt{2^{2F} - s_h^2} \\rfloor \\le c(s) \\le \\lfloor \\sqrt{2^{2F} - s_\\ell^2} \\rfloor + 1$, two exact integer square roots, no derivative bound; for $s_\\ell = 0$ the upper end is $2^F$. For a split stage $s_\\ell > 0$ always ($\\sin y \\cdot 2^F \\ge 2^{F-s}$ and a split stage has $s \\ll F$), the clamp is only a guard. The cosine ball is the interval of these two ends (midpoint the floor of their mean, radius the rest). The fixed-point stages and the fold by rotation are unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1787
msgid "*Evidence for the real stage.* The two trees are compared as integers (the real sine tree against the imaginary part of the complex tree, 390 combinations of numerator size, shift and term count), the stage balls against the balls of the complex stage kept in the test file as the reference (they intersect, and the sine radius is the same), the kernel against the series enclosure at working precision +256 bits by the existing burst tests, and the rounding rules by a deterministic test of the cosine ball: for a given $s$ the ends are the floor of the root and the floor plus one (so a cosine strictly between them is enclosed only with the plus one), for $s = 0$ the end is $2^F$; the mutations \"ends swapped\" and \"plus one omitted\" change the ends. Instructions per call of `sin` and `cos` at 2048, 4096 and 8192 bits: 0.68 to 0.83 of before on three arguments per size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1789
msgid "*Evidence for the `sin` and `cos` step.* White-box tests (`elementary_burst_trig_wbtest.mbt`): for every argument the kernel accepts in a corpus of 354 values (60 to 2000 bits, $|x|$ from $2^{-60}$ to $2^{100}$, both signs, and the neighbourhood of nine multiples of $\\pi/2$ for four precisions) and for both functions, the enclosure at $w$ contains the series enclosure at a precision 256 bits higher (and high enough for the series to certify the quadrant); it is no wider than the series enclosure; `sin_ctx` and `cos_ctx` return the value and flags of the series enclosure rounded at twice the precision, in four rounding directions; and the kernel declines out of its range. Three deliberately wrong variants are caught: a bias of $2^{64}$ units in every stage, 40 terms fewer in the binary-splitting stages, and $\\pi$ taken with 200 bits fewer for an argument near $2^{40}$. A bias below about $2^{40}$ units is absorbed by the 48 guard bits and the final rounding, so the tests cannot see it; likewise the radius of the fixed-point stages and the 2 units of the angle error are dominated by worst-case terms elsewhere, so removing them leaves an enclosure that still contains the value: their justification is the paper argument above, not the tests. MPFR 4.2.2 comparison: 23,424 rows for each of `sin` and `cos` (precisions 256 to 4096, four rounding directions, arguments from $2^{-60}$ to $2^{1000}$, and neighbours of multiples of $\\pi/2$; the thresholds were set to 256 for this run so that every row used the kernel) all match, and the output is byte-identical on Native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1811
msgid "*Measured for the `sin` and `cos` step (native, release, this machine, with the Karatsuba limbs and the division of #326 and #328).* Kernel only, $w = p + 40$ bits, $x \\approx 0.7$ for the sine and $x \\approx 16$ to $32$ for the cosine, against the reduction and fixed-point series of #321: sine 8.37 ms to 1.39 ms at 8256 bits, 1.58 ms to 0.57 ms at 4160, 338 to 239 µs at 2112, equal at 1536; cosine 5.31 ms to 1.20 ms at 8256, 1.01 ms to 0.47 ms at 4160; slower below about 1500 bits (0.5 to 0.9 times at 256 to 1280). End to end, `just bench external quick --ops sin` paired and alternating with `origin/main`, four rounds on a quiet machine, floating time per call: 8192 bits 6.92, 6.66, 6.73 and 6.66 ms against 1.47, 1.32, 1.29 and 1.38 ms (paired ratios 4.7, 5.1, 5.2 and 4.8), 2048 bits 282, 288, 276 and 268 µs against 203, 195, 183 and 204 µs (1.4, 1.5, 1.5 and 1.3), and 512 and 53 bits unchanged (0.89 to 1.12, the old code path). The raw ratio to the stored MPFR medians (floating time of the day over the stored MPFR median) of the 8192-bit call goes from 34 to 35 to 6.5 to 7.4, and of the 2048-bit call from 19 to 20 to 13 to 14; the machine reads about 1.45 times slower than the stored run (MPFR re-measured on it), so the drift-corrected ratios are about 4.5 to 5.1 at 8192 bits and about 9 to 10 at 2048 bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1830
msgid "**Constants by binary splitting.** `pi`, `ln 2` and `ln 10` are computed once per working precision and cached, but a first call pays the cold cost above. Binary splitting gives $O(M(w) \\log^2 w)$ with rigorous bounds:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1841
msgid "where $(P, Q, T)$ come from the Chudnovsky series (alternating, about 46 bits per term) and $\\operatorname{artanh}(1/k) = \\sum_j 1/((2j+1)k^{2j+1})$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1844
msgid "*Implementation (`elementary_constants_split.mbt`).* The state of a series on an index range is $(p, q, t)$ with $p = \\prod p(k)$, $q = \\prod q(k)$ and $t = q \\cdot (\\text{partial sum})$, combined as $t = q_R t_L + p_L t_R$; the `artanh` series has $p(j) = 2j - 1$ and $q(j) = (2j + 1) k^2$, which telescope to $1/((2j+1) k^{2j})$, and the Chudnovsky series has $p(k) = (6k-5)(2k-1)(6k-1)$, $q(k) = k^3\\, 640320^3 / 24$ and the leaf $t = \\pm p(k)(A + Bk)$. Only the Chudnovsky $t$ is signed, so a sign-and-magnitude wrapper over the unsigned `Coeff` suffices and the code is the same on every target. The partial sum $t/q$ is exact; the only roundings are the final directed divisions and $\\sqrt{10005}$ (directed `sqrt_ctx`), done at $w + 16$ bits, and the result is rounded outward to $w$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1857
msgid "*Tail bounds.* For $\\operatorname{artanh}(1/k)$ with $K = k^2$ take $N$ terms with $K^N \\ge 2^{F+9}$, $F = w + 16$ (using $\\lfloor \\log_2 K \\rfloor$): the first omitted term is at most $2^{-(F+9)}$ and the omitted terms are positive and shrink by at least $1/K \\le 1/4$, so they sum to less than $2^{-(F+8)}$, which is added to the upper end. For Chudnovsky the ratio of the factorial parts of consecutive terms is $8(6k+1)(6k+3)(6k+5)/(k+1)^3 \\le 1728$, the linear factor grows by at most 41.11 from $k = 0$ to $1$ and by less than 2 after that, and $640320^3 > 2^{57.8}$, so the ratios are below $2^{-41.7}$ and then $2^{-46.1}$, the terms decrease in size, alternate in sign, and the first is below $2^{24}$. The omitted sum from index $N$ is therefore at most the size of term $N$, below $2^{-17 - 46(N-1)}$, which is at most $2^{-(F+4)}$ for $N = \\lfloor F/46 \\rfloor + 3$. Then $S \\in [(t - e)/q, (t + e)/q]$ for the integer $e = \\lfloor q / 2^{F+4} \\rfloor + 1$, and $\\pi$ is enclosed by dividing $426880 \\sqrt{10005}\\, q$ by $t \\pm e$ with directed rounding. The identities were checked numerically to 60 digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1874
msgid "*Evidence.* Tests (`elementary_constants_split_wbtest.mbt`): the new enclosure at $w$ contains the old series enclosure at $w + 256$ (an independent computation) for $w$ from 64 to 1024; it is no wider than the old enclosure at $w$ up to 2048; enclosures at $w$ and $w + 300$ nest up to 20000 bits; and removing terms (a test hook) makes the containment test fail, so the tail count is checked and not just assumed. A one-off comparison against MPFR 4.2.2 (`mpfr_const_pi`, `mpfr_const_log2`, `mpfr_log(10)` at $2w + 64$ bits) at 64, 100, 257, 1000, 4096, 8192, 30000 and 100000 bits found every enclosure containing the MPFR value, one to two units of the last place wide. All `binary` tests pass on native, wasm, wasm-gc and js, `just pr` passes, and 30000 (native) and 10000 (each of wasm, wasm-gc, js) random `elem` cases agree with MPFR."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1887
msgid "*Measured (native, release, cold enclosure at $w = p + 64$, this machine).* `pi` 449 ms to 0.67 ms at $p = 8192$, 14 ms to 71 µs at 2048, 0.93 ms to 15 µs at 512; `ln 2` 178 ms to 1.33 ms, 5.6 ms to 156 µs, 0.36 ms to 32 µs; `ln 10` (with `ln 2` cached) 89 ms to 0.99 ms, 2.8 ms to 114 µs, 0.18 ms to 26 µs. At $p = 64$ the splitting is already 3 to 19 times faster, on wasm-gc and js as well (js at 8192 bits: `pi` 0.63 ms, `ln 2` 0.67 ms), so the threshold `binary_constants_split_min_precision` is 64 on every target. Warm (cached) calls are unchanged. A first `sin` or `ln` at 8192 bits used to pay the series cost on top of the warm time (about 450 ms for `pi`, about 180 ms for `ln 2`); now a cold call costs at most about 7 ms more than a warm one (`sin` 53.4 ms cold against 53.6 ms warm, `ln` 101.9 ms against 94.3 ms at 8192 bits)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1900
msgid "The cache itself is unchanged. The design asks for requests for `ln 2` at a canonical precision (the next multiple of 64 above $F + \\operatorname{bits}(n) + 2$) so that `exp`, `ln` and the trigonometric reduction share entries instead of evicting each other; that belongs to the `exp` kernel, which is the first caller with such an irregular precision, and is not part of the constants step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1907
msgid "**Working-precision schedule and the Ziv loop.** The attempt precisions $w_0 = p + 64$, $w_{i+1} = w_i + \\max(32, \\lfloor w_i/2 \\rfloor)$ and the budget of 12 attempts are unchanged. Each attempt picks the old or the new kernel by comparing $w$ with a per-target constant and, for `ln` and the sine, by the argument (the near-1 and tiny-argument cases above); a kernel that returns `None` or an enclosure wider than the old series' falls through to the old kernel at the same $w$. So an attempt never certifies less than before, and the exact cases, the tiny-argument measures, the inner-endpoint rounding and the flag argument of the loop are untouched. The result of a call is the correctly rounded value whenever either path certifies, hence identical to the current result; they can differ only in which inputs exhaust the budget, which the corpus comparison below counts. The faster attempts make a longer budget affordable, which is the point of [#15](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/15): the limit is a policy of the loop and does not interact with the kernels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1923
msgid "**Allocation and targets.** `Coeff` values are immutable, so each term of a fixed-point stage allocates four arrays of up to $w/32$ limbs (product, shift, quotient, sum); on native these are included in the measured unit costs above (shift 0.2 µs, one-limb division 1.5 µs, addition 0.3 µs at 8192 bits). The `ln` series loop already runs on limb arrays on the non-JavaScript targets (see \"The `ln` series loop on limb arrays\"). A fused in-place multiply-shift-divide loop on a mutable limb array for the other stages, which still allocate per term, would remove most of that for the non-JavaScript targets, and is a later optimization behind the same function, not part of the design. On JavaScript `Coeff` is a `bigint`: products, shifts and divisions are the engine's, with a larger constant per operation and an asymptotically faster product, so the stage boundaries and every threshold are measured separately on native, LLVM, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript and kept next to the coefficient-kernel thresholds. The source of the kernels is shared; only the constants differ."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1939
msgid "**Evidence the new path must produce.** Each kernel carries its own invariant tests: containment of the enclosure of the old path at $w + 256$ bits in the new enclosure at $w$ (an independent computation), the width bound, and boundary cases (arguments at chunk boundaries, $R$ near $0$ and $1$, the corrections of $n$, huge $|n|$, tiny and near-1 arguments). The end-to-end check compares every result bit for bit with the old path and with MPFR on a seeded random corpus plus the existing boundary corpus on all four targets, and a deliberately biased kernel (one unit too narrow) must make the tests fail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1948
msgid "**Limits of the speed-up.** The model reaches algorithmic parity with MPFR (`exp` at 8192 bits is about 65 to 100 products in MPFR and, projected, about 50 to 80 here), so the remaining gap is the product kernel: 43 µs against 3.2 µs at 8192 bits, a factor 13 (4 µs against 0.31 µs, a factor 12, at 2048 bits). Ratios to MPFR at 8192 bits in the same run (MPFR: `exp` 326 µs, `ln` 177 µs, `sin` 291 µs): today 55, 930 and 220; projected from this design about 6 to 11 for `exp`, 17 to 34 for `ln` and 14 to 21 for `sin`. A ratio below 10 at 8192 bits is therefore within reach for `exp` only if the product kernel also improves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1958
msgid "**Measured result.** On main `8badc55` (all four kernels merged), Apple M4, native, release, load average 1.3 to 3, three runs of `just bench external quick --ops exp ln sin --binary-precisions 2048 8192` agreeing within 1 to 10 %, and a second check on `952600f` within 3 %: floating time over the stored MPFR median is 6.0 to 6.6 (`exp`), 12.5 (`ln`) and 12.2 (`sin`) at 2048 bits, and 3.2 (`exp`), 10.6 (`ln`) and 5.8 (`sin`) at 8192 bits. The MPFR time measured on the same machine at the same moment was within 6 % of the stored one, so raw and drift-corrected ratios differ by at most 6 %. Three cells are below 10 times MPFR (`exp` at 2048 and 8192 bits, `sin` at 8192 bits); `ln` at 2048 and 8192 bits and `sin` at 2048 bits are above it. This is one machine and the native target only: the Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript targets were not compared with MPFR. The projections above are superseded by these numbers; the remaining gap is the multiplication and division kernel and allocation, not the algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1972
msgid "**Evidence checked and still to confirm.** The references were checked against the sources on 2026-11-19. Brent, *J. ACM* 23(2), 1976, 242-251, exists as cited, but it contains the AGM and ascending Landen transformation methods with cost $O(M(n)\\log n)$ (the logarithm by Landen transformations of incomplete elliptic integrals); it contains neither bit-burst nor theta functions, nor the nome, and it credits the $O(M(n)\\log^2 n)$ bound to Brent, \"The complexity of multiple-precision arithmetic\" (Proc. Seminar on Complexity of Computational Problem Solving, 1975, pp. 126-165), which was not read. So the footnote below supports the AGM logarithm only, and the bit-burst attribution is to be replaced by that 1975 paper (not yet read). The formula with the theta functions, $\\ln(1/q) = \\pi / \\mathrm{AGM}(\\theta_2^2(q), \\theta_3^2(q))$, is attributed to Sasaki and Kanada, *J. Inf. Process.* 5(4), 1982, 247-250, on the authority of later notes by Brent (secondary sources); its normalization of the nome (Brent's notes use $q^4$ and point out a typo in the $q$ form of the Borwein book) and its truncation bound have not been checked against the original, which the owner will obtain. Nothing in the design or in the code depends on the theta-function formula; the `ln` enclosure by the AGM depends on the model bound $F (1 - 4/s^2) \\le \\ln s \\le F$ only (see \"`ln`, by the arithmetic-geometric mean\" above), which follows from DLMF 19.8.5 and 19.9.2. The 1-ulp and 2-ulp widths quoted above come from the MPFR comparison, which is secondary evidence for the enclosures; the proof is the integer-bound argument in this section."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:1994
msgid "R. P. Brent, \"Fast multiple-precision evaluation of elementary functions\", *J. ACM* 23(2), 1976 (bit-burst and the AGM logarithm; citation to be confirmed against the paper). The truncation bound of the AGM kernel is taken from DLMF 19.8.5 and 19.9.2 (see \"`ln`, by the arithmetic-geometric mean\"), not from this paper. MPFR's `log.c` documents the same algorithm and is secondary evidence only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2001
msgid "The coefficient kernel"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2003
msgid "**Problem.** Precision costs integer multiplication and division of $p$-bit numbers, and $p$ ranges from 1 to $2^{28}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2006
msgid "**Choice.** The kernel is the internal package [`internal/binary_kernel`](internal/binary_kernel.md); `BinCoeff` is a newtype over its `Coeff`. `Coeff` stores up to 128 bits inline and larger values as little-endian 64-bit limbs (a host `bigint` on JavaScript), and dispatches on the operand length $n$ in 64-bit limbs. Every threshold below is in 64-bit limbs:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2011
msgid "Product or square"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2011
msgid "Native, LLVM"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2011
msgid "Wasm, Wasm-GC"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2013
msgid "schoolbook product below"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2014
msgid "Karatsuba product from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2015
msgid "Karatsuba product on 32-bit limbs from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2016
msgid "Karatsuba square from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2016
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:1108
msgid "off"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2017
msgid "square on 32-bit limbs from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2018
msgid "Toom-3 product from (balanced, where the NTT does not fit)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2019
msgid "two-prime NTT product from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2020
msgid "NTT square from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2022
msgid "The Native, LLVM column lists the values of the portable loops, which LLVM uses (unmeasured there). Native with the C rows has the Karatsuba square from 64 limbs, and with the assembly rows (arm64 Apple, where they are the default) the schoolbook product below and the Karatsuba product from 48 limbs and the Karatsuba square from 96. A product with $m > 2n$ limbs is cut inside the Karatsuba into $n$-limb blocks; a short operand against a long one beyond the NTT cutoff uses the NTT overlap-add. Squaring is a word-range Karatsuba square (three half-size squares $a_0^2$, $a_1^2$ and $(a_0 - a_1)^2$ instead of three products) on Native and LLVM, and the NTT square above its cutoff; Wasm keeps the 32-bit-limb route from 80 limbs, where a 64-bit product costs four `i64.mul`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2034
msgid "Operands with at least 4 limbs of which at most one eighth are nonzero use a sparse product of the nonzero limbs. Division uses a loop over half-limbs for a divisor below $2^{32}$, Knuth's algorithm D on 64-bit digits (32-bit digits on Wasm) below 96 divisor limbs, Burnikel–Ziegler recursion from 96 with leaves of up to 64 limbs, and never the Newton reciprocal (the routine stays for tests); GCD switches from the binary (Stein) algorithm to Lehmer batches above two limbs. The thresholds are measured, per target, by the benchmark suite; they are policy, not semantics. The 32-bit view of the storage exists only for the Wasm routes above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2044
msgid "A Lehmer round is valid only when its single-word digits come from the same bit window of both operands: digits taken from each operand's own top limb have unrelated scales, and the cofactors they produce subtract only a small multiple of the smaller operand per round. Each round therefore keeps $a \\ge b$, runs Euclid on the leading 63 bits of $a$ and the bits of $b$ in the same window, and takes one division step $a \\bmod b$ instead when $b$ has no bits in that window (then $a / b \\ge 2^{62}$) or when the cofactor matrix would make an operand negative or shrink neither. Every matrix applied has determinant $\\pm 1$, so the gcd is unchanged, and a long $a$ is reduced modulo a short $b$ in one step however different their lengths are; below eight limbs of $b$ the loop finishes with plain Euclid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2056
msgid "**Why exactness is preserved.** Schoolbook, Karatsuba and Toom-3 evaluate integer polynomial identities, for example"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2063
msgid "and Toom-3 (evaluation at $0, 1, -1, 2, \\infty$) interpolates with exact divisions by 2 and 3 of signed intermediates known to be multiples, so they are exact integer computations. The NTT is the only modular step. It splits each operand into 16-bit digits, so each coefficient of the digit convolution is at most"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2072
msgid "for transform lengths up to $2^{23}$. It computes the convolution modulo the primes $p_1 = 998244353 = 119 \\cdot 2^{23} + 1$ and $p_2 = 754974721 = 45 \\cdot 2^{24} + 1$, both of which have $2^{23}$-th roots of unity, and recombines by the Chinese remainder theorem, which is unique in $[0, p_1 p_2)$ with $p_1 p_2 \\approx 2^{59.4} > 2^{55}$. So the recombined coefficients are the exact integers. The length check precedes every transform; a longer product uses overlapping blocks of admissible length or falls back to Toom-3. Division paths return $(q, r)$ with $n = qd + r$ and $0 \\le r < d$ by construction; the Newton path corrects its approximate quotient with the remainder and aborts if more than two corrections would be needed, which would indicate a bug rather than a numerical event. Because all paths compute the same integers, the choice of algorithm cannot change any rounded result, flag or encoding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2086
msgid "Ordering NaN in `compare`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2088
msgid "**Problem.** MoonBit's `Compare` trait asks for a three-way comparison that sorting and ordered maps can rely on. IEEE comparison is a partial order: NaN is unordered with everything, itself included."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2092
msgid "**Options.** (1) Abort on NaN, as earlier versions did; every sort of data that might contain a NaN then becomes a crash. (2) Use IEEE `totalOrder`, which is total but distinguishes $-0 < +0$ and puts negative NaNs below $-\\infty$, so `compare` would disagree with numerical equality on zeros. (3) Keep the numerical order on numbers and put all NaNs in one class above it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2099
msgid "**Choice.** Option (3). Define the key $\\kappa(x) = (0, x)$ for a number and $\\kappa(\\mathrm{NaN}) = (1, 0)$, ordered lexicographically; `compare(x, y)` is the comparison of $\\kappa(x)$ and $\\kappa(y)$, with $-0$ and $+0$ mapped to the same number. A comparison of keys in a totally ordered set is reflexive, transitive and total, so `compare` is a total preorder; it is not antisymmetric ($-0$ and $+0$, or two NaNs with different payloads, compare equal but are different values), which `Compare` does not require. The cost is that `nan > 1` is true under `<`, so code that needs IEEE semantics must use `compare_checked` (error on NaN), `compare_quiet` / `compare_signaling` (four-valued, with flags) or `total_order`. `==` is `Eq`, which is `compare(x, y) == 0`, so it ignores the sign of a zero, the precision and the payload of a NaN; the structural equality, the only one that is a congruence for every method (precision and payload included), is `identical`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2115
msgid "**Canonical form.** Every finite value produced by the API has $c$ odd or $c = 0, e = 0$, and $\\operatorname{bits}(c) \\le$ its precision; stored exponents never saturate (a saturated exponent is classified as overflow or underflow first)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2119
msgid "**Correct rounding.** For every arithmetic operation, conversion and elementary function and every context, the returned finite value equals $\\circ(r)$ for the exact real result $r$, with the range rules above. By (R1), $\\circ(r) = r$ and no flag is raised whenever $r \\in F$; `apply_ctx` is idempotent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2124
msgid ""
"**Flags.** `inexact` iff $\\circ(r) \\n"
"e r$; `overflow` implies `inexact`; `underflow` iff tiny (per the context rule) and inexact; `division_by_zero` only for an exact infinite result of finite operands; `invalid_operation` iff a quiet NaN was produced from non-NaN operands or a signaling NaN was consumed. Exceptions to the last rule: `min_num` and `max_num` return a value and no flags; $\\operatorname{pow}(x, \\pm 0)$, $\\operatorname{pown}(x, 0)$ and $\\operatorname{pow}(1, y)$ return $1$ for a quiet NaN operand without the flag (a signaling NaN operand raises it). `combine` is associative, commutative and idempotent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2134
msgid "**Error model.** Consequently, in the normal range, $|\\circ(r) - r| \\le u|r|$ for nearest and $< 2u|r|$ for directed rounding, with the absolute term $\\eta/2$ (respectively $\\eta$) below $2^{e_{\\min}}$, and subnormal sums and differences are exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2138
msgid "**Monotonicity.** Each operation is monotone in each argument where the real function is, because it is $\\circ \\circ f$ with $\\circ$ monotone; in particular RD and RU results bracket the exact value, which `interval` and the directed square roots of `sqrt_ctx` rely on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2142
msgid "**Exactness theorems.** `remainder`, `scaleb` in the normal range, `copy_sign`, `neg`, `abs`, `logb` and decoding are exact; the integral roundings never need a second rounding; `fma(a, b, -RN(ab))` is exact without underflow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2146
msgid "**Complexity.** Addition is linear in the operand length (and independent of the exponent gap, by the far-operand rule); multiplication follows the kernel table, $O(n^2)$ to $O(n \\log n)$; division and square root cost a constant number of multiplications of the same size at large $n$; `remainder` costs $O(\\log(q_x - q_y))$ modular multiplications; an elementary function evaluates its series at the working precision $w$, and because $w$ grows geometrically the total cost of all attempts is within a constant factor of the last one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2155
msgid "The longer proofs (the far-operand addition rule, `nextUp`, the remainder reduction, the Ziv acceptance test and the NTT bound) are collected in the attachment."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2159
msgid "[Rounding and exactness proofs for the binary package](../../attachments/design_binary_rounding_proofs.typ)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2163
msgid "**Host `Double` for anything but `from_double`.** Routing binary16, binary32 or binary128 through `Double` double-rounds, loses signaling NaNs on some targets and cannot represent binary128 at all. Interchange encoding is done on `BinCoeff` bit patterns instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2167
msgid "**A fixed number of guard bits.** Three guard bits suffice for addition of two $p$-bit operands, but not for division, square root, conversions from decimal or operands wider than the context. Deciding from exact integer data (round, sticky, remainder sign, midpoint comparison) works for all of them with one finalizer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2172
msgid "**Rounding to $p$ bits, then to the subnormal grid.** This double rounding produces wrong subnormal results; the finalizer shifts once to the coarser of the two positions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2175
msgid "**Ziv with an estimated error bound.** It requires a separate error analysis for every function and every reduction, and a mistake in it silently returns a wrong last bit. Outward-rounded enclosures make the bound a computed quantity, at the cost of evaluating every operation twice."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2179
msgid "**Global flags and rounding state.** IEEE 754 describes flags as sticky global state. A returned `BinaryFlags` value composes with pure code, concurrent code and the `Result` style of `@lf_arith`, and `combine` recovers the sticky behaviour where wanted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2183
msgid "**`compare` aborting on NaN, or `compare` as `totalOrder`.** See [Ordering NaN in `compare`](#ordering-nan-in-compare)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2188
msgid "`binary` deliberately does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2190
msgid "provide interval or ball arithmetic: enclosures are internal to the certification loops; `interval` builds inf-sup interval arithmetic (a lower bound and an upper bound) on top of `Binary`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2193
msgid "implement IEEE 754 alternate exception handling (traps, substitution) or sticky global flags: flags are returned values;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2195
msgid "implement decimal floating-point (`decimal`, `decimal_gda`) or the non-binary IEEE operations on them;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2197
msgid "promise any NaN payload for a newly generated NaN (it uses payload 0), or propagate payloads of more than one input;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2199
msgid "guarantee completion of an elementary function within any time for every input: certification has a budget, and an exhausted budget is reported, not hidden;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2202
msgid "expose limb layout, thresholds or transform parameters: they may change without notice as long as every result, flag and encoding stays the same;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary.md:2204
msgid "claim conformance beyond the finite corpus recorded in [conformance](../conformance/binary.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:1
msgid "binary_checked design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:5
msgid "`binary_checked` lets a binary floating-point computation be written as a chain of operations in which every failure is explicit and none is lost, without an unwrapping step after each operation. `BinaryChecked` closes `Result[Binary, ArithmeticError]` under the operations of [`binary`](binary.md): it adds no arithmetic, only composition. The [API page](../api/binary_checked.md) lists the operations and the [tutorial](../tutorial/binary_checked.md) shows pipelines in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:15
msgid "The error monad"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:17
msgid "Let $V$ be the set of `Binary` values and $E$ the set of `ArithmeticError` values. A `BinaryChecked` is an element of the disjoint union"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:24
msgid "$M$ is the *exception* (or *Either*) monad with the error type fixed.[^moggi] Its unit and bind are `ok` and `bind`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:35
msgid "for $f : V \\to M V$. A function $f : V \\to M V$ is a *Kleisli arrow*: an operation that may fail. Every checked operation of `binary` (`Binary::sqrt_checked`, `div_checked`, `try_ln_ctx`, …) and every checked trait method of Luna-Flow/arithmetic (`SqrtChecked::sqrt_checked`, `DivChecked::div_checked`, `PowIntChecked::pow_int_checked`, …) is such an arrow once its non-value arguments are fixed. The wrapper turns them into methods that take *and* return $M V$, so arrows can be chained."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:43
msgid "`map` is the functor action, and it is bind followed by the unit:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:49
msgid "which is exactly how the code computes it (`Result::map`). Since $\\eta \\circ g$ never returns $\\mathrm{Err}$, `map` cannot introduce an error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:52
msgid "E. Moggi, \"Notions of computation and monads\", *Information and Computation* 93(1), 1991; P. Wadler, \"Monads for functional programming\", 1995."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:56
msgid "Lifting binary operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:58
msgid "A binary operation $\\oplus : V \\times V \\to M V$ (for infallible operators, $\\eta(x \\oplus y)$) is lifted to $M V \\times M V \\to M V$ by"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:65
msgid "which is the private helper `bin_result_lift2_checked` (and `bin_result_lift2` for infallible $\\oplus$, using `map` for the inner step). Unfolding the definition gives the three cases the API describes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:78
msgid "`clamp` nests three binds in the order value, `min`, `max`, so it selects among three operand errors the same way."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:83
msgid "A closed wrapper instead of `Result` everywhere"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:85
msgid "**Problem.** `binary` returns `Result` from its checked operations and plain values from its infallible ones. A computation that mixes them needs an unwrap or a `match` after every checked step, and it is easy to drop an error by unwrapping too early."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:90
msgid "**Options.** (a) Ask users to write `match` chains. (b) Use MoonBit's `raise` errors. (c) Provide a closed type whose every operation accepts the possibly failed operands."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:94
msgid "**Choice: (c).** Luna-Flow libraries report failures as structured `Result` values rather than raised errors, so (b) would break the convention of the checked traits. With (c) every operation takes `BinaryChecked` operands, so a formula `two / (one / a + one / b)` is written with ordinary operators, and the error of the first failing step is carried to the end. The wrapper stays a separate type so that plain `Binary` keeps its IEEE meaning (`1 / 0` is $+\\infty$) in algebraic code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:102
msgid "First error, not all errors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:104
msgid "**Problem.** When both operands of an operation have failed, one error must be returned."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:107
msgid "**Options.** (a) Return the left error. (b) Collect both, as an applicative validation would. (c) Return an arbitrary one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:110
msgid "**Choice: (a).** Collecting errors would need a monoid on `ArithmeticError`, which the type does not have, and a list of errors would be a different type. The left-biased choice is deterministic and agrees with how the expression is read; the [first-error theorem](#the-first-error-theorem) below states exactly which error a whole expression reports."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:116
msgid "Which context an operation uses"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:118
msgid "Operations without a context argument must pick a precision and an exponent range. The wrapper uses the operand's own format so that a pipeline started at some precision stays at that precision:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:122
msgid "Context used"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:124
msgid "as the `Binary` operator: the larger operand precision, nearest-even (`min_num` and `max_num` return an operand unchanged, with no rounding)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:125
msgid "`div_ctx` under `BinaryContext::new(precision=max(p_x, p_y))`, flags mapped as above"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:126
msgid "unary elementary functions, `rootn`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:128
msgid "`sqrt_ctx`, `pown_ctx` under `BinaryContext::new(precision=x.precision())`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:129
msgid "`ArithmeticContext::new(x.precision())`, passed to `PowNatChecked`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:130
msgid "every `*_ctx` method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:132
msgid "`BinaryContext::new(precision=p)` means precision $p$, round to nearest-even, and the implementation range of `binary` (the extendable format of IEEE 754-2019 3.7), so these operations overflow or underflow only near $2^{\\pm 2^{30}}$. For `pow_nat` the wrapper goes through the Luna-Flow/arithmetic trait, and `binary` maps the `ArithmeticContext` to a `BinaryContext` with `BinaryContext::from_arithmetic_context`: the precision is copied, the `RoundingMode` is mapped to the binary rounding attribute (`AwayFromZero` is an error), and `e_max` (or `e_min`, giving `e_max = 1 - e_min`) becomes the exponent range, which must be of the IEEE 754-2019 3.3 shape `e_min = 1 - e_max`; both absent gives the precision-alone format. A conversion that fails is an `Err`. The `clamp` field of `ArithmeticContext` has no binary meaning and is not used."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:144
msgid "One flag rule for every `_ctx` method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:146
msgid "`Binary::*_ctx` operations return $(v, \\phi) \\in V \\times F$ where $F$ is the set of `BinaryFlags`. Every `_ctx` method of the wrapper, the six arithmetic ones (`add_ctx`, `sub_ctx`, `mul_ctx`, `div_ctx`, `sqrt_ctx`, `pown_ctx`) and the elementary ones alike, is the plain method with an explicit context and the same map $\\kappa$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:165
msgid "Overflow, underflow and inexact are dropped: their default results are the values the wrapper returns. The map is one private function (`bin_result_checked_flags`); the wrapper does not recompute the exceptional cases, it reads the flags that `Binary::*_ctx` returns. Keeping the flags would make the wrapper a combination of the exception monad with the writer monad on $F$; the decimal pipeline makes that choice because IEEE decimal arithmetic is usually audited by its flags (see the [`decimal_checked` design](decimal_checked.md)). Use the unwrapped `Binary::*_ctx` when the flags, or the raw default result of an exceptional case, matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:176
msgid "The exceptional cases of the arithmetic methods (IEEE 754-2019):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:180
msgid "a signaling NaN operand, any `_ctx` method, `div`, `sqrt` or `pown`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:180
msgid "6.2, 7.2 a)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:181
msgid "a quiet NaN operand, no signaling NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:181
msgid "6.2: signals nothing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:182
msgid "`mul_ctx(0, inf)`, `mul_ctx(inf, 0)`, signs arbitrary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:182
msgid "7.2 b)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:183
#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:184
msgid "7.2 d)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:185
msgid "`div_ctx(0, 0)`, `div_ctx(inf, inf)`, signs arbitrary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:185
msgid "7.2 e)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:186
msgid "`div_ctx(x, +-0)`, `x` finite and nonzero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:187
msgid "`sqrt_ctx(x)`, `x < 0` including $-\\infty$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:187
msgid "7.2 g)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:190
msgid "`pown_ctx(x, 0)` for `x` not a signaling NaN"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:191
msgid "7.3 needs finite operands"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:192
msgid "overflow, underflow, inexact"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:194
msgid "The elementary functions, `pow`, `rootn`, `hypot` and `atan2` follow the same map. A pole such as $\\ln(\\pm 0)$, $\\operatorname{atanh}(\\pm 1)$ or $\\operatorname{pow}(\\pm 0, y)$ with a finite $y < 0$ (§9.2, Table 9.1) is a `DivisionByZero` error and a signaling NaN operand a `DomainError`, in the plain and in the `_ctx` form alike. Before this rule the seven arithmetic `_ctx` methods dropped every flag ([#371](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/371))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:203
msgid "The monad laws"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:205
msgid "For all $x \\in V$, $m \\in M V$ and Kleisli arrows $f, g$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:215
msgid "*Proof.* Left identity is the first defining equation of bind. For right identity, case $m = \\mathrm{Ok}(x)$: $\\mathrm{Ok}(x) \\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} \\eta = \\eta(x) = \\mathrm{Ok}(x)$; case $m = \\mathrm{Err}(e)$: both sides are $\\mathrm{Err}(e)$. For associativity, case $m = \\mathrm{Err}(e)$: the left side is $\\mathrm{Err}(e) \\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} g = \\mathrm{Err}(e)$ and the right side is $\\mathrm{Err}(e)$. Case $m = \\mathrm{Ok}(x)$: both sides reduce to $f(x) \\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} g$. $\\square$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:223
msgid "In code these read `BinaryChecked::ok(x).bind(f) ≡ f(x)`, `m.bind(BinaryChecked::ok) ≡ m` and `m.bind(f).bind(g) ≡ m.bind(x => f(x).bind(g))`, where $\\equiv$ compares `result()`. Associativity is what makes a pipeline independent of how it is split into helper functions: a helper that chains two checked steps can be inlined or extracted without changing the result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:230
msgid "The functor laws follow from $\\texttt{map}(m, g) = m \\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} (\\eta \\circ g)$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:242
msgid "So `normalized`, `neg`, `abs`, `ulp` and `with_precision`, which are all `map`s, can be fused or reordered exactly as the underlying `Binary` functions can."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:246
msgid "The laws can be checked on concrete arrows; here $f$ is the checked square root and $g$ the checked logarithm:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:275
msgid "The first-error theorem"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:277
msgid "Consider an expression tree whose leaves are `BinaryChecked` values and whose inner nodes are wrapper operations. MoonBit evaluates arguments eagerly, so every node is evaluated; the short circuit is inside a node: the `binary` operation of a node runs only when all its children are successes, and otherwise the node just passes an error on. (A leaf that is itself an expensive MoonBit expression, such as `f(b)` in `a + f(b)`, is still computed; only `bind` defers work.) Say that a node *originates* an error when all its children are successes and the operation itself fails (a leaf originates its error if it is an `Err`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:287
msgid "**Theorem.** If any node originates an error, the expression evaluates to the error originated by the first originating node in post-order (children left to right, then the node). Otherwise it evaluates to the success obtained by applying the `binary` operations to the leaf values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:292
msgid "*Proof* by induction on the tree. A leaf is immediate. For a node $n = \\operatorname{lift}_{\\oplus}(t_1, t_2)$, post-order lists the nodes of $t_1$, then those of $t_2$, then $n$. If $t_1$ contains an originating node, by induction $t_1$ evaluates to the error $e$ of the first one, which also comes first in the post-order of $n$, and $\\operatorname{lift}_{\\oplus}$ returns the left error $e$. Otherwise $t_1$ evaluates to $\\mathrm{Ok}(x)$; if $t_2$ contains an originating node, $t_2$ evaluates to the error of the first one, and by the second case of $\\operatorname{lift}_{\\oplus}$ it is returned, again matching post-order. Otherwise both are successes, $n$ originates an error exactly when $x \\oplus y$ fails, and that is the result. Unary nodes are binds and follow from the same case analysis; `clamp` is the three-child version. $\\square$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:305
msgid "For example, in `left + one / zero` the leaf `left` comes first in post-order, so its error is reported, and in `one / zero + left` the division is first. The lifted operation is therefore *not* commutative on errors even when $\\oplus$ is commutative on values: on successes $\\operatorname{lift}_{+}(\\mathrm{Ok}\\,x, \\mathrm{Ok}\\,y) = \\mathrm{Ok}(x+y) = \\mathrm{Ok}(y+x)$ because rounded binary addition is commutative, but with two errors the reported one depends on the order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:313
msgid "No other invariant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:315
msgid "The wrapper holds one `Result` and adds no state, so every invariant of `Binary` (normalized coefficient, precision at least one, correctly rounded operations) carries over unchanged to the success branch. Each wrapper step costs $O(1)$ on top of the delegated operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:322
msgid "**Storing `BinaryFlags` in the wrapper.** See the decision above; flags belong to the `(value, flags)` APIs of `binary`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:324
msgid "**Turning NaN results into errors.** IEEE defines NaN as a value, and `binary` already decides which cases are errors in its checked APIs; a second policy here would make the wrapper disagree with the checked trait implementations of the same type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:328
msgid "**Error accumulation.** Needs a combining operation on `ArithmeticError`, which Luna-Flow/arithmetic does not define."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:330
msgid "**Implicit recovery.** There is no method that turns an error back into a value; recovery is the caller's decision after `result()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:335
msgid "No arithmetic of its own: every numeric result comes from `binary`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:336
msgid "No IEEE flags, no context state, no interchange encoding and no decimal conversion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:338
msgid "No error recovery or error accumulation; only the first error is kept."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:339
msgid "No `Eq`, `Show` or `Compare` on the wrapper; compare through `result()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/binary_checked.md:340
msgid "The operation set is the one in the generated interface; operations of `binary` without a wrapper method are reached through `map` or `bind`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:1
msgid "cli design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:5
msgid "Conformance runs are driven by Python tooling that starts many native processes in parallel. One executable with a `--backend` switch keeps the build simple (one MoonBit package, one link step) while each backend keeps its own option syntax. The dispatcher's job is only to choose a runner and turn its return value into the process exit status."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:13
msgid "None beyond the runners'. The only contract is functional: the exit status is the runner's return value, and the runner's output depends only on its arguments and the files it reads (see [internal/runner_cli design](internal/runner_cli.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:20
msgid "Three layers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:22
msgid "Parsing and execution of corpora live in pure `frontend/*` packages; argument handling, file access and output live in `cli/*_expr_cli` runners, each a library with `run(arguments) -> Int32`; the process boundary (`@env.args()`, `@sys.exit`) lives only in `cli`, and the argument scanning that picks the runner lives in the library [`cli/dispatch`](cli/dispatch.md), so that it has tests and the main package holds no test inputs. Because runners are libraries, their usage paths are unit-testable without spawning processes, and the frontends stay usable on every target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:31
msgid "Forward the program name"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:33
msgid "The dispatcher removes `--backend` and its value and forwards `[program, rest…]`. Every runner skips element 0, so a runner behaves the same when called from the dispatcher, from a test, or (in principle) as its own executable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:38
msgid "One binary, copied per backend"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:40
msgid "`tools/conformance_cli.py` builds `tooling/src/cli` into one shared target directory and copies it to `<backend>-conformance.exe`. Tools always invoke an executable whose name says what it runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:44
msgid "Dispatcher help wins"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:46
msgid "`--help` is handled while scanning arguments, before the backend is known, so it prints the dispatcher usage and exits with `0` whatever backend is named. The scan stops at the first error, so a malformed `--backend` before `--help` still exits with `2`. Runner options are documented on their pages instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:53
msgid "Exactly one runner is called per invocation, or none when the arguments are invalid (exit `2`) or `--help` is given (exit `0`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:55
msgid "The exit status equals the runner's return value: `0`, `1` or `2`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:56
msgid "Arguments other than `--backend`, its value and `--help`/`-h` reach the runner unchanged and in order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:58
msgid "\"At most once\" is checked on whether `--backend` was given, not on its value, and an empty `--backend=` is itself an error (exit `2`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:63
msgid "**Four executables.** Four packages with identical `main` functions and four link steps for no behavioural gain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:65
msgid "**Subcommands (`floating-conformance gda …`).** A positional backend would collide with runner paths; an explicit option is unambiguous."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:67
msgid "**Exit codes from runners.** Calling `exit` inside a runner would make it untestable as a library."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:72
msgid "Native target only (file access through `moonbitlang/x/fs`, exit through `moonbitlang/x/sys`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:74
msgid "No corpus download, planning, parallelism or aggregation: those are in `tools/conformance.py` and the `tools/run_*_interpreter.py` scripts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli.md:76
msgid "No public MoonBit API."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:1
msgid "cli/dispatch design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:5
msgid "The dispatcher has behaviour worth testing (argument scanning, the at-most-once rule, the empty value, `--help`), so it lives in a library package. The main package `cli` keeps only the process boundary: it reads `@env.args()` and calls `@sys.exit` with the returned code. A main package that holds test inputs (`.mbt.md` files) is deprecated by the toolchain: main packages will stop generating blackbox tests."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:14
msgid "None. The dispatcher is a pure function of the argument vector: its result and its output depend on nothing else, and the runners it calls are libraries whose results depend only on their arguments and the files they read."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:20
msgid "Return the exit code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:22
msgid "`dispatch` returns an `Int32` and never calls `@sys.exit`, so a white-box test checks an invocation without spawning a process. The semantics of the options are those of the `cli` page and are unchanged by the split."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:26
msgid "Runners stay libraries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:28
msgid "The four runners (`cli/gda_expr_cli` and its siblings) are imported here and nowhere else; `cli` itself imports only this package and the process helpers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:33
msgid "Exactly one runner is called per invocation, or none for invalid arguments or `--help`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:35
msgid "The result equals the runner's return value, or `2` for an argument error, or `0` for `--help`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:40
msgid "**Keep the dispatcher in `cli`.** A main package that holds test inputs (`.mbt.md` files) is deprecated by the toolchain, and its tests would need the process boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:43
msgid "**A table of runners.** A `match` on the backend name is checked by the compiler, and each arm is one line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:45
msgid "**Exit inside `dispatch`.** It would make every usage error untestable without spawning a process."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/dispatch.md:50
msgid "No process exit, no file access of its own: those are in the runners and in `cli`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:1
msgid "cli/gda_expr_cli design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:5
msgid "Provide a thin, scriptable process interface to [`frontend/gda_expr`](../frontend/gda_expr.md): read files, pass options through, print counters that the Python tooling can add up across shards, and encode the verdict in the exit status."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:12
msgid "The run is the composition $\\text{files} \\xrightarrow{\\text{sort}} \\text{documents} \\xrightarrow{\\text{filter, shard}} \\text{rows} \\xrightarrow{\\text{execute}} \\text{summary}$. Row selection and the additivity of counters over shards are proved in the [gda_expr design](../frontend/gda_expr.md) and the [internal/conformance design](../internal/conformance.md); this runner adds only the deterministic file order that makes row ordinals well defined."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:23
msgid "All files form one run"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:25
msgid "Sharding is applied to the concatenated, filtered rows of all files, not per file. The tooling can therefore give every shard the same list of files and get balanced shards even when one file dominates the corpus."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:29
msgid "Strictness at the process boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:31
msgid "`--strict-supported` is evaluated here: the exit status is `1` when the summary has failed rows, or, in strict mode, any legacy or unsupported row. The frontend summary itself stays neutral, so in-process callers can apply their own policy. Diagnostic rows are excluded even in strict mode: they are placeholders or rows under an invalid context, without a scalar meaning, not features the library lacks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:38
msgid "Stable JSON keys"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:40
msgid "The JSON object uses fixed camelCase keys and puts execution counters in a nested `execution` object together with the shard, matching what `tools/run_dectest_interpreter.py` aggregates. `supportedCases` is the number of executable rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:45
msgid "Stop at the first parse error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:47
msgid "Corpora are pinned and expected to parse completely; a parse error is an infrastructure failure (exit `2`), reported with its location, not a test result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:53
msgid "Exit `0` implies `failed_cases() == 0`, and in strict mode also no legacy or unsupported row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:55
msgid "For a fixed argument vector and file contents the output is deterministic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:56
msgid "`totalCases` is the same in every shard of one run, so the aggregate total is the common value, not the sum. Every other counter is a sum over the shards, because each counts only the rows its shard selected and the shards partition the filtered rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:63
msgid "**Per-file shards.** Uneven: one large file would bound the wall time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:64
msgid "**Reporting every parse diagnostic.** Useful while editing a corpus, but the runner targets pinned corpora; the frontend API returns all diagnostics for tools that need them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:70
msgid "No corpus download or phase planning (Python tooling)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:71
msgid "No recursion into subdirectories; directory entries without the `.decTest` suffix are skipped, while a named file without it is an error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/gda_expr_cli.md:73
msgid "Only GDA `.decTest` semantics through `decimal_gda`; IEEE decimal vectors use a different runner."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:1
msgid "cli/itl_expr_cli design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:5
msgid "Expose [`frontend/itl_expr`](../frontend/itl_expr.md) as a process whose JSON report lists every case by outcome, so the interval tooling can check a phase claim (\"all cases of these operations in these files are executable and pass\") from one invocation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:12
msgid "A phase is a pair (files $F$, operations $O$). The runner executes the set $\\{ c \\in \\operatorname{cases}(F) : O = \\emptyset \\vee \\operatorname{op}(c) \\in O \\}$ and reports the partition of that set into passed, failed, unsupported and diagnostic cases (see the [itl_expr design](../frontend/itl_expr.md)). The strict verdict of a phase is \"failed, diagnostic and unsupported are empty\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:20
msgid "Its own small option parser"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:22
msgid "The runner does not use the shared `parse_common_options`: it has neither sharding nor a text mode, because ITF1788 files are small (the largest pinned file is 218 KB) and the tooling runs phases in parallel instead. It parses `--strict-supported`, repeatable `--operation` and paths itself, and rejects every other option, so a mistyped option never silently changes a phase."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:28
msgid "JSON lists, not only counts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:30
msgid "Besides counters the report lists `failedIds` with messages, `unsupportedIds` and `diagnosticIds`. Interval failures are usually few and need the exact case to reproduce; listing them makes the report self-contained."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:35
msgid "Diagnostics always fail"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:37
msgid "The frontend's `success()` already fails on diagnostic cases; the runner adds only the strict check on unsupported cases. Unreadable data in a pinned corpus is never an acceptable exclusion. The frontend classifies every unknown operation as unsupported whatever its operands, so a phase that runs a whole file without `--operation` (`sets` and `relations` in `interpreter_stages.json`) fails only on cases of operations it executes, and the unknown ones count against it only under `--strict-supported`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:47
msgid "Exit `0` implies no failed and no diagnostic case, and in strict mode no unsupported case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:49
msgid "`totalCases = executableCases + unsupportedCases + diagnosticCases` and `executableCases = passedCases + failedCases`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:51
msgid "The listed ids are exactly the cases in each class, in execution order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:55
msgid "**Sharding.** Not needed for the pinned corpus of 6,632 selected cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:56
msgid "**Directory expansion.** Phases name their files by pattern (`interpreter_stages.json`) and the tooling passes the runner explicit paths, which keeps the claim precise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:61
msgid "Precision is fixed at the frontend default (53 bits)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:62
msgid "No string conversions or signals (frontend boundaries apply)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/itl_expr_cli.md:63
msgid "No text output mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:1
msgid "cli/mpfr_expr_cli design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:5
msgid "Run one MPFR reference file through [`frontend/mpfr_expr`](../frontend/mpfr_expr.md) with a single command, so that the binary conformance tooling can add MPFR evidence next to the TestFloat matrix without knowing the seven file formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:12
msgid "Each row asserts $\\hat y = \\circ_{p,\\rho}(f(x))$ (and flags), as described in the [mpfr_expr design](../frontend/mpfr_expr.md). The runner adds nothing to the semantics; its summary is the frontend summary of the whole file."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:18
msgid "Format detection from content"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:20
msgid "The formats have no common header, so the runner looks for a marker: the elementary generator writes `mpfr-elementary-v1`, the power data has a header naming `input_coefficient_hex`, the other four formats write `mpfr-random-v1`, `mpfr-decimal-random-v2`, `mpfr-strtofr-v1` and `mpfr-decimal-output-v1`, and MPFR's own `tests/data/sqrt` has none of them. Content-based detection lets the pinned upstream file be used unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:27
msgid "No shards"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:29
msgid "MPFR files have at most a few thousand rows; `parse_common_options` is called with `allow_shard=false` so that shard options are rejected instead of being silently ignored."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:33
msgid "Corpus names in JSON"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:35
msgid "The JSON `corpus` field names the detected format and the MPFR release the data was produced with, so aggregated reports state their evidence source."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:40
msgid "Exactly one parser runs per invocation, determined by the file content."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:41
msgid "Exit `0` iff every row passed; `totalCases = passedCases + failedCases`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:42
msgid "Parse errors exit with `2` before any row is executed; an elementary `pow`, `powr`, `hypot`, `atan2` or `atan2pi` row without a second operand is such an error, so every outcome is an exit code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:48
msgid "**A `--format` option.** Redundant with the markers already present in the data and a source of mismatches."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:50
msgid "**Accepting several files.** The formats differ per file; one file per process keeps the report unambiguous."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:55
msgid "No sharding, no filtering of rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md:56
msgid "Only the seven formats of `frontend/mpfr_expr`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:1
msgid "cli/testfloat_expr_cli design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:5
msgid "Execute one TestFloat vector file per process with the exact configuration it was generated under, so the tooling can stream hundreds of millions of vectors through bounded files and parallel processes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:11
msgid "A TestFloat task is a tuple (format, operation, rounding, tininess, exactness, level, seed). Its vectors are checked by the pass rule of the [testfloat_expr design](../frontend/testfloat_expr.md). Splitting a task's vector stream into chunks $V_1, V_2, \\dots$ and each chunk into shards is a partition of the vectors, and every vector's verdict is independent of the others, so the task's counters are the sums over chunks and shards."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:20
msgid "Configuration as options, vectors untouched"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:22
msgid "The function, rounding, tininess and exactness are command-line options that mirror `testfloat_gen`'s own flags. Vector files stay exactly in TestFloat's format, so the generator's output can be piped to a file and executed without conversion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:27
msgid "One file per invocation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:29
msgid "A process handles one chunk. The Python driver (`tools/run_binary_interpreter.py`) writes the generator stream into chunk files, runs the runner on each, and adds the chunk offset to the reported `FUNCTION:LINE` ids. Bounded chunks keep memory constant for the largest `mulAdd` tasks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:35
msgid "Shared options for JSON and shards"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:37
msgid "`--json` and the shard options come from [internal/runner_cli](../internal/runner_cli.md), so they behave exactly as in the GDA runner."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:43
msgid "Exit `0` iff no selected vector failed; `selectedCases = passedCases + failedCases`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:45
msgid "A specification error (`TestFloatSpec::parse`) or parse error exits with `2` before any vector is executed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:50
msgid "**Encoding the configuration in the vector file.** It would require rewriting TestFloat output."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:52
msgid "**Running `testfloat_gen` from MoonBit.** Process management belongs in the tooling; the runner stays portable and testable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:57
msgid "No vector generation, chunking or id remapping (Python tooling)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cli/testfloat_expr_cli.md:58
msgid "Formats and operations are those of `frontend/testfloat_expr`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:1
msgid "consistency design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:5
msgid "`floating` has four numeric cores with overlapping claims: a binary value converted to a decimal and back must denote the same rational number; the checked wrappers must give the same values as the cores; an interval must enclose the exact result of the scalar operation. A package's own tests see one package; cross-package tests placed there would add test-only dependencies between the cores. `consistency` is the one place where these cross-package laws are written down and executed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:15
msgid "Every finite value of every core denotes a rational number. Let $\\llbracket\\cdot\\rrbracket$ map a value to $\\mathbb{Q}$ (implemented by `semantic`, with `ExactRational` as canonical form). The laws the suite checks have three shapes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:20
msgid "**Agreement:** for values $x$ of package $A$ and $y$ of package $B$ that represent the same input, $\\llbracket f_A(x) \\rrbracket = \\llbracket f_B(y) \\rrbracket$ when both operations are exact, and the results are the two packages' roundings of the same real otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:24
msgid "**Exact oracles:** an operation equals a `BigInt` or rational computation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:26
msgid "**Enclosure:** for an interval operation $F$ and a point operation $f$, $x \\in X \\Rightarrow f(x) \\in F(X)$, and enclosure relations behave as partial orders rather than total orders."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:30
msgid "Representation laws are checked as well: GDA results keep the right cohort, signed zero and NaN payload; interchange encodings round-trip bit for bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:35
msgid "White-box tests only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:37
msgid "The package has no source files besides tests, and imports the cores only `for \"wbtest\"`. It therefore never becomes part of a library build and has no API to maintain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:41
msgid "Fixed witnesses from official corpora"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:43
msgid "Many decimal tests use rows of the official decTest suite as named witnesses. They pin the exact cases that once disagreed between packages, in-process and without the corpus download."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:47
msgid "Oracles over expectations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:49
msgid "Where possible a test computes the expected result independently (with `BigInt` or `semantic`) instead of hard-coding output strings, so a law stays meaningful when formatting changes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:55
msgid "A passing run shows that each stated law holds on its witnesses; it is finite evidence, not a proof for all inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:57
msgid "Tests are deterministic and, except `context_vectors_wbtest.mbt` (native only, because it reads `testdata/context_vectors.txt`), target-independent, so the same run on native, Wasm and JavaScript checks the same claims."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:63
msgid "**Putting cross-package tests into each package.** It would create test-only dependency cycles between the cores."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:65
msgid "**Property-based random testing only.** Random inputs rarely hit cohort boundaries and ties; fixed witnesses do."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:70
msgid "No public API and no runtime code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/consistency.md:71
msgid "No external corpora (that is the role of the conformance frontends) and no performance measurements (the `bench` packages)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1
msgid "decimal design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:3
msgid "The arithmetic model of `Luna-Flow/floating/decimal` represents decimal values with a coefficient, an exponent and a sign, then rounds each context result once. Its cohort preserves the chosen exponent, while a context defines precision, rounding direction and exponent range. This page derives those rules, the interchange encodings and the certification of elementary functions. The [API](../api/decimal.md) specifies each function; the [tutorial](../tutorial/decimal.md) shows them in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:13
msgid "`decimal` implements the decimal arithmetic of IEEE 754-2019[^ieee] for any precision, with three properties:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:16
msgid "**Every context operation is correctly rounded.** The result is the exact mathematical result rounded once, in the selected direction, to the context's precision and exponent range — for the basic operations and for the elementary functions alike. No deviation from this goal is known; see [known deviations](#known-deviations)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:21
msgid "**Nothing is lost silently.** The exponent of a result (its *quantum*), the sign of zero, NaN payloads and every exceptional condition are part of the returned value or of the returned `DecimalFlags`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:24
msgid "**No hidden state.** Precision, rounding, exponent range and flags are ordinary immutable values passed and returned explicitly, as everywhere in Luna-Flow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:28
msgid "IEEE Std 754-2019, *Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic*, clauses 3.3–3.5 (decimal formats and encodings), 4 (attributes and rounding), 5 (operations), 7 (exceptions) and 9 (recommended operations). The General Decimal Arithmetic specification by M. F. Cowlishaw (version 1.70) gives the same model in an arbitrary-precision form; its terms *coefficient*, *adjusted exponent*, *Etiny* and *clamp* are used here."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:35
msgid "The checked ParseChecked adapter follows IEEE 754-2019 4.3 and 5.12.2. Text conversion uses the supplied rounding-direction attribute and context exponent range. parse_checked returns the converted value through Result. To inspect exception flags such as overflow, use `Decimal::from_string_ctx`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:42
msgid "Decimal floating-point numbers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:44
msgid "A decimal floating-point format with precision $p$ and adjusted-exponent range $[e_{\\min}, e_{\\max}]$ is the set of numbers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:53
msgid "together with $\\pm\\infty$ and NaNs. Here $c$ is the *coefficient* and $q$ is the *exponent*. The *quantum* is $10^q$, and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:60
msgid "Contexts require $1 \\le p \\le 999\\,999\\,999$, $-999\\,999\\,998 \\le e_{\\min} \\le 0$ and $1 \\le e_{\\max} \\le 999\\,999\\,999$. A shared constructor helper validates these bounds and computes $E_{\\text{tiny}}$ and $E_{\\text{top}}$; invalid parameters return an error naming the invalid field. These are the adopted decNumber `decContext.h` limits and keep context-derived exponent sums within `Int`. GDA 1.70 specifies no fixed context limits and requires a balanced range; this library does not enforce that separate balance rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:69
msgid "The *adjusted exponent* of a non-zero $x$ is $\\operatorname{adj}(x) = q + \\operatorname{digits}(c) - 1 = \\lfloor \\log_{10} |x| \\rfloor$: it is the exponent of $x$ written in scientific notation $d_0.d_1d_2\\ldots \\times 10^{\\operatorname{adj}(x)}$. A non-zero $x$ is *normal* when $\\operatorname{adj}(x) \\ge e_{\\min}$ and *subnormal* otherwise; the smallest positive subnormal is $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$ and the largest finite value is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:80
msgid "`DecimalContext` stores the precision $p$, $e_{\\max}$ (and $e_{\\min} = 1 - e_{\\max}$, IEEE 754-2019 3.3), the rounding direction and two limits (`refinement_limit`, `elementary_precision_limit`) that bound the elementary functions. Every context is an IEEE format: its exponents above $E_{\\text{top}}$ are not allowed (see [clamping](#clamping)) and tininess is detected before rounding. The library's own working contexts, which are not formats, are private. The interchange formats are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:88
msgid "bias $=-E_{\\text{tiny}}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:88
msgid "exponents $E_{\\text{top}}-E_{\\text{tiny}}+1$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:90
#: manual/design/decimal.md:759
msgid "decimal32"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:91
#: manual/design/decimal.md:760
msgid "decimal64"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:92
#: manual/design/decimal.md:761
msgid "decimal128"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:94
msgid "IEEE 754 fixes $e_{\\min} = 1 - e_{\\max}$, so the number of exponents is $E_{\\text{top}} - E_{\\text{tiny}} + 1 = e_{\\max} - e_{\\min} + 1 = 2e_{\\max}$; the standard chooses $e_{\\max} = 3 \\cdot 2^{w-1}$ so that this count is $3 \\cdot 2^{w}$, which is exactly what two leading exponent bits taking the values $0,1,2$ and $w$ further bits can encode (see [encodings](#interchange-encodings)). The bias that turns $q$ into a non-negative stored exponent is $-E_{\\text{tiny}} = p - e_{\\min} - 1$: for decimal64, $16 + 383 - 1 = 398$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:102
msgid "Why decimal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:104
msgid "A rational number $n/m$ in lowest terms has a finite expansion in base $b$ if and only if every prime factor of $m$ divides $b$. For $b = 2$ the only admissible denominators are powers of two; for $b = 10$ they are $2^{i}5^{j}$. So every binary floating-point number has a finite decimal expansion, but $0.1 = 1/(2\\cdot 5)$ has none in binary: the `Double` nearest to it is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:114
msgid "Quantities defined in decimal — prices, rates, measurements, protocol fields — are therefore represented exactly by decimal floating point, and decimal rounding happens at the decimal places a person or a regulation specifies. The trade-off is a larger *wobble* (below) and more expensive digit arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:119
msgid "Cohorts and quantum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:121
msgid "The map $(s, c, q) \\mapsto (-1)^s c\\,10^{q}$ is not injective. All representations of the same non-zero value form its *cohort*. If $c$ has $d$ digits and $k$ trailing zeros, the members are $(c\\cdot 10^{j},\\, q - j)$ for $-k \\le j \\le p - d$, ignoring the exponent range, so the cohort has $p - d + k + 1$ members. For example, $1000$ in decimal32 ($c = 1$, $q = 3$, $d = 1$, $k = 0$) has the seven members $1\\text{E+}3, 10\\text{E+}2, \\ldots, 1000000\\text{E-}3$. A zero has a member for every exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:130
msgid "The cohort member carries information numeric equality does not: `12.30` states two decimal places, `1.2E+3` states two significant digits. IEEE 754 therefore specifies, for each operation, a **preferred exponent**, and an exact result is delivered in the member whose exponent is closest to it. The preferred exponents follow from where the exact result naturally lives:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:147
msgid "For the sum and the product, the bracketed coefficient is an integer, so the preferred exponent is attained whenever the exact coefficient fits in $p$ digits: `1.20 + 3.40 = 4.60` and `1.25 × 2.50 = 3.1250`. For the quotient the exact result exists only when $c_a / c_b$ has a finite decimal expansion; it is then moved toward $q_a - q_b$ as far as $p$ digits allow, so `2.400 / 1.2 = 2.00`. An inexact normal result always uses all $p$ digits, which is the member with the smallest exponent; an inexact subnormal result ends at $E_{\\text{tiny}}$. `quantize_ctx` makes the exponent an explicit argument, and `normalized` chooses the member with the largest exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:157
msgid "For conversion from a character sequence, IEEE 754-2019 5.2 selects the least possible exponent for an inexact result. Thus parsing `1.0001` at precision 3 gives `1.00`, and `99999` gives `1.00E+5`, since a carry into $10^p$ still leaves $p$ digits; an exact result uses the representable cohort member closest to the preferred exponent (parsing `1.000` at precision 3 also gives `1.00`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:177
msgid "Rounding directions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:179
msgid "Let $x > 0$ be exact and let the target exponent be $t$ (the exponent that leaves $p$ digits, or $E_{\\text{tiny}}$ for a tiny result). Write"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:186
msgid "Each rounding direction returns $c$ or $c + 1$ (times $10^{t}$); the choice depends on $f$, the last digit of $c$ and the sign:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:189
msgid "The kernels implement eight algorithms. A context selects one of the five that IEEE 754-2019 4.3 names (public `DecimalRoundingMode`: `RoundTowardZero` is `Down`, `RoundTowardPositive` is `Ceiling`, `RoundTowardNegative` is `Floor`, `RoundTiesToAway` is `HalfUp`, `RoundTiesToEven` is `HalfEven`); `Up`, `HalfDown` and `ZeroFiveUp` are working modes of the library (directed bounds of the certified functions, the guard digit of division) that no context can select. The table uses the kernel names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:197
#: manual/design/decimal.md:565
msgid "Mode"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:197
msgid "IEEE name"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:197
msgid "returns $c+1$ when ($f > 0$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:199
msgid "roundTowardZero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:200
msgid "always"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:201
msgid "roundTowardPositive"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:202
msgid "roundTowardNegative"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:203
msgid "roundTiesToAway"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:205
msgid "roundTiesToEven"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:205
msgid "$f > \\tfrac12$, or $f = \\tfrac12$ and $c$ odd"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:208
msgid "For a negative $x$ the same rule is applied to $|x|$ and the sign restored, so `Ceiling` and `Floor` swap. Every mode $\\circ$ is **monotone**: $x \\le y \\Rightarrow \\circ(x) \\le \\circ(y)$. Monotonicity is what the certification of elementary functions relies on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:213
msgid "Error model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:215
msgid "Let $x$ be in the normal range, $10^{e} \\le |x| < 10^{e+1}$. Representable numbers in that decade are spaced by one unit in the last place, $\\operatorname{ulp}(x) = 10^{e - p + 1}$. Rounding to nearest is off by at most half of it, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:229
msgid "The bound is approached, but not attained, near the bottom of a decade: for $x = 10^{e} + \\tfrac12\\operatorname{ulp}(x)$ the relative error is $\\tfrac12 10^{1-p}/(1 + \\tfrac12 10^{1-p}) < u$. Near the top, $|x| \\approx 10^{e+1}$, the same absolute error is only $\\tfrac12 10^{-p}$ relative. The ratio between the worst and the best relative error inside one decade is therefore"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:240
msgid "the *wobble* of base $\\beta = 10$.[^wobble] In binary the wobble is 2, so for the same storage decimal has a slightly worse worst-case relative error: decimal64 has $u = \\tfrac12 10^{-15} = 5\\cdot 10^{-16}$, binary64 has $u = 2^{-53} \\approx 1.1 \\cdot 10^{-16}$. The directed modes have $|\\delta| < 10^{1-p} = 2u$. The machine epsilon, the distance from 1 to the next larger number, is $10^{1-p}$ — `epsilon_contextual` returns it and `next_up_ctx(1)` in decimal64 is `1.000000000000001`. Below 1 the spacing is ten times smaller: `next_down_ctx(1)` is `0.9999999999999999`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:249
msgid "Goldberg, \"What every computer scientist should know about floating-point arithmetic\", *ACM Computing Surveys* 23(1), 1991, §1.2; Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd ed., SIAM 2002, §2.1–2.2."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:254
msgid "For a subnormal result the spacing is the fixed $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$, so the error bound becomes absolute, $|\\operatorname{fl}(x) - x| \\le \\tfrac12 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$. Because every context operation is correctly rounded, the standard model $\\operatorname{fl}(a \\circ b) = (a \\circ b)(1+\\delta)$, $|\\delta| \\le u$, holds for $+,-,\\times,/,\\sqrt{\\ }$, `fma` and every elementary function whose result is normal, so the classical forward and backward error analyses of Higham carry over with $u = \\tfrac12 10^{1-p}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:264
msgid "One representation, many contexts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:266
msgid "**Problem.** Applications need decimal32/64/128 and arbitrary precision, IEEE semantics and GDA compatibility, without converting between types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:269
msgid "**Options.** One type per format (like hardware); one arbitrary-precision type with the format carried by a context; a type parameterised by the format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:272
msgid "**Choice.** One `Decimal` type holds a sign, a coefficient of any length, an exponent, a class, a NaN-kind bit and the precision of the value. An immutable `DecimalContext` supplies the parameters for contextual operations. A decimal64 computation uses `DecimalContext::decimal64()`; interchange encoders apply the format context before encoding. One representation keeps arithmetic in one place and lets a caller work at 50 digits before rounding to decimal64. It also matches the General Decimal Arithmetic model. The precision of the value sets the precision used by context-free operators and conversions only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:281
msgid "Round once, in one place"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:283
msgid "**Problem.** Double rounding — rounding an already rounded value again — can change a correctly rounded result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:286
msgid "**Choice.** Every finite context result goes through one finalization routine that receives an **exact** result $(s, C, Q)$, with $C$ possibly much longer than $p$. Operation kernels compute exact integers; they never round. The exceptions are operations whose exact result is infinite — division, square root, elementary functions — and two whose exact result would be needlessly long — a sum with a far smaller addend and an integer power — which are described below; each of them still decides the last digit from exact information."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:295
msgid "A single rounding needs a single target exponent. With $D = \\operatorname{digits}(C)$, the exact value has adjusted exponent $a = Q + D - 1$, and the correctly rounded result has exponent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:303
msgid "the first term keeping $p$ digits for a normal result and the second the subnormal grid; the result is $\\circ(C\\,10^{Q-t})\\,10^{t}$, followed by the overflow check (on the value rounded to $p$ digits with unbounded exponent), the underflow flag and the fold-down."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:308
msgid "The routine rounds to the subnormal grid only when $t = E_{\\text{tiny}} > Q$. It tests this condition as $Q + \\max(0, D - p) < E_{\\text{tiny}}$. Then the exact value is below $10^{e_{\\min}}$, and $C$ is rounded once with shift $E_{\\text{tiny}} - Q$. Every other coefficient is rounded once to $p$ digits only, even when $Q < E_{\\text{tiny}}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:314
msgid "Rounding a normal result first to the subnormal grid $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$ and then to $p$ digits can change the answer. The first rounding may land exactly on a midpoint of the second grid, leaving the tie rule to decide a case the exact value already decided. In decimal32, where $E_{\\text{tiny}} = -101$, consider $3.000001\\cdot 10^{-45} \\times 1.500001\\cdot 10^{-45} = 4500004500001\\cdot 10^{-102}$. The first rounding drops digit 1, which is below half, and gives $450000450000\\cdot 10^{-101}$. That is the exact midpoint $4500004.5\\cdot 10^{-96}$. A second `HalfEven` rounding gives $4.500004\\cdot 10^{-90}$, while rounding the exact product once gives $4.500005\\cdot 10^{-90}$ because it lies above the midpoint."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:326
msgid "The directed modes would be unaffected because, for every real $y \\ge 0$ and integers $s_1, s_2 \\ge 0$, $\\lfloor\\lfloor y\\,10^{-s_1}\\rfloor 10^{-s_2}\\rfloor = \\lfloor y\\,10^{-s_1-s_2}\\rfloor$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:331
msgid "Addition with a far smaller addend"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:333
msgid "When one addend $\\ell$ lies far below the other, $h$, the exact sum can have many more digits than the result keeps. Let $t$ be the target exponent of $h$ alone and $g = \\min(q_h,\\ t - 2)$. Every digit of $h$ lies on the grid $10^{g}$, and so does every rounding boundary of the sum: the grid $10^{t}$, its midpoints, and the grid $10^{t-1}$ with its midpoints, which a cancellation can reach. If $0 < |\\ell| < 10^{g}$, the sum $h + \\ell$ lies strictly between the same two neighbours on that grid as the short value $h \\pm 10^{g-1}$, with the sign of $\\ell$. `add_ctx` rounds that value once instead of the exact sum; result and flags are the same in every mode. With precision 7, $1598617.000000000001 - 2\\cdot 10^{-12}$ is `1598616` under `Down`, and $6.0000005\\cdot 10^{-73} + 10^{-101}$ is `6.000001E-73` under `HalfEven`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:346
msgid "How the rounding digit and sticky information are obtained"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:348
msgid "Rounding $C$ (a non-negative integer with $D$ digits) to $D - s$ digits divides by a power of ten,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:355
msgid "and decides between $Q$ and $Q+1$ by comparing $2R$ with $10^{s}$. This single comparison carries exactly the information of the classical *rounding digit* and *sticky bit*. Write $R = r\\,10^{s-1} + R'$ with rounding digit $r \\in \\{0,\\ldots,9\\}$ and rest $0 \\le R' < 10^{s-1}$. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:364
msgid "and since $0 \\le 2R' < 2\\cdot 10^{s-1}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:374
msgid ""
"So $2R > 10^{s}$, $2R = 10^{s}$ and $2R < 10^{s}$ mean \"above, at, below the midpoint\", which is all the half modes need; $R \\n"
"e 0$ is the sticky information the directed modes need; and the last digit of $Q$ is all `ZeroFiveUp` and `HalfEven` need. When $s \\ge D$ the whole coefficient is discarded, $Q = 0$, and only $s = D$ can reach the midpoint: the code then compares $C$ with $5\\cdot 10^{D-1}$. A carry that turns $10^{p}-1$ into $10^{p}$ is removed by one exact division by 10 and an exponent increment."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:382
msgid ""
"The finalizer drops trailing zeros before rounding and raises `inexact` whenever $R \\n"
"e 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:385
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:688
#: manual/performance/binary.md:203
msgid "Division"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:387
msgid "The quotient $a/b$ of two finite non-zero values takes one of three exact routes, in this order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:390
msgid "*Divisor a power of ten.* The quotient is $a$ with a shifted exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:391
msgid "*Terminating quotient.* Reduce $c_a / c_b$ by $g = \\gcd(c_a, c_b)$ to $c_a'/c_b'$. The quotient has a finite decimal expansion if and only if $c_b' = 2^{i}5^{j}$; with $k = \\max(i, j)$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:397
msgid "so the exact result is the integer $c_a' 2^{k-i}5^{k-j}$ with exponent $q_a - q_b - k$, handed to the finalizer like any exact result. Whether the quotient terminates is decided before any gcd is formed: with $c_b = 2^{a}5^{b}u$ and $u$ prime to 10, it terminates if and only if $u$ divides $c_a$ (the reduced denominator $c_b/\\gcd(c_a, c_b)$ contains no prime of $u$ exactly when the gcd takes all of $u$, and $u$ is coprime to the factors 2 and 5 of $c_b$). The valuations $a$ and $b$ are read from the lowest limb when both are below 9, so for a divisor prime to 10 (the usual case) the test is $c_b \\mid c_a$, one division of limb arrays. Only a terminating quotient runs the gcd route above. A non-terminating one, which is nearly every division of random operands, used to pay a gcd of the two coefficients (quadratic, hundreds of allocations at 34 digits) to learn that it has no exact result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:410
msgid "*Non-terminating quotient.* The result is certainly inexact. Let $a_c = \\operatorname{adj}(c_a/c_b)$, found exactly by comparing $c_a$ with $c_b$ scaled by the right power of ten. Scaling the numerator (or the denominator) by $10^{p-1-a_c}$ makes the integer quotient have exactly $p$ digits: $N = Q D + R$. The increment decision compares $2R$ with $D$ — the same midpoint test as above, now with the exact remainder as sticky information — so the quotient is rounded once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:418
msgid "The third route is used for normal results. For subnormal results the code forms the integer quotient of $c_a\\,10^{k}$ by $c_b$ with $k = p + \\operatorname{digits}(c_b) + 2$ (about $\\operatorname{digits}(c_a) + p + 2$ digits), rounds it with `ZeroFiveUp`, and rounds that guarded quotient once more in the context mode, straight to its final grid: $p$ digits, or $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$ for a subnormal quotient. The context-free operator `/` uses the same guarded scheme. By the `ZeroFiveUp` lemma below the two roundings equal one rounding of the exact quotient, so $15/83294$ at five digits is `0.00018009`,[^div-05up] and in decimal32 $1 / 1.9999999999998\\cdot 10^{101} = 5.0000000000005\\cdot 10^{-102}$ gives `1E-101`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:430
msgid "Rounding the guarded quotient in the context mode instead could manufacture a tie: at five digits the guarded quotient of $15/83294$ would end in an exact half unit, and half-even would return `0.00018008`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:434
msgid "`ZeroFiveUp` exists precisely to make such two-step schemes safe. If $x$ is first rounded with `ZeroFiveUp` to $p + k$ digits ($k \\ge 1$) and then with any mode $\\circ$ to $p$ digits, the result equals $\\circ(x)$: an inexact `ZeroFiveUp` result ends in a digit other than 0 and 5, so it is never a $p$-digit number nor a $p$-digit midpoint, and it lies on the same side of every $p$-digit number and midpoint as $x$. The proof is in the attachment.[^attachment] The guarded division above relies on it; the finalizer needs no such lemma, because it rounds once at the target exponent $t$ derived above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:444
msgid "Plain sums, products and quotients"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:446
msgid "The general route of `add_ctx`, `sub_ctx`, `mul_ctx` and `div_ctx` normalizes the exact result, finalizes it and returns flags, through temporaries that are records and tuples: a 34-digit sum made 20 allocations, a product 14 and a quotient 38. Most operations need none of that machinery. When both operands are finite and nonzero, the context has a precision $p$, and the result is neither absorbed, nor subnormal, nor beyond the range, nor folded down by the clamp, the result is the exact result rounded once to $p$ digits, with `inexact` raised when a nonzero digit was discarded and no other flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:455
msgid "The predicates are those of the finalizer turned around. The exact result has adjusted exponent $a$; the plain path requires $e_{\\min} \\le a \\le e_{\\max} - 1$. Then the result is not tiny (no subnormal rounding, no `underflow`), and a carry of the rounding into $10^{p}$ raises the adjusted exponent to at most $e_{\\max}$, so it cannot overflow; the exponent of the rounded result is $a - p + 1$ or one more after a carry, never below $E_{\\text{tiny}}$. For an exact result with fewer than $p$ digits the exponent is the exponent of the exact result, and in a clamping context the plain path requires it not to exceed $e_{\\max} - p + 1$ (otherwise the finalizer folds the digits down, which the general route does). A sum also requires that the smaller adjusted exponent is not below $\\hat e_1 - p + 1$ (no operand is absorbed; that case keeps its own sticky route) and a nonzero exact sum (the sign of a zero sum depends on the rounding direction); a product and a quotient require nonzero operands. A quotient with a finite decimal expansion is left to the general route, because its exponent follows the ideal-exponent rules."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:471
msgid "The direct sum path forms the sum in one limb array and rounds it in place; a product is the exact product rounded at the digit count. A quotient is one division of $c_a \\cdot 10^{k}$ with $k = \\max(0, p + 2 + n_b - n_a)$ ($n_a$, $n_b$ the digit counts of the coefficients), which has at least $p + 2$ digits, rounded with `ZeroFiveUp` and then once to $p$ digits, which by the `ZeroFiveUp` lemma above equals one rounding of the exact quotient in every mode. The remainder test of that division tells a terminating quotient from a non-terminating one; for the cheap cases (equal coefficients, a divisor of one limb) the test is made before the division, so that an exact quotient does not pay for a division it discards. The flag sets are shared constants, like the empty set (`DecimalFlags` is an immutable record compared by value). A 34-digit `add_ctx` makes 5 allocations and 1,739 instructions instead of 20 and 4,427, `mul_ctx` 8 and 2,500 instead of 14 and 3,251, `div_ctx` 13 and 3,840 instead of 38 and 9,212."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:485
msgid "The general route defines the behavior; the direct paths must return the same result and flags. The white-box test compares both routes on random operands of 1 to 130 digits, with mixed signs, cancellations, exponent gaps and carries, for every rounding direction, in clamped and unclamped formats with tight and storage-wide exponent ranges and operands at both ends of the range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:491
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:736
msgid "Square root"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:493
msgid "Exact roots are detected first: after removing trailing zeros and making the exponent even, the operand is $c'\\,10^{2m}$, and $\\sqrt{x}$ is a decimal if and only if the integer $c'$ is a perfect square. Such a root is returned at the member closest to the preferred exponent $\\lfloor q/2 \\rfloor$; if it has more than $p$ digits it is rounded like any exact result. For every other operand $\\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{c'}\\,10^{m}$ is irrational."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:500
msgid ""
"For the irrational case let the target exponent be $t = \\max(E_{\\text{tiny}},\\\n"
"\\lfloor \\operatorname{adj}(x)/2 \\rfloor - p + 1)$. The root has adjusted exponent $\\lfloor \\operatorname{adj}(x)/2 \\rfloor$: from $10^{a} \\le x < 10^{a+1}$ follows $10^{a/2} \\le \\sqrt x < 10^{(a+1)/2}$, and both for even and for odd $a$ the integer part of the exponent is $\\lfloor a/2 \\rfloor$. So $t$ leaves $p$ digits, or stops at the subnormal grid. When $q - 2t \\ge 0$ the operand is scaled to the integer $M = c\\,10^{q - 2t}$ and $r = \\lfloor \\sqrt{M} \\rfloor$ is the truncated root at exponent $t$. When $q - 2t < 0$ the code takes the integer root of $c$ (of $10c$ if $-(q-2t)$ is odd) and divides it by $10^{\\lceil -(q-2t)/2 \\rceil}$; nested floors of a positive real by positive integers compose, so this is again $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x}\\,10^{-t} \\rfloor$. The integer root is computed by the kernel (Zimmermann's Karatsuba square root from three limbs, see *Coefficient kernels*); the iteration it replaced, and that the tests keep as the reference, is Newton's iteration on integers,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:521
msgid "($N < 10^{\\operatorname{digits}(N)}$ gives $\\sqrt N < 10^{\\operatorname{digits}(N)/2} \\le a_0$). Because $\\lfloor (a + \\lfloor y \\rfloor)/2 \\rfloor = \\lfloor (a + y)/2 \\rfloor$ for an integer $a$, the AM–GM inequality $\\tfrac12(a + N/a) \\ge \\sqrt{N}$ gives $a_{k+1} \\ge \\lfloor \\sqrt N \\rfloor$; and $a_{k+1} < a_k$ exactly when $a_k^2 > N$. The iterates therefore decrease strictly to $\\lfloor \\sqrt N \\rfloor$, where the iteration stops."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:528
msgid "Since the root is irrational it is never exact and never a midpoint, so the increment decision needs only the comparison $\\sqrt{x}\\,10^{-t} \\gtrless r + \\tfrac12$, done exactly as $4M \\gtrless (2r+1)^2$ (or $4c \\gtrless (2r+1)^2\\,10^{-(q-2t)}$ in the second branch). Rounding directly at the subnormal target exponent avoids rounding twice for tiny roots."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:534
msgid "A root *can* be exactly halfway when it is exact but longer than $p$ digits: $\\sqrt{6.25} = 2.5$ at $p = 1$ gives `2` under `HalfEven` and `HalfDown` and `3` under `HalfUp`. A halfway root $(r + \\tfrac12)10^{t}$ squares to $(2r+1)^2\\cdot 25 \\cdot 10^{2t-2}$, an odd coefficient with at least $2p+1$ digits when $r$ has $p$ digits ($r \\ge 10^{p-1}$ gives $(2r+1)^2 \\cdot 25 > 10^{2p}$). A normal halfway root therefore needs an operand with more than $2p$ significant digits, and a subnormal one an operand below the format's range. An operand that fits the format (at most $p$ digits, value at least $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$) has a normal root whenever $e_{\\min} \\le 1 - p$, as in all interchange formats, because $\\sqrt{10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}} = 10^{(e_{\\min}-p+1)/2} \\ge 10^{e_{\\min}}$; for such operands the three half modes agree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:547
msgid "[Rounding proofs for decimal](../../attachments/design_decimal_rounding.typ) contains the full proofs of the double-rounding lemma, the overflow table, the fold-down bound, the certification lemma and the NTT bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:551
msgid "Exponent range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:553
msgid "The finalizer works on the rounded coefficient $C'$ with $p$ digits and exponent $Q'$, i.e. on the value rounded with an unbounded exponent range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:556
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:323
msgid "Overflow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:558
msgid "If $\\operatorname{adj}(C' 10^{Q'}) > e_{\\max}$ the result overflows. IEEE IEEE 754-2019 7.4 defines the delivered result as the rounding of the exact value in a format with the same $p$ but unbounded exponent, then saturated: a direction that never increases magnitude cannot leave the finite range, a direction that may increase it goes to infinity. Hence, with $N_{\\max} = (10^{p}-1)10^{E_{\\text{top}}}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:573
msgid "The half modes go to infinity because an overflowing exact value is at least $N_{\\max} + \\tfrac12 10^{E_{\\text{top}}}$ (anything smaller rounds to a finite value and does not overflow), which is at or beyond the midpoint between $N_{\\max}$ and the next power of ten. `ZeroFiveUp` saturates because the last digit of $N_{\\max}$ is 9. Every overflow raises `overflow` and `inexact`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:594
msgid "Subnormals, tininess and underflow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:596
msgid "If the exact value is below $10^{e_{\\min}}$ and needs an exponent below $E_{\\text{tiny}}$, it is rounded once to the subnormal grid: the shift becomes $s = E_{\\text{tiny}} - Q$ and the rounding rule above applies with fewer than $p$ digits kept. The result is **tiny** when its adjusted exponent is below $e_{\\min}$, measured on the exact value. IEEE 754-2019 7.5 requires this for decimal formats (\"tininess is detected before rounding\"). With $p = 3$ and $e_{\\min} = 0$, $0.9951$ and $0.99951$ are both tiny (their subnormal-grid result is `1.00`). A tiny and inexact result raises `underflow`, as required for default exception handling by IEEE 754-2019 7.5. A result that rounds to zero gets exponent $E_{\\text{tiny}}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:608
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:303
msgid "Clamping"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:610
msgid "In every context, exponents above $E_{\\text{top}}$ are not representable, because the encoding has room only for $E_{\\text{top}} - E_{\\text{tiny}} + 1$ exponents. A result with $Q' > E_{\\text{top}}$ that did not overflow is **folded down**: the coefficient is multiplied by $10^{Q' - E_{\\text{top}}}$ and the exponent set to $E_{\\text{top}}$. This never needs more than $p$ digits:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:623
msgid "using $\\operatorname{adj} \\le e_{\\max}$. The value is unchanged; only the cohort member changes. In decimal32, `1E+96` is stored as `1000000E+90`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:637
msgid "Zeros have no digits to pad, so their exponent is simply clamped into $[E_{\\text{tiny}}, E_{\\text{top}}]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:640
msgid "A zero is the one result `quantize_ctx` can accept with a target exponent above $E_{\\text{top}}$: the target may be as high as $e_{\\max}$. Since an IEEE format contains only exponents through $E_{\\text{top}}$ (IEEE 754-2019 3.3), `quantize_ctx` folds the zero exponent down to $E_{\\text{top}}$, as it does for other results. The bound follows from the one-digit coefficient, $e_{\\max} - \\operatorname{digits} + 1 = e_{\\max}$. The reference implementation calls `decFinalize` from `decQuantizeOp` at this bound and lowers the exponent in its clamp branch. Until 0.12, the fold in this package was controlled by the `with_clamp_fold` option."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:650
msgid "Flags as returned values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:652
msgid "**Problem.** IEEE 754 status flags are sticky process state in hardware; GDA adds traps. Hidden state conflicts with the Luna-Flow rule that semantics are explicit, and makes concurrent or compositional code fragile."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:656
msgid "**Choice.** Each context operation returns its own `DecimalFlags`. `combine` is the field-wise OR, so flag sets form a commutative idempotent monoid with identity `DecimalFlags::new()`: accumulating them over a pipeline in any grouping gives the same set, which is exactly the sticky-flag semantics without the state. `DecimalFlags` is an immutable record compared by value, so the identity is one shared value and `combine` returns the other operand when one is clear: no flag set is allocated for an operation that raises nothing. `decimal_checked` packages that accumulation; `decimal_gda` implements sticky status and traps as a separate model. The five IEEE exceptions are `invalid_operation`, `division_by_zero`, `overflow`, `underflow` and `inexact`; `DecimalFlags` has exactly these and `not_computed`. The GDA conditions (`rounded`, `subnormal`, `clamped`, `lost_digits`, `conversion_syntax`, `division_impossible`, `division_undefined`, `invalid_context`) belong to `decimal_gda`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:670
msgid "Quantize and same-quantum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:672
msgid "`x.quantize(y)` returns the value of $x$ with exponent exactly $t = q_y$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:682
msgid "The result must be representable *at that exponent*: the new coefficient must have at most $p$ digits, $t$ must lie in $[E_{\\text{tiny}}, e_{\\max}]$ and the result's adjusted exponent must not exceed $e_{\\max}$. Otherwise the operation is invalid. It never substitutes another exponent, because the exponent is the contract (an amount quantized to cents must have two decimal places). The only change of exponent is the fold-down of [clamping](#clamping): a target $t \\in (E_{\\text{top}}, e_{\\max}]$ is accepted and the result is stored at $E_{\\text{top}}$, exactly like any other result in that range. Rounding a coefficient up can add a digit ($9.99 \\to 10.0$ at two digits of precision), which is why the digit check comes after rounding. `same_quantum` is the predicate $q_x = q_y$ (true for two infinities or two NaNs); it is the test to use before combining values whose exponents must agree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:696
msgid "Conversion to integers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:698
msgid "`to_int_ctx` and its three siblings (IEEE 754-2019 5.4.1 and 5.8) share one function that rounds a finite value $c\\,10^{q}$ to an integer in the direction of the context and checks the range, so the four types differ only in their limits. A value with $q \\ge 0$ is an integer already; one with $q < 0$ is $c / 10^{-q}$ rounded by the same decision function that rounds decimal results (the kernel's rounding decisions: increment or not from the discarded digits and the mode), and `changed` is whether anything was discarded. The power of ten is never formed. An integer of 21 or more digits is out of range for every type ($2^{64}$ has 20 digits), so $\\operatorname{digits}(c) + q > 20$ is decided before any arithmetic, and a fraction with more than $\\operatorname{digits}(c) + 1$ places is the same as one with exactly that many: the value is below $0.1$, and the rounding decision only needs to know that the remainder is nonzero and below one half, so the shift is clamped to $\\operatorname{digits}(c) + 1$. `1E+999999` and `1E-999999` therefore cost as much as `1E+3` and `1E-3`. The invalid operation is signaled for a NaN, an infinity and an out-of-range result, and then only: `exact` adds `inexact` when `changed`, nothing else is ever raised (no clamping: the result is not a decimal), and a negative value that rounds to zero converts to `0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:718
msgid "Conversions between formats"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:720
msgid "`convert_format`, `from_binary_ctx` and `to_binary_ctx` (IEEE 754-2019 5.4.2) each round the exact source value once in the destination's context and return the flags of that rounding. Decimal to decimal is `apply_ctx` of the format's context, so the preferred exponent $Q(\\text{source})$ is the one the normalization keeps when the value fits. Binary to decimal does not form the expansion $c\\,2^e$, whose cost grows with $|e|$: it takes the first $p + 1$ significant digits of $|x|$, truncated, from the correctly rounded digit string of `Binary::to_decimal_string_ctx`, appends a sticky digit $1$ when digits were dropped, and rounds that decimal once with `apply_ctx`. A rounding of a decimal depends on the digits beyond the kept ones only as zero, below half, a tie or above half, and the surrogate (the digits and the sticky digit) lies strictly inside the unit after the truncated digits as $x$ does, so for every kept length up to $p$ (fewer digits for a subnormal result, so nothing is rounded twice) the result and its flags are those of $x$, and the tininess of the unrounded value is the one of $x$ (same leading digit). An exact value has no sticky digit; its trailing zeros are dropped and an integer takes exponent 0, the preferred exponent, or, when it has more than $p$ digits, the member closest to it (IEEE 754-2019 5.2): zeros are appended up to $p + 1$ digits, and the zero that `apply_ctx` rounds away leaves the $p$-digit member with the exponent closest to 0 (`1E+20` in decimal64 is `1000000000000000E+5`). The digits are not formed for a value whose estimate $\\lfloor 0.30103\\,(\\operatorname{bits} + e)\\rfloor$ lies more than two beyond $e_{\\max}$ or three below $E_{\\text{tiny}}$, which stands for every such value in the rounding (overflow, or a result of zero or the least subnormal). Decimal to binary is the exact decimal text rounded by `Binary::from_string_ctx`, which is correctly rounded for any exponent and is checked against MPFR; the corpus runs both directions through the MPFR rows of `mpfr_strtofr` and `mpfr_get_str` and through exact rows for every pair of decimal32/64/128 and binary16/32/64/128 (`tools/generate_decimal_conversion_rows.py`). A NaN keeps its sign and the payload it can hold ($p - 1$ digits, $p - 2$ bits), and a signaling NaN is quieted with the invalid operation exception."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:753
msgid "All three formats share one layout: a sign bit, a 5-bit *combination field* $G$, $w$ *exponent continuation* bits and a *trailing significand* of $10J$ bits, where $p = 3J + 1$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:763
msgid "The biased exponent $E = q + \\text{bias}$ has $w + 2$ bits whose top two bits take only the values 00, 01, 10; this is the $3\\cdot 2^{w}$ count derived above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:767
msgid ""
"**DPD.** The combination field holds the top two exponent bits and the leading digit $d_0$: if $G = ab\\,cde$ with $ab \\n"
"e 11$, the exponent bits are $ab$ and $d_0 = cde \\in [0,7]$; if $G = 11\\,cd\\,e$ with $cd \\n"
"e 11$, the exponent bits are $cd$ and $d_0 = 8 + e$. $G = 11110$ is infinity and $G = 11111$ is NaN, the next bit distinguishing signaling from quiet. The other $3J$ digits are stored in $J$ *declets*, 10 bits for 3 digits, by densely packed decimal.[^dpd] Three digits have 1000 values and 10 bits 1024 codes, an efficiency of $\\log_2 1000 / 10 = 99.66\\%$. Call a digit *small* if it is 0–7 (3 bits) and *large* if it is 8 or 9 (1 bit). The declet $pqr\\,stu\\,v\\,wxy$ uses $v = 0$ for three small digits, which are then stored verbatim in $pqr$, $stu$, $wxy$; $v = 1$ marks at least one large digit, and $wx$, then $st$, say which. Counting by the number of large digits,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:787
msgid "and $512 + 384 + 96 + 8 = 1000$. The first three cases use exactly 512, 384 and 96 codes. The last case has 32 codes for 8 values: $r$, $u$, $y$ carry the low bits of the three digits and $p$, $q$ are ignored, so **24 codes are redundant**: each value with three large digits has four encodings, of which the one with $pq = 00$ is canonical. Decoding accepts all four, `canonical()` rewrites them. For example, `125` is the declet `0010100101` = `0x0A5` (three small digits) and `999` is `0011111111` = `0x0FF`, also written `0x1FF`, `0x2FF`, `0x3FF`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:795
msgid "M. F. Cowlishaw, \"Densely packed decimal encoding\", *IEE Proceedings — Computers and Digital Techniques* 149(3), 2002. IEEE 754-2019 3.5.2 gives the encoding tables; the code implements them as Boolean formulas, and the conformance corpus checks all 1024 declets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:800
msgid "**BID.** The coefficient is stored as a binary integer. If the two bits after the sign are not 11, the next $w+2$ bits are the biased exponent and the remaining $10J + 3$ bits the coefficient. Otherwise the exponent follows the 11 and the coefficient is $2^{10J+3}$ plus the remaining $10J+1$ bits (\"100\" implied). Since $10^{7} - 1 < 2^{24}$, $10^{16}-1 < 2^{54}$ and $10^{34}-1 < 2^{114}$, every coefficient fits; encodings with a coefficient $\\ge 10^{p}$ are non-canonical and decode to zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:821
msgid "`DecimalInterchange` keeps the raw bits so that non-canonical input survives until the caller decides to canonicalize; arithmetic is always on `Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:826
msgid "**Problem.** For transcendental $f$, $f(x)$ is irrational for almost every decimal $x$, so it can only be approximated; a correctly rounded result needs an approximation good enough to decide the rounding (the *table maker's dilemma*)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:831
msgid "**Options.** A fixed-precision evaluation with an a-priori error bound (fast, but correctness then depends on the bound being right for every function and argument); Ziv's adaptive strategy[^ziv] with rigorous error bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:836
msgid "**Choice.** A Ziv loop over rigorous enclosures. For an operation $f$ and input $x$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:839
msgid ""
"Convert $x$ exactly into a binary interval: $\\underline{x} = \\n"
"abla_w(x)$ and $\\overline{x} = \\Delta_w(x)$, `to_binary` with `RoundTowardNegative` and `RoundTowardPositive` at $w$ bits, so $x \\in [\\underline{x}, \\overline{x}]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:843
msgid ""
"Evaluate $f$ on that interval with [`interval`](interval.md), which returns an interval $[L, U] \\n"
"i f(x)$ for every point of the input interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:845
msgid "Convert $L$ and $U$ (dyadic, hence exact decimals) to `Decimal` exactly and round both with the target context. If both give the same representation (`total_order_compare` equal) and the same flags, return it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:848
msgid "Otherwise increase $w \\leftarrow w + \\max(32, \\lfloor w/2 \\rfloor)$ and repeat; after $L$ evaluations report a certification failure, where $L$ is the refinement limit of the context (`DecimalContext::refinement_limit`, twelve by default; a value below one counts as one). With `with_refinement_limit(None)` the count is unbounded, but the loop stops, with the same failure, when $w$ exceeds $\\max(8\\,000\\,000,\\ 256\\,w_0)$ bits (at most $10^9$), so the work and the memory stay bounded. The default changes no result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:857
msgid "The acceptance test is sound because rounding is monotone: $L \\le f(x) \\le U$ implies $\\circ(L) \\le \\circ(f(x)) \\le \\circ(U)$, and if the outer two are the same representation, so is the middle one. The flags transfer as well, provided $f(x)$ is not itself representable: then one endpoint differs from the common result, so both are inexact, and `overflow` and `underflow` are monotone in $|f(x)|$ on an interval that does not contain zero. Representable results are therefore handled before the loop (below)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:866
msgid "The initial working precision is $w_0 = \\max(64,\\ \\lfloor 3.321929\\,\\max(D, p) \\rfloor + 31)$ bits, where $D$ is the number of input digits: one decimal digit needs $\\log_2 10 = 3.3219280\\ldots < 3.321929$ bits, so $\\lfloor 3.321929\\, \\max(D,p) \\rfloor$ bits represent the input and the target, and 31 guard bits more cover the loss of the enclosure. The schedule grows by a factor of about $3/2$ per step (exactly $w + \\lfloor w/2 \\rfloor$ once $w \\ge 64$); the 12 evaluations use $w_0, w_1, \\ldots, w_{11}$, and the last one works with about $w_0 \\cdot (3/2)^{11} \\approx 86\\,w_0$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:876
msgid "Three kinds of inputs are decided outside the enclosure-agreement test. Exact results need their preferred exponent and flags; integer powers use exact integer information; range-limit results can be decided by representatives; and non-integral powers use one composed enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:882
msgid "If $f(x)$ is a representable decimal and the enclosure is not a point, $L < f(x) < U$ round to different neighbours in a directed mode, forever. In a half mode both endpoints round to $f(x)$ itself, but with `inexact`, so the agreement test would *accept* the right value with wrong flags and with all $p$ digits instead of the preferred exponent: the soundness argument above assumed that $f(x)$ is not representable. The code therefore detects exact cases before the loop (`exp(0)`, `ln(1)`, $\\log_{10} 10^{k}$, integer and half-integer arguments of `sinpi`/`cospi`/`tanpi`, odd quarter-integers of `tanpi`, `acos(1)`, integer arguments of `exp2`/`exp10`, $x^{1/2}$, integer powers, …). For the algebraic functions this is a finite integer computation: `rootn` strips the trailing zeros of the coefficient, aligns the exponent to a multiple of the degree $n$ and tests the integer $n$-th root (Newton's iteration from above) of the result, so $\\sqrt[3]{0.008} = 0.2$; `hypot` forms $x^2 + y^2$ exactly when the adjusted exponents of the operands differ by at most $p + 2$ (beyond that, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2} - |x| \\approx y^2/(2|x|)$ is below the last digit the result could have) and tests it with the exact square root, so $\\operatorname{hypot}(0.3, 0.4) = 0.5$; a zero operand gives the other operand's magnitude. `interval` returns point intervals for exact binary results such as $\\log_2 8 = 3$, $\\log_2 0.125 = -3$ and $4^{1.5} = 8$. A power whose exact value is representable in decimal but not in binary is decided in decimal instead: the exponent is reduced to $p/q$ in lowest terms — a decimal exponent is $c \\cdot 10^{-k}$, so $q$ keeps only the twos and fives the coefficient cannot cancel — and the result is exact exactly when $x^{p/q}$ is a decimal. By Bézout there are integers $a, b$ with $ap + bq = 1$, so $x^{1/q} = (x^{p/q})^{a} x^{b}$ is rational whenever $x^{p/q}$ is, and a rational $q$-th root of a decimal is again a decimal (its denominator divides a power of ten). The test therefore writes $x = r \\cdot 10^{qe}$ with $r$ an integer keeping fewer than $q$ trailing zeros, decides whether $r$ is a perfect $q$-th power by a bisection over the root's digit count, and only then raises the root to $p$ (the reciprocal of the root, for a negative exponent). Every intermediate is then no longer than the result, so $0.0016^{0.25} = 0.2$, $32^{0.2} = 2$ and $(2^{1200} \\cdot 10^{-1200})^{1.5} = 2^{1800} \\cdot 10^{-1800}$ at precision 1000 are all decided at once, although $x^{3}$ in the last one has 1084 digits. Only a value of at most $p + 1$ significant digits can sit on a rounding boundary — a representable value in the directed modes, a midpoint in the half modes — so the search covers exactly those and leaves a longer exact value, which lies strictly inside its rounding cell, to the enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:923
msgid "Integer powers are not certified; they are rounded once from exact integer information. `pow_ctx` with an integral exponent $n$ that does not fit in $p$ digits first runs the General Decimal Arithmetic square-and-multiply with $p + \\operatorname{digits}(n) + 2$ working digits. That scheme only ever discards zero digits of an exact power, so it reports `inexact` exactly when the true power is inexact, and it gives exact powers their GDA cohort and flags. An inexact result is then replaced by the correctly rounded one. With $c$ the coefficient stripped of trailing zeros, if $|n| \\cdot \\operatorname{digits}(c) \\le \\max(4p, 64)$ the exact power $c^{|n|}$ is formed and rounded once (a negative $n$ divides once, correctly rounded). Otherwise $c^{|n|}$ has more than $p + 1$ significant digits and no trailing zero, so it is never a $p$-digit number or midpoint; lower and upper bounds from square-and-multiply in `Down` and `Up` at a working precision of $p + \\operatorname{digits}(n) + 8$ digits, doubled on each attempt, are refined until they round to the same representation. So $3.339434^3 = 37.240765000\\ldots$ is `37.24077` in decimal32. If the bounds leave the working exponent range, the GDA result is kept; its error is below one unit in the last place. A power that the exponent bounds place below $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-2}$ (including an exact power of ten below $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$) is not evaluated: it rounds like any non-zero value below $\\tfrac12 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$, to zero in the nearest modes and to $\\pm 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$ when the rounding direction is away from zero for its sign (IEEE 754-2019 7.5)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:948
msgid "An enclosure such as $[0, \\text{tiny}]$ has endpoints that round with different flags. Any value $\\ge 10^{e_{\\max}+2}$ overflows identically, and any non-zero value $\\le 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-2} < \\tfrac12 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}}$ rounds identically (to zero or to the smallest subnormal, depending only on the mode and sign), so far endpoints are replaced by such representatives; the binary test uses $3.322 > \\log_2 10$ so the replacement is conservative. `exp`, `exp2` and `exp10` share one range decision. For $b^x$ with $b \\in \\{e, 2, 10\\}$ let $k \\ge 1/\\log_{10} b$ be the integer 3, 4 or 1. Then $x \\ge k\\,(e_{\\max}+1)$ gives $b^x = 10^{x \\log_{10} b} \\ge 10^{e_{\\max}+1}$, above the largest finite number $(10^p - 1)\\,10^{e_{\\max}-p+1}$, so the overflow result of the rounding direction applies (IEEE 754-2019 7.4); and $x \\le -k\\,(1 - E_{\\text{tiny}})$ gives $b^x \\le 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-1}$, below half of the smallest subnormal and, as $E_{\\text{tiny}} \\le e_{\\min}$, tiny before and after rounding, so the result is zero or the smallest subnormal by direction with `inexact` and `underflow` (IEEE 754-2019 7.5). The comparison is exact and the margin is the whole slack between $k$ and $1/\\log_{10} b$: an argument between the true boundary and the threshold is not decided here but by the Ziv loop, which is sound there because the threshold is at most $4\\,(1 - E_{\\text{tiny}})$ with $E_{\\text{tiny}} \\ge -1999997$, far below the exponent range of the binary enclosure. Without the decision a very negative argument (for `exp2`, beyond $2^{31}$ binades) has the enclosure $[0, \\text{tiny}]$ and never certifies. The conformance page records tests on both sides of each threshold and at results just inside and outside the range, in all eight rounding directions. `power` bounds $y \\log_2 x$ with directed 128-bit arithmetic for the same purpose."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:976
msgid "For a positive base the Ziv loop evaluates $x^y = \\exp(y \\ln x)$ in interval arithmetic: one `ln` enclosure, one interval product and one `exp` enclosure, instead of the four corners times two directions (eight correctly rounded binary powers) of `interval`'s `try_pow`. The loop needs an enclosure, not tight endpoints, and correct rounding makes the delivered value independent of the enclosure's width, so the result, cohort and flags are those of the tighter evaluation. The composition loses about $\\lceil \\log_2 |y \\ln x| \\rceil$ bits of relative accuracy, because the relative error of $\\exp(t)$ is the absolute error of $t$. The initial working precision therefore grows by a bound of that quantity, taken from the adjusted exponents of $x$ and $y$ ($|\\ln x| \\le \\ln 10\\,(|\\operatorname{adjusted}(x)| + 1)$ and $|y| < 10^{\\operatorname{adjusted}(y)+1}$), so the first attempt usually decides when this bound is sufficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:992
msgid "`exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `log2p1` and `log10p1`, along with the later IEEE 754-2019 9.2 families, share three range and exact-result rules. An exact result is formed as an exact decimal, rounded once by the context and then moved to the cohort member nearest the preferred exponent, using the cohort rule of `power`. A context that cannot hold the value rounds it with its own flags; a result whose certified bracket of $\\log_2$ lies above $\\log_2 10\\,(e_{\\max}+2)$ or below $\\log_2 10\\,(E_{\\text{tiny}}-2)$ rounds like the representative $10^{e_{\\max}+2}$ or $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-2}$ using a certified logarithmic range bound; every other result comes from the Ziv loop above. No result is a zero or an infinity chosen because the argument is small or large: a tiny result is the rounding of its certified endpoints, so subnormals, `Underflow` under the before-rounding rule and the sign of a zero are those of the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1008
msgid "The four functions evaluate `expm1(x ln b)` and `log1p(x) / ln b` on the input interval ($\\ln 2$ and $\\ln 10$ as certified `interval` intervals). Both primitives are series in $x$, so the relative width of the enclosure is a few units of the working precision for every legal operand, down to $x = 10^{-1\\,999\\,996}$; no tiny-argument anchor is needed because the slopes $\\ln b$ and $1/\\ln b$ are irrational. Before the loop:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1016
msgid "$b^x$ is rational only for an integer $x$ ($2^r$ and $10^r$ are irrational for a non-integer rational $r$), and $\\log_b(1+x)$ is rational only when $1+x = b^k$. For $0 < |n| \\le 4(p+2)$ the value $2^n-1$, $10^n-1$, $-(1-2^{-m})$ or $-(1-10^{-m})$ is formed and rounded once; $k$ is found from $x$ without forming $1+x$ ($x+1$ a power of two, compared first on its lowest nine digits; all nines; a coefficient $10^m - 5^m$ or $10^m - 1$ with $m$ fraction digits). The preferred exponent is 0 (IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2), so `exp2m1(4)` is `15` and `log2p1(-0.50)` is `-1`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1025
msgid "For an integer $n > 4(p+2)$, $2^n$ has $D \\ge p+3$ digits and every rounding boundary of a $D$-digit integer is a multiple of the even number $5 \\cdot 10^{D-p-1}$; neither $2^n$ (no factor 5) nor the odd $2^n - 1$ is one, so both lie in one open rounding cell and `exp2m1(n)` is `exp2(n)`. $10^n - 1$ lies above the midpoint $10^n - 5 \\cdot 10^{n-p-1}$ and below $10^n$, where an enclosure can never separate it from the representable $10^n$; the representative $10^n - 10^{n-p-2}$ has the same rounding and is formed instead of a number of $n$ digits. Towards $-1$, for $x \\le -(p+2)$ (base 10) or $x \\le -(4p+8)$ (base 2) the value is $-1 + \\delta$ with $0 < \\delta \\le 10^{-(p+2)}$ and rounds like $-(1 - 10^{-(p+2)})$, as for `expm1`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1037
msgid "$x \\ge \\lceil 3.322\\,(e_{\\max}+2) \\rceil$ (base 2) or $x \\ge e_{\\max}+2$ (base 10) gives the overflow result of the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1040
msgid "For $|x| \\le 10^{-5}$ all four satisfy $0 < |f(x)| < 3|x|$ with the sign of $x$, so $|x| < 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-3}$ puts $f(x)$ below $10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-2}$, where it rounds like the representative $\\pm 10^{E_{\\text{tiny}}-2}$ that the enclosure would use for its endpoints. The result is that rounding, decided without converting $x$ to binary (the conversion of $10^{-1\\,999\\,997}$ alone costs about a second)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1048
msgid "For $|x| < 10^{-(p+11)}$, write $x = m\\,10^{q}$; then $f(x) = c\\,x\\,(1 + d)$ with $c = \\ln b$ or $1/\\ln b$ and $|d| \\le 2.31|x|$, so $f(x)/10^{q}$ lies in $c\\,m\\,[1 - 2^{k}, 1 + 2^{k}]$ for $2^{k} \\ge 4|x|$. The endpoints of that enclosure have a few dozen digits, where the binary endpoints of $f(x)$ itself would expand to about $0.7|q|$ digits; scaled back by $10^{q}$ they are rounded with the context and accepted under the same agreement test as the loop, which decides the rare value too close to a rounding boundary. The certified $\\ln 2$ and $\\ln 10$ intervals are kept per working precision. Both only change the cost: on 10,000 random rows of each function the executed instructions fell from 57 to 12 (`exp2m1`), 60 to 14 (`exp10m1`), 102 to 17 (`log2p1`) and 102 to 18 (`log10p1`) billion, the level of `exp` (13) and `log1p` (18)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1062
msgid "$x = -1$ is $-\\infty$ with `division_by_zero` and $x < -1$ is invalid for `log2p1` and `log10p1` (Table 9.1). Near $-1$ an operand of $D$ digits keeps $1 + x \\ge 10^{-D}$, which $w_0$ resolves, as for `log1p`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1068
msgid "`rSqrt`, `compound` and `powr` take their exact results from the exact-case path, and none of them expands a binary endpoint near an extreme power of ten."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1072
msgid "`rsqrt(x)` terminates exactly when $x = 2^{2i}5^{2j}$: with $c$ the coefficient stripped of trailing zeros and $e$ its exponent, $c$ is $2^a$ or $5^b$ with $a$ (or $b$) and $e$ even, and the value is $5^{a/2}10^{-(e+a)/2}$ or $2^{b/2}10^{-(e+b)/2}$. It is formed when it has at most $p + 1$ digits and given the preferred exponent $-\\lfloor Q(x)/2 \\rfloor$ (IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2); a longer one ends in a 5 or a non-zero even digit beyond the $(p+1)$-th place, so it is neither representable nor a midpoint, and the enclosure decides it. Every other $x$ is written $x = s\\,10^{2k}$ with $s \\in [1, 100)$: the Ziv loop encloses $1/\\sqrt{s}$, whose endpoints are moderate, and multiplies them by $10^{-k}$ (an exponent shift) before the context rounds them, under the same agreement test. An operand $10^{-1\\,999\\,997}$ costs what $1$ costs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1086
msgid "`compound(x,n)` uses the special values of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 (with $x < -1$ invalid also for $n = 0$, Table 9.1). For a tiny $x$, $(1+x)^n - 1$ has the sign of $nx$ and $|(1+x)^n - 1| \\le |nx|\\,e^{|nx|} < 10^{\\operatorname{adj}(x) + 12}$ for $|n| \\le 2^{31}$, so a bound decides a one-sided neighbour of 1 and $1 + x$ (up to two million digits) is never formed. Otherwise $1 + x = c\\,10^{e}$ is formed exactly ($c$ without trailing zeros, $d$ digits, exponent $\\min(0, Q(x))$ before stripping). For $n > 0$, $c^n$ has more than $\\max((d-1)n, 0.301n)$ digits and no trailing zero, so beyond $p + 1$ digits it is neither representable nor a midpoint; shorter powers are formed and rounded once with the preferred exponent $\\lfloor n \\min(0, Q(x)) \\rfloor$. For $n < 0$, $c^{n}$ terminates only for $c = 2^a$ or $5^b$ (the values $5^{a|n|}10^{-(a+e)|n|}$ and $2^{b|n|}10^{-(b+e)|n|}$), formed while they have at most $p + 2$ digits. Every other case is the Ziv loop on $\\exp(n \\operatorname{log1p}(x))$ ($\\operatorname{log1p}$ of $x$ itself, so $1 + x$ is never rounded), after `decimal_range_from_log2_bracket` on $n \\operatorname{log1p}(x)/\\ln 2$ whenever $|n| \\max(\\operatorname{adj}(x) + 2, -Q(x), 1)$ does not already keep the result inside the range. `Int32`'s minimum $-2^{31}$ is taken as an `Int64`. `pown(1 + x, n)` is not used: when this function was written, its exponents beyond the machine count gave wrong values and some negative ones a spurious `Clamped` for a base near 1 (issues [#348](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/348) and [#350](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/350), since fixed). Instead, the Ziv loop evaluates $\\exp(n\\operatorname{log1p}(x))$ without forming $1 + x$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1112
msgid ""
"`powr(x,y)` follows the invalid pairs listed in IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1: $(\\pm 0, \\pm 0)$, $(+\\infty, \\pm 0)$, $(1, \\pm\\infty)$ and $x < 0$, and a NaN result for a NaN operand even when $y = \\pm 0$. Two cases are not stated and are read as follows: $x < 0$ with $y$ a quiet NaN is invalid (the NaN rule is stated \"for $x \\ge 0$\"), and $+\\infty^{y}$ for $y \\n"
"e 0$ and $x^{\\pm\\infty}$ for $x \\n"
"otin \\{0, 1\\}$ follow $e^{y \\ln x}$ by continuity. Every remaining pair has $x \\ge 0$, where $x^y$ is the same function as `pow`, and the preferred exponents agree ($\\lfloor y Q(x) \\rfloor$), so the result is `power` on $|x|$ (a $-0$ is passed as $+0$, where `pow` would apply its odd-integer sign rule)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1124
msgid "`asinPi`, `acosPi`, `atanPi` and `atan2Pi` evaluate $\\operatorname{asin}(x)/\\pi$, $\\operatorname{acos}(x)/\\pi$, $\\operatorname{atan}(x)/\\pi$ and $\\operatorname{atan2}(y, x)/\\pi$ in the existing Ziv driver, with $\\pi$ enclosed as $[\\operatorname{RD}(\\pi), \\operatorname{RU}(\\pi)]$ taken from the cached binary-splitting constant of `binary` (twice the correctly rounded $\\pi/2$ of `atan(+inf)`, the same interval as $4\\operatorname{atan}(1)$ bit for bit) and the angle enclosure divided by it (error: the angle, $\\pi$ and the division, about 4 ulp relative)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1135
msgid "By Niven's theorem a rational $x$ has a rational $\\operatorname{asin}(x)/\\pi$ or $\\operatorname{acos}(x)/\\pi$ only at $0, \\pm\\tfrac12, \\pm 1$ and a rational $\\operatorname{atan}(x)/\\pi$ only at $0, \\pm 1$, so the dyadic exact results are those of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 and $\\operatorname{atanPi}(\\pm 1) = \\pm\\tfrac14$, $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(\\pm a, \\pm a) = \\pm\\tfrac14, \\pm\\tfrac34$. They are built by the exact-case path at the preferred exponent 0 (IEEE 754-2019 9.2.2). $\\pm\\tfrac16$, $\\tfrac13$ and $\\tfrac23$ do not terminate, so they sit strictly inside a rounding cell and the loop decides them. No Pi function is anchored at a tiny argument: $x/\\pi$ is irrational and nowhere near a representable value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1147
msgid "The clusters near $\\pm\\tfrac12$ and $\\pm 1$ are the only inexact results that approach a representable value. Their distance from the anchor does not depend on the digits of the operand, so a $w_0$ that grows with $D$ cannot cover it. A bound decides each case on one side of the anchor: $\\operatorname{atanPi}(x) = \\pm(\\tfrac12 - \\delta)$ with $0 < \\delta < 1/(\\pi|x|) < 10^{-\\operatorname{adj}(x)}$ for a large $|x|$; $\\operatorname{acosPi}(x) = \\tfrac12 - \\operatorname{asin}(x)/\\pi$ with distance at most $|x|/2 < 10^{\\operatorname{adj}(x)+1}$ for a tiny $|x|$ (the easy one to miss: $\\operatorname{acos}$ of a tiny $x$ is near the irrational $\\pi/2$, but $\\operatorname{acosPi}$ is near the representable $\\tfrac12$, below it for $x > 0$ and above for $x < 0$); $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(y, x)$ with $D = \\operatorname{adj}(y) - \\operatorname{adj}(x)$ is $\\pm(\\tfrac12 \\mp \\delta)$ with $\\delta < 10^{1-D}$ for a large ratio (below $\\tfrac12$ in magnitude for $x > 0$, above for $x < 0$), and $\\pm(1 - \\delta)$ with $\\delta < 10^{D+1}$ for $x < 0$ and a small ratio. The guard (error exponent below the anchor's adjusted exponent minus $p + 3$) picks the threshold; between it and the moderate range the enclosure resolves the gap at the working precision. The tests compare every anchored result with the plain loop in all eight rounding modes, on both sides."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1168
msgid "For $|x| \\le 10^{-4}$, $\\operatorname{asin}(x)/x \\in [1, 1 + x^2]$ and $\\operatorname{atan}(x)/x \\in [1 - x^2, 1]$, and the same holds for $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(y, x)$ with $x > 0$ and the ratio $y/x$. Once $x^2 < 2^{-(8\\max(D,p)+400)}$ the loop encloses the moderate value $(s/\\pi)(1 \\pm 2^{-k})$ with $s = x\\,10^{-\\operatorname{adj}(x)}$ (or the quotient of the two scaled mantissas) and shifts the endpoints by $10^{\\operatorname{adj}(x)}$ before the context rounds them, as for `rsqrt` (`decimal_certified_scaled_result`): an operand $10^{-1\\,999\\,996}$ costs what $10^{-300}$ costs, where the exact decimal endpoints of a binary number near $2^{-6.6\\cdot 10^6}$ have about two million digits (an unscaled `asinpi(7E-9999)` took more than a minute in a debug build before this path existed). For $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(y, x)$ with $x > 0$ the derivation is: $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}(y, x) = \\operatorname{sign}(y) \\operatorname{atan}(r)/\\pi$ with $r = |y/x| = (s_y/s_x)\\,10^{D}$, where $s_y, s_x \\in [1, 10)$ are the operands scaled by their adjusted exponents and $D = \\operatorname{adj}(y) - \\operatorname{adj}(x)$, so $r < 10^{D+1}$; for $r \\le 1$, $\\operatorname{atan}(r)/r \\in [1 - r^2, 1]$, hence $\\operatorname{atan}(r)/\\pi = (s_y/(s_x \\pi))\\,10^{D}(1 - e)$ with $0 \\le e \\le r^2 < 10^{-t}$, $t = -(2D + 2)$, and the loop encloses the moderate factor $(s_y/(s_x\\pi))(1 - 2^{-k})$, $k \\le 3.32\\,t$, and shifts it by $D$. The path is taken when $3t \\ge 8\\max(D_{\\text{digits}}, p) + 400$, where it is decidable; the derivation itself needs only $t > 0$. The operands of every other case are first multiplied by $10^{-\\operatorname{adj}(x)}$ (the ratio, hence the angle, is unchanged), so operands with exponents of millions of digits and a moderate ratio cost what moderate operands cost. `library/src/decimal/atan2pi_scaled_wbtest.mbt` and the generated rows of `tools/generate_decimal_atan2pi_scaled_rows.py` (Python `decimal` at 450 digits, a cross-check only) cover ratios on both sides of the condition, both signs of $y$ and of $x$ (for $x < 0$ the result is $\\pm(1 - \\operatorname{atan}(r)/\\pi)$, next to $\\pm 1$, and takes the other anchored path), the four formats and all eight rounding directions; replacing $1 - 2^{-k}$ by $1 + 2^{-k}$ or the condition by $t > 0$ makes rows fail (checked by mutation)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1202
msgid "Operands of extreme exponent and the cost of a first attempt"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1204
msgid "The following paths keep the cost of a call independent of the size of its operands' exponents while preserving correctly rounded results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1207
msgid "*Conversion to binary.* For $x = c\\,10^{-s}$ the quotient $\\lfloor c\\,2^{u} / 10^{s} \\rfloor$ is formed once, with $u = p + 3 + \\lceil s \\log_2 10 \\rceil$ (so $10^s < 2^{u-p-3}$ and the quotient keeps at least $p + 3$ bits: two bits below the target position and a sticky bit), made odd when the remainder is not zero; the remainder test is a multiplication, not a second division, and the lower and upper bound of a ball come from the same quotient. For $|{\\rm exponent}_{10}| \\ge 4096$ the exact $5^s$ (rebuilt on every call above the power cache) is replaced by a lower and an upper bound $m\\,2^{k}$ with $m$ of $p + \\text{bits}(c) + 80$ bits, made by binary powering with a directed trim after every product; the two bounds are rounded in the requested mode and the result is kept only if they agree (monotonicity of rounding), otherwise the exact conversion decides, so the result is the exact conversion bit for bit. For a negative exponent the quotient of the bounds is used only when $2 s > 4\\,\\text{bits}(c)$, where $5^s > c$ guarantees that the division is inexact (the sticky bit is set)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1222
msgid "*First working precision.* $\\max(64, \\lfloor 3.321929\\,\\max(D, p) \\rfloor + 31)$ bits instead of $\\max(128, 4 \\max(D, p) + 64)$: the schedule grows by a factor of 1.5 when an enclosure does not decide, so a low start costs an attempt and never a result. On 16,536 rows (exp, exp2, exp10, expm1, the inverse functions of $\\pi$ and atan2Pi, five formats) the number of operand conversions was 14,489 before and 14,490 after."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1228
msgid "*Tiny arguments of exp, exp2, exp10, expm1 and log1p.* For $|x| < 10^{t}$, $t \\le -1$: $|b^x - 1| \\le |x|\\ln b\\,e^{|x| \\ln b} < 10^{t+1}$ for $b \\in \\{e, 2, 10\\}$ ($\\ln b < 2.31$, $e^{0.231} < 1.3$) with the sign of $x$, so the result is $1$ plus or minus less than $10^{t+1}$, one-sided next to the representable 1 and decided by a one-sided bound once $t + 1 < -(p + 3)$; and $\\operatorname{expm1}(x) - x = x^2/2 + \\dots > 0$, $x - \\operatorname{log1p}(x) = x^2/2 - \\dots > 0$, both below $x^2$ for $0 < |x| < 1/2$, so expm1 lies above $x$ and log1p below it, within $10^{2t}$. Rows on both sides of the thresholds, both signs, three formats and all eight directions have independently computed expected values; flipping a side makes rows fail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1239
msgid "*Exact tests before the loop.* An exact $n$-th root of a decimal needs $\\text{exponent} \\bmod n = 0$ once trailing zeros are removed ($c\\,10^{\\text{shift}}$ with $0 < \\text{shift} < n$ and $\\min(v_2(c), v_5(c)) = 0$ has $v_2$ or $v_5$ equal to the shift, not a multiple of $n$), and the root comes from the kernel (square roots first for even degrees, Newton from the root of the leading digits); `atan2` forms the exact ratio only when $2D < -(p + 4)$ with $D = \\operatorname{adj}(y) - \\operatorname{adj}(x)$, the necessary condition of the tiny-argument decision; `compound` does not form $1 + x$ when $x$ is an integer multiple of 10 with $\\operatorname{adj}(x) \\ge p + 2$ (the sum ends in 1: no power of 2, 5 or 10) or for $n > 0$ when $\\operatorname{adj}(x) < -(p + 2)$ (more than $p + 2$ digits); `sqrt` reuses the remainder of the integer root."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1250
msgid "*Negative integer power.* For $n = |{\\rm count}|$, $|b|^n \\in [10^{n\\,lb}, 10^{n\\,ub})$ with $lb = \\operatorname{adj}(b)$, $ub = lb + 1$, hence $1/|b|^n \\in (10^{-n\\,ub}, 10^{-n\\,lb}]$: it overflows when $-n\\,ub > e_{\\max}$ and lies below half of the smallest subnormal when $-n\\,lb \\le E_{\\text{tiny}} - 2$; the exponent product of the exact path is formed in 64 bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1256
msgid "Outward-rounded endpoints"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1258
msgid "The Ziv driver turns the two binary endpoints $L \\le U$ of an enclosure into decimals and rounds both to the context. Expanding $c \\cdot 2^{-s}$ exactly costs $c \\cdot 5^{s}$, about $0.7 s$ digits, which is quadratic in the decimal exponent of the argument under a wide-range context (`1E-999990` took more than 80 s in `expm1`). For a non-degenerate enclosure the driver instead rounds $L$ down and $U$ up to $D = \\max(p + 12,\\ 0.3\\,b + 4)$ digits, $b$ being the bit length of the endpoint coefficients (so a refined enclosure is never widened beyond about one binary ulp). The powers $5^{s}$ and $2^{s}$ are computed by binary powering with a directed trim to $D + 4$ digits after every product: $O(\\log s)$ products of $D$-digit numbers, independent of $s$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1269
msgid "*Soundness.* Every trim moves towards the chosen side (down for the lower magnitude bound, up for the upper one, with signs swapped for negative endpoints), and products of positive bounds are bounds, so by induction the computed power brackets the exact power, and $L' \\le L \\le U \\le U'$. Rounding to the context is monotone, so $\\mathrm{rnd}(L') = \\mathrm{rnd}(U')$ implies $\\mathrm{rnd}(x)$ is that value for every $x \\in [L, U]$, and the flags are compared as before. The guard digits only affect tightness, not soundness. A degenerate enclosure ($L = U$, binary-exact) keeps the exact expansion, and infinite, zero and out-of-range endpoints are converted as before. A certified result is unchanged; only an enclosure that straddles a rounding boundary at a distance below $10^{-D}$ relative is refused one step earlier, and the next refinement widens $D$ with the binary precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1282
msgid "The elementary functions and the `pow` family refuse contexts with $p$ or $|e|$ above 999,999 for an ordinary operand (the precision part is the field `elementary_precision_limit` of the context, `Some(999_999)` by default and `None` for no limit, an escape entry with no guarantee of time or memory); the special operands and the exact points of IEEE 754-2019 9.2.1 are answered first, in every context, and the limit is tested before the first step that computes (`not_computed`, and `Err` of the certification-failure kind in the `try_` forms; the decNumber limit, see the `decimal_gda` design page), which bounds the size of the exact endpoint conversions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1286
msgid "A. Ziv, \"Fast evaluation of elementary mathematical functions with correctly rounded last bit\", *ACM TOMS* 17(3), 1991; J.-M. Muller et al., *Handbook of Floating-Point Arithmetic*, 2nd ed., Birkhäuser 2018, ch. 12; J. van der Hoeven, \"Ball arithmetic\", 2009, for the enclosure model."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1291
msgid "Coefficient kernels"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1293
msgid "**Problem.** Rounding at decimal positions needs fast division by powers of ten and fast digit counts; large precisions need sub-quadratic multiplication and division."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/decimal.md:1297
msgid "**Choice.** `DecCoeff`, defined in the internal package `internal/decimal_kernel` that `decimal` shares with `decimal_gda` (neither package exposes it), is either a `Small(UInt64)` below $10^{18}$ or a little-endian array of base-$10^{9}$ limbs with no leading zero limb and a cached digit count. The package holds only status-free integer arithmetic; the finalizer, flags and contexts stay in each decimal package (see [the shared kernel](decimal_gda.md#a-gda-package-independent-of-decimal)). Base $10^9$ is the largest power of ten below $2^{30}$, so a limb product is below $10^{18} < 2^{60}$; shifting by a multiple of nine digits moves limbs, and `digits10` is a limb count plus the digits of the top limb. `BigInt` appears only at the public boundary."
msgstr ""

