msgid "2 (also 2 with one extremum inside, 0 with an extremum of each kind)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:368
msgid "8 exact-rational series per refinement round, plus $\\pi$ per round"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:368
msgid "2 correctly rounded binary evaluations, and one $\\pi$ enclosure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:369
msgid "8 series per round, plus $\\pi$ per round"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:369
msgid "2 evaluations, and one $\\pi$ enclosure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:371
msgid "**Division and square root.** The lower bound of $\\boldsymbol{x} / \\boldsymbol{y}$ is one quotient of two endpoints (chosen by the signs, see the corner argument above) rounded down, the upper bound one quotient rounded up. The square root needs $\\sqrt{\\underline{x}}$ rounded down and $\\sqrt{\\overline{x}}$ rounded up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:377
msgid ""
"**Products.** If $\\boldsymbol{a}$ straddles zero and $\\boldsymbol{b} \\ge 0$ ($\\boldsymbol{b} \\n"
"e [0, 0]$), then $a \\mapsto ab$ is nondecreasing for each $b \\ge 0$ and $b \\mapsto ab$ is nondecreasing for $a \\ge 0$ and nonincreasing for $a \\le 0$, so the minimum is $\\underline{a}\\,\\overline{b}$ and the maximum $\\overline{a}\\,\\overline{b}$ (the other three sign cases are mirror images). Two factors that both straddle zero keep the four products."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:384
msgid "**`atan2`.** Away from the origin and the branch cut, $\\partial \\theta / \\partial y = x / r^2$ and $\\partial \\theta / \\partial x = -y / r^2$. For $y \\ge 0$ the angle is nonincreasing in $x$, so the minimum has $x = \\overline{x}$ and the maximum $x = \\underline{x}$; at that $x$ the angle is nondecreasing in $y$ if $x > 0$, nonincreasing if $x < 0$, and constant ($\\pi/2$) at $x = 0$. For $y \\le 0$ (with $\\underline{y} < 0$) the angle is nondecreasing in $x$ and the same rule applies to $y$. If $\\boldsymbol{y}$ straddles zero the branch-cut case has been excluded, so $\\underline{x} \\ge 0$ and both bounds sit at $x = \\underline{x}$ (the minimum at $\\underline{y}$, the maximum at $\\overline{y}$). A zero endpoint of $\\boldsymbol{y}$ belongs to the side of the other endpoint: $\\mathrm{atan2}(+0, x < 0) = \\pi$ is the limit from $y > 0$ and $\\mathrm{atan2}(0, x > 0) = 0$ the limit from $y < 0$. Every edge of the box is therefore monotone and the extrema over a box are at corners, also when the origin is a corner or lies on an edge, since the angles seen near the origin are those of the neighbouring corners. The axis points that earlier versions added are between these two extremes. A box whose extreme corner is the origin (a degenerate box) uses the general corner hull."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:404
msgid "**`sinpi`, `cospi`, `sin`, `cos`.** With the index range $K$ of the previous section, an extremum of each kind inside gives $\\pm 1$ without evaluation. Without any extremum inside, $\\boldsymbol{x}$ lies in one arc between two consecutive extrema and the function is monotone there: sine increases through an index $\\equiv 0 \\pmod 4$ and decreases through $\\equiv 2$ (when $K$ contains an index), and otherwise the arc is $k = \\lceil q^- \\rceil - 1$, increasing for $k \\equiv 0, 3$ and decreasing for $k \\equiv 1, 2$. Cosine is sine shifted by one index. The lower bound is then the endpoint value rounded down at the lower end of the arc direction and the upper bound the other endpoint rounded up; with one extremum inside, the opposite bound is the better of the two endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:416
msgid "**`sin`, `cos`, `tan` (`try_` forms).** Each endpoint value is the correctly rounded value of the binary kernel in the directed mode, which is the value the exact-rational series produced (it rounded an enclosure that rounded uniquely). The enclosure of $\\pi$ is needed only for the index range $K$ (the critical points and the poles); it is taken once from `certified_pi_interval` with $w + 48$ bits, where $w = p + m + 96$ is the working precision of the first refinement round ($p$ the precision of the interval, $m$ the binade of the larger endpoint). Its width, about $2^{-(w + 47)}$, is smaller than that of the rational enclosure of the old refinement loop (about $2^{-(w + 19)}$), so $K$ is contained in the first-round range of the old code. The old code decided a pole at its first round too, so `try_tan` returns the whole line only where the old code did. What is identical by construction: every endpoint value, and the result whenever the old code needed no refinement round. What is not: when the old code refined (an endpoint within about $2^{-w}$ of a multiple of $\\pi/2$) it used a narrower $K$ of a later round, and the new $K$ can then hold a spurious index, only for $|\\xi - k\\pi/2| < 2^{-(p + 140)}$ (an absolute bound that does not depend on the number of bits of the endpoint). A spurious extremum does not change a sine or cosine bound, since that close to an extremum the directed value already rounds to $\\pm 1$; a spurious pole makes the tangent the whole line, which keeps the inclusion property. The non-`try` `sin`, `cos` and `tan` are these `try_` forms with a fallback: they return $[-1, 1]$ (Entire for `tan`) for an argument the kernel cannot certify, which the `try_` forms report as an error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:442
msgid "Certified evaluation of transcendental endpoints"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:444
msgid "$e^{\\underline{x}}$ is not a dyadic number, so an endpoint needs a directed rounding of the exact value: $\\operatorname{RD}_p(f(a))$ is a valid lower endpoint and $\\operatorname{RU}_p(f(b))$ a valid upper endpoint by property (i). This package does not implement the series itself. Each endpoint is evaluated by the certified `try_*_ctx` function of [`binary`](binary.md) in an unbounded context of the interval's precision, rounded toward $-\\infty$ for a lower bound and toward $+\\infty$ for an upper bound; those functions refine a working precision until the two directed roundings agree (a Ziv-style acceptance test[^ziv]) and report a certification failure when the work budget runs out."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:455
msgid "What this package adds is the interval analysis around the point kernels: the domain restriction (an argument outside the domain is dropped, and the result is Empty when nothing is left, which is decided by comparisons before any kernel runs), the monotonicity and critical-point analysis of the previous section (which endpoint gives which bound, where an extremum of `sin` or a pole of `tan` lies), the handling of infinite endpoints, and the fallback range. An enclosure of $\\pi$ is needed only to locate critical points and poles of the trigonometric functions, and for the fallbacks of the inverse trigonometric functions; it comes from the cached binary-splitting constant of `binary` (`atan(+inf)` is $\\pi/2$ correctly rounded in each direction, and the scaling by 2 is exact)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:467
msgid "A. Ziv, \"Fast evaluation of elementary mathematical functions with correctly rounded last bit\", *ACM TOMS* 17(3), 1991; J.-M. Muller et al., *Handbook of Floating-Point Arithmetic*, 2nd ed., Birkhäuser, 2018, §10."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:471
msgid "The IEEE 1788 decoration model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:473
msgid "A bare interval result says where the values lie, not whether the function was defined. IEEE 1788 attaches a *decoration* to each result of evaluating $f$ on a box $\\boldsymbol{x}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:477
msgid "Decoration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:477
msgid "Property $p_d(f, \\boldsymbol{x})$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:479
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is non-empty and bounded, $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq D_f$, $f$ is continuous at each point of $\\boldsymbol{x}$, and the result is bounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:480
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is non-empty, $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq D_f$ and $f \\vert_{\\boldsymbol{x}}$ is continuous"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:481
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ is non-empty and $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq D_f$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:482
msgid "always true"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:483
msgid "the value is NaI, not an interval"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:485
msgid "The decorations are totally ordered by strength, $\\text{com} > \\text{dac} > \\text{def} > \\text{trv} > \\text{ill}$, because each property implies the next (continuity of $f$ at each point of $\\boldsymbol{x}$ implies continuity of the restriction, but not conversely: `atan2` restricted to a box lying on its branch cut from above is continuous, while `atan2` is not). A decorated operation $g$ applied to decorated inputs $(\\boldsymbol{y}_j, d_j)$ returns"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:497
msgid "*Why the minimum is sound.* Suppose $\\boldsymbol{y}_j$ was produced by $f_j$ on $\\boldsymbol{x}$ with property $p_{d_j}$, and $g$ has $p_{d_g}$ on the box of the $\\boldsymbol{y}_j$. If $d \\ge \\text{def}$, each $f_j$ is defined on $\\boldsymbol{x}$ with values in $\\boldsymbol{y}_j$ (inclusion property), and $g$ is defined on those values, so $g \\circ (f_1, \\dots, f_k)$ is defined on $\\boldsymbol{x}$. If $d \\ge \\text{dac}$, the same holds for continuity, because a composition of continuous functions is continuous. Boundedness for `com` is a property of the final result and is checked on it. By induction the decoration of a whole expression is a true statement about the expression on the input box. This is what interval existence proofs need: for example, if $\\boldsymbol{x}$ is bounded and $F(\\boldsymbol{x}) \\subseteq \\boldsymbol{x}$ with decoration at least `dac`, the function is continuous on the compact non-empty interval $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and maps it into itself, so Brouwer's theorem gives a fixed point in $\\boldsymbol{x}$. Boundedness matters: $\\xi \\mapsto \\xi + 1$ maps Entire into itself, continuously, without a fixed point. Without the decoration, $\\sqrt{[-1, 4]} = [0, 2]$ would wrongly suggest that $\\sqrt{\\cdot}$ is defined on $[-1, 4]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:516
msgid "The package computes $d_g$ from the operands by the domain tests listed in the [API reference](../api/interval.md#decorated-intervals): division by an interval containing 0, a logarithm reaching $\\xi \\le 0$, `sqrt` below 0, a pole of `tanpi` or of a negative-degree `rootn` in the argument and so on give `trv`; `atan2` across its branch cut gives `def` (defined but discontinuous) and touching the cut from above gives `dac`. Set operations (`intersection`, `convex_hull`, `cancel_*`) are not point functions and always give `trv`. The result is made canonical: an empty result is always `trv` (the properties above require a non-empty box), and `com` on an unbounded result becomes `dac` (this is how overflow is reported). NaI, the result of an invalid decorated construction, absorbs every operation, and is distinct from $\\emptyset$, which is a valid set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:529
msgid "Integer functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:531
msgid ""
"`sign`, `ceil`, `floor`, `trunc`, `roundTiesToEven` and `roundTiesToAway` (IEEE 1788-2015 Table 9.1) are nondecreasing with integer values, so the bare result is $[f(\\underline{x}), f(\\overline{x})]$ and no rounding happens. Their decoration needs one observation: a function with values in $\\mathbb{Z}$ (or $\\{-1, 0, 1\\}$) restricted to an interval is continuous exactly when it is constant on it, by the intermediate value theorem. So the strongest decoration $d_g$ of 11.2 is `def` when $f(\\underline{x}) \\n"
"e f(\\overline{x})$. When $f$ is constant, `dac` holds, and `com` holds when in addition $\\boldsymbol{x}$ is bounded and contains no point where $f$ is discontinuous on the real line. With $k = f(\\underline{x}) = f(\\overline{x})$ that last test is an endpoint comparison, because a constant box lies in a single step of the function:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:544
msgid "Points of discontinuity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:544
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{x}$ contains one iff"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:546
msgid "integers, $\\operatorname{ceil}(n) = n$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:547
msgid "integers, $\\operatorname{floor}(n) = n$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:548
msgid "nonzero integers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:548
msgid "$k > 0$: $\\underline{x} = k$; $k < 0$: $\\overline{x} = k$; $k = 0$: never"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:550
msgid "$\\underline{x} = k - 1/2$ or $\\overline{x} = k + 1/2$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:552
msgid "This is why the ITF1788 corpus has `ceil([1.1, 2.0]_com) = [2, 2]_dac` (the restriction is constant, but $2$ is a point of discontinuity) next to `floor([-1.2, -1.1]_com) = [-2, -2]_com`. An unbounded box is never `com`, and is `def` for `ceil`, `floor`, `trunc` and the roundings (the function is not constant on it), `dac` for `sign` on a half line that excludes 0. The corpus's 55 decorated integer-function cases and the tests of the package (each function with the jump as the lower endpoint, the upper endpoint, a point box and in the interior) agree with this rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:561
msgid "Reverse multiplication and two-output division"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:563
msgid "$\\boldsymbol{c} /_{\\text{set}} \\boldsymbol{b}$ (IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.5) is computed by cases on whether $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ and $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{b}$. Both intervals are nonempty (otherwise the set is empty)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:567
#: manual/design/interval.md:804
msgid "Set"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:569
msgid "$\\mathbb{R}$: $x \\cdot 0 = 0 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ for every $x$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:570
msgid ""
"the interval quotient $\\boldsymbol{c} / \\boldsymbol{b}$ (closed endpoints; the endpoint 0 of an unattained limit is open when $0 \\n"
"otin \\boldsymbol{c}$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:571
msgid "empty"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:572
msgid ""
"only $y \\n"
"e 0$ contributes: one or two components below"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:574
msgid "In the last row let $n$ be the end of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ nearest to zero. The divisor values $y \\in [\\underline{b}, 0)$ give $x \\le n / \\underline{b}$ when $\\boldsymbol{c} > 0$ and $x \\ge n / \\underline{b}$ when $\\boldsymbol{c} < 0$; the values $y \\in (0, \\overline{b}]$ give $x \\ge n / \\overline{b}$ when $\\boldsymbol{c} > 0$ and $x \\le n / \\overline{b}$ when $\\boldsymbol{c} < 0$. A divisor side exists only when $\\underline{b} < 0$, respectively $\\overline{b} > 0$. The quotient $n / \\pm\\infty$ is 0 and is the unattained end of the component, so that component is open there. Each finite quotient is rounded outward, which keeps the closure a valid enclosure and tight in the type (the exact endpoint is attained, because $y = \\underline{b}$ or $\\overline{b}$ is in $\\boldsymbol{b}$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:586
msgid "`mul_rev_to_pair` closes the components. `mul_rev(b, c, x)` intersects each component, with its open ends, with the closed interval $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and takes the hull of what is nonempty (a lower end $L$ and upper end $U$ meet when $L < U$, or $L = U$ and both ends are closed). This is why the hull of the set and the intersection of $\\boldsymbol{x}$ with its hull differ: for $\\boldsymbol{b} = [-1, 1]$, $\\boldsymbol{c} = [1, 1]$ the set avoids $(-1, 1)$, so the prior $[0, 0.5]$ gives Empty. All 533 corpus cases of `mulRev`, `mulRevTen` and `mulRevToPair` agree with the construction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:595
msgid "Reverse functions of even and monotone algebraic functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:597
msgid "`sqr_rev`, `abs_rev`, `cosh_rev` and `pown_rev` (IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4) share one scheme. The preimage of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ under $\\varphi$ is a union of at most two intervals, found from the inverse of $\\varphi$ at the ends of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ after cutting $\\boldsymbol{c}$ to the range of $\\varphi$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:602
msgid "for an even $\\varphi$ ($x^2$, $\\lvert x\\rvert$, $\\cosh$, an even $x^p$) with inverse $r$ on the positive side, and $\\boldsymbol{c}' = \\boldsymbol{c} \\cap \\text{range}$: the two pieces $[-r(\\overline{c'}), -r(\\underline{c'})]$ and $[r(\\underline{c'}), r(\\overline{c'})]$;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:606
msgid "for an odd $x^p$ with $p > 0$, increasing: $[r(\\underline{c}), r(\\overline{c})]$;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:607
msgid "for a negative power, $x^p$ is decreasing on each side of the pole 0, so the roles of the two ends swap, $x = 0$ is excluded, and the end of a piece at 0 is open: the unbounded end of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ gives $r = 0$, which is only a limit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:612
msgid "Each finite end is one directed rounding of the exact root of one exact endpoint (`sqrt`, `rootn`, `acosh` of a point interval), so the closed pieces are the tightest in the type. The hull with the prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$ meets each piece, with its open ends, with $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and takes the hull of the nonempty parts, the same routine as `mul_rev`. The trivial decoration (`trv`) is attached by the decorated forms. All 360 corpus cases of these functions in `libieeep1788_tests_rev.itl` and `abs_rev.itl` agree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:620
msgid "Reverse sine, cosine and tangent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:622
msgid "`sin_rev`, `cos_rev` and `tan_rev` (IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4) return the hull of the part of a periodic union of intervals that lies in the prior $\\boldsymbol{x}$. A piece end has the form $t = \\tfrac{m}{2}\\pi + \\sigma\\,\\alpha(s)$ with an integer $m$, $\\sigma = \\pm 1$ and $\\alpha$ one of $\\arcsin$, $\\arccos$, $\\arctan$ of an endpoint $s$ of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ (a pole of $\\tan$ has no $\\alpha$). The result takes the piece ends that matter, so the comparison of a prior end with a piece end has to be exact; a wrong answer there moves a result end by a whole period (for example `sin_rev([0, 0], x = [RU(pi), 6])`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:630
msgid "*Which piece ends can be floats.* If $t = d$ with $d$ a nonzero float, then $d$ is algebraic, and so is $s$. For $\\sin d = \\pm s$ the Lindemann-Weierstrass theorem says $e^{id}$ is transcendental, so $\\sin d$ cannot be algebraic; the same holds for $\\cos d$ and $\\tan d$. Every end is therefore irrational (a pole $\\pm\\pi/2 + \\pi k$ and the ends from $s = \\pm 1$ are nonzero rational multiples of $\\pi$) except $t = 0$, which needs $m = 0$ and $\\alpha = 0$: $\\arcsin 0$ and $\\arctan 0$, and $\\arccos 1$. Those ends are compared with the prior by an exact test, and every other end has a strict answer for every float $d$. A pole therefore never equals a float, and the trigonometric reverse functions need no open flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:640
msgid "*Comparison and rounding.* Each end is evaluated as an enclosure at a working precision $w$, starting at $p + b + 96$ bits ($b = \\max(0, k + 1)$ for the adjusted exponent $k$ of the larger prior end), from the certified enclosure of $\\pi$ and the certified `asin`, `acos`, `atan` of the exact point $s$. A comparison with a prior end $d$ is decided as soon as the enclosure excludes $d$, else $w$ is doubled. The value written for a result end is the outward rounding of an enclosure that contains no float of the result precision, which is exactly $\\operatorname{RD}(t)$ or $\\operatorname{RU}(t)$; here too $w$ is doubled until that holds. The first step is enough for almost every input, and not for all: a float can lie within a small fraction of an ulp of a multiple of $\\pi/2$, which is why the loop is needed. The loop is capped at 12 doublings (the same budget as the certified forward functions). At the cap an undecided comparison counts as met in both conditions (a piece that might meet the prior is never dropped) and the end written for such a piece is the prior's own end, a valid bound of both possibilities; a rounding is that of the last enclosure. The result is then an enclosure of the hull, possibly wider. A white-box test sets the cap to 0 and to 1 and checks, on cases of the reference test, that the result contains the hull and stays inside the prior. The pinned ITF1788 corpus never leaves the first step: its 136 cases need $w = p + b + 96$ at most."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:657
msgid "*Pieces and indices.* A piece $[\\mathrm{lo}(k), \\mathrm{hi}(k)]$ belongs to a family with $m = \\text{offset} + \\text{period} \\cdot k$ (in units of $\\pi/2$, the period is 4 for $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ and 2 for $\\tan$). The first piece that reaches the lower prior end $x_l$ has its upper end in a window of a dozen consecutive values of $m$ around $2 x_l/\\pi$, which is computed exactly in rational arithmetic from the lower and upper bound of the enclosure of $\\pi$, widened by a stated margin: an end lies in $[(m-2)\\pi/2, (m+2)\\pi/2]$ since $\\lvert\\alpha\\rvert \\le \\pi$, so a piece below the window cannot reach $x_l$ and the predecessor of a piece above it already does. The same holds at $x_h$ with the lower ends. Every piece of the window is tested for meeting the prior (`hi >= x_l` and `lo <= x_h`, both exact), the lower end of the result is the minimum over the met pieces of $x_l$ or $\\operatorname{RD}(\\mathrm{lo})$, and the upper end the maximum of $x_h$ or $\\operatorname{RU}(\\mathrm{hi})$; no two transcendental ends are compared with each other, since $\\operatorname{RD}$ is monotone. The work is two searches of a few pieces each, whatever the prior spans, and its cost follows the number of bits of the period index through the working precision. The only limit is the one of the forward functions: an end of the prior whose reduction needs more than 8,000,000 working bits ($\\max(0, k) + p + 96$ for the adjusted exponent $k$ of the end) is kept (valid, not tight). It is decided before any work (the working precision comes only from the ends inside it, and a prior whose finite ends are all beyond it is returned at once), so such an end never costs $\\pi$ at its precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:676
msgid "*Tightness against the corpus.* The tests compare the result with an enumeration of every piece (`trig_reverse_reference_wbtest.mbt`), with no index arithmetic in common with the implementation, over 4,200 random cases at precisions 24 and 53 that include priors with an end next to a piece end. Six distinct corpus cases (twelve with the decorated copies) have an expected value that is one or two ulps wider than the hull; they are listed on the [conformance page](../conformance/interval.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:683
msgid "Reverse power with an interval exponent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:685
msgid "`pow_rev1(b, c, x)` (IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4) reduces to the set division of `mul_rev` in logarithm coordinates (see the [API page](../api/interval.md#intervalpow_rev1)): the pieces of $\\exp(L /_{\\text{set}} \\boldsymbol{b})$ come from the same case split as $c /_{\\text{set}} b$ (whether $0 \\in L$, that is $1 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$, and whether $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{b}$), with the sign of an end of $L$ decided by comparing the end of $\\boldsymbol{c}$ with 1. The end $x = 0$ of a piece is open (it comes from $\\ln x \\to -\\infty$); the zero part $\\{0\\}$ is a piece of its own; $x = 1$ is open when it is the limit of a quotient by an unbounded $\\boldsymbol{b}$ and attained when $c_\\text{end} = 1$; every other finite end is attained."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:694
msgid ""
"*Exactness of an end* $x = c^{1/\\beta}$, $c > 0$, $\\beta \\n"
"e 0$ finite. Write $\\beta = s\\,u\\,2^j$ and $c = C\\,2^\\gamma$ with $u$ and $C$ odd, $p = 2^{\\max(-j, 0)}$ and $q = u\\,2^{\\max(j, 0)}$. Then $x = c^{s p / q}$ with $\\gcd(p, q) = 1$, and $x$ is rational iff $c$ is a perfect $q$-th power in $\\mathbb{Q}$, that is $C = m^q$ and $q \\mid \\gamma$ (for $C > 1$ this needs $q \\le \\operatorname{bitlen}(C)$, a comparison, and then one integer root; for $C = 1$ it needs $q \\mid \\gamma$, a divisibility test; no power of $c$ is formed). The value is then $(m\\,2^{\\gamma/q})^{s p}$. There are three classes of end and each has its own line in the procedure:"
msgstr ""

