#: manual/design/interval.md:708
msgid "A rational that is not dyadic is never a float, so its comparisons are strict without any refinement. An irrational value is not rational, so it differs from every float, and the loop terminates: the working precision starts at $p + 64 + e_t$, with $e_t$ the exponent of $t = \\ln(c)/\\beta$ (the result needs the absolute error of $t$ below $2^{-p}$), and doubles up to the cap of 12 as in `sin_rev`; at the cap an undecided comparison counts as met in both conditions and the end written for that piece is the prior's own end. The sign of $t$ is exact, so $\\exp(t) > 1$ iff $t > 0$: the enclosure is strict at the float 1, and an exponent such as $\\beta = 2^{10^9}$, where $x = 1 + 2^{-10^9}\\ln c$, is decided at once ($\\operatorname{RD} = 1$, $\\operatorname{RU} = 1 + \\text{ulp}$). The stored range is decided with a certified upper bound of $\\ln 2$, by a test that is sufficient and never wrong. Above: every finite float is below $2^{e_{\\max}+1}$ ($e_{\\max}$ is the exponent of the leading bit), so if the enclosure of $t$ lies wholly at or above $(e_{\\max}+1)\\ln 2$ the enclosure of the value is [largest finite float at $w$ bits, $+\\infty$], built explicitly, and a lower end then rounds down to the largest finite float of the result precision; the top binade $[2^{e_{\\max}}, 2^{e_{\\max}+1})$ is finite and goes through $\\exp$. Below: the stored values at $w$ bits go down to the leading bit $e_{\\min}-w+1$, so if the enclosure of $t$ lies wholly at or below $(e_{\\min}-w)\\ln 2$ the enclosure is $[0, 2^{e_{\\min}-w+1}]$, the smallest positive stored value at $w$ bits, and an upper end rounds up to the smallest positive stored value of the result precision. Between the limits $\\exp$ is used, with gradual underflow (the enclosure is at most one quantum $2^{e_{\\min}-w+1}$ wide). `Interval::exp` of an interval with an end past the range returns the range of the function $[0, +\\infty]$ at some precisions (issue [#569](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/569)); the point `exp` of the lower end is a tight bound, so the two ends give the enclosure. If the enclosure of $t$ straddles the upper limit, $\\exp$ returns an infinite upper end; the loops stop with it only if its lower end is checked to be at least $2^{e_{\\max}+1}(1 - 2^{-(w-64)})$ (the largest finite float at $w - 64$ bits), which says exactly that the upper end is $+\\infty$ and the lower end rounds down to the largest finite float of the result at every precision $p \\le w - 64$, hence at every further precision; otherwise the loops go on to their cap. If it straddles the lower limit the loops go on and terminate, since the quantum shrinks with $w$ while the value is fixed. An exact dyadic end in the subnormal range is kept exact, at a datum precision of at least $e_{\\min} - \\text{low} + 1$ bits within `pow_value_budget`, where $\\text{low}$ is the exponent of the coefficient's lowest bit (the stored values at $P$ bits are the multiples of $2^{e_{\\min}-P+1}$; the leading-bit formula is too small for a coefficient of more than one bit), and the datum built is checked to carry the value asked (an exact class never carries a rounded value) (for example $2^{-2^{30}}$ is the stored value $2^{e_{\\min}-2}$, and $3 \\cdot 2^{e_{\\min}-5}$ needs 6 bits; the result for a point set is $[v, v]$ for every prior that contains it, in about 0.2 s); an exact end above $2^{e_{\\max}+1}$ (for example $2^{(2^{30})}$), a rational end in the subnormal range, and a dyadic end below the budget are decided by the loop, which clamps at the limits (a rational is never a float, so its separation terminates at the first precision)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:717
msgid "*Budgets*, in bits and not in terms of 53: the exactness test runs only for $C$ and $q$ within `pow_int_budget` = 65,536 bits (an exact 65,535-bit square takes about 80 ms, against about 20 ms for the forward `sqrt` of the same operand) and an exact value is materialised only within `pow_value_budget` = 262,144 bits (a value of millions of bits would be built only to be rounded to $p$ bits). Above a budget the end goes to the loop with its documented fallback. The corpus needs none of this: its 429 cases need the first working precision of the loop at most ($w = p + 64 = 117$ at $p = 53$, measured), and the oracle reproduces all of them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:725
msgid "*Tests.* The definition tests cover the classes (including a 2000-bit perfect square and a $\\beta$ with 500 fractional bits), the open ends at 0 and 1 against a prior that touches them from both sides, and exponents and prior ends of magnitude $2^{\\pm 10^9}$. The reference test (`pow_reverse_reference_wbtest.mbt`) does not use the pieces: it evaluates the condition for a float $x$ with the forward `Interval::pow` at 200 bits, since $\\beta \\mapsto x^\\beta$ is monotone and its range over $\\boldsymbol{b}$ is the interval `pow(x, b)`, and checks, at precisions 24, 53 and 113, that every float certainly in the set is in the result, that a point a quarter of a float outside either end that comes from a piece is not in the set, and that the float one step inside is not certainly outside while the next one is certainly inside. A sample runs in every test run; the long run is an interval gate stage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:735
msgid "Reverse power with an interval base"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:737
msgid "`pow_rev2(a, c, x)` (IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4) is the same set division with the roles exchanged (see the [API page](../api/interval.md#intervalpow_rev2)): the unknown is the exponent, and the set is $L /_{\\text{set}} M$ with $L = \\ln(\\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$ and $M = \\ln(\\boldsymbol{a} \\cap (0, +\\infty))$, plus $(0, +\\infty)$ when $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{a}$ and $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$. The pieces come from the same case split as `pow_rev1` (whether $1 \\in \\boldsymbol{c}$ and whether $1 \\in \\boldsymbol{a}$, the signs of the logarithms being comparisons with 1), and a piece end is the quotient $\\ln(c_\\text{end}) / \\ln(a_\\text{end})$, with the infinities and the open 0 as in `pow_rev1` (here no value is mapped through $\\exp$, so there is no end at 1)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:746
msgid ""
"*Exactness of an end* $r = \\ln(C)/\\ln(A)$ for floats $A \\n"
"e 1$ and $C > 0$. Write $A = \\alpha\\,2^i$ and $C = \\gamma\\,2^j$ with $\\alpha$ and $\\gamma$ odd and positive (the exponents $i$ and $j$ carry the sign of the logarithms). $r$ is rational iff $A$ and $C$ are multiplicatively dependent, and then:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:750
msgid "End"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:754
msgid "exactly one of $\\alpha$, $\\gamma$ is 1"
msgstr ""

