"irrational ($C \\n"
"e 1$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:755
msgid "both exceed 1"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:755
msgid "the multiplicative Euclidean algorithm gives $B$ with $\\alpha = B^{i'}$, $\\gamma = B^{j'}$ and $\\gcd(i', j') = 1$; $r = j'/i'$ iff $i\\,j' = j\\,i'$, else irrational"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:757
msgid "The algorithm divides the larger odd integer by a power of the smaller, $y^k$ with $k = \\lfloor (\\operatorname{bitlen}(x) - 1) / \\operatorname{bitlen}(y) \\rfloor$ (so that $y^k \\le x$; it divides $x$ whenever both are powers of one base), and an inexact division proves independence; a high power of a small base ($3$ against $3^{40000}$) takes a few steps, not one per factor. The exponents of $B$ are recovered by running the steps backwards, and the exact check $\\alpha = B^{i'}$, $\\gamma = B^{j'}$ is made before the value is used. The value $j'/i'$ is a float iff the reduced denominator is a power of two (class dyadic), and otherwise a rational that is not a float (compared by exact cross-multiplication, rounded by integer division with directed rounding, the sign taken into account). An odd part beyond `pow_int_budget` = 65,536 bits gives the class Unknown, decided by the loop like an irrational end."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:767
msgid "*Working precision.* The first working precision of the loop is $p + 64 + e_r$, with $e_r$ the exponent of the quotient: $\\ln$ has a relative error of about $2^{-w}$ also for an argument next to 1 (the enclosure of $\\ln(1 + 2^{-52})$ keeps its full width in bits), so only the integer part of the quotient costs bits. A base one unit from 1 gives a quotient of about $2^{51}$, and the tests check that its enclosure is tight (adjacent floats) at precision 53."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:772
msgid "*Budgets*, in bits: one call of the exactness test at the budget ($3^{41340}$ against $3$, 65,524 bits) takes about 7 ms, against about 6 ms for the forward `pow` of the same operand at 200 bits, so the budget of `pow_rev1` is kept. The corpus needs none of it: the oracle finds no irrational piece end among its 375 statements (every end is exact, a float or a rational), so the loop and its fallback are covered by the tests below and not by the corpus."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:776
msgid "*Corpus.* Two statements of the corpus expect a value that is valid but wider than the hull (`powRev2 [0.25, 0.5] [2, inf]` expects Entire and `powRev2 [0.25, 1] [2, inf]` expects $[-\\infty, 0]$, where $a_0^\\beta \\ge 2$ with $a_0 < 1$ needs $\\beta \\le -1/2$); they are refinements, with the oracle (`tools/itl_pow2_reverse_oracle.py`, which derives the set from the corner quotients and shares no code with `interval`) giving 373 equal and 2 refined of 375 statements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:782
msgid "*Tests.* The definition tests cover the classes (including the Euclid steps, $3$ against $3^{40000}$, and a beyond-budget odd part), exact and rounded rational ends with their sign, the examples of the corpus, the open ends at 0 against a prior that touches them, a base one unit from 1 on both sides, and bases, results and prior ends of magnitude $2^{\\pm 10^9}$. The reference test (`pow_reverse2_reference_wbtest.mbt`) evaluates the condition for a float $\\beta$ with the forward `Interval::pow` at 200 bits (the range of $a_0^\\beta$ over $\\boldsymbol{a}$ is the interval `pow(a, beta)`) and checks, at precisions 24, 53 and 113, that every float certainly in the set is in the result, that a point a quarter of a float outside an end that comes from a piece is not in the set, and that the float one step inside is not certainly outside while the next one is certainly inside. A sample runs in every test run; the long run is an interval gate stage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:792
msgid "Reverse arctangent of two arguments"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:794
msgid "`atan2_rev1(b, c, x)` and `atan2_rev2(a, c, x)` (IEEE 1788-2015 10.5.4, equations (6) and (7), Table 10.1; see the [API page](../api/interval.md#intervalatan2_rev1-and-intervalatan2_rev2)). With $\\Theta = \\boldsymbol{c} \\cap (-\\pi, \\pi]$ the set is the projection of the sector $\\{\\, r(\\cos t, \\sin t) : r > 0,\\ t \\in \\Theta \\,\\}$ on the axis of the unknown (the ordinate for Rev1, the abscissa for Rev2), cut by the strip of the given operand: a point of the ray at the angle $t$ has the ordinate $r \\sin t$ and the abscissa $r \\cos t$. Away from the axis directions this is the product $y = b_0 \\tan t$ (Rev1) or $x_0 = a_0 \\cot t$ (Rev2) of an operand point and the trigonometric value, so each piece is an interval product, and the hull is taken over the pieces inside the prior as for `pow_rev1`. Write $b^+ = \\boldsymbol{b} \\cap (0, \\infty)$ and $b^- = \\boldsymbol{b} \\cap (-\\infty, 0)$ (likewise $a^\\pm$)."
msgstr ""

