msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:863
msgid "`from_string` (IEEE 1788-2015 `textToInterval`, 9.7, 12.11, 12.12.7, 13.2; see the [API page](../api/interval.md#intervalfrom_string-and-decoratedintervalfrom_string)) is a hand-written parser of the portable grammar (Tables 9.5 and 12.2) over the characters of the string. A number literal is read into one of four exact descriptions: an infinity, a decimal $d \\cdot 10^{e}$ (the digits without the point and the exponent minus the number of digits after the point), a hexadecimal $h \\cdot 2^{e}$ (the exponent minus four times the number of hexadecimal digits after the point), or a rational $p/q$ with $q$ positive. An exponent field is saturated at $10^{15}$ in magnitude (beyond every range). The uncertain form $m?rve$ is turned into two decimal descriptions by the definition of 9.7.3: $\\operatorname{ulp}(m) = 10^{-k}$ with $k$ the number of digits after the point of $m$, the bounds are $(M \\mp r) 10^{e-k}$ with $M$ the integer of the digits (one ulp being $10^{-k}$; for the empty radius, half an ulp, as $(10M \\mp 5) 10^{e-k-1}$), the direction $u$ or $d$ keeps one side, and the radius $?$ is unbounded; the arithmetic is on big integers, so no rounding precedes the final one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:874
msgid "*Rounding.* Each bound is rounded once, outward and exactly: a decimal and a hexadecimal description go through the correctly rounded directed conversions of `Binary` (`from_string_ctx`, `from_hex_ctx`, which accept any exponent), and a rational is divided by integer division to a quotient of at least $p + 3$ bits with the sticky remainder (the same rule as the exact rational ends of `pow_rev1`). A literal whose exact value is beyond the stored range is therefore decided by those conversions from the exponent without forming the value, and `1e400000000` has the lower end the largest finite float and the upper end $+\\infty$. Both converters accept an exponent field of any length (`from_hex_ctx` saturates it at $2^{60}$ like `from_string_ctx`; before issue 597 it rejected an exponent beyond $10^{12}$ and the parser clamped it). The invariants are checked: a failure of a converter or of the digit parser aborts instead of returning a bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:881
msgid "*The order $l \\le u$.* It is decided from the exact values, so rounding never makes a nonempty literal Empty and an empty one nonempty: two decimal bounds are compared by the adjusted exponent and then the digit strings (trailing zeros removed, the shorter padded with zeros), which needs no power of ten whatever the exponent; two hexadecimal bounds are compared exactly by the position of the leading bit and then the aligned coefficients, without a power; any other pair is first separated by roundings at 64 bits and otherwise compared by cross-multiplication of the exact numerators and denominators, a power being formed only for an exponent of magnitude at most 65,536. A pair that is neither separated nor within that limit (two bounds of different radices that agree to 64 bits and have an exponent beyond 65,536) is undecided: 12.12.7 case (b) allows it and requires the result to contain both bounds, so the result is the hull of the four roundings (a false positive, which the standard permits; a false negative is not possible)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:890
msgid "*Rejections.* Every failed call is an `Err` of the parse kind whose message names the rule of the grammar or of 12.11 that was broken (a missing digit, the exponent field of a hexadecimal literal, the denominator of a rational, the order of the bounds, an infinite bound on the wrong side, the characters after the literal, the decoration literal, a decoration not permitted for the value by 11.4, a decoration on a bare literal). The test table asserts the reason of every rejected string. A literal longer than `interval_literal_max_length` is not parsed and gives Entire (12.11.5)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:896
msgid "*Evidence.* The independent oracle `tools/interval_literal_oracle.py` (pure Python, no `interval` code) evaluates a literal from whole-string patterns (a special form, the two bound strings of an inf-sup form cut at the comma, the groups of an uncertain pattern) to an exact `Fraction`, derives the uncertain form from the definition of the ulp, and rounds each bound with integer arithmetic against the stored range (gradual underflow to the quantum $2^{e_{\\min}-p+1}$, the largest finite float $(2^p - 1) 2^{e_{\\max}-p+1}$); it also checks every one of 20,000 rational literals against the rounded ends by cross-multiplication (the rounded lower end is not above the value and the float below it is below, and likewise for the upper end). The generated table (9,919 literals: the vectors of Tables 9.4 and 12.1, the negative examples, and random decimal, hexadecimal, rational, uncertain, decorated and special literals at precisions 24, 53 and 113, bare and decorated) is compared with the parser in `interval_literal_wbtest.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:904
msgid "Relations: certainly and possibly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:906
msgid "An interval stands for an unknown point, so a comparison of two intervals asks a quantified question. Two quantifiers give the useful relations:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:920
msgid "For the first line, \"$\\Leftarrow$\" is $\\xi \\le \\overline{x} < \\underline{y} \\le \\eta$. For \"$\\Rightarrow$\": if $\\overline{x}$ and $\\underline{y}$ are finite they are elements, so $\\overline{x} < \\underline{y}$; if $\\overline{x} = +\\infty$ or $\\underline{y} = -\\infty$, a large $\\xi$ or a small $\\eta$ violates $\\xi < \\eta$, and the endpoint test is false as well. The second line is the nonempty intersection of two intervals. \"Possibly less\" is the negation of \"certainly not less\", so the two families are dual: `DefinitelyLt::definitely_lt(x, y)` is false exactly when some pair satisfies $\\xi \\ge \\eta$. With empty operands the universal statements would be vacuously true, which would let a caller prove anything from an empty enclosure; the `DefinitelyLt` and `DefinitelyLe` relations (and `Overlaps`, `MaybeEq`) therefore return false for empty operands, whereas the IEEE 1788 relation `precedes` ($\\forall\\xi\\,\\forall\\eta: \\xi \\le \\eta$) is defined to be vacuously true."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:933
msgid "The set relations (`subset`, `interior`, `disjoint`, `==`) and the IEEE 1788 orders (`less`: $\\forall\\xi\\,\\exists\\eta\\, \\xi \\le \\eta$ and $\\forall\\eta\\,\\exists\\xi\\, \\xi \\le \\eta$, which reduces to comparing both endpoint pairs) are evaluated by endpoint comparisons in the same way. None of these relations is a total order, and the trait method `@lf_arith.Contains::contains(x, y)` is set inclusion $\\boldsymbol{y} \\subseteq \\boldsymbol{x}$, matching the enclosure reading of the [`arithmetic`](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/) traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:944
msgid "Endpoints, not midpoint and radius"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:946
msgid "*Problem.* An interval can be stored as endpoints $[\\underline{x}, \\overline{x}]$ (inf–sup) or as a midpoint and radius $\\langle m, r\\rangle$, as in Arb.[^arb]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:949
msgid "*Midpoint–radius arithmetic.* With $x = m_x + \\delta_x$, $|\\delta_x| \\le r_x$ and likewise for $y$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:959
msgid "With a rounded midpoint $m = \\operatorname{RN}_p(m_x \\circ m_y)$, the rounding error $|m - m_x \\circ m_y| \\le 2^{-p}|m|$ is added, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:967
msgid "These are cheap (the radius needs only a few bits), but the product radius overestimates: for $\\boldsymbol{x} = \\boldsymbol{y} = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle = [0, 2]$ it gives $\\langle 1, 3\\rangle = [-2, 4]$ while the exact product is $[0, 4]$. Rump showed that the width can grow by a factor up to $1.5$ per multiplication compared to inf–sup.[^rump]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:973
msgid "*Options.* (a) midpoint–radius storage with low-precision radii; (b) endpoint storage with full-precision endpoints; (c) both."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:976
msgid "*Chosen: (b).* IEEE 1788 is defined on endpoints, half-unbounded and empty sets have no midpoint–radius form, and the hulls in the [endpoint formulas](#endpoint-formulas) are exact before rounding, so inf–sup results are as tight as the precision allows. The cost is that both endpoints carry $p$ bits, so wide intervals at high precision store many useless bits. The midpoint–radius *view* (`from_midpoint_radius`, `midpoint`, `radius`) is kept for users who reason in $c \\pm r$, with the conversions derived [above](#from-midpointradius-to-endpoints)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:985
msgid "J. van der Hoeven, \"Ball arithmetic\", 2009; F. Johansson, \"Arb: efficient arbitrary-precision midpoint-radius interval arithmetic\", *IEEE Trans. Computers* 66(8), 2017."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:989
msgid "S. M. Rump, \"Fast and parallel interval arithmetic\", *BIT* 39(3), 1999."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:992
msgid "Endpoint operations: one directed rounding of the exact result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:994
msgid "*Problem.* Endpoint candidates can be computed with directed rounding at each step, or exactly and rounded once. Rounding at each step would not give the tightest enclosure; forming the exact value of a sum or a product by hand costs alignment and bookkeeping the binary layer already does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:999
msgid "*Chosen.* Every endpoint sum, difference and product of two `Binary` endpoints is one directed contextual operation of `binary` (`add_ctx`, `sub_ctx`, `mul_ctx`, `fma_ctx`) in a context of the precision $p$ of the result and the direction of the endpoint: lower endpoints toward $-\\infty$, upper endpoints toward $+\\infty$, radii and widths upward. Each of them computes as if its exact operands were summed or multiplied exactly and rounds that value once, so by property (iii) the result is the tightest $p$-bit endpoint. The exponent range is the one of the library, so the three issues that an exact route had to handle itself belong to the binary layer:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1009
msgid "*Far addends.* Adding $A = 2^{10^9}$ and $s = 2^{-10^9}$ exactly aligns two coefficients over two billion bits (about 750 MB for one interval addition, issue #24). `add_ctx` does not align them: a term whose leading bit lies beyond the rounding position only decides the side of the rounding, and it is a sticky bit there. Soundness and tightness do not depend on a bound for the distance, as the replacement of the small addend by a surrogate that earlier versions made here did, and no precision label of an operand enters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1016
msgid "*The edge of the exponent range.* A result beyond the range is clamped by the rounding mode of the operation: a lower endpoint to $-\\infty$ or the largest finite number, an upper endpoint to $+\\infty$ or the largest finite number, a tiny result to zero or the smallest positive number. This keeps, for example, $\\exp([10^9, 10^9])$ an enclosure $[\\text{largest finite}, +\\infty)$ instead of collapsing it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1022
msgid "*Gradual underflow.* A result below the normal range lies on the grid of the format, and the directed rounding goes to that grid, not to the grid of the few bits the exact result happens to have."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1026
msgid "The sign of a zero that is the result of an exact cancellation is the one of the direction ($-0$ toward $-\\infty$); it is not part of the interval (see the observers). Division and square root, whose exact results are not dyadic, are computed directly with directed rounding at precision $p$. The midpoint needs the exact half-sum of the two bounds, not a rounded one: it adds the bounds rounded toward zero to $p + 65{,}538$ bits and makes the result odd when that rounding was inexact (round to odd), so that the one rounding that follows, to at most $p$ bits in any direction, is the rounding of the exact value; an exponent gap of less than 65,536 places gives the exact center."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1036
msgid "Contexts and double rounding"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1038
msgid "`IntervalContext` carries a target precision $q$ and exponent range; `apply_ctx` rounds endpoints outward into it, and the operations compute at the operands' precision $p$ and the context is applied to the result. Rounding twice is harmless for directed rounding when $q \\le p$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1047
msgid "because $F_q \\subseteq F_p$ (a $q$-bit significand is a $p$-bit one padded with zeros): every $f \\in F_q$ below $a$ is in $F_p$, hence below $\\operatorname{RD}_p(a)$, and conversely. So `(x + y).apply_ctx(ctx)` equals a single outward rounding of the exact sum into the context whenever the operands are at least as precise as the context. Overflow is resolved in the enclosing direction: an upper endpoint above the range becomes $+\\infty$, a positive lower endpoint becomes the largest finite number (still below the true value). Underflow rounds outward on the subnormal grid, so a tiny positive upper bound becomes the smallest subnormal and never 0. The flags are returned, never stored globally."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1058
msgid "Total and `try_` forms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1060
msgid "*Problem.* Certified evaluation can fail: the refinement budget may run out, or an argument may be too large to reduce. Aborting would break long computations; returning a silent wide interval hides a lost guarantee of tightness."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1065
msgid "*Chosen.* Both, with one kernel. The `try_` forms return an `ArithmeticError` with a certification detail (operation, stage, reason, precision). The total form is the `try_` form with a fallback: when the `try_` form fails it returns the range of the function over its domain, which keeps the inclusion property: $[0, +\\infty)$ for `exp`, `exp2`, `exp10` and `pow`, $[-1, +\\infty)$ for `expm1`, Entire for the logarithms, `sinh`, `asinh`, `atanh`, `tan`, `tanpi` and `log1p`, $[-1, 1]$ for `sin`, `cos`, `sinpi`, `cospi` and `tanh`, $[1, +\\infty)$ for `cosh`, $[0, +\\infty)$ for `acosh`, $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$ for `atan` and `asin`, $[0, \\pi]$ for `acos`, $[-\\pi, \\pi]$ for `atan2`. The $\\pi$ of a fallback is evaluated at no more than 64 bits and rounded outward, so the fallback of `atan`, `asin` and `acos` cannot fail at any precision; the fallback of `atan2` evaluates $\\pi$ at the full precision. The domain is decided before the kernel runs, so a failure is always a certification failure and never a domain error: an argument outside the domain gives Empty, a wide interval means the kernel gave up. Trigonometric reduction is capped: when $\\max(0, k) + p + 96$ exceeds 8,000,000, with $k$ the adjusted exponent of the larger endpoint magnitude, reduction would need more working bits than the limit, so the `try_` forms report a resource limit and the total forms return the fallback."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1081
msgid "*Why one kernel.* Until this was settled the total forms of fifteen functions had a kernel of their own (exact-rational series), which aborted at a high precision (about 32,000 bits for `ln`, issue 449) and lost all relative accuracy for a tiny argument in the hyperbolic functions, while the `try_` forms used the `binary` kernels and had neither limit. Keeping two kernels for the same function meant two sets of bugs, and results that depended on which form the caller chose."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1089
msgid "Decorations in a separate type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1091
msgid "*Problem.* Decorations cost a field and a minimum per operation, and most users do not need them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1094
msgid "*Chosen.* `Interval` is the bare set; `DecoratedInterval` wraps it with a decoration and the NaI state. Bare operations stay cheap and simple, and the decorated type cannot be mixed with bare values by accident."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1098
msgid "Precision as a tag"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1100
msgid "Each interval carries a precision tag; binary operations use the larger tag. This keeps a sequence of operations at the precision of its most precise input without a context argument, which is what a set-valued type needs to compose with operators (`x + y`). An `IntervalContext` is used when a specific format must be imposed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1108
msgid ""
"**Representation invariant.** A non-empty `Interval` has non-NaN endpoints, $\\underline{x} \\le \\overline{x}$, $\\underline{x} \\n"
"e +\\infty$, $\\overline{x} \\n"
"e -\\infty$ and precision $\\ge 1$; every construction path ends in `store_interval`, which checks this and aborts otherwise. Empty is a flag with endpoints $(+\\infty, -\\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1114
msgid "**Inclusion.** Each public operation $F$ of `Interval` satisfies $f(\\boldsymbol{x}) \\subseteq F(\\boldsymbol{x})$, by the endpoint formulas and Corollary 2 for arithmetic, by the critical-point analysis and certified endpoint enclosures for elementary functions, and by the fallbacks for uncertified cases. By the fundamental theorem, so does every composition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1121
msgid "**Tightness.** Basic arithmetic, `sqr`, `pown`, `fma`, `abs`, `min`, `max`, `intersection`, `convex_hull` of non-empty operands and `sqrt` return the outward rounding of the exact hull, so each endpoint is within one ulp of optimal. Trigonometric and arctangent endpoints are correctly directed roundings when certified; the other elementary functions are usually within a few ulps. Not tight: fallbacks; `pown` with $n < -4096$ and 0 inside (Entire);  `with_precision` and `normalized` round the endpoints outward, which is the tightest enclosure and the identity on an interval already representable at the target precision, so `convex_hull` with an Empty operand and the checked capabilities do not widen either."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1133
msgid "**Decorations.** The decoration of a result is a true statement about the function evaluated, by the induction argument above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1136
msgid "**Complexity.** Arithmetic performs at most four endpoint products or two sums, $O(M(p))$ for $p$-bit endpoints with multiplication cost $M(p)$. Elementary functions cost one or two directed evaluations of the `binary` kernels at precision $p$ (see [`binary`](binary.md) for their cost), plus the index range for the trigonometric functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1142
msgid "**Evidence.** Package tests check directed endpoints, the far-addend and exponent-range cases, decorations and relations; the pinned ITF1788 corpus runs 6,632 cases in strict mode (see [conformance](../conformance/interval.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1146
msgid "Known limitations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1148
msgid "No limitation is open at present. Decorated `rootn` with a negative degree lowers the decoration to `trv` when 0 is in the argument, as the decoration rules of IEEE 1788-2015 11.6 require; `library/src/interval/interval_decorated_wbtest.mbt` checks degrees `-1`, `-2` and `-3`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1155
msgid "**Midpoint–radius storage** (Arb-style): cheaper radii, but overestimating products and no representation for half-unbounded sets; see [above](#endpoints-not-midpoint-and-radius)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1158
msgid "**Hardware `Double` endpoints with switched rounding modes:** fixed precision, process-global state, and rounding-mode control is not available on every MoonBit target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1161
msgid "**Round to nearest and inflate by one ulp** (\"epsilon inflation\"): simpler, but about one ulp wider per endpoint than directed rounding, and only valid for operations whose nearest-rounded result is known to be within one ulp, which excludes most elementary function kernels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1165
msgid "**Aborting on uncertified elementary functions:** a single hard argument would stop a whole computation; the `try_` forms cover callers who need the failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1168
msgid "**Treating division by zero-containing intervals as an error:** IEEE 1788 defines these results as sets (Entire, half-unbounded or empty), and extended division is what makes interval Newton methods work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1174
msgid "The package deliberately does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1176
msgid "provide reverse versions of division and reciprocal (Table 10.1 does not require them: change `z = x / y` to `x = y z` and use `mul_rev`);"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1178
msgid "promise tight results: fallbacks and the dependency problem may widen enclosures arbitrarily;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1180
msgid "define a total order on intervals;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1181
msgid "convert decimal data outward on its own: `from_double` and `exact` enclose binary values, and enclosing a decimal literal is the caller's job (see the [tutorial](../tutorial/interval.md#enclose-a-decimal-constant));"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1184
msgid "implement intervals over decimal endpoints, complex intervals, interval vectors or matrices, or Taylor models;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval.md:1186
msgid "report status through global flags: flags come back from `apply_ctx` and `midpoint_ctx` and decorations travel with values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:1
msgid "interval_checked design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:5
msgid "`interval_checked` lets an interval computation be written as one chain of operations in which construction mistakes and uncertifiable steps are explicit errors, while every successful value remains a rigorous enclosure. `IntervalChecked` closes `Result[Interval, ArithmeticError]` under the operations of [`interval`](interval.md) and adds no interval algorithm. The [API page](../api/interval_checked.md) lists the operations and the [tutorial](../tutorial/interval_checked.md) shows pipelines in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:15
#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:428
msgid "Enclosures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:17
msgid "Write $\\mathbb{IR}$ for the set of closed intervals of the real line in the sense of IEEE 1788-2015 (set-based flavor): the empty set, bounded intervals $[\\ell, u]$, half-lines and $\\mathbb{R}$ itself.[^ieee1788] An `Interval` $X$ denotes an element of $\\mathbb{IR}$ whose finite endpoints are binary floats. An interval operation $F$ is an *enclosure* of a real function $f$ when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:28
msgid "and likewise for binary operations. This is the fundamental theorem of interval arithmetic in the form `interval` implements: evaluating a formula with enclosures yields an enclosure of the formula's range, because enclosure is preserved by composition. If $F$ encloses $f$ and $G$ encloses $g$, then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:39
msgid "using monotonicity of images under $\\subseteq$ for the first step and the enclosure property of $G$ for the second.[^moore]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:42
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015, *Standard for Interval Arithmetic*, clauses 7–10."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:43
msgid "R. E. Moore, R. B. Kearfott, M. J. Cloud, *Introduction to Interval Analysis*, SIAM, 2009, chapter 5; van der Hoeven, \"Ball arithmetic\", 2009."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:46
msgid "The wrapper as an error monad"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:48
msgid "`IntervalChecked` is the exception monad $M V = V + E$ with $V$ the set of `Interval` values and $E$ the set of `ArithmeticError` values, with $\\eta = $ `ok` and $\\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} = $ `bind`. The laws (left and right identity, associativity) and the functor laws of `map` are proved in the [`binary_checked` design](binary_checked.md#the-monad-laws); the proof is a case analysis on $\\mathrm{Ok}/\\mathrm{Err}$ that does not use any property of $V$. The operators are lifted by the same left-biased scheme (`interval_result_lift2`), so the [first-error theorem](binary_checked.md#the-first-error-theorem) applies unchanged: an expression reports the error of the first originating node in post-order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:62
msgid "What is an error and what is a value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:64
msgid "**Problem.** An interval computation can end in four kinds of situation: the input does not describe an interval; the operation has no meaning for an argument (degree-zero root); the library cannot certify a tight enough enclosure; or the true range is empty or unbounded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:69
msgid "**Choice.** The first three are errors; the fourth is a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:71
msgid "Situation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:71
msgid "Outcome"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:73
msgid "NaN bound, $\\ell = +\\infty$, $u = -\\infty$, $\\ell > u$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:74
msgid "non-finite `exact`, `from_double`, `from_float` source"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:75
msgid "`rootn` of degree $0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:76
msgid "enclosure not certifiable within budget"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:77
msgid "range empty ($\\ln$ of negatives, $1/\\{0\\}$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:77
msgid "success: empty interval"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:77
#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:78
msgid "interval operation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:78
msgid "range unbounded ($1/[-1,1]$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:78
msgid "success: entire or half-line"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:80
msgid "**Why.** An empty or unbounded result is the *correct* enclosure of the true range, so turning it into an error would make the wrapper disagree with the set-based semantics: $\\ln([-1, 1]) = [-\\infty, 0]$ is a sound answer, and $\\ln(\\{-2\\}) = \\varnothing$ is the exact answer. By contrast, $[+\\infty, +\\infty]$ is not a set of reals at all, and a degree-zero root is undefined for every argument; these are mistakes of the caller. A certification failure means that the library refuses to return a result it cannot prove, which is also not an enclosure. Callers who consider an empty or unbounded result a failure in their application add that rule with `bind` (see the tutorial)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:90
msgid "Arithmetic operators cannot fail"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:92
msgid "`add`, `sub`, `mul` and `div` lift the `Interval` operators with `interval_result_lift2`, so they never introduce an error: the four set operations always have an enclosure in $\\mathbb{IR}$ (for division, $X / Y$ is defined as the hull of $\\{ s/t : s \\in X, t \\in Y \\setminus \\{0\\} \\}$, empty when $Y = \\{0\\}$). The only errors in an arithmetic expression are those of its leaves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:98
msgid "Powers go through the arithmetic traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:100
msgid "`pown` calls `PowIntChecked::pow_int_checked` of Luna-Flow/arithmetic with `ArithmeticContext::new(x.precision())`. `Interval` reads only the precision from the context (rounding direction and exponent bounds have no meaning for an outward-rounded enclosure) and re-rounds the base and the result with `with_precision`, which is sound and adds nothing when they are already representable. The two traits `Interval` implements, `PowIntChecked` and `PowNatChecked`, are implemented differently, and only one of them avoids the *dependency problem* (`IntervalChecked` offers only `pown`; `PowNatChecked` is reached through `Interval::pow_nat_checked`). $X \\cdot X$ treats the two factors as independent, so for $X = [-1, 1]$ it gives $[-1, 1]$, whereas $\\{t^2 : t \\in X\\} = [0, 1]$. `pown` uses `Interval::pown`, which evaluates $t \\mapsto t^n$ on the monotone pieces of one variable $t$ and returns $[0, 1]$. `pow_nat_checked` uses binary powering, $X^{n} = X^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor} \\cdot X^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor} \\cdot X^{n \\bmod 2}$ evaluated with interval products, so it encloses the larger set $\\{t_1 t_2 \\cdots t_n : t_i \\in X\\}$ and returns $[-1, 1]$; both are enclosures of $\\{t^n\\}$, but only `pown` is tight. `pow_nat_checked(0)` of a non-empty interval is $\\{1\\}$, and of an empty interval is empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:119
msgid "No decorations and no flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:121
msgid "IEEE 1788 decorations (`com`, `dac`, `def`, `trv`, `ill`) record whether a function was defined and continuous on the input; `interval` provides them in its decorated type `DecoratedInterval`. The wrapper does not carry them, because a decoration is information about a successful evaluation, not a failure, and combining it with the error channel would make every `map` responsible for decoration propagation. The same holds for `IntervalFlags`. Applications that need either use `interval` directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:129
msgid "Construction precision"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:131
msgid "The constructors use the defaults of `interval` (53 bits for integers and for `Double`, 24 for `Float`, the source precision for `exact`, the larger bound precision for `from_bounds`). All constructors round outward, so the source value is always enclosed: `from_int` and `from_coefficient` convert the integer exactly and then round the singleton outward, which gives a two-point interval when the integer needs more bits than the precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:140
msgid "**Soundness.** If every leaf of an expression is a success enclosing the intended real input, and the expression evaluates to $\\mathrm{Ok}(Y)$, then $Y$ encloses the range of the real formula over the inputs. This is the composition argument above together with the fact that the wrapper applies exactly the `interval` operations on successes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:145
msgid "**Error determinism.** If the expression evaluates to $\\mathrm{Err}(e)$, then $e$ is the error of the first originating node in post-order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:147
msgid "**No hidden recovery.** No method turns an error into a value, and `map` never turns a value into an error ($\\texttt{map} = \\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} \\circ\\, (\\eta \\circ -)$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:150
msgid "**Cost.** Each wrapper step adds $O(1)$ work to the interval operation it delegates to."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:155
msgid "**Empty results as errors.** Breaks set-based semantics and would report an error for $\\ln([-1, 1])$ where a sound enclosure exists."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:157
msgid "**Carrying decorations in the wrapper.** Would duplicate the decorated API of `interval` with a second propagation rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:159
msgid "**Context variants (`exp_ctx`, …).** Enclosures are outward-rounded at the interval's precision; changing precision is done explicitly with `with_precision`, which always rounds outward (but may widen by one ulp per side)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:163
msgid "**Accumulating errors.** As for the binary wrapper, there is no combining operation on `ArithmeticError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:168
msgid "No interval algorithm of its own; every enclosure comes from `interval`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:169
msgid "No decorations, `IntervalFlags`, interval contexts or interchange formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:170
msgid "No error recovery or accumulation; only the first error is kept."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:171
msgid "No `Eq`, `Show`, or enclosure relations on the wrapper itself; extract the `Interval` with `result()` to test containment or ordering."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/interval_checked.md:173
msgid "Out-of-domain parts of an argument are ignored, not reported."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:1
msgid "numeric_expr design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:5
msgid "The conformance frontends of `floating` read four very different corpus formats (GDA `.decTest`, IEEE 1788 `.itl`, MPFR data files, Berkeley TestFloat vectors) and run them against four number types. `numeric_expr` offers one typed intermediate form for \"apply this operation to these operands\" and one evaluator, so that parsing, number semantics and error reporting are separated. On the current branch only `frontend/gda_expr` lowers its rows to it; the ITL, MPFR and TestFloat frontends execute their parsed rows directly. Where it is used, the roles split as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:14
msgid "a frontend turns text into an `Expr` and keeps source positions;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:15
msgid "a backend, given as two callbacks, says what literals and operations mean;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:16
msgid "`evaluate` combines them and reports which node failed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:18
msgid "The package itself contains no arithmetic, no parsing of numbers, no IO and no global state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:25
msgid "Let $L$ be the set of literals (raw text with a span) and $O$ the set of operations (a name with a span). The public constructors generate exactly the terms of the grammar"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:34
msgid "Internally a term is stored as a `@tt_syntax.Term[Atom]` of the `Luna-Flow/type_theory` package, where `Atom` is a private enum with a literal case and a primitive-operation case. `Expr::literal(ℓ)` is `Value(LiteralAtom(ℓ))` and `Expr::invoke(o, args)` is `Apply(Value(PrimitiveAtom(o)), args)`. The general `Term` type also has `Variable` and `Bind` forms and allows any term in function position; the public constructors never create those."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:42
msgid "Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:44
msgid "Fix a value set $V$, an error set $E$ and the two callbacks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:50
msgid "Write $\\mathrm{Err}$ for the error set $L \\times E \\;+\\; O \\times E \\;+\\; \\mathrm{Span}$ of `EvalError[E]`. The meaning $[\\![e]\\!] \\in V + \\mathrm{Err}$ is defined by structural recursion:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:72
msgid "This is the fold (catamorphism) of the syntax tree in the error monad $V \\mapsto V + \\mathrm{Err}$, with the arguments sequenced from left to right. The evaluator is a direct transcription: `evaluate_term` matches the three shapes, evaluates the arguments in a `for` loop, and returns at the first `Err`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:80
msgid "Callbacks instead of a number trait"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:82
msgid "**Problem.** The same row format is executed against several number types, and each frontend needs its own value type: `gda_expr` evaluates to an enum of decimals, integers, booleans and strings, each with thirteen GDA status flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:87
msgid "**Options.** A trait such as \"`V` can parse literals and apply operations\"; a fixed value enum shared by all frontends; or two plain function arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:90
msgid "**Choice.** Two function arguments. MoonBit traits have only the `Self` parameter, so a trait could not take the operation table as data, and one value type could not have two interpretations (for example, the same `Decimal` read under an IEEE context and under a GDA context). Functions also capture per-row state: `gda_expr` closes over the row's `GdaContext`, so the same literal text is read with the precision and rounding of the directive block it appears in."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:98
msgid "Opaque `Expr` over `type_theory` syntax"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:100
msgid "**Problem.** Callers should be able to build and evaluate expressions without depending on how trees are stored, and the package should be able to grow variables and binders later."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:104
msgid "**Choice.** `Expr` wraps a private `@tt_syntax.Term[Atom]`. `type_theory` owns binding and substitution for the organisation's syntax trees, so variables and `Bind` can be added later by reusing it, without a second tree type. Because the field is private, adding constructors later does not break callers that only use `Expr::literal`, `Expr::invoke` and `evaluate`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:110
msgid "Left-to-right, fail-fast evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:112
msgid "**Problem.** When several operands are invalid, which error is reported, and does work continue?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:115
msgid "**Choice.** Arguments are evaluated from left to right and the first error is returned without evaluating the rest. The reported error is therefore deterministic (the leftmost failing leaf or node in post-order), and no callback runs on values that will be discarded. A frontend that wants all diagnostics of a document collects them while parsing, before evaluation, as the frontends in this repository do."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:122
msgid "Errors keep the syntax node"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:124
msgid "`LiteralFailure` and `OperationFailure` carry the `Literal` or `Operation` itself rather than only a message. The node has the raw text or name and the span, which is what a corpus runner prints. The callback's error `E` is kept unchanged, so a typed error survives evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:131
msgid "**Proposition 1 (closed shape).** Every `Expr` built with the public API is either `Value(LiteralAtom(ℓ))` or `Apply(Value(PrimitiveAtom(o)), args)` with every element of `args` of the same two shapes. Consequently `evaluate` never returns `UnsupportedExpression` for such trees."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:136
msgid "*Proof.* By induction on construction. `Expr::literal` produces the first shape. `Expr::invoke(o, args)` produces the second shape, and its arguments are the terms of `Expr` values, which have the stated shapes by the induction hypothesis. `evaluate_term` returns `UnsupportedExpression` only for `Value(PrimitiveAtom(_))` at the root of a subterm, `Variable`, `Bind`, or an `Apply` whose head is not `Value(PrimitiveAtom(_))`; none of these occurs. $\\square$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:144
msgid "**Proposition 2 (post-order prefix).** Let $u_1, u_2, \\dots, u_N$ be the nodes of a tree in post-order (children left to right, then the parent). The sequence of callback calls made by `evaluate` is $c(u_1), c(u_2), \\dots, c(u_m)$, where $c$ is `decode` for a literal and `invoke` for an operation node, and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:157
msgid "*Proof sketch.* By induction on the tree. A leaf makes exactly one call. For $\\mathsf{op}(o)(e_1, \\dots, e_n)$ the post-order is the concatenation of the post-orders of $e_1, \\dots, e_n$ followed by the node itself. The loop evaluates $e_1, \\dots, e_n$ in order; by the hypothesis each makes the calls of its own post-order, stopping at its first failing call, and the loop returns as soon as one child fails. If all children succeed, `invoke` is called once for the node. Concatenating gives the claim. $\\square$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:165
msgid "Corollaries: on success `decode` runs once per literal and `invoke` once per invocation node; no callback runs twice for the same node; the error returned belongs to node $u_m$ and is wrapped exactly once (child errors are returned unchanged, not re-wrapped by their ancestors)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:170
msgid "**Proposition 3 (purity and determinism).** `evaluate` reads only the tree and calls only the two callbacks. If the callbacks are deterministic functions, so is `evaluate`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:174
msgid "**Complexity.** For a tree with $N$ nodes and height $h$, evaluation makes at most $N$ callback calls, allocates one argument array per invocation node (total size $N - 1$ at most), and uses recursion depth $h$. Construction with `Expr::invoke` copies the argument array, $O(n)$ for $n$ arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:181
msgid "**A public enum for `Expr`.** Callers could pattern-match the tree, but every new syntax form (variables, binders) would break them. The opaque struct keeps that freedom."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:184
msgid "**Collecting all errors during evaluation.** An applicative evaluator that continues after an error would have to invent values for failed operands or skip operations, which makes the meaning of later errors unclear. Frontends collect parse diagnostics before evaluation instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:188
msgid "**Arity in `Operation`.** A declared arity would only duplicate the check the backend has to do anyway (it must also check operand kinds), and would not work for variadic operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:191
msgid "**An explicit stack instead of recursion.** Corpus rows are shallow (one operation over literals), so the simpler recursive fold is kept."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:196
msgid "No tokenizer, parser or pretty-printer: frontends turn text into `Expr`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:197
msgid "No number type, rounding, precision, flags or contexts: callbacks own all numeric semantics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:199
msgid "No arity or type checking of operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:200
msgid "No variables, binders, sharing or user-defined functions are exposed yet, although the internal representation can hold them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:202
msgid "No IO, logging or global state; source spans are plain data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/numeric_expr.md:203
msgid "No equality or printing of whole expressions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:1
msgid "semantic design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:5
msgid "`semantic` answers one question independently of representation: *which number does this datum denote?* A binary float, an IEEE decimal and an interval endpoint are mapped to exact rationals, signed infinities, NaN and closed intervals, so that values computed by different packages, at different precisions and in different radices, can be compared exactly. Checked errors are mapped to a small common vocabulary for the same purpose. The package is a boundary for tests, diagnostics and protocols; it performs no arithmetic. The [API page](../api/semantic.md) lists the items and the [tutorial](../tutorial/semantic.md) shows them in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:17
msgid "Floating-point numbers are rationals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:19
msgid "A finite binary float with sign bit $s$, integer coefficient $c \\ge 0$ and exponent $e$ denotes $(-1)^{s} c\\, 2^{e}$, and a finite decimal denotes $(-1)^{s} c\\, 10^{q}$. Writing $m = (-1)^{s} c$, both have the shape $m\\, r^{k}$ with an integer $m$, a radix $r \\ge 2$ and an integer $k$, which is a rational number:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:33
msgid "The finite binary values are therefore exactly the *dyadic rationals* $\\mathbb{Z}[1/2] = \\{\\, a/2^{j} : a \\in \\mathbb{Z}, j \\in \\mathbb{N} \\,\\}$ and the finite decimal values are exactly $\\mathbb{Z}[1/10] = \\{\\, a/10^{j} \\,\\}$, both subrings of $\\mathbb{Q}$. This is the map `ExactRational::from_scaled_integer` computes, with $r^{|k|}$ evaluated exactly in `BigInt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:39
msgid "The reduced form is canonical"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:41
msgid "Every rational $x$ has exactly one representation $n/d$ with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:47
msgid ""
"*Existence.* From any $n'/d'$ with $d' \\n"
"e 0$, multiply numerator and denominator by $\\operatorname{sgn}(d')$ and divide both by $g = \\gcd(|n'|, |d'|)$. *Uniqueness.* Suppose $n/d = n'/d'$ with both pairs reduced and $d, d' > 0$. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:61
msgid "`ExactRational::new` establishes exactly this form (sign moved to the numerator, Euclid's algorithm for the gcd, zero normalized to $0/1$), and the fields are private, so every `ExactRational` is reduced. Consequently the derived structural equality on the two `BigInt` fields *is* equality of rational numbers: $n/d = n'/d' \\iff (n, d) = (n', d')$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:67
msgid "Comparing values of different radices"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:69
msgid "Equality of two projected values is decided by the canonical form. Ordering is not provided by the package, but the representation makes it a two-multiplication test: for reduced $a/b$ and $c/d$ with $b, d > 0$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:81
msgid "which is what the [tutorial](../tutorial/semantic.md#order-two-values-exactly) implements over `numerator()` and `denominator()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:84
msgid "The canonical denominators also explain which decimals have a binary representation. A reduced binary value has denominator $2^{j}$; a reduced decimal value $a/10^{j}$ reduces to a denominator $2^{u} 5^{v}$. By uniqueness of the reduced form, a decimal value equals some finite binary float (of sufficient precision, and inside the `Binary` exponent range of about $2^{\\pm 2^{30}}$) if and only if its reduced denominator has no factor $5$. One tenth reduces to $1/(2 \\cdot 5)$, so no binary float equals it, and the projection of binary64 `0.1` is $3602879701896397/2^{55}$, a different number.[^goldberg]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:93
msgid "Goldberg, \"What every computer scientist should know about floating-point arithmetic\", *ACM Computing Surveys* 23(1), 1991, section on base conversion; Knuth, *TAOCP* vol. 2, section 4.4."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:119
msgid "Intervals as pairs of extended rationals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:121
msgid "A non-empty `Interval` denotes $X = \\{\\, t \\in \\mathbb{R} : \\ell \\le t \\le u \\,\\}$ for endpoints $\\ell \\le u$ in $\\mathbb{Z}[1/2] \\cup \\{-\\infty, +\\infty\\}$, with an infinite endpoint meaning an unbounded side, as in IEEE 1788-2015 set-based intervals.[^ieee1788] `SemanticInterval::from_interval` projects $\\ell$ and $u$ independently with `from_binary`, so the pair determines $X$ exactly. The empty interval is stored by `interval` with $\\ell = +\\infty$ and $u = -\\infty$, and the projection keeps that reversed pair; in the extended order, *lower > upper* holds exactly for the empty set. A membership test $t \\in X$ for a rational $t$ is then two comparisons of the kind derived above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:132
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015, *Standard for Interval Arithmetic*, clause 7 (set-based flavor): intervals are closed connected subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$, possibly unbounded or empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:138
msgid "Exact rationals, not a common floating format"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:140
msgid "**Problem.** Comparing a binary and a decimal value needs a common domain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:142
msgid "**Options.** (a) Convert both to `Double` or to a wide binary float. (b) Convert both to a decimal string. (c) Project both to $\\mathbb{Q}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:145
msgid "**Choice: (c).** Conversion to a float rounds, so two different values may compare equal after conversion (binary64 `0.1` and decimal `0.1` both round to the same `Double`). A string comparison depends on formatting and cohort. $\\mathbb{Z}[1/2]$ and $\\mathbb{Z}[1/10]$ both embed into $\\mathbb{Q}$ without loss, and the reduced form makes equality a field comparison. The price is size: $r^{|k|}$ has about $|k| \\log_2 r$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:152
msgid "What the projection forgets"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:154
msgid "The projection keeps only the denoted value. It drops precision, decimal quantum (cohort), the sign of zero, NaN payload, sign and signalling state, and all flags and context state. Interval decorations never reach it: `from_interval` accepts only an undecorated `Interval`. These are properties of representations and of computations, and the concrete packages expose them. Keeping any of them would make two equal numbers from different packages compare unequal, which defeats the purpose of the package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:162
msgid "NaN as a single value with structural equality"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:164
msgid "`SemanticScalar` derives `Eq`, so `NaN == NaN`. The type is a model in which \"this computation produced no number\" is one outcome among others, and tests need to assert that two packages both produce it. IEEE comparison, where NaN is unordered with itself, stays in the concrete packages and in [`PartialOrder`](def.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:170
msgid "A separate error vocabulary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:172
msgid "`ArithmeticError` carries a message and, for certification failures, a detail record with precisions and refinement counts that differ between packages for the same mathematical failure. `SemanticError` keeps only the kind, so `semantic_scalar_result` makes the outcome comparable across packages. The mapping tests the kind predicates in a fixed order and falls back to `UnsupportedOperation`; since every `ArithmeticErrorKind` constructor has its own predicate, each kind maps to the `SemanticError` of the same name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:180
msgid "Projections as plain functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:182
msgid "`semantic_scalar_result` takes the projection as an argument instead of dispatching on a trait. With $\\mathrm{Res}(T) = T + E$ and $\\mathrm{Sem}(S) = S + E'$, the function is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:190
msgid "the coproduct of the two maps, applying $f$ on the left summand and the error map on the right. It satisfies the functor law $\\texttt{semantic\\_scalar\\_result}(r.\\texttt{map}(g), f) = \\texttt{semantic\\_scalar\\_result}(r, f \\circ g)$, which lets callers reuse one projection for every pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:198
msgid "**Reduced form.** Every `ExactRational` satisfies $d > 0$, $\\gcd(|n|, d) = 1$ and $n = 0 \\Rightarrow d = 1$; `new` aborts on $d = 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:200
msgid "**Exactness.** For finite $x$, $\\texttt{from\\_binary}(x) = \\texttt{Rational}([\\![x]\\!])$ and $\\texttt{from\\_decimal}(x) = \\texttt{Rational}([\\![x]\\!])$; nothing is rounded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:204
msgid "**Soundness and completeness of equality.** For finite $x$, $y$ of any of the two supported scalar types, the projections are equal if and only if $[\\![x]\\!] = [\\![y]\\!]$; this is the uniqueness of the reduced form."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:207
msgid "**Class preservation.** Infinities project to `Infinity` with the same sign, every NaN projects to `NaN`, finite values to `Rational`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:209
msgid "**Intervals.** `from_interval` is the pair of endpoint projections; the entire line gives $(-\\infty, +\\infty)$, the empty interval $(+\\infty, -\\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:212
msgid "**Cost.** `from_scaled_integer` performs one exact power and, for negative exponents, one gcd; both are polynomial in the bit length $O(|k| \\log r + \\log |m|)$ of the operands."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:218
msgid "**Implementing arithmetic on `ExactRational`.** Exact rational arithmetic is a different library; here it would invite using the projection as a number type, which it is not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:221
msgid "**An `Ord`-style instance on `SemanticScalar`.** NaN and the reversed empty interval have no place in a total order; callers order rationals explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:223
msgid "**Keeping the signed zero.** It would make `-0.0` and decimal `0` unequal, although they denote the same number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:225
msgid "**A projection for `@decimal_gda.Decimal`.** Not provided on the current branch; GDA values reach this package through their string form or through `@decimal.Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:231
msgid "No arithmetic, rounding, parsing, formatting, interchange encoding or interval tightening."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:233
msgid "No ordering of semantic values; equality only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:234
msgid "Representation details (precision, quantum, signed zero, NaN payload and signalling, flags, context) are deliberately not preserved, and decorated intervals are not accepted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:237
msgid "Only `Binary`, `@decimal.Decimal` and `Interval` have projections."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/semantic.md:238
msgid "Projections of values with very large exponents are exact and therefore large; the package does not guard against that cost. A `Binary` exponent near its implementation limit $\\pm(2^{30} - 1)$ gives a `BigInt` of about $2^{30}$ bits, and a decimal exponent near `decimal_value_e_max` ($2.1 \\cdot 10^{9}$) one of about $7 \\cdot 10^{9}$ bits."
msgstr ""

