msgid "You need the MoonBit toolchain 0.10.20 or later (`moonc` ≥ 0.10.20). Add the module, and `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` if you name its rounding modes or contexts yourself:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:43
msgid "[!NOTE] The manual describes the current branch. Operations listed under \"Unreleased\" in the changelog (for example `Binary::fma_ctx`, `remainder_ctx`, `next_up_ctx` and decimal string formatting) are not in a published release yet."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:49
msgid "Import only the packages you use in your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:60
msgid "Imports name packages, not files: each directory with a `moon.pkg` is one package, and its files share one namespace. By convention the manual imports `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` as `@lf_arith` and `Luna-Flow/luna-generic` as `@lf_alg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:65
msgid "Build values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:67
msgid "A `Binary` is an exact dyadic number $c \\cdot 2^e$ with a precision; a `Decimal` is $c \\cdot 10^q$ and remembers the exponent $q$ of its literal; an `Interval` is an interval with binary endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:95
msgid "`Binary` prints in the exact form `<coefficient>p<exponent>`; use `to_shortest_string` or `to_decimal_string_ctx` for decimal text. Call `normalized()` on a decimal only when you want to drop its cohort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:99
msgid "Other constructors: `Binary::from_int`, `from_double`, `from_string` and `from_string_ctx` (correctly rounded decimal parsing), `from_hex`; `Decimal::from_int`, `from_string` and `from_string_ctx`; `Interval::from_int`, `from_double`, `exact` and `from_bounds`. Constructors take an optional `precision`; `Interval::from_int` requires it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:105
msgid "Choose a context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:107
msgid "The plain operators (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `add`, `mul`, …) work at the operands' precision with round-to-nearest-even and an effectively unbounded exponent range, and they discard status. When precision, exponent range, rounding direction, tininess or the status flags are part of your result, use the `*_ctx` form with an explicit context:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:140
msgid "Contexts are immutable values; nothing in the library reads a global rounding mode. IEEE contexts return the flags of one operation, which you `combine` yourself. A GDA context instead travels with the result: every `GdaOutcome` returns the next context, whose flags accumulate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:145
msgid "Choose a failure model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:147
msgid "The library exposes several failure channels on purpose. Pick the one that matches what your caller must observe:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:150
msgid "`Option` from simple constructors such as `BinaryInterchange::from_hex`, when bad input needs no diagnostic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:152
msgid "`Result[T, ArithmeticError]` from checked operations (`div_checked`, `sqrt_checked`, `compare_checked`, `from_string`, the `try_*_ctx` elementary functions)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:155
msgid "`BinaryFlags`, `DecimalFlags` and `IntervalFlags` beside a defined result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:156
msgid "`GdaOutcome[T]`, which keeps the GDA-defined result even when a trap fires."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:157
msgid "The pipeline wrappers: `BinaryChecked` and `IntervalChecked` stop at the first error; `DecimalChecked` keeps defined NaN and infinity results and accumulates flags; `GdaDecimalChecked` stops at a trap and keeps its outcome."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:161
msgid "Empty, Entire and NaI, which are interval values, not errors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:180
msgid "Do not fold these channels into one exception type or one `Result`: that erases semantics the standards make observable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:183
msgid "Read results correctly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:185
msgid "Signed zero, infinities, quiet and signaling NaN, and NaN payloads are observable on scalars."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:187
msgid "`compare`, `<` and sorting use a total preorder in which every NaN is equal to every other NaN and above every number, and $-0 = +0$. For IEEE semantics use `compare_checked`, the quiet and signaling predicates of `binary`, or the `total_order*` functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:191
msgid "`==` agrees with `compare` in the number types: on `Binary` it ignores the sign of a zero and the precision (`-0 == +0`) and treats two NaNs as equal, on `Decimal` it compares values, and on `Interval` it compares the bounds by value (two empty intervals are equal). `a.identical(b)` compares every field of a `Binary`. See [numeric semantics](./numeric_semantics.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:196
msgid "An `Interval` has containment and set relations (`contains`, `subset`, `strict_less`, …), not a scalar order. A result is correct when it encloses the exact result; tightness is a separate quality."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:199
msgid "`SemanticScalar` compares mathematical values across packages and drops precision, exponent, signed zero, payloads, decorations and flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:202
msgid "Continue reading"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:204
msgid "[Numeric semantics](./numeric_semantics.md) defines rounding, ulp, flags, exponent, signed zero, NaN and enclosures with their derivations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:206
msgid "[Architecture](./architecture.md) explains the package layers and the certified elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:208
msgid "[Verification](./verification.md) lists the gates and the exact scope of each conformance claim."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:210
msgid "Each package has a tutorial, an API reference and a design page; start with the [`binary` tutorial](./tutorial/binary.md), the [`decimal` tutorial](./tutorial/decimal.md) or the [`interval` tutorial](./tutorial/interval.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:1
msgid "floating"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:3
msgid "This manual documents the `main` branch of `Luna-Flow/floating`, which builds on the latest published stable release, `0.11.0`; the changes since that release are listed in the changelog."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:7
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:9
msgid "`floating` is arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic for MoonBit: binary values with IEEE 754 semantics at any precision, IEEE 754 and General Decimal Arithmetic decimals, and certified interval arithmetic following IEEE 1788. Precision, rounding, exponent range, special values, status flags, traps and enclosures are explicit values in every API rather than hidden global state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:16
msgid "The library is built around three contracts:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:18
msgid "A rounded operation computes the exact real result and rounds it once, so the error of one step is at most one unit in the last place, and at most half of one under round-to-nearest."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:21
msgid "A decimal keeps its quantum: `1.20` and `1.2` are equal numbers with different exponents, and every operation states which exponent it prefers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:23
msgid "An interval operation returns a set that contains every exact result of its operands, so a chain of interval operations encloses the exact value of the whole expression."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:27
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:33
msgid "Then import the packages you need in your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:41
msgid "The module needs the MoonBit toolchain 0.10.20 or later (`moonc` ≥ 0.10.20). It depends on `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` (rounding modes, contexts, checked and contextual traits) and `Luna-Flow/luna-generic` (algebraic traits); add `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` yourself when you name its types. [Getting started](getting_started.md) walks through the first program."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:47
msgid "Modules"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:49
msgid "The repository is a workspace of two modules. `Luna-Flow/floating` (directory `library/`) is the library: the numerical cores, the checked wrappers, `def`, and the `internal` helpers. `Luna-Flow/floating-tools` (directory `tooling/`, versioned with the library) holds the developer tools: expressions and corpus frontends, the conformance command line, the differential harness, the benchmarks and the documentation examples. The sections below say which module a package belongs to; their package names are import paths below the module name, so `binary` is `Luna-Flow/floating/binary` and `frontend/gda_expr` is `Luna-Flow/floating-tools/frontend/gda_expr`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:60
msgid "Pages"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:62
msgid "Each documented package has a tutorial (how to use it), an API reference (what you can call) and a design page (why it works this way). The four numerical cores also have a conformance page (which standard behaviour is verified, and how) and a performance page (measurements and their method)."
msgstr ""

