#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:58
msgid "What is timed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:60
msgid "Each case is one operation at one precision with eight different operand sets read from the same file by both sides (`inputs.txt`, written by one seeded generator; checksums are in `machine.json`). One timed iteration is one pass over the eight sets, so a time per operation is the batch time divided by the iterations and by eight."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:66
msgid "**Binary.** Operands and results have the case precision in bits. MPFR: `mpfr_add`, `mpfr_mul`, `mpfr_div`, `mpfr_sqrt`, `mpfr_exp`, `mpfr_log`, `mpfr_sin` with `MPFR_RNDN`. `floating`: the `Binary` methods `add`, `mul`, `div`, `sqrt`, `exp`, `ln`, `sin` on operands of that precision (round to nearest even, unbounded exponent range)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:71
msgid "**`decimal_gda`.** Operands and results have the case digit count, rounding half even, exponent range ±999999. libmpdec: `mpd_qadd`, `mpd_qmul`, `mpd_qdiv`, `mpd_qsqrt`, `mpd_qexp`, `mpd_qln` with `allcr = 1`. `floating`: `decimal_gda` `add`, `multiply`, `divide`, `sqrt`, `exp`, `ln` in a context of that precision. libmpdec has no `sin`, so `decimal_gda` has six operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:76
msgid "**Included in the time.** The library call itself, with whatever temporary storage it allocates internally. For `floating` this includes constructing the immutable result value. The loop around the call, and for `floating` Maremark's per-iteration bookkeeping, are in the raw time and are removed in the net time by the null of the case (see the [design page](../../design/bench/external.md#the-null-of-a-case-the-same-loop-without-the-library-call) for what the null contains and what it cannot remove)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:83
msgid "**Excluded.** Parsing operands, creating contexts, `mpfr_t` and `mpd_t` initialisation and result buffers (MPFR and libmpdec write into results allocated before timing), reading files, and printing. Process start-up and the first warm-up passes are outside the measured blocks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:87
msgid "**Correctness.** Before it is timed, every `floating` output is compared with the MPFR or libmpdec result for the same operand, as canonical text: the reference text (`%Ra` of MPFR, `mpd_to_sci` of libmpdec) and the text the library prints are each reduced by one small parser of the benchmark package (binary: sign, odd integer significand and exponent, or `0`, `-0`, `inf`, `nan`; decimal: sign, coefficient with its trailing zeros, exponent), so the sign of zero, the decimal cohort, infinities and NaN are compared and no reader of the library stands between the reference and the verdict. A case checks its 8 distinct operand sets; the rounds and blocks repeat the same check, so the published run (59 cases, 7 rounds) makes 8 checks per case, not one per round (an earlier version of this page counted 427 comparisons that way). A run without a reference file (`quick`) is not validated and says so (`EXTBENCH_NOT_VALIDATED`, never `EXTBENCH_FAILED=0`). Both libraries round every result correctly (MPFR always; libmpdec with `allcr = 1`; `floating` by certified refinement), so at equal precision the results are identical and the ratio compares equal work. The reference side is another implementation (level A); the benchmark specs of the other `bench` packages compare the library with itself or with its own sibling and are timing harness checks, not validation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:106
msgid "**Not trivially cached.** The operands are 8 distinct random sets per case, every result is overwritten on the next pass, and neither library keeps a result cache. Both keep internal caches of constants such as $\\pi$ and $\\ln 2$ per precision; these are warm during timing on both sides (the warm-up passes fill them), so the numbers are steady-state rates, not cold-start costs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:115
msgid "Both sides run the Maremark protocol with the same numbers: 3 warm-up passes and at least 5 ms of warm-up, batches calibrated to about 20 ms (at least one pass of eight operations), 3 exploratory pairs that are discarded and 12 confirmatory pairs. A pair is a block of the case and a block of its null, each with its own calibrated batch, back to back, the order alternating. The two sides alternate over 7 rounds in seeded AB/BA order, each round with its own shuffled case order, so every cell has 84 confirmatory pairs per side from 7 separate processes. The rules of the verdict (84 pairs, the width bound of 1.25 on the ratio interval, the 5% drift and order thresholds, the target of 3) are fixed in the method and written into the `protocol` block of every summary. Before each side starts, the runner waits until other processes use under 150% CPU. The slowest cells (elementary functions at 8192 bits and 1000 digits) take seconds per pass, so their batch is a single pass."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:129
msgid "Configurations and results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bench/external.md:131
msgid "The floating side is measured in two configurations of the native build, each published as its own summary and shown in its own section: `native`, with the native limb primitives where the build has them, and `native-portable`, with the portable path forced. The target has to be reached by the portable path too. It is also measured on two other runtimes, `wasm` and `js`, which are judged on the same net ratio to the native reference with two bounds: a case meets the target when the upper end of the interval is at most 5, is tolerated up to 10 and is not met above 10 (the native configurations have the one bound 3). The ratio of a runtime to `native-portable` is a diagnostic and has no verdict. Each runtime section counts its cases met, tolerated and not met. The configuration is chosen when the run starts (`--target`, `--configuration NAME`, `--moon-env KEY=VALUE`) and recorded in the summary. The tables of a configuration are generated by `just bench external publish` between the marker comments below and copied without editing."
msgstr ""

