msgid "Balanced multiplication uses schoolbook below 32 64-bit limbs (2,048 bits on LLVM, Wasm and Wasm-GC and on Native with the portable and C rows; 48 limbs on Native with the assembly rows), then Karatsuba on limb arrays (a few small temporaries per step, no intermediate `BinCoeff`), Toom-3, and a two-prime Montgomery NTT with CRT when transform bounds fit. Unbalanced inputs use block multiplication or overlap-add. Native and LLVM select Toom-3 and NTT multiplication at 4,096 64-bit limbs and NTT square at 3,584; Wasm and Wasm-GC at 2,048 and 1,536 (the values of the version before the 64-bit limbs, 4,096 and 3,072 in 32-bit limbs, halved; not measured again on those targets). A 64-bit limb product costs four `i64.mul` on Wasm, so from 160 limbs (10,240 bits) Wasm and Wasm-GC split the operands into 32-bit limbs, multiply there with the same Karatsuba and pack the result back (the conversions are linear); squaring uses the 32-bit half-product schoolbook from 80 limbs. On Native and LLVM the square dispatch runs the word Karatsuba square from its cutoff (see Squaring below), never the recursive multiplication; on Wasm and Wasm-GC the schoolbook square runs below 384 64-bit limbs (768 of 32 bits) and Toom-3 from there. Cutoffs measured per target with paired runs of coefficient multiplication at 1,024 to 65,536 bits."
msgstr ""

