#: manual/performance/binary.md:91
msgid "`Binary::add`, `mul`, `div` and `sqrt` on operands whose coefficients fit one machine word (up to 64 bits) and whose precision is at most 62 bits (60 for `div` and `sqrt`) skip the general path, which builds several coefficient values, a context, a flags record and a result tuple for each operation. The fast path computes on `UInt64` pairs (128-bit sums and products, a 128 by 64 bit division, a double-precision estimate refined by a Newton step and settled by exact comparisons for the square root), rounds to nearest even with the round and sticky bits, and allocates only the result. It declines a NaN, an infinity, a zero, an exact zero sum, wider coefficients or precision, operands whose exponents are far apart or near the implementation limits, and JavaScript, where the general path answers; the results are the same values with the same precision and exponent. White-box tests compare 40,000 random operand pairs per operation, ties, carries, cancellation and perfect squares with the general path. Operands of 128 bits and more are unchanged."
msgstr ""

