#: manual/performance/binary.md:106
msgid "Above the single-word range, `Binary::add` and `mul` on coefficients of up to 128 bits at a precision of at most 128 bits run on `UInt64` words as well: a 192-bit aligned sum or difference and a 256-bit product (four word products, the high halves by 32-bit pieces), rounded to nearest even from the words with the round and sticky bits, and only the result is allocated. Wider operands, `div` above 128 bits (up to 128 bits it has a two-word path of its own in `binary_fast_pair_nonjs.mbt`) and `sqrt` at any size go through coefficient-level paths that do the same exact integer arithmetic as the general path but skip its context, flags record, result tuple and repeated rounding decisions: the sum is built in one window array of the width of the result plus three bits (the operand with the larger top bit placed, the other read through its shift, a sticky bit for what lies below it), the product is the short product of the high limbs when its rounding is certain (`bin_words_mul_short`), otherwise the limb-array product (`bin_words_mul`), the quotient carries at least two bits more than the precision plus a sticky bit from the remainder, the square root rounds from the integer root and its remainder (`remainder > root` decides, there are no ties), and the kept bits are copied once into the array that becomes the coefficient. Each path declines a NaN, an infinity, a zero, an exact zero sum, coefficients or precisions above 2^20 bits, operands whose exponents are far apart or near the implementation limits, and JavaScript, where the general path answers; the results are the same values with the same precision and exponent. White-box tests compare tens of thousands of random, all-ones, sparse, tie, exact-quotient, perfect-square and cancelling operand sets per operation with the general path on Native, Wasm and Wasm-GC (JavaScript keeps the general path)."
