#: manual/performance/binary.md:128
msgid "Division uses a one-limb path, Knuth's algorithm D on 64-bit digits (a 128 by 64 bit quotient estimate and a 128-bit correction test) below the Burnikel–Ziegler cutoff (also as the leaf of its recursion, up to 64 limbs of 64 bits), and Burnikel–Ziegler from 96 limbs of 64 bits (192 of 32 bits) on Wasm, Wasm-GC and LLVM and from 208, 352 or 752 limbs of 64 bits on Native, with the portable, C or assembly rows. The dispatch does not select Newton reciprocal division, because it is slower than Burnikel–Ziegler from 32,768 bits (first table below), so the dispatch never reaches it; the routine stays, covered by tests, and the threshold constant is per target. Native multiplication switched to the NTT at 8,192 limbs of 32 bits (4,096 limbs of 64 bits, see Multiplication) instead of 2,048 limbs of 32 bits now that the Karatsuba is faster. Square root uses fixed-width kernels through 256 bits (the word kernels on Native, LLVM and Wasm-GC, the restoring loops on linear Wasm) and Zimmermann's `SqrtRem` on word arrays above that on every target except JavaScript; the recursion stops at the same fixed-width kernels once a block is at most 256 bits (a restoring loop on heap integers at 128 bits before), which makes a 1,024-bit radicand (a 512-bit `sqrt`) faster in paired runs on Native and Wasm (second table below). The cutoff is per-target policy measured with paired runs; the LLVM value is not measured because that backend is not available in the toolchain used. Large GCD uses Lehmer batching on a common 63-bit window of both operands and takes a single division step when the shorter operand has no bits in that window, so operands of very different lengths are reduced at once."
