#: manual/performance/binary.md:169
msgid "Above 256 bits the integer square root on every target except JavaScript runs Zimmermann's `SqrtRem` on word arrays instead of the `BinCoeff` divide-and-conquer root, which built about twenty-five temporaries per level (a `BinCoeff` operation on a few limbs costs 600 to 700 instructions, more than the division it surrounds). The radicand is shifted left by an even number of bits, below 128, to an even number $n$ of 64-bit limbs with the top two bits not both zero; each level cuts it into blocks of $n/4$ limbs, takes the root and remainder of the upper part from the recursion, one division by twice that root and one squaring of the quotient through the coefficient dispatch, and corrects the root by at most one; a quotient of a whole block is capped one below, which is the root. The remainder of the original radicand comes back from the normalized one with a one-limb product, $(r' + e(2s' - e)) \\gg 2t$ with $e = s' \\bmod 2^t$ for the shift $2t$. The recursion ends in the fixed-width kernels at 256 bits. No size is slower on any target (see the table below), so the word recursion is selected on every target except JavaScript (there is no per-target switch: `bin_coeff_sqrt_words_enabled` only selects the word or the restoring kernels of the base case, and the previous recursion remains as the test reference). White-box tests compare it with the previous recursion, the restoring loop and a BigInt oracle from 257 to 4,097 bits (random, all ones, single bits, zero limbs inside the value, the neighbours of exact squares), and with the defining inequalities up to 33,000 bits."
