#: manual/performance/binary.md:195
msgid "The last stage of the 64-bit limb work moves the remaining coefficient kernels off the 32-bit view of the storage, so that only the 32-bit-limb routes of Wasm (multiplication, squaring and Knuth division on 32-bit limbs) use it, and every cutoff is named and documented in 64-bit limbs (`bin_coeff_ntt_mul_min_words`, `bin_coeff_ntt_square_min_words`, `bin_coeff_toom3_min_words`, `bin_coeff_bz_division_min_words`, `bin_coeff_bz_leaf_words`, `bin_coeff_radix_format_dc_words`, `bin_coeff_sparse_min_words` (in `coeff_nonjs.mbt`) and the others of the threshold files, the same values halved). Converted: the multiplication and squaring dispatch, the unbalanced product (the block multiplication of the word Karatsuba replaces the `BinCoeff`-level one), the Burnikel–Ziegler recursion, the NTT digit conversion and overlap-add (the transforms keep their 32-bit Montgomery primes), the one-limb division, the sparse product, the bit operations, the byte and power-of-two radix conversions, the Lehmer threshold and the 256-bit square root kernel. The old divide-and-conquer root, its 512-bit kernel and the restoring loop are test-only references now. Results are unchanged: the regression digests recorded with the 32-bit implementation, the BigInt oracle tests, and new tests of the converted kernels against their 32-bit-limb definitions, the Knuth division, the schoolbook product and each other. The cutoffs keep their values (halved from limbs of 32 bits), except that LLVM now carries the cutoffs of the Native portable loops, not the values of the C and assembly rows of Native (its Burnikel–Ziegler cutoff is 96 limbs as on Wasm, against 208 on the Native portable loops), instead of an old copy of the Wasm ones; the elementary bit-burst precisions depend on multiplication cost, which this stage does not change. The NTT digit conversion stays on the 32-bit view on Wasm and Wasm-GC (`bin_coeff_ntt_limb32_conversion`), because the 64-bit limb conversion measured slower on Wasm-GC (