#: manual/performance/binary.md:223
msgid "`BinCoeff::to_radix_string` and `BinCoeff::parse` in a radix that is not a power of two work on one limb array in place below their divide-and-conquer cutoffs. Formatting divides a copy of the 64-bit limbs by the chunk power (9 decimal digits, the largest power of the radix below $2^{32}$) one pass per chunk, and parsing accumulates `value * chunk_power + chunk` in place; each limb is two 32-bit halves with one 64 by 32 bit division or multiplication each, and the digits of a chunk are written into one string builder from a scratch array. The earlier path sent every chunk through the 32-bit limb accessors and allocated a coefficient (the first table below gives its cost per limb step). Chunks of 64 bits (19 decimal digits) are not used: a 128 by 64 bit quotient digit needs two hardware divisions where two 64 by 32 bit steps need one each, so by construction a pass over the limbs costs about the same per digit, and what was wrong was the per-chunk overhead, which is gone either way (not measured separately). The divide-and-conquer conversion starts at 6,144 32-bit limbs (196,608 bits) for formatting and at 4,608 chunks (about 41,000 digits) for parsing on Native and LLVM. Paired wall-clock runs of the conversion on Wasm and Wasm-GC put the parsing cutoff at 4,608 chunks as on Native and keep the formatting cutoff at 128 limbs of 32 bits (64 of 64 bits); the second table of Kernels on 64-bit limbs gives the runs. White-box tests check, for the radixes 3, 10 and 36, the powers of the radix and their neighbours around chunk edges up to 130 chunks, and the in-place conversion against divide and conquer (formatting and parsing) at 1 to 1,025 limbs on both sides of the cutoffs; a CPython oracle checks both directions for further radixes."
msgstr ""

