#: manual/performance/binary.md:268
msgid "The Taylor series of `sin` and `cos` on the reduced argument (at most 1 in magnitude) is summed in fixed point when the argument is exact at the working precision, with 20 guard bits and, for the sine, the leading zero bits of the argument, so a small argument keeps `precision + 20` significant bits. Each computed term rounds down and is short by at most 4 units, the series alternates with decreasing terms, and so `signed sum +- (4n + 24)` encloses the value, with the sign flipped for a negative sine argument. Arguments that are not exact, or so small that its leading zero bits would exceed four times the working precision plus 64, use the previous floating-point series; the refinement loop and its working precisions are unchanged. White-box tests check containment of a much tighter enclosure, a bound on the width, and the sine and cosine identities."
