#: manual/performance/binary.md:280
msgid "The certified `ln` enclosure on the normalized argument in [1, 2] (and, below a working precision of 64 bits, the `ln 2` and `ln 10` constants built from it) sums the series `2 * sum z^(2k+1) / (2k+1)`, `z = (v - 1) / (v + 1)`, in fixed point when the argument is exact at the working precision. The fraction has 16 guard bits plus the leading zero bits of `z`, so the enclosure keeps `precision + 16` significant bits even for an argument close to 1; every operation rounds down, which makes the sum a lower bound, and a proven error of less than 2 units per term and a tail bound give the upper bound. Arguments that are not exact, or so close to 1 that the leading zero bits of `z` would exceed four times the working precision plus 64, use the previous floating-point series, so the refinement loop and its working precisions are unchanged. White-box tests check containment of a much tighter enclosure, a bound on the width, and equality of the final correctly rounded results and flags with those of the previous series."
