#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:27
msgid "The baseline needs a C compiler and the headers and libraries of MPFR, GMP and mpdecimal. The runner looks for them under `/opt/homebrew`; set `--prefix` or `EXTBENCH_PREFIX` for another location. From the repository root:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:35
msgid "The run builds `tools/external_baseline.c`, writes the operand file, asks the C harness for the reference results, checks `floating` against them while it is timed, and alternates the two sides over seven rounds, waiting before each side until the machine is quiet (under 150% CPU used by other processes; `--no-gate` turns this off). Every block is a pair of the case and its null, so a run takes about twice as long as it did before the net-ratio method (about 40 minutes then), because the slowest cases (elementary functions at 8192 bits and 1000 digits) run for hundreds of milliseconds per operation. Run it on AC power and close other heavy work: the load of other processes is the main source of noise. The files land in `.tmp/external`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:48
msgid "the operands both sides read"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:49
msgid "MPFR and libmpdec results for those operands"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:50
msgid "CPU, OS, compiler, library, `moon` and `moonc` versions, revision, checksums of the two files above"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:51
msgid "raw observations of each round"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:52
msgid "raw and net times, null times, intervals, ratios, verdicts (schema `external-baseline-v2`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:53
msgid "the pairs of case and null blocks of every case, per side and round: everything `results.json` is computed from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:57
msgid "Restrict the matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:63
msgid "A restricted run writes its own directory, so it never mixes with a full run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:65
msgid "Try a run quickly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:71
msgid "The `quick` preset uses short batches and two rounds, and is far noisier than the `standard` one. It exercises every path but is not a measurement to publish."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:74
msgid "Check the gaps quickly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:80
msgid "`quick` times only `floating`, in two rounds with short batches, and divides by the MPFR and libmpdec medians stored in `testdata/external_baseline/summary-0.11.0.json` (`--stored` names another summary). Those reference timings do not change, so the C side is not built or run, and the runner does not wait for a quiet machine. The table shows the time of `floating`, the stored reference time, the ratio, the ratio of the stored run and a flag for ratios above `--flag-above` (10 by default). The machine state of the stored run differs from today's, so read a single absolute ratio as a guide and decide a change by timing the old and the new build alternately on the same machine. The floating results are checked against an untimed pass of the same function, not against the C library; only `run` validates against MPFR and libmpdec."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:92
msgid "Re-reduce and publish"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:99
msgid "`report` reduces the raw rounds in `.tmp/external` again, for example after a change of the statistics, without measuring (the rounds must come from a harness that writes null blocks). `publish` copies the compact summary into `testdata/external_baseline` as `summary-VERSION-CONFIGURATION.json`, one file per release and configuration, the samples as `samples-VERSION-CONFIGURATION.json.gz`, the tables as `summary-CONFIGURATION.md`, and regenerates the tables of that configuration in the [performance page](../../performance/bench/external.md); the raw JSONL stays out of the repository. The summaries of earlier releases (schema v1) keep their names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:109
msgid "Choose a configuration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:117
msgid "`--target` chooses the `moon` target the floating side is built and run for (`native`, `wasm` under `moonrun`, `js` under node; the reference side stays the native C process), `--warmup-us` overrides the warm-up time, and `--configuration` names the build configuration of the floating side (lower-case letters, digits and hyphens; `native` for the native target and the target's name otherwise) and is recorded in the summary and in the names of the published files. `--moon-env` passes an environment variable to the `moon` process that builds and runs the floating side and can be repeated; how a configuration is selected, for example how the portable path is forced, is defined by the kernel work, not by the benchmark."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:127
msgid "Calibrate the warm-up of a runtime"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:133
msgid "runs the calibration experiment of the [design page](../../design/bench/external.md#the-calibration-experiment-of-the-warm-up) on the floating side only (nothing is published): the warm-up drift of every case, warm-up time and repeat, and the smallest warm-up within the limit. It writes `warmup-<target>.json` under `--out`. It is run once per runtime, before any cell is measured, in the same bench window as the measurement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:139
msgid "Recompute a summary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:145
msgid "recomputes every case from the samples and the protocol of the summary and says how many cases agree with the stored numbers (the exit status is 1 when one differs)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:149
msgid "Compare two releases"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:155
msgid "prints the ratio of every case in both files, the change in percent, a verdict: `slower` when the interval of the new ratio lies entirely above the old one, `faster` when it lies entirely below, `same` otherwise, and which ratio was compared: the net ratios when both summaries are resolved v2 results of the case, the raw ratios otherwise (a v1 summary has only raw ratios). A line says when the two summaries are of different configurations. Ratios, not absolute times, are compared, so a faster machine does not hide a regression; the two runs should still come from the same machine class."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:164
msgid "Table of a run beside a release"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:172
msgid "writes one table per new summary, that is per configuration, with a heading that names the bounds of the criterion and a line of counts (met, tolerated, not met, unresolved). A row has the case, the time of the reference and of `floating` (the net times of the new summary), the ratio of the old summary, the net ratio of the new one with its 95% interval, the verdict against the bounds recorded in the new summary, the quotient new over old (below 1 is faster) and the quotient of the raw ratios, new raw over old raw. The rows are sorted by package (`binary`, then `decimal_gda`), precision and operation. Without `--markdown` the Markdown goes to standard output; the CSV has the same rows for all configurations in one file, with a `configuration` column and plain numbers (nanoseconds and ratios), meant for a chart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:184
msgid "The old summary may be of schema v1 (the 0.11.0 baseline) or v2. A v1 ratio is a ratio of medians (marked `(raw)`): the loop floor of the timing harness is in both of its times. The new net ratio has the floor subtracted on both sides, so the two are not the same quantity and the quotient new over old is indicative, above all for the small cases whose time is mostly floor. The column `raw new / old` divides the raw ratio of the new summary (every v2 case has one) by the raw ratio of the old one: the same quantity on both sides, medians with the floor in both times, so it is the like-for-like change since the old release; it is empty when either raw ratio is missing. The verdict is made on the net ratio, not on this quotient. An unresolved case shows its raw ratio, marked `(raw)`, and the verdict `unresolved (raw met)` or the like. The same old summary is used for every configuration; the heading of each section names the configuration of the new summary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:197
msgid "The three states of the native limb primitives are three runs with their own output directory, each with an explicit configuration name and environment, so that their summaries sit side by side in one table (a name outside the table of criteria is judged like its target, here `native`, bound 3):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:210
msgid "The configuration name is recorded in the summary and is the heading of its section; `native-c` is a name of this example, any lower-case name with letters, digits and hyphens is accepted. Use the same `--seed` and preset for all of them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:214
msgid "Read a ratio"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:216
msgid "A cell of the net table such as `2.80 [2.71, 2.93] met` is the net time of `floating` divided by the net time of the reference library, with a 95% bootstrap interval; above 1 means `floating` is slower. `met` says the upper end is at most 3. `unresolved, raw 3.77 not met` says the net ratio could not be resolved (the reason is listed under the tables) and the raw ratio, shown in its own table, decides. `d` and `o` after a cell flag a drift of the null time between rounds and an order effect; an asterisk after a net ratio says the interval contains the target, after a raw ratio that the cell spread more than the dispersion thresholds on the performance page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:228
msgid "Add a case: extend the matrix constants of `tools/external_baseline.py` (for example `BINARY_PRECISIONS`) and the matching benchmark of `tooling/src/bench/external`; the operand file is read by both sides, so the new case is measured on the same inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:232
msgid "Understand the protocol: [bench/external design](../../design/bench/external.md) states why the sides alternate and defines the net ratio, the null and the rules of the verdict."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:235
msgid "Read the numbers: [external baseline](../../performance/bench/external.md) records the machine, the method and the ratios of the latest published release."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:241
msgid "Compare runs only when `machine.json` agrees on CPU, compilers and library versions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:243
msgid "A validation failure is reported but does not stop the timing: look for `WARNING` lines and `validation_failed_cases` in `results.json`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bench/external.md:245
msgid "Do not run other heavy work during a measurement; the load average is recorded at the start and at the end only."
msgstr ""

