#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:42
msgid "The smallest useful program divides one by three, once with 53 bits and once in the binary32 format, and prints both results as the shortest decimal that reads back exactly:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:59
msgid "The operator `/` rounds to nearest-even at the precision of its operands (53 bits by default). `div_ctx` rounds under the binary32 context and also returns the IEEE flags; here `inexact` says that one third is not a binary number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:65
msgid "Build exact values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:67
msgid "A finite `Binary` has value $c \\cdot 2^{e}$, where $c$ is an integer coefficient and $e$ is an exponent. `make` builds a value from the coefficient, exponent and precision; `to_string` prints its exact stored form `c p e`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:87
msgid "Read decimal input without double rounding"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:89
msgid "Parse decimal text with `from_string`, which rounds the decimal value itself. Converting through a host `Double` rounds twice when you want a precision other than 53 bits, and `from_double` faithfully keeps the host's binary approximation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:108
msgid "Compute in an IEEE format and keep the flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:110
msgid "Set an operation's precision, exponent range, rounding direction and tininess rule with `BinaryContext`. Each `*_ctx` method returns the value and flags for that operation. Combine the flags to accumulate them across operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:133
msgid "$120000$ exceeds the largest binary16 value $65504$, so the sum overflows to infinity. The product $10^{-7}$ lies below the smallest normal binary16 number $2^{-14} \\approx 6.1 \\cdot 10^{-5}$, so it is rounded on the subnormal grid and raises underflow. The rounding direction changes the overflow result: toward zero it stays at the largest finite value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:153
msgid "Read and write interchange bits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:155
msgid "Use `BinaryInterchange` to read or write binary16, binary32, binary64 or binary128 bit patterns in a file or protocol. It never goes through a host `Double`, so signaling NaNs, payloads and binary128 values survive."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:174
msgid "Encoding rounds the value into the selected format, so it returns flags as well. Decoding is always exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:177
msgid "Classify a value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:179
msgid "`class_of` returns one of the ten classes in IEEE 754-2019 5.7.2. Because a `Binary` has no format of its own, its context determines where the subnormal range starts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:197
msgid "With a lower `e_min`, the same value is normal. `is_finite`, `is_infinite` and `is_nan` need no context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:200
msgid "Print decimal text"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:202
msgid "Use `to_shortest_string_ctx` for the shortest decimal that reads back as the same value in a format; in binary64 it matches the host formatter. `to_decimal_string_ctx` prints a fixed number of significant digits, rounded in the context's direction, and reports whether it dropped digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:224
msgid "Every binary number has a finite decimal expansion. Here, 55 digits print the binary64 value of `0.1` exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:227
msgid "Call elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:229
msgid "Each elementary function has a plain form, a `*_ctx` form and a `try_*_ctx` form. All three return the correctly rounded result, and all three return a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation` for a domain error (in `Ok` for `try_*_ctx`). They differ only in how they report an exhausted refinement budget."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:235
msgid "If the refinement budget is exhausted, the plain and `*_ctx` forms return a quiet NaN with `not_computed`, while `try_*_ctx` returns an `@lf_arith.ArithmeticError` that says what went wrong. The budget is twelve refinement steps by default; `context.with_refinement_limit(Some(n))` changes the count and `with_refinement_limit(None)` removes it (the working precision then stops at a cap of at least 8,000,000 bits), for a rare input that sits very close to a rounding boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:263
msgid "`sinpi(0.5)` is exactly one, and the function knows it: exact results raise no `inexact` flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:268
msgid "Enclose a real number with directed rounding"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:270
msgid "Rounding the exact result toward negative and positive infinity gives lower and upper bounds. Together, they give a guaranteed enclosure without an interval library:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:295
msgid "`interval` computes with inf-sup intervals (a lower bound and an upper bound); the midpoint–radius form is a view of them (`from_midpoint_radius`, `midpoint`, `radius`); see the [interval tutorial](interval.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:299
msgid "Use fused and exact IEEE operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:301
msgid "`fma` rounds $x y + z$ once, which lets you recover the rounding error of a product. `remainder` is always exact when its operands have the same precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:319
msgid "The two-product error term is the basis of compensated algorithms such as Kahan summation and double-double arithmetic. The design page explains why the error is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:323
msgid "Watch underflow and tininess"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:325
msgid "IEEE 754 allows tininess detection before or after rounding. For a value just below the smallest normal number, the choice changes the underflow flag. Choose the rule with `BinaryContext`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:350
msgid "Both operations return the same value; only the underflow flag differs. Hardware differs too: x86 detects tininess after rounding and ARM before rounding; TestFloat checks both rules."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:354
msgid "Write generic code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:356
msgid "Write a function against `@def.Floating` or the checked and contextual traits of `@lf_arith`. `Binary` implements these traits, which are shared by all scalar cores of `floating`; the same code runs on `Binary`, `Decimal` and the other cores:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:387
msgid "The contextual traits return errors for IEEE `division_by_zero` and `invalid_operation` flags, and report the other flags as diagnostics. To stop a whole pipeline at its first error, use [`binary_checked`](binary_checked.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:392
msgid "Choose one precision"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:394
msgid "Pick one precision for an algorithm instead of changing it repeatedly. Operation cost grows with precision roughly like the cost of multiplying integers with the same number of bits. The coefficient kernel switches automatically from schoolbook to Karatsuba, Toom-3 and number-theoretic transforms, so thousands of bits are practical. Elementary functions refine their working precision until the result is certain, and the first attempt succeeds for almost every input. The [performance page](../performance/binary.md) records measurements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:404
msgid "**`==` uses `compare`'s numerical relation.** A 53-bit `1` is `==` to a 24-bit `1`; $-0 = +0$; and all NaNs compare equal. It is not IEEE equality. Use `compare_quiet_equal` for IEEE equality; `identical` compares zero sign, precision and NaN kind."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:408
msgid "**`compare` orders NaN last.** It does not abort. Every NaN compares equal to every NaN and greater than every number, so `nan > x` is true. Use `compare_checked`, `compare_quiet` or `compare_quiet_less` when NaN must be unordered."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:412
msgid "**Plain operators ignore your format.** `x + y` uses the larger operand precision and an exponent range of about $2^{\\pm 2^{30}}$. It never overflows at binary32 limits. Use `add_ctx` with a format context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:415
msgid "**`from_double(0.1)` is not one tenth.** The host has already rounded it to binary64. Parse decimal text with `from_string`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:417
msgid "**`to_string` prints binary notation.** It prints `3602879701896397p-55`. Use `to_shortest_string` or `to_decimal_string_ctx` for decimal output."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:420
msgid "**`from_hex` needs the `p` exponent and reports no flags.** `0x1.8p0` and `0x3p-1` are both $1.5$, but `0x1.8` is rejected. An inexact conversion looks exact. Use `from_hex_ctx` or `from_string_ctx` for decimal text when rounding direction or flags matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:424
msgid "**`with_precision` hides flags.** Use `apply_ctx` to check whether narrowing was inexact or overflowed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:426
msgid "**The sign of zero is an input.** IEEE 754 special cases use it: `atan2` of $(-0, -1)$ is $-\\pi$, but of $(+0, -1)$ is $+\\pi$; `tanpi(1)` is $-0$; and $(-0)^{-1}$ is $-\\infty$. Keep the sign: changing $-0$ to $+0$ changes these results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:433
msgid "[binary design](../design/binary.md): the error model, how correct rounding is decided, overflow and tininess, certified elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary.md:435
msgid "[binary API](../api/binary.md): every item with its special values."
msgstr ""

