msgid "On plain `Binary` values the second call would silently produce a number ($1/0 = \\infty$ and $2/\\infty = 0$); the wrapper reports where the computation left the real numbers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:99
msgid "Add your own checks with `bind`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:101
msgid "`map` applies a function that cannot fail; `bind` applies one that returns a `BinaryChecked` and may fail. Use `bind` to put an application rule into the pipeline:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:130
msgid "Control precision and rounding with a context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:132
msgid "Every elementary function has a `_ctx` form that takes a `BinaryContext`. The plain form rounds to nearest at the operand's own precision; the context form rounds to the context:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:151
msgid "Tell domain errors from certification failures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:153
msgid "Errors are `ArithmeticError` values from Luna-Flow/arithmetic. Their kind says whether the input was outside the function's domain or the library could not certify a correctly rounded result:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:179
msgid "Generic code over Luna-Flow/arithmetic traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:181
msgid "`Binary` implements the checked traits of Luna-Flow/arithmetic (`SqrtChecked`, `DivChecked`, `PowIntChecked`, `PowNatChecked`). A function written against those traits returns `Result[T, ArithmeticError]`, and `from_result` brings it into a pipeline:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:213
msgid "Keep IEEE flags when they matter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:215
msgid "The wrapper stores a value or an error, nothing else. When the inexact, overflow or underflow flags are part of your contract, call the `Binary::*_ctx` operations, which return `(value, BinaryFlags)`, and combine the flags yourself:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:237
msgid "**A quiet NaN is a success.** `BinaryChecked` reports an invalid operation ($\\infty - \\infty$, `sqrt_ctx` of a negative number, a signaling NaN) as `DomainError` and a division by zero as `DivisionByZero`, with or without a context. A quiet NaN operand is not an exception and stays a successful NaN; test `@def.is_nan` on the final value if that matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:242
msgid "**`x` and `x_ctx` have one error contract.** `x_ctx` is `x` with an explicit context; `div_ctx` by zero fails like `div`. Overflow, underflow and inexact are not errors in either form."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:245
msgid "**Only the first error survives.** In `a + b` with both operands failing, the error of `a` is reported. Errors are not collected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:247
msgid "**The `_ctx` methods drop the range flags.** Overflow, underflow and inexact are dropped; use `binary` directly for flags and for the raw default result of an invalid operation or a division by zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:250
msgid "**Precision of mixed operands.** Binary operations use the larger operand precision; `from_float` defaults to 24 bits, so mixing it with `from_double` values gives 53-bit results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/binary_checked.md:253
msgid "**Operands are always evaluated.** The wrapper skips the *operation* after an error, but MoonBit evaluates every argument first, so in `a + f(b)` the call `f(b)` runs even when `a` is an error. Put expensive steps behind `bind` when they should be skipped."
msgstr ""

