#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:87
msgid "Keep and set the quantum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:89
msgid "A GDA number has a coefficient and an exponent. The quantum is $10^q$, where $q$ is that exponent, so `2.50` and `2.5` have the same value but different quanta. Arithmetic chooses the exponent required by the exact result, while `quantize` sets a target exponent. Use it to round an amount to cents:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:117
msgid "`reduce` removes trailing zeros, so `reduce(7.50)` is `7.5`. Use it only when you really want the shortest cohort member."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:120
msgid "Handle a trap without losing the result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:122
msgid "Call `trap` on a context to enable a trap. A trapped operation returns `Trapped(signal, value, next_context, raised)`. The result specified by GDA remains available as `value`, and `next_context` contains the updated flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:144
msgid "An `InvalidOperation` trap also covers conversion syntax, division impossible, division undefined and invalid context. These four detailed conditions appear as flags in the outcome, not as signals. Only the specification's nine signals can be trapped. `GdaContext::basic_default()` enables `InvalidOperation`, so a malformed literal traps:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:166
msgid "When an operation raises several trapped conditions, the package reports one according to a fixed precedence. The [API page](../api/decimal_gda.md#trap-selection) lists that precedence, so you never have to invent your own order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:170
msgid "Respect the exponent range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:172
msgid "The context bounds the adjusted exponent to `[e_min, e_max]`. A result above `e_max` overflows; its rounding algorithm determines what the result becomes. A result below `e_min` becomes subnormal and loses digits:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:200
msgid "Call the elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:202
msgid "`sqrt`, `exp`, `ln` and `log10` are correctly rounded and always round half to even, whatever the context's rounding algorithm says. `power` with a non-integer exponent is also correctly rounded, but under the context's own rounding algorithm. `exp`, `ln`, `log10`, `power` with a non-integer exponent other than 0.5 and `power` with an integer exponent above 999,999,999 or below $-1\\,999\\,999\\,997$ only accept contexts with precision, `e_max` and `-e_min` at most 999,999 (decNumber's limit, which the decTest suite requires and GDA's Invalid context condition permits; the operation text of the specification states no such bound); the decimal32/64/128 presets qualify, but the default of `GdaContext::new` (exponent range ±999,999,999) does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:237
msgid "GDA 1.70, page 30 (exp) and page 31 (ln, log10), allows an inexact result to be up to 1 ulp from the correctly rounded result; this package returns the correctly rounded result. GDA 1.70, page 39 (square-root), specifies half-even rounding for an inexact square root, and this package also returns the correctly rounded result. GDA 1.70, page 35 (power), allows up to 1 ulp of error for a non-integer exponent; this package returns the correctly rounded result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:244
msgid "Here, certification means that an interval known to contain the function value lies inside its rounding cell. Each function refines its enclosure at most twelve times by default. For an argument very close to a rounding boundary, `ctx.with_refinement_limit(Some(24))` allows more steps; `with_refinement_limit(None)` removes the count, so the working precision stops at a resource bound of at least 8,000,000 bits. Read the current setting with `refinement_limit()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:253
msgid "Compare and print"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:255
msgid "Use `compare` for numerical order: it returns a decimal containing `-1`, `0`, or `1`, and returns NaN when an operand is NaN. Use `compare_total` when the representation matters; it orders `2.50` before `2.5`. Printing a value uses scientific notation, while `class_name` returns its class name:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:283
msgid "Long chains with `decimal_gda_checked`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:285
msgid "Manual context threading is clearest at calculation boundaries. For a long linear pipeline, use [`decimal_gda_checked`](decimal_gda_checked.md): it keeps one outcome, threads the flags and context through each operation, and stops after the first trap:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:304
msgid "Generic code through `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:306
msgid "`Decimal` implements the contextual traits of [`Luna-Flow/arithmetic`](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/), so code written against `AddContextual`, `DivContextual`, `SqrtContextual` and friends runs on it. These adapters use the five IEEE-style rounding algorithms of `ArithmeticContext`, return errors as `Err`, and know nothing about traps:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:336
msgid "Subset arithmetic and lost digits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:338
msgid "`GdaContext::basic_default()` is the GDA basic default context: precision 9, `HalfUp`, `extended=false`, and traps for `DivisionByZero`, `InvalidOperation`, `Overflow`, `Underflow` and `Clamped`. A non-extended context rounds an operand with more digits than the precision before using it and raises `LostDigits` when that rounding loses information:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:358
msgid "`GdaInterchange` represents a decimal32, decimal64 or decimal128 bit pattern in densely packed decimal (DPD) encoding. The pattern is written as `#` followed by hexadecimal digits:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:374
msgid "**Reusing the original context.** Flags accumulate only when you pass each outcome's `next_context()` to the next operation. Trap settings and flags also travel with every context derived from the original."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:377
msgid "**Treating `Trapped` as \"no value\".** A trap changes the outcome variant, not the result. Read `value()` before deciding whether to stop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:379
msgid "**Expecting `normalized` and `reduce` to keep zeros.** `Decimal::from_int(100)` and `Decimal::make` keep the exponent they are given, but `Decimal::normalized` and `reduce` remove trailing zeros, so `Decimal::from_int(100).normalized()` prints `1E+2`. Use `Decimal::from_string(\"100\")` or `to_number` when the quantum matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:384
msgid "**Using the operators for GDA work.** `+`, `-`, `*` and `/` take no context. They use a private context with larger operand precision, half-even rounding, no clamp and no traps, so they round every result to that private precision, return the GDA cohort member (`1.10 + 3.50` is `4.60`) and never signal. Use the package functions when GDA results, flags or traps matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:389
msgid "**Equality and NaN.** `==` and `compare` put every NaN equal to every other NaN and above every number (a total preorder, so sorting never aborts). Use the package `compare`, `compare_signal`, or `Decimal::compare_checked` when NaN must remain unordered."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:393
msgid "**Mixing the two decimal packages.** `@decimal_gda.Decimal` and `@decimal.Decimal` have different contracts and types. Convert between them through strings or interchange bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:396
msgid "**Expecting elementary functions to follow the context rounding.** `exp`, `ln`, `log10` and `sqrt` always round half to even; only `power` follows the context's rounding algorithm. The [elementary-functions task](#call-the-elementary-functions) gives the required context range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:400
msgid "**Using a default context for elementary functions.** `GdaContext::new()` allows exponents up to ±999,999,999, outside the implementation limit for these operations. `exp`, `ln`, `log10` and `power` with a non-integer exponent other than 0.5 return NaN and raise `InvalidContext` (an `InvalidOperation`). Use a preset or pass `e_min=-999_999, e_max=999_999`. The same applies to an `ArithmeticContext` without `e_min` and `e_max`: no bounds are filled in, and `exp_contextual` returns `Err(unsupported)` with an `invalid context:` message naming the missing bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:411
