msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:88
msgid "Change precision generically"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:90
msgid "`with_precision` rounds to the requested number of significant digits in the value's radix: bits for binary and digits for decimal. It rounds to the nearest value, with ties to even. The trait method has no rounding-direction argument, so use the concrete type's `with_precision` method when you need a different direction:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:111
msgid "Eleven is `1011` in binary; three significant bits round it to `1100`, which `to_string` prints as $3 \\cdot 2^2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:114
msgid "Read an IEEE comparison"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:116
msgid "An IEEE 754 comparison returns a `PartialOrder`. Its fourth value, `Unordered`, reports that at least one operand is NaN:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:143
msgid "Normalize before comparing representations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:145
msgid "Decimal values with the same value can have different stored exponents (`1.5` and `1.500` belong to one *cohort*). `normalized` returns the canonical member of the cohort, so their printed forms agree:"
