msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:42
msgid "Decimal elementary functions against MPFR"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:44
msgid "The IEEE `decimal` package's elementary functions have one family each, `decimal-<function>` (`decimal-exp`, `decimal-atan2`, `decimal-pown`, `decimal-exp2m1`, `decimal-log10p1`, ...), checked against certified MPFR enclosures instead of libmpdec. They are not in the default set (there are as many families as functions); name them with `--family`, or select all of them with `--decimal` (without `--family`, `--decimal` runs only these families). A case id reads `m<seed>.<function>.<index>`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:56
msgid "Operands and contexts are drawn so that results reach overflow, underflow and the subnormal range; exact results are not sampled (an enclosure cannot certify them), and exponent ranges stay within ±20,000 because the cost of the exact endpoint conversion grows steeply beyond that."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:61
msgid "Run a campaign"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:63
msgid "A campaign needs the oracles: a C compiler with MPFR and GMP (Homebrew puts them in `/opt/homebrew`; pass `--mpfr-prefix` elsewhere) for the binary families, and Python 3 with the `decimal` module for the decimal families."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:82
msgid "`--family` is repeatable and defaults to every family that is not opt-in (see below). The binary families are `add sub mul div sqrt fma strtofr getstr shortest elem elem2 elem3 elem4` (`elem2` holds `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `log2p1` and `log10p1`, `elem3` holds `rsqrt`, `compound` and `powr`, `elem4` holds `asinpi`, `acospi`, `atanpi` and `atan2pi`, which `elem` does not draw); the `decimal_gda` families (General Decimal Arithmetic against libmpdec) are `decimal_gda-add decimal_gda-subtract decimal_gda-multiply decimal_gda-divide decimal_gda-squareroot decimal_gda-fma decimal_gda-apply decimal_gda-tosci decimal_gda-toeng decimal_gda-exp decimal_gda-ln decimal_gda-log10 decimal_gda-power`. The `decimal_gda-*wide` families sample the contexts on which the elementary functions refuse by design and are opt-in. The `decimal-<function>` families belong to the IEEE `decimal` package (see above). The former prefixes `dec-` (now `decimal_gda-`) and `decm-` (now `decimal-`), and the option `--decm` (now `--decimal`), are deprecated aliases and still accepted. `--count` is per family, `--shard` bounds the rows generated per file, `--batch` the cases validated together, `--jobs` the parallel shards."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:96
msgid "The run directory holds `records-<target>.jsonl` (append-only, one file per backend) and `summary.json` with the exact number of cases per backend, family and operation. The command exits with `1` when a batch disagreed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:100
msgid "Read the record"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:118
msgid "Each `validation` names the batch by its first and last case id and its size. An `invalid` one is followed by a `validation_failure` that carries the minimized case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:122
msgid "Replay a case"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:130
msgid "The generators are pure functions of seed, family and index, so the id is the whole reproduction. The output is the frontend's summary; a failing row is printed with the expected and the actual value and flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:134
msgid "Minimize a failure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:140
msgid "The minimizer tries simpler variants (smaller precision, plain rounding, no clamp, narrower exponent range, shorter operands, operands rewritten as `1E+k`), asks the oracle for the correct answer of each variant, and keeps a variant when the library still disagrees. The result is a row the oracle produced itself, so it is a consistent regression case, not an edited one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:146
msgid "Sweep the range limits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:148
msgid "The random rows rarely land on an underflow or overflow threshold. For the binary elementary functions, `tools/range_boundary_rows.py` writes rows on the thresholds themselves, for every precision in a range, with the oracle columns left as `?`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:159
msgid "The generator binary is built by any `just random-diff run`. A failing row is named with its expected and actual value and flags, so it can be pasted into a regression fixture."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:163
msgid "Other backends"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/differential.md:165
msgid "`--target` selects the backend of the harness: `native`, `wasm`, `js` or `llvm` (the mare_mark runner has no Wasm-GC runtime). For Wasm-GC, run the same fixtures through the synchronous runner:"
