msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:31
msgid "Import the package, plus `binary`, which supplies the endpoint type (the generic example below also uses `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` as `@lf_arith`):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:42
msgid "An `Interval` is a closed interval $[\\underline{x}, \\overline{x}]$ whose two endpoints are `Binary` values. The examples on this page print an interval with this helper: it writes the lower endpoint rounded down and the upper endpoint rounded up, so the printed decimal interval still contains the stored one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:68
msgid "The smallest useful program encloses $1/3$ at 53 bits:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:83
msgid "No `Binary` with 53 bits equals $1/3$, so the result is the smallest 53-bit interval around it: the lower endpoint is $1/3$ rounded down, the upper endpoint $1/3$ rounded up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:89
msgid "Build intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:91
msgid "Choose the constructor by what you know about the value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:115
msgid "`from_bounds` and `exact` abort on invalid input (a NaN endpoint, reversed bounds, a non-finite point); their `try_` forms return the same condition as an `ArithmeticError`, and so does `try_from_midpoint_radius(center, radius)` for a non-finite center or a negative or non-finite radius. `from_midpoint_radius(center, radius)` is a constructor view: the stored value is always the endpoint pair, and `midpoint()` / `radius()` read the midpoint–radius form back as IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8 defines it. The whole real line and the empty set are `Interval::entire()` and `Interval::empty()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:123
msgid "Enclose a decimal constant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:125
msgid "`Interval::from_double(0.1)` encloses the binary64 number nearest to $0.1$, which is not $0.1$. To enclose the decimal value itself, round the decimal string once down and once up, and use the two results as bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:152
msgid "Do this at every boundary where data enters from decimal text or from a rounded computation; inside the interval domain the guarantee is then kept automatically."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:156
msgid "Compute and read the result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:158
msgid "The arithmetic operators return an interval that contains every result of the operation applied to points of the operands."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:182
msgid "Read the result with `lower_bound()` / `upper_bound()` for the endpoints, `width()` for $\\overline{x} - \\underline{x}$ rounded up, `midpoint()` for a representative point, and `is_common_interval()` / `is_entire()` / `is_empty()` for the shape."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:187
msgid "Compare intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:189
msgid "An interval stands for an unknown point inside it, so \"is $x < y$?\" has three answers: certainly, possibly, or certainly not. The relations say which."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:216
msgid "`is_member` tests a point; `subset`, `interior` and `==` (the `equal` of IEEE 1788) compare sets; `DefinitelyLt::definitely_lt` and `DefinitelyLe::definitely_le` of Luna-Flow/arithmetic hold only when the order holds for every pair of points; `MaybeEq::maybe_eq` (and `Overlaps::overlaps`) holds when some pair may be equal; `disjoint`, `precedes` and `strict_precedes` are the relations of IEEE 1788 and are true for an empty operand; `overlap` gives the full IEEE 1788 classification of two intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:224
msgid "Evaluate elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:226
msgid "Functions are evaluated so that the result contains $f(\\xi)$ for every $\\xi$ in the argument where $f$ is defined. Extrema inside the interval and poles are taken into account."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:254
msgid "Every elementary function of `Interval` (`sin`, `exp`, `ln`, …) has a total form, which always returns a valid enclosure at any precision, and most also have a `try_` form (`try_sin`, …). They use the same kernel and give the same interval whenever the certified evaluation succeeds. When it runs out of its work budget the total form widens to the range of the function (for example $[-1, 1]$ for `sin`), and the `try_` form returns an `ArithmeticError` describing the failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:261
msgid "Round to a target format"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:263
msgid "`IntervalContext` describes a binary format (precision and exponent range). `apply_ctx` rounds an interval outward into it and reports what happened in `IntervalFlags`; to round the result of an operation, apply the context to it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:291
msgid "`DecoratedInterval` pairs an interval with an IEEE 1788 decoration that records what is known about the function evaluation that produced it: `com` (defined, continuous and bounded), `dac` (defined and continuous), `def` (defined), `trv` (nothing known) and `ill` (the special value NaI, \"not an interval\")."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:321
msgid "Use decorations when a caller must learn whether a result is backed by a function that was defined and continuous on the whole input — the condition under which interval results prove existence theorems such as Brouwer's fixed point theorem. When only the enclosure matters, bare `Interval` is simpler."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:326
msgid "Generic code over the enclosure traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:328
msgid "`Interval` implements the enclosure relations of [`Luna-Flow/arithmetic`](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/) (`Contains`, `Overlaps`, `DefinitelyLt`, `DefinitelyLe`, `MaybeEq`), so code written against those traits works for any enclosure type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:357
msgid "The checked capabilities `DivChecked`, `PowNatChecked` and `PowIntChecked` take an `@lf_arith.ArithmeticContext`; its `precision` sets the result precision. They never fail for `Interval`: division by an interval containing zero returns an unbounded enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:362
msgid "Checked pipelines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:364
msgid "When a computation has several steps that may fail (invalid construction, uncertified elementary functions), the companion package [`interval_checked`](interval_checked.md) threads the first error through a chain of operations so that you test for failure once at the end."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:369
msgid "Precision and cost"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:371
msgid "Each interval carries a working `precision` in bits; results of binary operations use the larger precision of the two operands. Arithmetic costs a few endpoint operations at that precision. Elementary functions evaluate the certified `binary` kernels at the interval's precision (with their own guard bits, up to 12 work precisions on hard inputs), so they cost several times more than one multiplication. Raising the precision narrows the rounding part of the width, but not the part that comes from the width of the inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:381
msgid "**`x - x` is not zero.** Interval arithmetic treats the two operands as independent unknowns, so $[1, 2] - [1, 2] = [-1, 1]$. The same holds for `x * x`, which is wider than `x.sqr()` when `x` contains zero:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:399
msgid "Rewrite expressions so that each uncertain quantity appears once, and prefer `sqr`, `pown` and the elementary functions over hand-written products."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:402
msgid "**`==` compares sets, not representations.** `a == b` is true when the two intervals are the same set, whatever their precision tags (IEEE 1788-2015 `equal`); use `a.identical(b)` when the precision tag matters too. On decorated intervals `==` is false for NaI, even against itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:407
msgid "**`is_member` takes a point.** The method `Interval::is_member` tests a `Binary` point. Set inclusion is `subset` (or the trait method `@lf_arith.Contains::contains`, which means \"contains as a subset\")."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:411
msgid "**Intervals are not ordered.** There is no total order on intervals; never sort them with `less` (an IEEE 1788 set relation) as if it were a comparison of numbers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:415
msgid "**Unbounded and empty intervals have no useful center.** `midpoint()`, `radius()`, `width()`, `magnitude()` and `mignitude()` are total and follow IEEE 1788-2015 12.12.8 instead of aborting: NaN for the empty set, `midpoint()` 0 for the whole line and the largest finite value (with the sign of the unbounded side) for a half-bounded interval, `radius()` and `width()` $+\\infty$ for any unbounded interval. Test `is_empty()` and `is_common_interval()` first when a NaN or an infinity would be a problem; `midpoint_ctx` takes the result format as an `IntervalContext`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:424
msgid "**Integers wider than the precision.** `from_int(n, precision=p)` and `from_coefficient` enclose `n` exactly, but when `n` needs more than $p$ bits the result is the two-point interval of its $p$-bit neighbours, not a point. At 53 bits every `Int32` is a point, but a `BinCoeff` wider than 53 bits, or an explicit small precision, is not: pass a precision at least as large as the bit length of the integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:431
msgid "**Inputs from `Double`.** `from_double(x)` encloses the binary value of `x` exactly; it cannot know which decimal number `x` approximated. Enclose decimal data as in [Enclose a decimal constant](#enclose-a-decimal-constant)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:437
msgid "The [design page](../design/interval.md) proves why these results are enclosures, derives the endpoint formulas, and explains decorations and certified elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:440
msgid "The [API reference](../api/interval.md) documents every item with its special cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:442
msgid "[`interval_checked`](interval_checked.md) composes fallible interval operations; [`binary`](binary.md) is the endpoint arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/interval.md:444
msgid "[Conformance](../conformance/interval.md) states what the IEEE 1788 test corpus covers."
msgstr ""

