#: manual/verification.md:30
msgid "`ci.yml` runs `just pr` on every pull request and every push to `main`. It restores the newest verification ledger first and only a push to `main` saves one: GitHub scopes a cache to the ref that created it, so what a pull-request run would save can be restored by neither `main` nor another pull request. The native tests of the pull-request gate run as incremental units (`tools/native_units.py`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:35
msgid "`nightly.yml` runs `just gate` for `quick`, `decimal`, `decimal_gda`, `binary` and `interval` as parallel jobs every night and on demand. It caches the SHA-256-pinned upstream corpora by manifest hash (the hashes are still verified after download) and uploads each `summary.json` as an artifact, so the published results are reproduced outside maintainers' machines. It runs the gates with `--no-reuse` and restores no ledger. It also runs the unit tests of the whole workspace on `native`, without reuse (the audit of the native units of the pull-request gate), `js`, `wasm` and `wasm-gc` (the pull-request gate tests native and only type-checks these; `QUICK_TEST_TARGETS` in `tools/run_ci.py` is the one line that adds a target to it), and, on an arm64 macOS runner, the tests of `internal/binary_kernel` and `binary` on native with the row primitives portable (`LUNA_FLOATING_PORTABLE=1`), forced to the C tier (`LUNA_FLOATING_LIMBS=c`) and in the default state; `tools/check_limbs_tier.py` fails the job when the runner is not arm64, a state reports another tier than expected, or the switch did not take (`override=1` in the portable and C states, `override=0` in the default state). The Linux runner is x86-64, so nothing else executes the arm64 code. `wasm-gc` runs the whole workspace except what needs `moonbitlang/async`, which has no wasm-gc runtime (its module initialiser is rejected by the runtime at validation); the workflow states the reason next to the job."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:56
msgid "`docs.yml` runs the organization's `lunadoc check` workflow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:57
msgid "`publish.yml` builds, runs the documentation gate, the format check, an all-target `--deny-warn` check and the tests, then publishes to mooncakes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:60
msgid "Quick-gate table of the π-inverse rows"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:62
msgid "The rows of `asinpi`, `acospi`, `atanpi` and `atan2pi` are the largest table of the repository. The quick gate (`just pr`, the native test stage of CI) compiles all 9,691 of them into `library/src/decimal/elementary_pi_inverse_rows_wbtest.mbt` (1.1 MB of source), and the same rows are data (`testdata/decimal/ieee/rows/pi_inverse.txt`, `mpfr-decimal-random-v2` rows, see `tooling/src/frontend/mpfr_expr`) that `just gate decimal` and the nightly run feed to the release-built conformance CLI on native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript. Both compare value, cohort (`total_order_compare`) and all flags exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:71
msgid "This was not always so. While about 50 `atan2pi` rows with an operand of `1E-1999996` cost 90 % of a 466 s debug run, the quick gate compiled only a deterministic sample. After the `atan2pi` path for tiny operands was fixed the full table of 19,134 rows (the table before the 0.12 context restriction, 2.2 MB of source) ran in 2.4 s of a debug native build (4,416 sampled rows: 0.5 s) and added about 1.4 s to the build, under the 5 s the quick gate allows it, so the sample was dropped. The mechanism stays in the generator (`tools/generate_decimal_pi_inverse_rows.py`) as an option: `--sample-step N` compiles every row that is not the rounding of an MPFR enclosure (the exact Niven values and the other cases decided by exact rational arithmetic) and, of the MPFR rows stratified by (operation, rounding mode), one row in N, taken equidistantly in generation order (a stratum shorter than N keeps its middle row). The data file is always the full table."
msgstr ""

