#: manual/verification.md:119
msgid "Published claims"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:121
msgid "**GDA.** All 64,986 legal executable scalar rows of the 144-file `official` corpus pass, and all 16,124 legal rows of `official0`. The 141 `#` placeholder or non-scalar rows are diagnostic exclusions, not unsupported legal behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:125
msgid "**Binary.** The TestFloat level-1 matrix (seed 1) has 254,280,504 vectors in 534 tasks over binary16/32/64/128: 7,461,360 for add, subtract, multiply, divide and square root, 52,632 for the format conversions (each of the four formats to each of the other three, 66 tasks), and 246,766,512 for the IEEE operations mulAdd (245,329,920 of them), rem, roundToInt, the conversions to 32- and 64-bit signed and unsigned integers, and the comparisons eq, le, lt, eq_signaling, le_quiet and lt_quiet. It runs five rounding directions where the operation uses one, both tininess modes for the arithmetic operations, mulAdd and the narrowing format conversions, and both exact and inexact variants for roundToInt and the conversions. Invalid integer conversions are compared by flags only, because SoftFloat returns platform-specific sentinel values where the API returns `None`. The gate judges the operation, not the operation followed by a rounding of the harness: the result is packed into the bit fields of its format by the front end itself and a result that is not a number of the format fails the vector; the flags compared are the operation's own. The MPFR part adds the 1,055 executable rows of the pinned square-root data, 1,740 hash-pinned witnesses covering 29 elementary operations at binary32/64/128 precision under the five `BinaryRoundingMode` values, and the decimal character conversions of IEEE 754-2019 §5.12: 3,699 `mpfr_strtofr` witnesses (decimal text to binary at 11 to 200 bits, including exact halfway points and the exponent limits) and 2,688 `mpfr_get_str` witnesses (fixed digit counts, exact ties and the shortest round-trip digit count)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:147
msgid "**IEEE decimal.** Committed decimal32/64/128 DPD and BID fixtures cover encoding, special values, flags, core arithmetic and all 1,024 DPD declets on native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript; LLVM is outside this gate. A committed elementary oracle adds 3,060 rows computed from 768-bit MPFR enclosures under the five IEEE decimal rounding-direction attributes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:152
msgid "**Interval.** The strict ITF1788 phases pass 6,632/6,632 selected cases: sets, relations, numeric observations, cancellation, arithmetic, elementary-core, exponentials and logarithms, general power, trigonometric, hyperbolic, inverse trigonometric, `atan2`, FMA, integer power, extrema, the integer functions, reverse multiplication and the algebraic and trigonometric reverse functions, `powRev1` and `powRev2`. `atan2Rev1` and `atan2Rev2` have no ITF1788 cases; the gate runs 16,260 generated cases of ours (`testdata/interval/generated`, written and validated by `tools/itl_atan2_reverse_oracle.py`) as the separate `atan2-reverse` phase."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:159
msgid "The committed smoke fixtures that `just pr` runs are subsets of these: the binary smoke, for example, has 8,514 rows (288 TestFloat vectors, 3 square-root witnesses, 96 integer-power witnesses, 1,740 elementary witnesses, 3,699 `strtofr` witnesses and 2,688 decimal output witnesses). These counts are read from the manifests (`testdata/binary/corpora.json` and `testdata/binary/smoke/manifest.json`) by `tools/check_manual_counts.py`, which runs with the unit tests of `just pr` and fails when a page and a manifest disagree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:168
msgid "These are finite claims. They do not imply every IEEE 754 or IEEE 1788 operation, arbitrary resource sizes, every NaN payload policy, or future corpus revisions. The per-package [binary](./conformance/binary.md), [IEEE decimal](./conformance/decimal.md), [GDA](./conformance/decimal_gda.md) and [interval](./conformance/interval.md) conformance pages state the exact matrix and its exclusions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:175
msgid "External performance baseline"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:177
msgid "`just bench external run` times `floating` against MPFR and libmpdec on the same operands and records the ratios; every `floating` result is first compared with the result of the reference library for the same operand. The method, machine and results are on the [external baseline page](./performance/bench/external.md). It is evidence about speed, not a conformance gate, and is not part of `just pr`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:183
msgid "Interval differential stage against MPFR"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:185
msgid "`python3 tools/interval_diff.py run` checks `Interval` against MPFR with no timing. A seeded generator writes rows of operand intervals for `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `div`, `sqr`, `sqrt`, `fma`, `exp` and `ln`; `tools/interval_mpfr_oracle.c` computes the tightest result of every row with MPFR directly (one directed rounding per bound, in the exponent range and with the gradual underflow of `binary`, defined once in `tools/mpfr_ours.h`); the test of `tooling/src/differential/interval_mpfr` runs the rows on `Interval` and compares both bounds as canonical text. IEEE 1788-2015 12.10.2 makes the first seven operations tightest, so the bounds must be equal; `exp` and `ln` are only required to be valid, so their result must enclose the tightest one and must not be wider than 1,024 units of the last place (the standard says \"should be accurate\"), with `exp` rows at the overflow and underflow thresholds and `ln` rows at the ends of the range. The classes of operands are ordinary values, results one unit from the overflow threshold on both sides, results and operands in the subnormal band, operands whose exponents differ by far more than the precision, operands with fewer bits than the precision, bounds with more bits than the precision (the interval rounds them outward), and intervals with a zero bound. A class that does not reach its band is an error of the generator, decided from the oracle's own flags. The stage is part of `just gate interval`. An oracle in Python integers, which shares nothing with MPFR, decides every arithmetic row with nothing but CPython and is the required one; MPFR decides all rows, `exp` and `ln` included, when its headers are found, and the two must agree on every arithmetic row. The report says which oracles ran and how many rows each decided, and on a machine without MPFR it lists the rows only MPFR decides as not checked. For `exp` and `ln` it also reports the excess over the tightest bound in units of the last place. A row that differs from MPFR today is listed with its issue in `tools/interval_diff_known.txt`; a new difference, or a listed row that agrees again, fails the run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:213
msgid "Randomized differential testing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:215
msgid "The fixed corpora above check rows someone chose. The randomized campaign (`just random-diff`, [tutorial](./tutorial/differential.md), [design](./design/differential.md)) generates seeded cases and compares the library with two independent implementations: GNU MPFR 4.2.2 (GMP 6.3.0) for `binary` and libmpdec 4.0.0 (Python 3.13 `decimal`) for `decimal_gda` (the IEEE `decimal` package is checked against MPFR, below). Both are oracles, not specifications: every disagreement was read against IEEE 754-2019 or the General Decimal Arithmetic 1.70 text before it was called a bug."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:224
msgid "**What is varied.** Binary: output precisions 2 to 10,000 (a mix of small, format-sized, log-uniform and large), operand widths independent of the output precision, all five MPFR directions plus the nearest-away wrapper, unbounded contexts and bounded ones (binary16/32/64/128 ranges and random ranges) with both tininess rules, operand exponents at the overflow threshold, the smallest normal number and in the subnormal range, exact halfway and near-halfway results (precision one bit below the exact result), cancellation, alignment gaps around the precision, zeros, infinities and NaNs, decimal text in both directions (`strtofr` with exact halfway literals, `getstr` with 1 to 1,200 digits, the shortest round-trip form) and 28 elementary functions (including `pow`, `pown`, `rootn`, `hypot` and `atan2`). Decimal: decimal32/64/128 contexts and random GDA contexts (precision up to 5,000, Emax up to 999,999,999, all eight rounding modes, clamp on and off), the same structured operands, string conversion (`apply`, `tosci`, `toeng`) and `exp`, `ln`, `log10`, `power`. Every value and every flag (inexact, overflow, underflow, invalid, division by zero; the GDA conditions for decimal) is compared."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:242
msgid "**Reproducibility.** Case `I` of family `F` under seed `S` is a pure function of `(S, F, I)`; the seed of every run below is 20261118. Each run is recorded as mare_mark JSONL (validations per batch of 1,000 cases, delta-debugged failures, replay commands) and summarized in `summary.json` next to it. The record of a backend stays in `.tmp/random-diff/<run>/records-<backend>.jsonl`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:248
msgid "**Cases run, per operation and backend.** Native: 26,900,000 cases (28,900,000 executed, counting 2,000,000 `sqrt` cases made before the MPFR defect filter); Wasm, JavaScript and Wasm-GC: 2,730,000 each. Wasm-GC has no mare_mark runtime (the runner needs `moonbitlang/async`), so it ran the same corpora through the synchronous `floating-conformance` runner without the JSONL record. The three other backends ran the first 200,000 (binary) or 100,000 (`decimal_gda`) cases of each family of the native run, so their failing batches can be compared with it: Wasm and JavaScript fail on exactly the same batches as native within that range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:258
msgid "**MPFR (binary)**"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:299
#: manual/verification.md:318
msgid "**total**"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:301
msgid "**libmpdec (`decimal_gda`)**"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:320
msgid "(`sqrt` on native: the table counts the 2,000,000 cases of the final run; the first run of 2,000,000 `sqrt` cases, made before the MPFR defect filter, is not counted: 2,000,000.)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:322
msgid "**Names.** The family prefixes `dec-` (`decimal_gda` against libmpdec) and `decm-` (IEEE `decimal` against MPFR), and the option `--decm`, are now `decimal_gda-`, `decimal-` and `--decimal`; the old spellings are deprecated aliases. The run logs below quote the commands as they were run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:327
msgid "**MPFR enclosures (decimal elementary functions)**, a third corpus (`mpfr-decimal-random-v2`, `just random-diff run --decimal`). The IEEE `decimal` package's elementary functions are checked against MPFR through certified enclosures, not single results: 2048-bit directed operand bounds, 768-bit directed results on every corner, one ulp of slack, both endpoints rounded to the context with Python `decimal`; a row exists only if the endpoints agree and the rounded value lies outside the enclosure (so exact results are not sampled here). Contexts are decimal32/64/128 and random ones (precision 1 to 120, exponent range up to ±20,000; since 0.12 only legal contexts: the five rounding directions of IEEE 754-2019 4.3, `e_min = 1 - e_max`, clamp, tininess before rounding; the campaign counts below are those of the runs before that change); operands are drawn so that results reach overflow, underflow and the subnormal range. Native: 559,979 cases, 20,000 for each of 28 functions (`exp exp2 exp10 expm1 sinh cosh ln log2 log10 log1p sin cos tan sinpi cospi tanpi atan tanh asinh asin acos atanh acosh pow hypot atan2 rootn pown`; `cospi` and `tanh` have a few candidates that never certify within eight attempts); Wasm, JavaScript and Wasm-GC: 55,996 each (the first 2,000 of every function)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:342
msgid "**Outcome.**"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:344
msgid "*Binary arithmetic, string conversion and `shortest`*: no disagreement in 16,500,000 native cases (plus 1,650,000 on each of the other three backends) apart from the MPFR defect below. Every one of the 5 rounding directions, both tininess rules, the exponent limits and the subnormal range were exercised; a mutation check (swapping the two tininess rules, or nearest-even with nearest-away, in the executor) fails 0.15% to 2.8% of the rows, so the rows that decide those cases are present."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:351
msgid "*Decimal elementary functions against MPFR*: 26 of the 28 functions agree with MPFR in value, cohort and flags in every one of their 20,000 native cases, and the same rows pass on Wasm, JavaScript and Wasm-GC. Two disagreements, with the identical failing ids on all four backends: `atan2` returns the zero of an underflowed result with exponent 0 instead of the least exponent `e_min - p + 1` (54 of 20,000 rows, e.g. `atan2(1E-500, 9)` in decimal64 gives `0`, not `0E-398`; IEEE 754-2019 §5.2: an inexact result uses the cohort member of least possible exponent), and `exp` raises no Underflow and Subnormal for a result that underflows to the smallest subnormal when `precision = 1`, the non-standard `AfterRounding` tininess option is selected and the direction is `Up`, `Ceiling` or `ZeroFiveUp` (22 of 20,000 rows; the smallest subnormal equals the smallest normal number then, and the library's own after-rounding rule says the result is tiny; the standard rule for decimal formats, tininess before rounding, was correct). Both are reported separately and become regression rows when fixed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:366
msgid "*Decimal `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `log2p1` and `log10p1`* ([#236](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/236)): families `decm-exp2m1`, `decm-exp10m1`, `decm-log2p1` and `decm-log10p1`, seed 20261118, 250,000 native cases each (1,000,000 in all) agree with MPFR in value, cohort and all eight flags, and so do 100,000 cases each on Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript (400,000 per target, 1,200,000 in all, 804 validated batches, run on 2026-11-19 with `just random-diff run --family decm-exp2m1 --family decm-exp10m1 --family decm-log2p1 --family decm-log10p1 --count 100000 --seed 20261118 --target wasm --target wasm-gc --target js`). On the final code the native 1,000,000 cases of seed 20261118 were run again (1,000 validated batches) and a second seed, 20261202, with 100,000 cases per function on native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript (400,000 per target, 1,600,000 in all, 1,204 validated batches), both on 2026-11-19 and both without a disagreement. Exact results are not sampled here; they, the clusters near the integers and near -1, the tiny arguments and the tininess and clamp boundaries are the 7,175 rows of `library/src/decimal/elementary_pm1_rows_wbtest.mbt` (exact rational arithmetic and certified MPFR enclosures, `tools/generate_decimal_pm1_rows.py`), which pass on native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:385
msgid "*Decimal `rsqrt`, `compound` and `powr`* ([#236](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/236)): families `decm-rsqrt`, `decm-compound` and `decm-powr`; `compound` rows are certified from exact arithmetic (the rational $(1+x)^n$, an exact decimal $1 + nx \\pm 2(nx)^2$ for $|x| < 10^{-150}$, or libmpdec $\\exp(n\\ln(1+x))$ with a proven slack), never from `mpfr_compound_si`, which MPFR 4.2.2 rounds wrongly for $n < 0$ in the directed modes. Seed 20261118: 250,000 native cases per function (750,000) and 100,000 per function on each of Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript (900,000); seed 20261202: 100,000 per function on native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript (1,200,000); all on 2026-11-19 and all in agreement in value, cohort and flags. The 8,459 rows of `library/src/decimal/elementary_rcp_rows_wbtest.mbt` (`tools/generate_decimal_rcp_rows.py`) add the exact cases, the preferred exponents, the `Int32` minimum of `compound`, operands down to $10^{-1\\,999\\,997}$ and the range limits, on all four targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:400
msgid "*Decimal `exp`, `exp2` and `exp10` at the range limits* ([#363](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/363)): families `decimal-exp`, `decimal-exp2` and `decimal-exp10`, 100,000 native cases per function for seed 334 and for seed 20261120 (600,000) and 20,000 per function on each of Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript for seed 334 (180,000), run on 2026-11-20, in agreement with MPFR in value, cohort and flags. The generator reaches the failing arguments only rarely (case `m334.exp2.30728` is one of them), so the boundaries are covered by deterministic rows: `library/src/decimal/exp_range_rows_wbtest.mbt` (`tools/generate_decimal_exp_range_rows.py`, Python `decimal` at 400 digits, a cross-check only) holds 8,305 rows for the context of the issue, decimal32, decimal64, decimal128 and a wide context, the five rounding directions, at results just inside and outside the range and at the thresholds of the decision; they also cover `expm1`, `exp2m1`, `exp10m1`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `pow`, `powr`, `pown`, `compound` and `rsqrt`, none of which failed before the change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:415
msgid "*Decimal `asinPi`, `acosPi`, `atanPi` and `atan2Pi`* ([#236](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/236)): families `decimal-asinpi`, `decimal-acospi`, `decimal-atanpi` and `decimal-atan2pi`, certified by `mpfr_asinpi`, `mpfr_acospi`, `mpfr_atanpi` and `mpfr_atan2pi`. Seed 20261118: 100,000 native cases per function (400,000) and 20,000 per function on each of Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript (240,000), 564 validated batches, run on 2026-11-19 with `just random-diff run --family decimal-asinpi --family decimal-acospi --family decimal-atanpi --family decimal-atan2pi --count 100000 --seed 20261118 --target native` and the same with `--count 20000 --target wasm --target wasm-gc --target js`, in agreement with MPFR in value, cohort and all eight flags. The 9,691 rows of `testdata/decimal/ieee/rows/pi_inverse.txt` (`tools/generate_decimal_pi_inverse_rows.py`) add the exact Niven values at the preferred exponent 0, the clusters next to $\\pm\\tfrac12$ and $\\pm 1$ in the five rounding directions on both sides, operands down to $10^{-1\\,999\\,996}$ and the range limits of decimal32/64/128. They run in the full gate and the nightly run on all four targets, and all of them run in the quick gate (see [Quick-gate table of the π-inverse rows](#quick-gate-table-of-the-π-inverse-rows))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:433
msgid "*Binary elementary functions*: one finding in 1,000,000 native cases (and 300,000 on the other backends): `exp2` of exactly −2^30 returned +0 with no flags where the result 2^−1073741824 is an exact subnormal (13 cases, [#271](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/271)). It is fixed, and its rows are regression rows of `testdata/binary/random-regressions.txt`. After the fix two seeds (20261118 and 20261201) agree with MPFR in 1,000,000 native `elem` cases each. The random sample reaches the range limits rarely, so `tools/range_boundary_rows.py` generates the thresholds directly: for every precision from 2 to 300, six contexts (unbounded, the binary16, 32, 64 and 128 ranges, `e_min = -6`) and the thresholds log2(result) in {q − 1, q, e_min, e_max, e_max + 1}, the arguments of `exp`, `exp2`, `exp10`, `expm1`, `pow`, `pown` and `rootn` are placed on each threshold and at relative offsets of 2^−20 to 2^−64, rounding direction and tininess rule cycling (1,269,554 rows). That sweep found four more disagreements, all fixed with regression rows or tests: `pow` of an exact power of two below 2^e_min ([#272](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/272)), the underflow flag of `pown` under after-rounding tininess near the implementation limit ([#273](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/273)), `expm1` just past its overflow threshold ([#274](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/274)) and `pown` of a value between half and the whole smallest subnormal, rounded to the wrong neighbour ([#278](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/278)); after the fixes it agrees with MPFR in all 1,269,554 rows. A probe of the neighbouring `hypot` found that operands with an exponent beyond ±2^29 give 0 or infinity without flags ([#275](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/275), fixed): the random generator does not draw such operands and the sweep does not include that function."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:461
msgid "*`rSqrt`, `compound` and `powr`* (family `elem3` of the generator): 1,000,000 native cases and 100,000 on each of Wasm, JavaScript and Wasm-GC agree with MPFR 4.2.2, and so do 718,824 range-boundary rows (precisions 2 to 300, six contexts, both tininess rules; the thresholds of $1/\\sqrt{x}$, $(1 + x)^n$ for $n \\in \\{\\pm 3, \\pm 7, \\pm 100\\}$ and $x^y$ with $y$ placed on $\\log_2$ of each threshold). The comparison needed two corrections of the oracle, both documented in `testdata/binary/README.md`: `mpfr_compound_si` is wrong for a negative `n` in the directed modes and near the range limits (proved with exact rational arithmetic on all 2,017 boundary rows; the rows use the exact rational instead), and `mpfr_rec_sqrt(-0)` is +inf where the standard says -inf. The library had no disagreement in either case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:472
msgid "*`asinPi`, `acosPi`, `atanPi` and `atan2Pi`* (family `elem4` of the generator): 1,000,000 native cases and 100,000 on each of Wasm, JavaScript and Wasm-GC agree with MPFR 4.2.2 (`mpfr_asinpi`, `mpfr_acospi`, `mpfr_atanpi`, `mpfr_atan2pi`), and so do 1,654,315 range-boundary rows (precisions 2 to 300, six contexts, both tininess rules): the underflow thresholds ($\\operatorname{asinPi}(x) \\approx x/\\pi$ at $x = \\pi 2^{k}$), the anchors $\\pm 1/2$ and $\\pm 1$ for $\\operatorname{atanPi}$ of a huge argument, $\\operatorname{acosPi}$ of a tiny one and $\\operatorname{atan2Pi}$ of an extreme ratio (leading-exponent difference $D$ from $\\pm(p + 4)$ to $\\pm(p + 12)$, around the point $|D| = p + 8$ where the library switches to the anchor rule), and arguments next to $\\pm 1$. 11,661 `atan2pi` rows with operands more than 100,000 binades apart are not oracle rows: `mpfr_atan2pi` of MPFR 4.2.2 slows down with the exponent difference (5 s at 2^(2*10^7)), so they are skipped and covered by `library/src/binary/pi_inverse_wbtest.mbt` against an independent model and against `atanpi` of an exact power of two. The library had no disagreement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:488
msgid "*Decimal arithmetic*: `multiply`, `divide`, `squareroot`, `apply`, `tosci` and `toeng` agreed with libmpdec in all 6,000,000 native cases. `add`, `subtract` and `fma` disagreed in 83 of 3,000,000 (overflow reported when an operand rounds past Nmax: [#264](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/264)). The campaign's first run had 10 further disagreements whose operand was above Emax; decTest converts operands subject to the directives, so they are a property of the rows, not findings (the generator now keeps operands within Emax)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:496
msgid "*Decimal `power`*: 100,000 native cases disagreed with libmpdec in at least 300 of them; four defects are filed: the sign of an overflowing result ([#265](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/265)), trailing zeros of inexact results ([#266](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/266)), a spurious Clamped ([#267](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/267)) and missing Subnormal and Underflow ([#268](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/floating/issues/268)). One more symptom (the exact result `power(0.05, -8) = 2.56E+10` raises Rounded) is not filed yet. `exp`, `ln` and `log10` agreed in all 300,000 native cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:506
msgid "**Known intentional differences, filtered where the rows are generated.** The numbers above exclude, and the test-data READMEs document, these cases: MPFR has no subnormals (emulated with `mpfr_subnormalize`; nearest-away rows that would be subnormal are drawn in nearest-even) and its range is one binade narrower than that of `Binary` at the extreme limits (the rows set MPFR's range to the row's own range, so those limits are compared); NaN payloads are not compared; the decimal elementary functions answer `Invalid_context` when |Emax| or |Emin| reaches 10^6 (the decNumber `DEC_MAX_MATH` limit the package adopts as a deliberate implementation limit, which decTest requires and GDA 1.70's Invalid context condition permits, but which the operation text does not state; opt-in `decimal_gda-*wide` families sample those contexts); libmpdec decides Subnormal and Underflow of `exp`, `ln`, `log10` and `power` after rounding where GDA 1.70 says before any rounding, so elementary rows keep Emin below 0; `apply` of a signaling NaN is not specified; `tosci` and `toeng` apply the context precision as decTest specifies."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:520
msgid "**An oracle defect.** `mpfr_sqrt` of MPFR 4.2.2 (Homebrew bottle, arm64) reports an exact result, or a result on the wrong side of the root, for some operands with an odd binary exponent whose precision is near the output precision (`tools/mpfr_sqrt_ternary_repro.c` is a standalone reproducer). The first run of 2,000,000 `sqrt` cases had 22 failing batches from it; the generator now checks each `sqrt` row with exact arithmetic and draws the case again when MPFR is wrong, and the final run of 2,000,000 `sqrt` cases agreed in all cases. This is a claim about the Homebrew build I used, not about every MPFR release."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:529
msgid "**Not covered.** Operations the oracles lack (`rem`, `roundToIntegral`, the integer conversions, `scaleB`/`logB`, decimal operations other than the thirteen families); NaN payload policy; nearest-away results in the subnormal range; decimal contexts with |Emax| or |Emin| of 10^6 or more for the elementary functions; precisions above 700 for the binary elementary functions; the LLVM backend; interval arithmetic. The campaign samples a finite space with fixed seeds: the counts are what was run, not a proof of absence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:537
msgid "Incremental verification"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:539
msgid "A full `just gate binary` took about 19 minutes on 2026-11-21 on one development machine with 3 jobs, 17 of them the 534 TestFloat tasks. Incremental verification does not run a verification unit again when what it could depend on has not changed, and says so in the report. It is available for the binary gate (`just verify binary`, or `python3 tools/conformance.py run --backend binary --incremental`); the full gates stay, nightly and before a release, as the audit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:545
msgid "What a unit is, and what \"reused\" means"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:547
msgid "A unit is one TestFloat task (operation, format, rounding, tininess) or one MPFR corpus file. A unit is **reused** when its key is in the ledger: the recorded result of the same unit, run on the commit the report names, is cited and the unit is not executed in this run. A reused unit is never counted as run: the report has separate lines for the units executed in this run, the sampled reruns and the reused units, and the `TOTAL` line says that it counts only what was executed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:553
msgid "The key of a unit is the hash of:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:555
msgid "part"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:555
msgid "what it is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:557
msgid "frame"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:557
msgid "the fingerprint of the compiled code reachable from the entry of the frontend (`run` of the TestFloat or MPFR frontend), start-up code included, the dispatcher covered by its own text and the operation functions left out"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:558
msgid "arms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:558
msgid "the fingerprints of the operation functions (`arm_add`, ...) that the process recorded at run time (the summary prints them as `arms`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:559
msgid "input"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:559
msgid "the sha256 of the data, or for TestFloat the sha256 of the generator binary with its arguments and seed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:560
msgid "arguments"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:560
msgid "the arguments of the interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:561
msgid "toolchain"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:561
msgid "`moon`, `moonc`, the C compiler, the compile and link command lines that `moon` prints, the runtime sources, headers and objects, the native stub sources of the library and of the dependencies, and the environment variables the stubs read (`LUNA_FLOATING_PORTABLE`, `LUNA_FLOATING_LIMBS`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:562
msgid "verifier"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:562
msgid "the `tools/` modules the runner has imported, so a change to a judging tool reruns everything"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:563
msgid "normalisations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:563
msgid "the set of normalisations of the fingerprint, and the version of the key"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:565
msgid "The fingerprint is taken from the C file that the native backend generates (about 18 MB, 6,300 definitions). It splits the file into definitions and hashes each one after four normalisations and nothing else: `#line` directives are deleted; names that are local to one definition are renamed by kind and order of appearance; names that carry a compiler counter (closures, records, string literals, generic instances) are renamed by where they are first referenced; and object-header layout indices are replaced by the layout entry they select. The last is the only one that rests on a fact about the runtime (the index is read only as a table position) and it is a flag of the tool (`layout=True`, `--no-layout` to compare). Whatever it cannot attribute to a definition goes into a residue that is part of every key. When in doubt a unit runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:575
msgid "The guards"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:577
msgid "Every run executes a random 2 % of the units it would reuse (at least one), prints the seed (`--sample-seed` fixes it) and compares the result with the ledger. A difference is a defect of the system, not a verification failure: the exit status is 70 and the line stops."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:580
msgid "The key is bound to the executable that runs: `build_backend` writes `<exe>.build.json` with the hashes of the executable and of the C file; any mismatch, or a key that cannot be formed (an entry that is not in the build, a parse error), prints `incremental disabled: <reason>`, runs every unit and records nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:584
msgid "A unit is recorded only when it passed. The process says which frontend ran, and a mismatch with the unit is a defect."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:586
msgid "The system has its own mutation test (`python3 tools/verify_mutation.py`): edits to the kernel, to `binary` arithmetic and elementary functions, to `interval`, to both decimal packages, to the TestFloat frontend and to an initialiser. For every edit that a vector or corpus can see, the whole selection is also run on the mutated build, and a unit that fails there must not have been reused (the check that shares no code with the keys); comment-only and formatting-only edits must change no key. `python3 tools/verify_mutation.py --stages` does the same for the whole-stage units of the `decimal` and `decimal_gda` gates, on keys: an edit or a comment in a package a stage reaches, an edited corpus row and a comment in the oracle program must change the key of exactly the stages that read them, an edit outside every stage must change none, and the edited oracle row must make the stage fail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:593
msgid "The ledger"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:595
msgid "`.tmp/verification-ledger/ledger.jsonl` of the main checkout, shared by all worktrees on the machine (`--ledger` or `LUNA_VERIFICATION_LEDGER` chooses another). It is append-only and not committed; a record holds the key, its components (frame, input, toolchain, verifier, arm fingerprints), the result, the commit, the date, the host and the path of the log. A result recorded from a dirty tree is as valid as any: the key is of the code that was built, the commit is information only (shown as `+dirty`), so the ledger is never cleaned by commit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:602
msgid "What a change costs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:604
msgid "Measured by replaying the keys over 30 consecutive commits of `main` (2026-11-21, 62ac93e0 to 95fae256, one build per commit, `tools/verify_replay.py`), by the cause of the change:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:607
msgid "kind of change"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:607
msgid "commits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:607
msgid "units that would run (of 538)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:609
msgid "no key changes (documentation, tests, code no unit reaches)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:610
msgid "only the MPFR frontend changes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:610
msgid "4 (the MPFR files, under a second)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:611
msgid "code that every TestFloat task reaches changes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:611
#: manual/verification.md:612
msgid "538 (the whole stage)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:612
msgid "the start-up code changes (the initialisers of top-level values)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:614
msgid "The start-up code is part of every key, so one changed initialiser reruns every unit; in this window that happened once. Few commits rerun the whole stage, because most changes are in code that no TestFloat task reaches."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:618
msgid "The kernel stages"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:620
msgid "The seven kernel stages of the binary gate (the product routes and the oracles under three states of the limb tier, and the oracle tables) took about 220 s and always ran in full. The package under test, `library/src/internal/binary_kernel`, is a leaf of the module: it imports only `moonbitlang/core/...`, so nothing else of the repository is compiled into its test executable. Each stage under each state is therefore a unit of its own, keyed by the content of the package's files (sources, tests, row tables, the native stub, `moon.pkg`) and of the module files, the command line (which holds the state), the `moon test` wrapper, the build commands that `moon test --dry-run` prints, the toolchain, the variables of the environment that `moon` and the stubs read, the verifier and, for the oracle tables, `tools/kernel_oracle.py` and the Python version. The key is of file content, which is coarser than code: a comment-only edit of the package reruns all seven. The program checks the two assumptions behind it: the package imports only `moonbitlang/core/...` (otherwise no kernel unit is reused) and `tools/kernel_oracle.py` imports only the standard library (otherwise that unit runs)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:633
msgid "A reused kernel stage is printed as `REUSED` with the commit it ran on and is not executed. A stage that ran is recorded when it passes and when its files are still those of the key (a stage runs for minutes: an edit meanwhile is reported as \"passed but not recorded\"). Each reusable stage is rerun with probability 2 %, drawn per stage (the \"at least one\" rule of the TestFloat units would rerun a stage on every run with only seven units); the seed is printed, and a sampled stage that fails although its key is recorded as passing is a defect of the system (status 70). `just verify binary` runs the stages this way; `--full-kernel` runs them in full."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:641
msgid "The stages of the interval, GDA and decimal gates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:643
msgid "`just verify interval`, `just verify decimal_gda` and `just verify decimal` run five whole stages as units, with the same ledger, `REUSED` lines, 2 % sampling and exit status 70 as the kernel stages. Two are `moon test` stages (the reverse trigonometric reference tests of `interval`, and the package tests of `decimal_gda`): the key is the content of the packages and of everything they import inside the two modules, read from `moon.pkg`, with the module files, the command, the build commands of `moon test --dry-run`, the toolchain and the verifier. A package whose closure imports the file system, the environment or async is not keyed, because a test that cannot import them cannot read a data file at run time; and a `moon test` run that selects no test, or a package that is not found, fails instead of passing. The other two are Python stages (the oracle checks of the interval gate, about two minutes, and the table of the decimal kernel oracle of the `decimal` gate, which recomputes the committed rows of `library/src/internal/decimal_kernel` from CPython integers and compares them): its key is a declared set of files and directories written in `tools/stage_units.py`, chosen from an audit of one run and rounded up to whole directories, with the `sys.version` of the interpreter of the stage command (asked of that interpreter, not taken from the verifier) and the directory of the audit hook, which is an input of every unit. What a run happened to open is not a key, so every real run of the stage executes under an audit hook: the process of the stage reports what it opens and lists, and a read of anything below the repository outside the declared set fails the stage with exit status 70 and the path: the declaration cannot go stale silently. A path with a `.git` or `__pycache__` part is left out of the key of a declared directory, so a read of one is undeclared too, unless the unit declares that very file. Every such run also gets an empty directory of its own as the cache of compiled modules (`PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX`), so that a compiled module of the tree, stale or made by hand, cannot run in place of a source file that the key covers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:664
msgid "A pass of a Python stage is recorded only when the log of the hook shows that the audit saw the whole run. The log must hold exactly one start line, the one of the stage's own process, and no launch event: nothing that starts or replaces a process (`subprocess.Popen`, `os.posix_spawn`, `os.exec*`, `os.spawn*`, `os.system`, `os.fork`, `os.forkpty`, `pty.spawn`, `os.startfile`, `webbrowser.open`) and no `ctypes` event. A Python process started with `-I`, `-E` or `-S` writes no start line; a second start line without a launch event is another Python process, for example a `multiprocessing` child. A stage that starts children through one of these events is therefore never recorded, whatever the children read; if a later unit needs children, that is a design of its own. Besides, the start line has the version of the key, the stage read no compiled module from the tree, and it read or listed nothing outside the repository except the standard library of the interpreter (not its site-packages), the directories that are entries of the interpreter's own `sys.path` at start-up (the import system looking for a module; opening a file below one of them is still an outside read), the cache directory of the run, and what the run created before reading it (a file that did not exist and that an open with `O_CREAT` creates, whatever the mode, as `tempfile` does; a directory that `os.mkdir` creates, and everything below it). A file that existed is not the run's own, with any mode. A read or a listing outside the repository that the key does not cover (a cache file, a toolchain file, a shared library, an installed package, a directory of another tool) does not fail the stage; the stage passes, `verify` says why it was not recorded, and the stage runs again next time. A stage that imports an installed package is therefore never recorded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:683
msgid "The audit is a check against an honest mistake, not a sandbox. It finds an input that the author of a stage forgot to declare, through the events that CPython reports. A stage written to deceive it, for example by starting a process through a private or foreign-function interface that raises no launch event (`_posixsubprocess.fork_exec`, `ctypes`), is outside what it claims. One such route is not a deception but a configuration: `multiprocessing` with the `spawn` method after `set_executable` of a program that is not Python starts the child through `_posixsubprocess.fork_exec`, raises no launch event and writes no second start line, so that run is recorded although a child ran. The stage scripts are code of the repository and are reviewed as such, and the 2 % reruns of the verifier exist for what a key misses. A `ctypes` event, which is a call into a foreign library, is still refused."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:693
msgid "What the audit still cannot see: `os.stat`, `os.path.exists`, `os.path.isfile`, `os.path.getsize`, `os.path.getmtime`, `os.access` and `os.readlink` raise no audit event, so a stage whose result depends on whether a file exists, how large or how old it is, or where a link points, without opening it, is not seen (only a directory that the key lists as a whole covers a new file in it); a stage whose result depends only on whether an installed package exists is not seen either, because the import system's own listing of the package directories is not counted; environment variables, the clock, random numbers and sockets are not files and the hook does not log them; and a process started through an interface that raises no launch event is not seen. The oracle checks of the interval gate need the ITF1788 corpus, which the continuous-integration runner does not have, so the real unit is not run there; two tests that run on every interpreter show that a stage that reads only declared files, and a stage that imports only standard-library modules, are recorded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:705
msgid "The fifth is the interval corpus run (the IEEE 1788 stage): a Python runner that builds `tooling/src/cli` and runs the executable on the corpus files, so no audit hook can see what it reads. Its key is the content of the packages that the executable imports inside the two modules, the module files, the build commands of `moon --dry-run` for the executable, the wrapper, the content of `testdata/interval/`, the content of every file of `tools/` that the runner can import (read from the syntax tree; a dynamic or relative import disables the unit), the interpreter's version, the toolchain, the verifier and the environment. A pass needs exit status 0 and exactly one `PASS  TOTAL  n/n passed` line with n above zero. The key is of source files, not of compiled code, so any edit of those packages reruns the stage; the stage is mostly the build of the executable (the 22,892 cases run in under a second), which is what a reuse skips. In a fresh worktree the first recorded key may never be formed again: the toolchain digest counts the stub files under `.mooncakes`, which exist only after the dependency stage ran. The cost is one false rerun, never a false reuse."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:718
msgid "The stages that stay full are listed by `verify`: the differential stage of the interval gate (it builds a C oracle against the host's MPFR and GMP), and the three short stages of the GDA gate (the frontend tests and the two decTest corpora, about seven seconds)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:722
msgid "The native tests of the quick gate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:724
msgid "`tools/native_units.py` runs `moon test --target native` of the packages as units (the six heavy packages one each, the rest in one unit). The key of a unit hashes every file of the packages of the unit itself, and of the packages it only imports every file except the test files: `_wbtest.mbt`, `_test.mbt` and `.mbt.md`. Moon compiles those into the test executables of their own package, never into a package that imports it, so an edit of a test file of `interval` reruns the `interval` unit and not `decimal` or `decimal_gda`. The program checks the naming against moon's own dry run: a build of a package that is not a test build and names a test file turns the rule off, and the key says which rule was used. Replayed over 30 consecutive commits of `main` (2026-11-25, 139e339b to 526b2a3e), the rule reduced the units that would run from 61 to 27 and the release run time of the blocks they hold from 777 s to 417 s. A key per test block was measured and rejected: only release builds write C that can be fingerprinted, every block of the heavy packages reaches a shared mutable cache, and reading the C of the packages above a changed one cost more than the reuse saved."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:737
msgid "What it does not cover"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:739
msgid "The decimal gate (`just gate decimal` runs generated `moon test` fixtures on native, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript), the elementary gate, the other `moon test` stages (units per test block), the other targets, and the full stages listed above are outside this version."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:743
msgid "Reproducibility"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:745
msgid "External artifacts are pinned by revision and SHA-256 in each corpus manifest (`testdata/*/corpora.json`). Builds use backend-named outputs and isolated target directories so that parallel jobs do not overwrite each other. Shards select deterministic case indices, and merged summaries keep exact totals and failed IDs. Large generated fixtures are split across files (the IEEE decimal public-API fixture is written 400 tests per file) so that every toolchain compiles them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:753
msgid "The MoonBit frontends parse and execute numeric rows. Python orchestrates downloads, task planning, subprocesses, target selection and aggregation. An optional oracle is never silently replaced by a weaker one; a missing requirement is reported explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:758
msgid "Performance evidence is separate from semantic conformance. Benchmark manifests pin the baseline source, dependencies, toolchain, target, schedule, sample count and dispersion limits, and a performance threshold never changes correctness. See [performance audit](./performance_audit.md) and the per-package performance pages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:764
msgid "Failure triage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:766
msgid "Re-run the failing backend with the smallest case, ID filter, phase or shard that reproduces it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:768
msgid "Distinguish parse diagnostics, unsupported cases, legacy classifications, executable mismatches and infrastructure failures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:770
msgid "Record the expected value, actual value, flags, context, target, corpus revision and command."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:772
msgid "Run the matching white-box package test to decide whether the defect is in parsing, arithmetic, interchange or aggregation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:774
msgid "After a fix, run the focused case, the committed smoke fixture, the backend gate, and finally `just pr` or `just ci`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:777
msgid "Do not weaken strict support, discard flags, or change the denominator to make a failing gate pass."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:780
msgid "Release gate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:782
msgid "Before publishing:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:784
msgid "align `library/moon.mod`, `library/README.md`, the manual and `library/CHANGELOG.md`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:785
msgid "run `just docs`, `lunadoc check`, and inspect generated interface differences;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:787
msgid "run `just pr` while iterating;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:788
msgid "run `just ci` on the release candidate;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:789
msgid "publish through the repository's `publish.yml` GitHub Actions workflow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:791
msgid "Local `moon publish` is not the Luna-Flow release path, because the organization's credentials are supplied by the workflow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:794
msgid "Conformance checklist"
msgstr ""

#: manual/verification.md:796
msgid "One line per row of the clause traceability tables of the conformance pages ([binary](./conformance/binary.md#clause-traceability), [IEEE decimal](./conformance/decimal.md#clause-traceability), [GDA](./conformance/decimal_gda.md#clause-traceability), [interval](./conformance/interval.md#clause-traceability)). A checked box is a `conforming` row; an unchecked box names its state (`partial`, `not implemented`, `not applicable`) and carries the clause level in brackets: `required` (the standard says \"shall\"), `recommended` (\"should\") or `optional`. The counts separate gaps in required clauses from gaps in recommended or optional ones. The block is generated by `tools/conformance_checklist.py` and `just docs` fails when it is stale."
msgstr ""

